window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"jelly-roll-morton-black-bottom-stomp":["Three minutes that contain more incident than most records twice the length. Morton's Red Hot Peppers — George Mitchell on cornet, Omer Simeon on clarinet, Kid Ory on trombone, Johnny St. Cyr on banjo — move through two strains and a string of contrasting choruses, each with a different texture: full ensemble, stop-time cornet, banjo alone, piano alone, everyone together for the ride-out. Nothing repeats without being changed.","Recorded for Victor in Chicago in September 1926, with a rehearsal discipline unusual in jazz at the time. Morton hired New Orleans players he knew, drilled them for hours, told each man where to improvise and where to stay out, and then paid for a second session when he was not satisfied. Victor's electrical recording process, new that year, finally let a rhythm section be heard properly.","Listen closer: the breaks are the architecture. Morton drops the whole band out for two bars at a time, roughly a dozen times, handing the gap to a different instrument each time, so the record's momentum comes from silences rather than volume. Notice too the shift into a Charleston rhythm behind the last choruses, a dance figure then only three years old.","This is the record that settles whether jazz could be composed. Writers from the 1930s onwards used it to argue that arrangement and improvisation need not be enemies, and Gunther Schuller's analysis of it became a standard piece of jazz scholarship. Morton's reputation collapsed in the swing years and was rebuilt largely on these Red Hot Peppers sides.","Count the choruses on a second hearing and name the featured instrument in each; the variety is the point and it is easy to miss at speed. Listen for the two-bar holes. Then play Ellington's later suites to hear where composed jazz went next. Any decent transfer of the Victor original will do; the electrical sound is surprisingly full."],"jelly-roll-morton-dead-man-blues":["A slow blues that opens with a spoken exchange between two of the musicians, joking about a funeral, over a trombone playing Chopin's funeral march. The joke over, the band settles into a medium blues: cornet lead, a trombone chorus, and then the passage everyone remembers, three clarinets in close harmony winding around each other above the rhythm like a small woodwind choir, before the ensemble takes it out.","Cut for Victor in Chicago in 1926 at the same run of Red Hot Peppers sessions as Black Bottom Stomp. Morton was recreating, for a record-buying public far from Louisiana, the New Orleans funeral procession: dirge on the way to the cemetery, stomp on the way back. He had grown up hearing those bands. The spoken introduction is part vaudeville and part documentary.","Listen closer: that three-clarinet trio is written, not improvised, and it is the most obviously composed thing on any Morton record — a texture borrowed from the concert band rather than from jazz. Morton hired two extra clarinettists purely for those choruses. The Chopin quotation at the start is played straight, which is what makes the transition into the blues so abrupt.","For some listeners the comic opening dates the record badly; for others it is the only surviving trace of how a New Orleans band actually addressed an audience. The trio chorus has been the piece's defence since the 1930s revival: it demonstrates Morton's ear for orchestration on a scale nobody else in jazz was attempting in 1926.","Get past the spoken gag and wait for the clarinets; play that chorus again on its own. Then notice how the cornet stays deliberately plain so the harmony behind it can be heard. Compare with Black Bottom Stomp for the other side of Morton — manic variety there, sustained mood here. A good Victor transfer keeps the three clarinets separate."],"jelly-roll-morton-library-of-congress-recordings":["Eight hours of one man at a piano in an empty auditorium, talking and playing. Morton narrates his life and the life of New Orleans, demonstrating at the keyboard as he goes: how a quadrille turned into a stomp, how ragtime differed from jazz, how three different pianists he had known would have played the same tune. He sings, swears, boasts, and plays some of the finest solo piano of his career.","Recorded in 1938 in the Coolidge Auditorium at the Library of Congress by Alan Lomax, who was then building the Archive of American Folk Song and had found Morton running a shabby club in Washington, forgotten by the music business. Lomax expected a folk informant and got a professional with a thesis. The discs were acetates, made for an archive rather than for sale.","Listen closer: Morton keeps returning to what he calls the Spanish tinge, the habanera lilt in the left hand that he insisted was essential to jazz — and then demonstrates the same tune with and without it, which is the clearest lesson in the whole archive. He also names Buddy Bolden and describes his playing; this is close to the only testimony of that kind we have.","Historians have spent eighty years separating Morton's documentary value from his self-mythology, beginning with his claim to have invented jazz in 1902. Lomax's own book drawn from the sessions was criticised for shaping the material into a narrative. Yet no other early jazz musician left anything comparable, and the piano playing alone would justify the set.","Do not try to hear it whole. Start with the quadrille-into-jazz demonstration and the Spanish-tinge passages, then follow with any of the extended solo performances. Play the 1923 King Porter Stomp beside its 1938 counterpart to hear fifteen years of thinking. The complete Rounder edition restores the sequence and the profanity that earlier issues cut."],"armstrong-heebie-jeebies":["A cheerful medium-tempo novelty tune played by the Hot Five — Armstrong's cornet, Johnny Dodds's clarinet, Kid Ory's trombone, Lil Hardin's piano, Johnny St. Cyr's banjo — with one extraordinary event in the middle. Armstrong sings a verse of lyrics, and then, for a full chorus, sings nonsense syllables instead of words, phrasing them exactly as he would phrase a cornet solo.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in February 1926, at the second Hot Five session. The Hot Five never played a live engagement; it existed only in the studio, assembled from musicians working elsewhere around the city. Okeh marketed the discs to Black buyers on its race records list, and this one sold in numbers that made Armstrong a name well beyond the South Side.","Listen closer: the wordless chorus was not the first scat singing ever recorded, whatever the legend says, and the story that Armstrong dropped the lyric sheet mid-take is almost certainly a sales department's invention. What matters is the phrasing — he treats the voice as a brass instrument, accenting off the beat and sliding between notes in ways no popular singer of 1926 attempted.","Contemporary buyers treated it as a comedy record; critics later treated it as the moment jazz singing began. Both readings survive. Armstrong's vocal style, imitated first by Cab Calloway and Bing Crosby and eventually by everyone, arguably changed popular music more than his trumpet playing did, and this three-minute side is where the public first heard it.","Ignore the tune and listen only to the voice, then play any Armstrong cornet solo from the same year and hear the same rhythmic habits. Notice how the band stiffens slightly behind him — they are still playing 1923 while he is playing something newer. Compare with Ella Fitzgerald's scat singing twenty years on."],"armstrong-potato-head-blues":["The Hot Seven, the Hot Five plus tuba and drums, playing a bright multi-strain tune. Ensemble choruses and a clarinet solo lead to the chorus the record exists for: Armstrong improvising over stop-time, where the band hits a single chord every two bars and then falls silent, leaving him alone in the gaps. He builds a line that carries across those holes as if the beat were still sounding.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in May 1927, in the week-long burst of sessions that produced the Hot Seven sides. Armstrong was by then playing nightly in Chicago theatre orchestras and had switched, or was about to switch, from cornet to trumpet. Okeh brought in the extra players to give the records a fuller dance-band bottom; the tuba's presence dates the sound instantly.","Listen closer: the stop-time chorus works because Armstrong is not filling the silences — he plays across them, starting phrases before the band's chord and finishing after it, so the listener supplies the missing beats. Count along and you will find yourself carrying the pulse for him. This displacement of accent, more than any particular note, is what his contemporaries meant by swing.","The stop-time chorus has been transcribed and analysed more often than almost anything else in early jazz, and it is routinely cited as the first fully formed extended improvisation on record. Its fame reached beyond music when Woody Allen's Manhattan named it among the things that make life worth living, which sent a generation of filmgoers to the Hot Seven.","Skip to the stop-time chorus and tap the beat out loud; the whole effect depends on the pulse you keep in your head. Then go back and hear how ordinary the tune itself is. Follow with West End Blues from the next year. The 1927 Okeh sound is dry and close — any well-mastered Hot Five and Sevens set works."],"armstrong-struttin-with-some-barbecue":["A buoyant medium-fast tune for the Hot Five, built on a simple rising figure that sticks after one hearing. Kid Ory's trombone and Johnny Dodds's clarinet take their turns, and Armstrong closes with a chorus that starts low and conversational and climbs, chorus-long, to a held high note over the final ensemble. The band is small and dry-sounding; there is nowhere to hide.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in December 1927, at the last of the Hot Five sessions with the original New Orleans personnel. The tune is credited to Lil Hardin Armstrong, the pianist on the date and Armstrong's wife, who had pushed him to leave King Oliver's band and go out under his own name. Their marriage was by then coming apart; the credit was later disputed for years.","Listen closer: Armstrong's final chorus is not a new melody but a paraphrase — he keeps the tune's shape while displacing nearly every note away from where the beat expects it. Trumpeters have used this chorus as a teaching text ever since, and it turns up almost verbatim, harmonised for a full brass section, in later big-band arrangements of the tune.","Long treated as a lighter companion to Potato Head Blues, the record has risen in critical standing as scholars have taken Lil Hardin Armstrong's role seriously; the authorship question is now part of a wider re-examination of the women written out of early jazz. The tune itself became a Dixieland-revival staple, played faster and more crudely than the original almost every time.","Learn the tune in the first chorus, then follow Armstrong's last chorus against it and hear him bend it out of shape without ever losing it. Notice how little the rhythm section does. Compare a 1950s revival-band version to hear what gets lost when the tempo goes up. Seek a transfer that keeps the banjo audible."],"armstrong-west-end-blues":["Three minutes and fifteen seconds of twelve-bar blues that begin with a shock: an unaccompanied trumpet cadenza, twelve seconds of fanfare and cascade, before any beat arrives. Then a slow blues. Armstrong plays the theme, the trombone takes a chorus, Armstrong hums wordlessly in dialogue with the clarinet, Earl Hines plays a solo chorus of glittering right-hand runs, and the trumpet returns to hold one high note for four bars before spilling downward.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in June 1928 by a reconstituted Hot Five built around Earl Hines, a pianist as advanced as Armstrong himself. The tune was King Oliver's, named for a resort on Lake Pontchartrain, and recording his old teacher's blues was a deliberate gesture. Electrical recording, two years old, could now capture the full range of a trumpet without distortion — which is why the opening works at all.","Listen closer: the wordless vocal chorus is a duet, Armstrong answering Jimmy Strong's clarinet phrase for phrase with the same inflections, so that voice and instrument become indistinguishable. And the famous held note near the end is not held for effect alone — it sets up a descending run that resolves the whole record, which is why the chorus feels finished rather than merely loud.","Musicians treated the opening cadenza as a gauntlet from the moment it appeared; it was memorised, transcribed and quoted for decades. Critics from Gunther Schuller onwards have called this the record on which jazz proves it can carry the weight of serious music, and it is among the most frequently named recordings in any list of the century's essential performances.","Play the first fifteen seconds twice before hearing the rest. Then follow the record as a sequence of four voices — trumpet, trombone, voice with clarinet, piano — and notice how each raises the stakes. Compare Oliver's own version of the tune. Any of the standard Hot Five and Sevens reissues carry it in good sound."],"armstrong-weather-bird":["Two musicians, no rhythm section, no safety net: Armstrong on trumpet and Earl Hines on piano running through an old three-strain rag at speed. They trade the lead, interrupt each other, leave gaps the other has to fill, and repeatedly arrive at the same beat from different directions. The final exchange, a sequence of short breaks traded back and forth, ends on an unresolved joke of a cadence.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in December 1928, at the end of the year in which Armstrong and Hines had been working together nightly. The tune was King Oliver's Weather Bird Rag, which Armstrong had recorded with the Creole Jazz Band five years earlier as a seven-piece; stripping it to two instruments was a private demonstration of how far both men had travelled since.","Listen closer: what makes it dizzying is that neither player keeps time for the other. Hines abandons the stride left hand for long stretches, so the pulse exists only as an agreement between them; both repeatedly displace their phrases by a beat and then meet again exactly where they should. Near the end Hines simply stops, daring Armstrong to hang in the air, and he does.","Critics have reached for counterpoint and for the word conversation ever since, and the record is regularly described as the first jazz duet of real equality between horn and piano. Some listeners find it wilfully tricky next to the gravity of West End Blues from six months earlier. Both descriptions are fair; it is the sound of two players showing off to each other.","Listen once for pleasure and once following only the piano; Hines is doing at least as much as the trumpet. Try to keep the beat yourself through the final exchanges. Then hear the 1923 Creole Jazz Band version of the same rag for the distance travelled. Transfers vary; look for one that does not smother the piano."],"armstrong-st-james-infirmary":["A slow minor-key lament taken at funeral pace. Armstrong sings the old ballad of a man who goes down to the infirmary and finds his lover dead on a table, in a voice by turns conversational and raw, then plays a trumpet chorus that keeps the same shape as the sung melody but darkens it. Earl Hines's piano fills the spaces with unsettled chords rather than comfort.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in December 1928. The song is old and its ancestry is tangled — a British broadside about a soldier dying of disease, reshaped in America and claimed as a copyright by a publisher writing under a pseudonym. Armstrong's version fixed it in the repertoire, and it has been recorded by hundreds of singers since, in almost every idiom.","Listen closer: the band plays in a minor key, which early jazz rarely did, and the harmony under the vocal keeps refusing to settle — that instability is what gives the record its chill rather than any lyric detail. Note also how Armstrong sings the word table: he stretches it across the bar line, the same displacement he uses on the trumpet.","It became one of the two or three songs an Armstrong audience always demanded, and he sang it for forty years. Critics who preferred the instrumental Hot Fives long treated his vocal records as a step towards showbusiness; later writers have argued the opposite, that this is where his sense of a song's drama first shows fully. The copyright history remains murky.","Hear it next to Kurt Weill's Berlin ballads of the same year — two very different cities telling stories about corpses over a dance beat. Listen for the piano's refusal to resolve. Then compare a later Armstrong concert version, slower and more theatrical. The 1928 Okeh original has a bleakness the live versions trade away."],"earl-hines-57-varieties":["Three minutes of solo piano that keep changing their mind. Hines states a tune, abandons it, plays octave runs in the right hand like a trumpet line, drops into shimmering tremolos, and repeatedly lets the left-hand beat disappear for a bar or two before catching it again. The title is a joke about the brands of pickle, and the piece really does present that many ideas without settling on one.","Recorded in Chicago in December 1928 for the QRS company, better known for piano rolls, during the same extraordinary year in which Hines made the Armstrong sides and opened at the Grand Terrace ballroom with his own big band. He was twenty-five. Solo piano records had been dominated by the Harlem stride players; a Pittsburgh-born pianist working in Chicago was proposing something else.","Listen closer: the trick everyone borrowed is the right-hand octave tremolo. Hines plays a melody note doubled an octave above and shakes it, which on an acoustic piano imitates a trumpeter's vibrato and lets a single line cut through a loud band. It is why his style was called trumpet piano, and why it worked on a bandstand where stride would have vanished.","Critics have always found Hines's solo records harder to love than his work with Armstrong — the deliberate collapses of the beat can sound like showing off rather than shaping. Later pianists disagreed with their hands: Teddy Wilson, Nat King Cole and Bud Powell all worked from this vocabulary, and the line from here to modern jazz piano is direct.","Listen for the moments when the left hand stops keeping time and notice how uneasy it makes you feel; that is the innovation. Then play a Fats Waller solo from the same year for the steady alternative. Hines's own later solo recordings, made in the 1960s and 1970s in better sound, take the same ideas further."],"jimmie-noone-apex-blues":["A slow blues by a five-piece band with an unusual front line: no brass at all, just Noone's clarinet and Joe Poston's alto saxophone, the saxophone holding the melody steady while the clarinet ornaments it from above and below. Earl Hines is at the piano. The texture is light, almost chamber-like, and Noone's tone stays smooth and even whether he is at the bottom of the instrument or the top.","Recorded in Chicago in 1928 by the band Noone led at the Apex Club, a South Side cabaret that stayed open until the early hours and drew musicians after their own jobs finished. The two-reed format was a practical solution to a small bandstand and became the group's signature. Noone had studied with Lorenzo Tio and taken lessons from Sidney Bechet as a teenager in New Orleans.","Listen closer: the division of labour between the two reeds is the whole design. Poston plays the tune almost without decoration, freeing Noone to treat the melody as a fixed object to circle. It is an arranging idea, not an improvising one, and it anticipates how swing bands would set a soloist against a written line. Hines, meanwhile, is already breaking the beat behind them.","Maurice Ravel, in Chicago on his American tour in 1928, is reported to have gone to the Apex Club to hear Noone, and admirers have made much of the story. Benny Goodman's debt is better documented and less disputed. Critics have sometimes called the band too polite; the counterargument is that this polish is exactly what the clarinet needed to survive into the swing era.","Follow the saxophone rather than the clarinet on the first hearing, then swap; the pleasure is in the two lines fitting. Notice Noone's evenness across the registers, a classical virtue rarely prized in 1928. Then play a Benny Goodman small-group side from the 1930s and hear where it went."],"bechet-wild-cat-blues":["Despite the title this is not a blues but a fast multi-strain rag, and it is really a soprano saxophone record with a band attached. Bechet plays with a vibrato so wide and a tone so large that the cornet beside him sounds like an accompanist. He takes the melody, ornaments it, and drives the ensemble from the top rather than from the middle, which is not how a New Orleans front line is supposed to work.","Recorded in New York in 1923 with Clarence Williams's Blue Five, a studio group assembled by a Black publisher and entrepreneur who used records to sell his sheet music. The tune was his, written with the young Fats Waller. Bechet had spent 1919 to 1922 in Europe, where he bought the straight soprano saxophone in a London shop and where the conductor Ernest Ansermet heard him.","Listen closer: Ansermet had written in 1919 that this young clarinettist's road might turn out to be the whole road music would follow — the first time a European classical musician had said anything of the kind about a jazz player, and four years before this record was made. Listen for the moments when Bechet simply plays over the ensemble rather than with it; the soloist's future is audible there.","The Blue Five sides are where the jazz solo begins to detach itself from the collective, three years before Armstrong's Hot Fives. Bechet's dominance has been read both as a breakthrough and as bad manners; New Orleans purists have never quite forgiven him for refusing to blend. Clarence Williams's role as producer and publisher has been reassessed as central rather than incidental.","Hear how much space Bechet takes and ask who is accompanying whom. Then play Armstrong's Heebie Jeebies from three years later to see the same idea arrive by another route. Bechet's soprano is unmistakable within two notes; once you know the sound you will find it everywhere in the 1920s."],"bechet-summertime":["Gershwin's lullaby from Porgy and Bess, taken slowly by a quintet with Bechet's soprano saxophone alone in front. He barely states the tune before beginning to bend it, each phrase entering late and swelling through that enormous vibrato. There is a piano chorus, and then the saxophone returns and stays, climbing over the rhythm section for the last minute without ever raising the tempo.","Recorded in New York in June 1939 for Blue Note, a label founded months earlier by Alfred Lion, a German émigré who had arrived with a passion for the music and very little money. The session became the young company's first real success. Bechet was fifty-two, out of fashion in a country that wanted big bands, and recording an opera aria only four years old.","Listen closer: Bechet takes a melody written to be sung gently and plays it with the intensity of a blues shout, which reverses the whole point of the original. And Gershwin's tune was itself modelled on the Black American sacred and blues idiom Bechet grew up inside — so this is not a jazz musician borrowing from opera but a New Orleans Creole taking something home.","The record's success persuaded Alfred Lion that a jazz label could survive, and Blue Note went on to define recorded jazz for the next three decades; in that sense this side has consequences out of all proportion to its length. Critics of the period, deep in the argument between swing modernists and New Orleans revivalists, used Bechet as a weapon on the traditionalist side.","Play Gershwin's sung Summertime first, then this, and listen for every place where Bechet arrives late. Notice that he never plays loudly and never plays fast; the force is all in the vibrato. Then hear his 1940s clarinet blues for the other half of his art. Blue Note's own reissues have the best transfers."],"bechet-blue-horizon":["Six choruses of slow blues on the clarinet, and nothing else happens. Bechet begins in the lowest register of the instrument, almost muttering, and climbs one chorus at a time until the last is played high, loud and ragged at the edges. The band behind him plays chords and keeps out of the way. There is no theme to speak of, no arrangement, no second soloist.","Recorded in New York in 1944 for Blue Note, in the middle of a war that had emptied the bandstands and during a period when the New Orleans revival had given Bechet a second career. He was playing clarinet rather than his usual soprano saxophone, returning to the instrument he had learned as a child in New Orleans thirty-five years before the session.","Listen closer: the whole performance is a single deliberate climb, and Bechet marks it by changing register rather than volume — each chorus sits a little higher on the instrument than the last, so the emotional rise is built into the physical act of playing. In the final chorus his tone breaks slightly on the top notes. He left it in.","It is routinely named the finest blues clarinet solo on record, and it is unusual among Bechet's sides for its complete absence of display. Writers who find his soprano playing domineering tend to make an exception here. The record also helped fix the idea, central to the revival, that early jazz was at bottom a blues music rather than a ragtime one.","Do not do anything else while it plays; the piece asks for five undistracted minutes. Track the rise chorus by chorus and notice how late the first loud note arrives. Then play a Johnny Dodds blues from the 1920s for the older clarinet manner. Blue Note transfers are clean and close-miked."],"bix-beiderbecke-davenport-blues":["Beiderbecke's own tune, and not really a blues: a wistful sixteen-bar melody with an unexpected turn in its harmony, played by a pick-up band that includes the young Tommy Dorsey on trombone. Bix's cornet enters with the soft, rounded tone that made him famous — no vibrato to speak of, notes struck cleanly in the middle register, phrases that end where you do not expect them to.","Recorded at Gennett in Richmond, Indiana in January 1925, under the name Bix and his Rhythm Jugglers, by a group of young white musicians drifting between dance-band jobs. Beiderbecke was twenty-one, had been thrown out of a military academy, and was named for a river town in Iowa where he had first heard jazz from the riverboats coming up from New Orleans.","Listen closer: the tune keeps sliding into chords that ragtime and blues would not use, borrowed from the impressionist piano music Bix played at home. He never learned to read music well and worked these harmonies out by ear at the keyboard, then wrote them for horns. Listen for the moment the melody resolves a bar later than it should — that hesitation is his signature.","The record was barely noticed on release and has grown steadily in reputation as the first documented Beiderbecke composition. Arguments about him tend to circle the same question: whether his reputation rests on the playing or on the early death and the novel and film that followed it. This side, made before the legend, is the usual evidence for the defence.","Listen for the tone first — compare thirty seconds of Bix with thirty seconds of Armstrong from the same period and the difference is immediate: bell-like and inward against blazing and outward. Then hear In a Mist to find the same harmony on the piano. Gennett originals are noisy; a good needle-drop reissue is worth seeking."],"bix-beiderbecke-singin-the-blues":["A medium-slow popular song turned into two solos and very little else. Frankie Trumbauer plays a chorus on the C-melody saxophone, light and curling; then Beiderbecke's cornet takes a chorus that has been memorised by trumpeters ever since, built from long shapely phrases that carry across the bar lines rather than sitting inside them. Eddie Lang's guitar fills the corners. Nobody raises their voice.","Recorded for Okeh in New York in February 1927 and issued under Trumbauer's name, with Beiderbecke as a sideman; both men would shortly join Paul Whiteman's enormous and enormously popular orchestra. The song was three years old. Making a ballad-tempo record with no vocal and no dance function was commercially odd in 1927, and the record sold anyway.","Listen closer: Bix's chorus is a composition, not a decoration — it abandons the song's melody almost entirely and builds a new one that could stand alone. Fletcher Henderson's band later had the solo harmonised for a whole saxophone section and played it as a written arrangement, which is the surest measure of how completely other musicians had learned it by heart.","This is the record usually credited with inventing the jazz ballad: improvisation as lyrical melody rather than rhythmic display, the line that runs through Lester Young to Miles Davis. Trumbauer's own reputation rests largely on it, and Lester Young named him as his model. The perennial argument is whether Bix's Whiteman years afterwards were a sell-out or a reasonable living.","Play Bix's chorus, then play Armstrong's Potato Head Blues chorus from three months later; the two poles of jazz improvisation are right there, one lyrical, one rhythmic. Listen for how few notes Bix uses. Then hear the Fletcher Henderson harmonised version if you can find it."],"bix-beiderbecke-in-a-mist":["Three minutes of solo piano by a cornet player, and not a jazz performance in any usual sense: no steady beat to swing against, no improvisation over changes. Instead a sequence of episodes built from whole-tone scales and unresolved chords, with a syncopated middle section that briefly remembers ragtime before drifting back into haze. The title is exact — the harmony never quite settles on a home key.","Recorded for Okeh in New York in September 1927, while Beiderbecke was earning good money with Paul Whiteman's orchestra and spending his evenings at the piano. He had absorbed Debussy, Ravel and the American composer Eastwood Lane by ear from records and sheet music, could not notate properly, and needed help from an arranger to get the piece onto paper for publication.","Listen closer: the whole-tone scale, which divides the octave into six equal steps and so belongs to no key at all, is what produces the drifting quality — the same device Debussy used to make water and fog. Bix plays it with a jazz musician's touch rather than a pianist's, hitting chords slightly unevenly, which is why the piece sounds improvised even where it is fixed.","Sceptics have called it a party piece by a man out of his depth in composition, and it is undeniably short of development. Its importance is as a first: a jazz musician openly using impressionist harmony as material, two decades before the cool school did the same thing systematically. Pianists still record it, usually more correctly and less atmospherically than its author.","Play Debussy's Préludes or Ravel's Jeux d'eau first and the vocabulary will be unmistakable. Then listen for the ragtime fragment in the middle and hear how uncomfortably the two worlds sit together — that discomfort is the interesting part. Bix's own recording is the one to hear; later pianists tidy it."],"james-p-johnson-carolina-shout":["Three minutes of solo piano at a hard dancing tempo, in the multi-strain shape of a rag but doing something ragtime never did. The left hand strides between a low bass note and a mid-range chord, faster and further than any ragtime bass; the right hand answers it with call-and-response figures, as if two voices were trading. The last strain is a sequence of hand-crossing tricks that sound impossible.","Recorded in New York in October 1921, three years after Johnson had cut the piece as a piano roll. The shout of the title is a ring dance from the Sea Islands of the Carolinas and Georgia, brought north by migrants; Johnson had watched them dance to it at Harlem rent parties, where pianists competed all night and a man's reputation rested on what his left hand could do.","Listen closer: the right-hand figures are imitating the dancers' hand-clapping and the shouted responses of the ring shout, which is why the piece feels like a crowd rather than a soloist. The young George Gershwin learned it by slowing the piano roll and following the depressed keys with his fingers, and he was not the only one; it was the test piece every Harlem pianist had to pass.","It is the founding document of stride, the point where ragtime's fixed notation gives way to a swinging, improvised piano style, and it carried directly into Fats Waller, Duke Ellington's early playing and eventually Art Tatum. Johnson's later concert works have never found an audience, and the standard complaint is that jazz history remembers him for three minutes of a long career.","Listen to the left hand alone for a chorus — the distance it covers at that speed is the whole difficulty. Then hear Fats Waller's Handful of Keys for what a pupil did with it, and Gershwin's Rhapsody in Blue for where it leaked into the concert hall. Johnson's own 1921 recording is rhythmically sharper than his later remakes."],"james-p-johnson-charleston":["A song of almost brutal rhythmic simplicity: a short phrase, a hole where the next beat should be, then the answer landing early. That displaced accent is the whole tune, and it is enough to make a room move. The melody itself barely rises above five notes, and the lyric, by Cecil Mack, is mostly an invitation to dance. Almost every version adds hand-claps or stop-time breaks.","Written for the Broadway revue Runnin' Wild, which opened in New York in 1923, one of the Black musical comedies that followed the success of Shuffle Along two years earlier. Johnson said the rhythm came from dockworkers from Charleston, South Carolina, whom he had accompanied at a Harlem dance hall. The dance it named spread from the show to the whole Western world within two years.","Listen closer: the rhythm is not a dance-band invention but a survival. The pattern Johnson wrote down had been in Black American and West African music long before 1923, and it turns up in New Orleans records of the same decade under no particular name. Once you know the figure you hear it everywhere in the 1920s, including in the last choruses of Jelly Roll Morton's Black Bottom Stomp.","It became the sound of an entire decade and remains its shorthand in every film about the 1920s, which has made it hard to hear as music. Johnson earned well from it and was thereafter treated as a songwriter rather than the serious composer he wanted to be. Recent scholarship has restored his Broadway work to a central place in the history of Black musical theatre.","Clap the rhythm before listening: long, short, rest, short. Then play Johnson's own piano version and notice how much space he leaves. Compare it with Weill and Krenek's Berlin attempts to use the same rhythm a few years later, where the figure arrives second-hand and slightly stiff."],"james-p-johnson-yamekraw":["A single-movement rhapsody of roughly a quarter of an hour for piano and orchestra, stitched together from blues strains, a spiritual-like slow section, a stride episode for the soloist alone and a stomping finale. The piano is never far from the front, and the writing for it is Johnson's own Harlem manner rather than a concert pianist's. The seams between sections are frank rather than disguised.","Written in 1927 and named for Yamacraw, a Black district of Savannah, Georgia. Johnson wrote the piano score; William Grant Still made the orchestration. It was performed at Carnegie Hall in 1928 with Fats Waller, Johnson's own pupil, at the piano, in a concert of Black American music. Johnson had wanted for years to be taken seriously as a composer and had been told repeatedly that stride pianists did not write symphonies.","Listen closer: the slow middle section is built on the harmonic language of the spiritual rather than the blues, and Still's orchestration gives it to the strings in a way that sounds deliberately like a hymn heard from outside a church. Note too that the piano writing is genuinely idiomatic stride — the left hand does what it would do at a rent party, not what a concerto would ask.","It was overshadowed from the start by Rhapsody in Blue, written three years earlier by a composer who had learned stride from Johnson's piano rolls, and the comparison has followed it ever since. A short film version was made in 1930. Performances remain rare, the orchestral parts have a complicated history, and Johnson's concert music as a whole is only now being recorded properly.","Play Rhapsody in Blue first and then this, and consider who was borrowing from whom. Listen for the stride episode where the orchestra drops out — that is Johnson writing in his own voice. Then hear Still's Afro-American Symphony from a few years later for the orchestral idiom fully worked out. Recordings are scarce; take whichever you can find."],"fats-waller-handful-of-keys":["Stride piano at its most athletic and most relaxed at once. Waller states a bright tune and then plays variation after variation over a left hand that never falters — bass note, chord, bass note, chord, at a tempo that leaves no room for error, while the right hand spins runs, broken chords and sudden two-handed flourishes. The title is a boast, and it is earned in the first twenty seconds.","Recorded for Victor in New York in March 1929, the year Waller broke through as a recording star. He had learned his trade from James P. Johnson, played organ in a Harlem cinema as a teenager, and by 1929 was writing shows, cutting piano solos and drinking heavily. Victor's electrical recording captured a piano better than any previous process, which mattered for music this fast.","Listen closer: the left hand is doing something physically brutal that the recording makes sound easy — the leaps span more than an octave and land on every beat for three minutes without a stumble. Notice how Waller varies the bass pattern to keep it interesting, occasionally walking up in single notes, and how the right hand's phrases end a fraction late, which is where the relaxation comes from.","Among pianists this is the stride showpiece, the one against which technique is measured, and it stayed in Waller's repertoire all his life. Critics who wrote him off as an entertainer because of his comic vocal records have always had to make an exception for the solo piano sides, where no joke intervenes. Art Tatum was listening, and said so.","Listen once to the right hand and once to the left; they are almost independent performances. Play James P. Johnson's Carolina Shout beside it for the teacher's version of the same style. Then hear Waller's Ain't Misbehavin' from the same year to see him hide this technique behind a song."],"fats-waller-aint-misbehavin":["A medium-tempo song whose melody climbs a small ladder and falls back, over harmony that keeps stepping sideways rather than resolving. Waller recorded it in 1929 both as a solo piano performance and with a band; in the solo version the left hand strides steadily while the right treats the tune as raw material. The lyric, by Andy Razaf, is a promise of fidelity delivered with a straight face.","Written for Connie's Hot Chocolates, a Harlem revue that moved to Broadway in 1929. Louis Armstrong played it from the pit and then from the stage, and the song made both men money. Waller had a reputation for selling songs outright for cash when he needed it; this one he kept. The Harlem Renaissance was at its height and downtown audiences were paying to come uptown.","Listen closer: the harmony under the first phrase keeps shifting a half step at a time, a trick from Waller's church-organ training rather than from ragtime, and it gives a cheerful tune a persistent undertow. Razaf's lyric was probably read by its first audiences as domestic comedy; it has also been heard, plausibly, as the boast of someone with nowhere else to go.","It became one of the most recorded American songs of the century and the title of a long-running Broadway revue built from Waller's catalogue. Critics have argued for eighty years about whether Waller's clowning served the songs or buried them; the solo piano recordings are usually produced as evidence that he knew exactly what he was doing.","Hear Waller's piano solo version and his vocal version in that order, then Armstrong's. The song survives all three treatments, which is the test of a standard. Listen for the sideways harmony in the first eight bars, and then for the release in the middle section, where everything suddenly settles."],"fats-waller-jitterbug-waltz":["A jazz piece in three-four time, which in 1942 was close to unheard of, played by Waller on a Hammond organ rather than a piano. The tune descends in a long, lazy curve — reportedly taken from an exercise his son was practising — and the organ's sustained tone lets the line float in a way no piano could manage. A small band shuffles underneath, keeping the waltz light rather than grand.","Recorded in New York in 1942, late in Waller's short life, when he was touring constantly and in poor health. He had played organ since his teens in a Harlem cinema and never lost his affection for the instrument; the Hammond, an electric imitation of a pipe organ introduced in the 1930s, gave him something portable that could hold a note indefinitely.","Listen closer: the waltz was a European dance that jazz had almost entirely refused, associating it with the parlour and the concert hall. Waller does not swing it in the usual sense — he lets the three beats stay even and puts the swing into the melody's phrasing instead. The Hammond's slight electrical waver, audible on sustained notes, is part of the sound.","It became a standard, taken up by flute and vibraphone players in the 1950s who liked its gentleness and by many since, and it is now among the most covered pieces Waller wrote. Critics have treated it as the proof that he was a composer of real subtlety rather than a manufacturer of novelty songs, and it is the piece most often cited against his clownish reputation.","Notice the descending shape of the melody and how it seems to keep falling without ever landing. Play Ravel's La Valse afterwards for the opposite attitude to three-four time — one dance dissolving, the other quietly reinvented. Then hear a 1950s flute version for how portable the tune turned out to be."],"willie-the-lion-smith-echoes-of-spring":["Stride piano with the aggression removed. The left hand keeps a gentle alternating pattern rather than a stomping one, and the right plays a delicate, almost fluttering figure that circles over shifting harmony without ever landing hard. There is no showpiece chorus and no climax; the piece simply turns over on itself for three minutes and stops. It sounds more like a character piece than a dance.","Recorded in New York in 1939 during a session of solo piano pieces of his own, made for a small label run by enthusiasts at a moment when the older jazz was being rediscovered by collectors. Smith was in his mid-forties, a fixture of Harlem since the rent-party days, a decorated veteran of the Western Front, and by his own account a synagogue cantor as well as a nightclub pianist.","Listen closer: the harmony is impressionist rather than bluesy — chords that colour rather than move, resolutions delayed or simply avoided — and Smith was open about having taken it from the French piano music he admired. Duke Ellington, who called him a major influence and wrote a portrait of him, took the same delicacy of touch into his own playing; you can hear the debt in Ellington's introductions.","The 1939 solos rescued Smith's reputation from anecdote. He had been known chiefly as a talker, a cigar and a derby hat, and these recordings showed a composer with a recognisable harmonic world. Critics have since placed him as the third of the great stride pianists, less powerful than Johnson and less famous than Waller, and the only one who was genuinely a miniaturist.","Play Debussy's Clair de lune first and the family resemblance will be obvious, then notice that Smith's left hand is still, unmistakably, stride. Listen for how rarely a chord resolves where you expect. Follow with Ellington's solo piano recordings to hear the inheritance passed on."],"ellington-mood-indigo":["Three minutes for a small Ellington unit rather than the full band: muted trumpet on top, muted trombone in the middle, clarinet underneath in its low register, the reverse of the usual order. That inversion, plus the close spacing, produces a soft blurred haze that sounds like one instrument rather than three. The tune is a short, slow, blues-tinged strain, stated, embellished and returned to, with Ellington's piano placing chords like punctuation and no solo ever raising its voice.","Recorded in New York in October 1930, when Ellington's band held the stand at the Cotton Club and radio wires carried it nationwide. It first appeared as 'Dreamy Blues'; Barney Bigard, the New Orleans clarinettist, brought in the melodic idea, and Irving Mills, Ellington's publisher and manager, attached a lyric and his own name to the credit. Ellington remembered letters arriving by the hundred asking for it. The band was still small, around a dozen players, and the record made his name beyond dancers.","Listen to the very bottom of the chord. Most dance-band scoring of 1930 put the clarinet on top, bright and agile; Ellington buried it below the brass, where its lowest notes are breathy and almost pitchless. The trumpet and trombone are both muted so their edges soften, and microphone placement did the rest. This voicing barely works in a hall. It works on a record, and Ellington was writing for the studio before most bandleaders knew they had one.","The record sold, and the tune became a standard Ellington re-orchestrated for decades, later for the full band with the saxophones added. Bigard's share of the authorship has been argued over ever since; most scholars now treat the theme as his, the setting as Ellington's, and Mills's name as the price of publication. Critics who wanted jazz hot heard a lullaby. Later writers heard the first jazz orchestration that could not simply be handed to another band.","Play the 1930 original before any later version and keep the volume low, because the blend depends on it. Try to pick out the clarinet; if you cannot, the effect is working. Then hear Ellington's 1950s remake with the full reed section to feel what he gained and lost. Ravel's Pavane is a good companion for the same soft, bruised colour."],"ellington-it-dont-mean-a-thing":["Three minutes built around a slogan. Ivie Anderson sings the title line and then scats the doo-wah answer the brass had been playing with plunger mutes for years; Joe 'Tricky Sam' Nanton takes a growling trombone chorus, the reeds answer, and the band rides out. The song's opening strain leans minor and the answering one brightens, so the record keeps tipping between shade and light. The tempo is brisk and absolutely even, four beats to the bar with no let-up.","Recorded in New York in February 1932, three years before the word swing became a marketing category. Ellington said the phrase came from Bubber Miley, the trumpeter who had built the band's growling sound in the 1920s and who was dying of tuberculosis that year. The Depression had gutted the record business; the Cotton Club broadcasts kept the band working. In a dance number, Ellington was making an argument: rhythm, not repertoire, is the measure of the music.","Ivie Anderson's scat is not decoration. The syllables she sings are a transcription of a brass technique, the plunger mute opening and closing over a note, turned back into a human voice, which is where it came from in the first place. Notice too that the band never plays the title phrase as a written riff. It is only ever sung, delivered by the one voice on the bandstand that cannot be muted.","It handed the swing era its motto three years early, and the title has been quoted ever since by people who have never heard the record. Ellington was later accused of reducing his art to a catchphrase; he pointed out that he had been playing that rhythm since the Cotton Club. Anderson, the band's first regular singer, is now recognised as a major stylist who was overshadowed by the soloists surrounding her.","Count along in four and notice how little the rhythm section does: an even pulse with almost no fills to help you. Then follow Nanton's trombone, which talks rather than sings. Afterwards, Shostakovich's Jazz Suite waltz from the same decade shows what Europe imagined swing sounded like, and how much it was missing."],"ellington-ko-ko":["Under three minutes, and the densest thing Ellington ever recorded. A minor blues, seven choruses deep: baritone saxophone and tom-toms lay the ground, Juan Tizol's valve trombone states the theme over sustained reeds, Tricky Sam Nanton takes two plunger-muted choruses, the saxophones climb in sliding blocks, and Jimmy Blanton's bass steps out of the rhythm section to answer the whole orchestra. Ellington interrupts with two bars of clashing piano. Then it stops, abruptly, with no ritard and no fade.","Recorded in Chicago in March 1940, at the first session of what collectors call the Blanton-Webster band, with Jimmy Blanton newly on bass and Ben Webster newly on tenor. Ellington had just signed back to Victor and was writing at a rate he never matched again. He had been planning a stage work about Black American history called Boola; Ko-Ko survived from it, and its subject, he said, was the slave dances held in Congo Square, New Orleans.","Blanton is the key. Before 1940 the string bass kept time; here it plays two-bar melodic answers in the gaps, in tune and in the clear, because Victor's engineers could finally capture it. Notice too that the saxophone section never plays a tune. It plays chords sliding chromatically upward beneath the brass, a device Ellington took from nobody. And the piano entry that sounds like a mistake is the only place the harmony openly cracks.","It sold modestly on release and is now routinely named the finest single record of the big-band era. Gunther Schuller's analysis in the 1960s argued that its construction bears comparison with composed concert music, which irritated writers who wanted jazz kept in its own category. The argument over whether Ko-Ko is composition or arrangement has never really ended. Ellington seems to have regarded the distinction as somebody else's problem.","Three minutes, so play it three times. First follow Nanton's muted trombone; second, follow only the bass; third, hear the saxophones as a single sliding mass. Then go to Stravinsky's Rite of Spring for the same trick of building enormous pressure from short repeated figures. Take the 1940 Victor original over any later concert version."],"ellington-concerto-for-cootie":["A concerto in everything but scale. Cootie Williams plays the whole record, changing mutes as he goes: a plunger-muted opening that speaks in half-words, a middle section open and high, then back behind the mute for the close. The orchestra does not accompany so much as frame him, holding long reed chords and answering in clipped brass punctuations. There is no improvised solo in the usual sense. The trumpet part is a worked-out role that only one player in the world could fill.","Recorded in Chicago in March 1940, weeks after Ko-Ko, for Victor. Williams had been with Ellington since 1929, having replaced Bubber Miley, and had spent a decade learning plunger technique alongside Tricky Sam Nanton. Within the year he left to join Benny Goodman, a departure notable enough that Raymond Scott wrote a lament called 'When Cootie Left the Duke'. Ellington's practice of writing for named individuals rather than for chairs in a section has no real parallel in band music.","The melody outlived the record. In 1943 Bob Russell fitted words to it and it became the ballad 'Do Nothin' Till You Hear from Me', a hit most listeners never connect with this instrumental. Listen for the point where Williams drops the mute: the open sound is the event, more than anything happening in the harmony. Ellington builds the piece out of timbre the way another composer builds one out of modulation.","The French critic Andre Hodeir devoted a celebrated chapter to it, arguing that every note is placed and that the piece answers the question of whether jazz can be composed. Some musicians objected that the analysis mistook a worked-out routine for a score. What nobody disputes is that Williams is irreplaceable: later Ellington trumpeters played the part, and it was never quite the same piece again.","Follow the mute. Notice how much articulation Williams gets from a rubber plunger held against the bell, almost consonants. Then hear the same man on 'Do Nothin' Till You Hear from Me' to feel how a lyric domesticates the tune. Ravel's Bolero is the obvious companion: one idea, carried entirely by changing colour."],"ellington-take-the-a-train":["Three minutes, and the most recognisable opening in big-band music: a saxophone riff over a rocking piano vamp, rising in one clean arc. Ray Nance takes the trumpet solo, muted first, then open for a shouting second chorus, and the band answers in stacked brass. The form is a thirty-two-bar song in the standard shape, at an easy jogging tempo that never pushes. Nothing about it is complicated. Everything about it is placed exactly where it needs to be.","Billy Strayhorn wrote it, not Ellington. Strayhorn had joined the organisation in 1939, and the title came from the directions Ellington gave him for reaching his Harlem apartment by the new A subway line. In January 1941 a dispute between the broadcasters and the publishing society ASCAP barred Ellington's own catalogue from the airwaves, so the band urgently needed theme music it could legally play. They recorded Strayhorn's tune in February and used it for the next thirty years.","The melody contains a note that does not belong to its chord, a raised fourth early in the phrase, and that is why the tune sounds modern rather than merely cheerful. Strayhorn nearly threw the sketch away for sounding too much like Fletcher Henderson. Listen also to Ray Nance: this was his first recorded solo with the band, and every later Ellington trumpeter learned it note for note, as though it had been written down.","It became the band's signature and, for many listeners, the sound of the swing era itself, which has made it easy to underrate. Strayhorn's contribution to Ellington's output was obscured for decades by contracts and by Ellington's public persona; scholarship since the 1990s, and David Hajdu's biography in particular, has reset the credit. Collectors generally prefer the compact 1941 studio take to the long later concert versions with their extended Nance features.","Start with the 1941 Victor record, then hear a 1960s live version to see how far the band stretched it. Listen for the piano introduction: Ellington plays a few bars nobody would miss if they were cut, and the record would be poorer without them. Follow Nance's second chorus for the moment the mute comes off."],"basie-one-oclock-jump":["A head arrangement, nothing written down, running about three minutes and consisting of riffs stacked over the blues. Basie opens alone at the piano, spare and boogie-flavoured, then the rhythm section settles into an even four: Freddie Green's guitar on every beat, Walter Page's walking bass, Jo Jones keeping time on the hi-hat rather than the bass drum. Solos pass from tenor to trombone to trumpet, the key changes partway through, and brass and reeds trade riffs to the end.","Recorded for Decca in New York in July 1937, a year after John Hammond heard the band on a late-night radio wire from the Reno Club in Kansas City and brought it east. The tune had an unbroadcastable working title and an announcer renamed it for the hour. Basie's men had come up in the territory bands of the southwest, where a night's work meant spinning riffs for hours; the record is that practice compressed. It stayed the band's theme for half a century.","Listen to what the drummer is not doing. Jo Jones moved the pulse off the bass drum and onto the hi-hat, so the beat floats instead of thudding, the single change that separates Kansas City rhythm from everything before it and seeds everything after. Underneath, Freddie Green plays four chords to the bar and is never amplified and never given a solo. The band's swing is a rhythm-section invention that no arranger could have written.","It sold steadily rather than spectacularly and became a standard almost at once, recorded by Goodman, Shaw and Harry James. Basie's Decca contract paid a flat fee with no royalties, a piece of exploitation now inseparable from the record's story. Critics have argued ever since about whether a head arrangement counts as composition. The Basie band's answer, for the next fifteen years, was to keep not writing things down.","Ignore the soloists on the first pass and listen only to guitar, bass and drums; the record is a rhythm-section demonstration with decoration on top. Then follow Lester Young's tenor, light and behind the beat. Compare it with Ellington's Ko-Ko from three years later to hear two opposite ideas of what a big band is for."],"basie-jumpin-at-the-woodside":["Three minutes at a fast but unhurried clip. The riff is a few notes long and never develops; the record's life is in the solos and in the way the ensemble riffs behind them, adding layers until the last chorus is three separate figures going at once. Lester Young's tenor chorus is the centre, dry and light-toned, phrasing across the barlines as though the bar were only a suggestion. The rhythm section does not vary its pulse for a single beat.","Recorded for Decca in New York in August 1938. The Woodside Hotel in Harlem was where the Basie band stayed and rehearsed when they came to New York, in rooms too small to hold the whole orchestra. By then the band was a fixture at the Famous Door on 52nd Street, where an air-conditioning unit paid for by John Hammond kept the club open through the summer and a radio wire carried Basie to the rest of the country.","Count the choruses and notice that the arrangement, such as it is, works purely by addition: each time a soloist finishes, another riff is stacked behind the next one, and nothing is ever taken away again. That is a Kansas City device, learned in cutting contests where a band had to keep raising the temperature for dancers. Lester Young's entrance is the moment most tenor players of the following decade set out to copy.","One of the records that convinced New York that Kansas City had something it did not. Musicians treated it as a textbook; Charlie Parker, still a teenager back in Kansas City, is said to have learned Young's solo by heart. Critics who valued written arrangement thought the Basie band crude and said so in print. The judgment reversed completely inside twenty years and has not moved since.","Play it loud and try to hear the final chorus as three tunes at once: saxophone riff, brass riff, and soloist over both. Then isolate Lester Young's solo and sing it, which is how a generation learned it. Basie's later Roulette records show the same band with the riffs written down, polished and a little tamed."],"basie-atomic-mr-basie":["Eleven tracks, around forty minutes, all arranged by Neal Hefti for the band collectors call the New Testament Basie. The sound is enormous and exact: brass that enters like a door slamming, saxophone sections in perfect unison, dynamics that drop to a whisper and climb back inside four bars. 'Li'l Darlin'' is the famous one, taken slower than any band had dared, the melody almost falling over. Basie himself plays less than anyone on the record, a few notes, placed.","Recorded for Roulette in New York in 1958. Basie had broken up the band in 1950 and rebuilt it in 1952 around written arrangements instead of riffs, hiring Hefti, Ernie Wilkins and Frank Foster to supply them. The LP was now the unit of jazz, high fidelity was the selling point, and the sleeve carried a photograph of a nuclear explosion, a piece of 1958 taste that later pressings quietly dropped along with the title.","Hefti's trick is space. Listen to how often the whole band stops dead for a beat before a brass entry; the silence is what makes the chord land. On 'Li'l Darlin'' the tempo is so slow that the players cannot ride the beat and must place each note deliberately, which means the entire band has to agree on exactly how late to be. Basie's piano fills are the joke: three notes where a lesser bandleader would play thirty.","It sold, won the band a new audience, and fixed the public image of Basie as master of the big-band shout chorus. Older listeners who preferred the loose 1930s band heard the Hefti charts as slick, and the argument between Old Testament and New Testament Basie is still live among collectors. The engineering, unusually clean for 1958, is part of why the record became a hi-fi demonstration disc.","Start with 'Li'l Darlin'' and resist the urge to speed it up in your head. Then 'Whirly-Bird' for the opposite extreme. Listen for the endings: Basie's band stops together in a way almost no other ensemble manages. Bernstein's Symphonic Dances, from the same year, shows a classical composer chasing the same brass sound."],"goodman-after-youve-gone":["Three musicians and no arrangement: Benny Goodman's clarinet, Teddy Wilson's piano and Gene Krupa's drums, mostly brushes, on a song from 1918 taken at a gallop. After the big bands the texture is startlingly transparent; you hear every note of the piano's left hand and every consonant of the clarinet's tonguing. Goodman plays a long, fluent chorus, Wilson answers with the even running lines that made him the model for a generation, and the three of them scramble to the finish together.","Recorded in New York in July 1935, weeks before the Palomar Ballroom engagement that started the swing craze. John Hammond had put Goodman and Wilson together at a private party first. The trio existed partly because a record could be made and sold when the same group could not appear on a stage in most of America with a Black pianist in it. Goodman began using the trio in public the following year and refused to drop Wilson; Lionel Hampton made it a quartet in 1936.","There is no bass. Wilson's left hand carries the entire harmonic floor, which is why the record sounds so clear and why it needs a pianist of exactly his precision to work at all. Krupa plays brushes on a snare for most of it, almost inaudible until suddenly he is not. The absence is deliberate: Goodman wanted a chamber group inside a dance-band career, and this is the format he kept returning to for fifty years.","Its importance is usually stated in social terms, as one of the first widely sold records by a racially mixed jazz group, and Goodman's willingness to tour with it mattered more than the disc itself. Musically, listeners at the time were startled by how little sound three players needed. Goodman's own reputation has fluctuated since: praised as organiser and technician, doubted as an improviser, and defended by clarinettists who have tried to play this chorus.","Listen to Wilson's left hand and ask yourself where the bass went. Then follow Goodman's articulation, every note started cleanly at that speed, which is the thing clarinettists mention first. Copland's Clarinet Concerto, written for Goodman a decade later, is the natural next step, and the Quintette du Hot Club is the European answer to a jazz group with no drums or horns."],"goodman-sing-sing-sing":["Eight and a half minutes spread across both sides of a twelve-inch record, at a time when three minutes was the limit. It opens with Gene Krupa's tom-toms alone, a rolling figure that never stops for the whole performance; Harry James takes a trumpet chorus, Goodman a long clarinet one, and the band shouts riffs in between. Louis Prima's tune is barely a tune, a repeated minor phrase, and the record is really a drum feature with an orchestra attached to it.","Recorded for Victor in Hollywood in 1937, at the height of the Goodman band's fame, when the King of Swing was a radio brand and teenagers danced in cinema aisles. Jimmy Mundy's arrangement stitched Prima's tune to other material and let the band stretch out past every commercial convention. Krupa had become the first drummer to be a star in his own right, a development older bandleaders regarded as a category error and dancers regarded as the whole point.","Partway through, the band plays a riff that is not from this tune at all: it is 'Christopher Columbus', a Fletcher Henderson band hit from the year before, spliced in by the arranger. Listen also to Krupa's tom-tom figure. He is not keeping time so much as playing a melody on unpitched drums. The famous quiet piano solo is not on this record; that happened at Carnegie Hall six months later and is a different performance.","The biggest instrumental hit of the swing era and, almost immediately, the thing serious critics used as evidence against it: loud, long, and built on a single riff. Krupa's showmanship divided musicians, and he left the band within a year after a public row with Goodman. Later writers have been kinder, hearing a genuinely radical piece of formal risk, since nobody else was making eight-minute dance records for a mass audience.","Play it at volume and follow only the drums; the rest is scenery. Then listen for the quiet passage before the final chorus, where Goodman plays almost alone over the toms, the record's best moment and the one people forget. Afterwards, the 1938 Carnegie Hall version, which is longer, wilder and has the piano solo."],"goodman-carnegie-hall-1938":["Close to two hours of a live concert, released twelve years after the night it happened. The programme moves from a sketched history of jazz through the Goodman band's hits to a jam session on 'Honeysuckle Rose' with players borrowed from the Ellington and Basie orchestras, and ends with 'Sing, Sing, Sing' stretched past twelve minutes. The sound is one microphone's worth, thin and boxy with the audience close, and the playing is faster and rougher than any studio version.","16 January 1938, in a hall built for symphony orchestras that had never presented a jazz band. The promoter's idea was half publicity stunt; Goodman reportedly asked how long an intermission they wanted and was told the same as Toscanini's. Johnny Hodges, Harry Carney and Cootie Williams came over from Ellington's band, Lester Young and Buck Clayton from Basie's, sharing a Carnegie stage in a segregated country. Acetate discs of the evening sat in a cupboard until 1950.","Jess Stacy's piano solo on the closing 'Sing, Sing, Sing' was not planned. Goodman simply pointed at him when the arrangement ran out, and Stacy played two minutes of quiet, almost impressionist music that has nearly nothing to do with the number surrounding it. He was never asked to repeat it. Listen for the moment the band drops away and a single piano is left alone in a hall holding three thousand people.","It was received at the time as the night jazz entered the concert hall, a framing that irritated musicians who had been playing this music for twenty years without anyone's permission. The 1950 release was among the first double LPs and sold enormously. Modern transfers from the original acetates have restored missing announcements and corrected pitch; collectors argue over which one is least processed.","Do not start at the beginning. Go to 'Sing, Sing, Sing' and wait for Stacy, then go back for the 'Honeysuckle Rose' jam to hear Lester Young and Johnny Hodges in the same room. Gershwin's Rhapsody in Blue at Aeolian Hall in 1924 was the first attempt at this argument; this is the night that settled it."],"fletcher-henderson-king-porter-stomp":["Three minutes containing the blueprint for the next fifteen years of American dance music. Jelly Roll Morton's old piano rag is rebuilt as a conversation between two teams: the saxophones play a smooth phrase, the brass answer with a stabbing one, back and forth, until the whole band joins for a final shout chorus over a rocking trombone figure. Coleman Hawkins takes a tenor chorus. The rhythm section keeps an even four with no ornament whatsoever.","Recorded in New York in 1933, one of several versions Henderson made of the tune across a decade. He was a chemistry graduate from Georgia who had come north for postgraduate study, found the laboratories shut to him, and ended up leading the best band in Harlem at the Roseland Ballroom, with Louis Armstrong passing through it in the 1920s. By 1933 his business was collapsing; two years later he was selling arrangements to Benny Goodman, whose version of this one helped start the swing era.","The idea that changed everything here is not a melody but a layout: set reeds and brass in opposition and write for the sections rather than for the players. Don Redman had begun it in this band in the 1920s and Henderson perfected it. Listen for the trombone figure under the last chorus, a riff repeated without variation while everything above it climbs. Goodman's 1935 hit is essentially this chart played tighter and recorded better.","Henderson's own records sold poorly and his band was famously undisciplined; the money and most of the credit went to Goodman, who at least said publicly where his arrangements came from. Whether Henderson was exploited or rescued is still argued. What is not argued is the authorship of the method: historians of the big band trace almost every swing chart back through him, and through Redman, to this pattern.","Play Henderson's version and Goodman's 1935 one back to back, the same chart in two different worlds. Listen for the call and response and try to hear the band as two choirs rather than one ensemble. Then Morton's original piano version, which sounds like it comes from another century, because it does."],"fletcher-henderson-wrappin-it-up":["Under three minutes, and the most polished thing Henderson wrote for himself. The reeds open with a long, smoothly harmonised line that moves in even notes rather than riffs, the brass answer with clipped figures, and the two exchange places across the chorus with an ease that makes the writing sound improvised. There are short solos, but the interest lies in the scoring: four saxophones moving together as one flexible voice, breathing in the same places.","Recorded in New York in September 1934, near the end of Henderson's time as a bandleader. His orchestra had lost Louis Armstrong years earlier and Coleman Hawkins that spring, to Europe, and the payroll was failing. Within months he would be writing for Goodman rather than for himself. The subtitle, 'The Lindy Glide', names the dance it was made for: the Savoy Ballroom crowd set the tempos that the whole industry then followed.","Count the saxophone line at the start and notice that it does not repeat. Most swing writing is built from riffs, short figures said over and over; here Henderson writes a long melody for the section instead, the way an orchestrator writes for strings. It is much the harder thing to do, and the reason arrangers still study this chart. The brass never once play the same rhythm as the reeds.","Barely noticed on release and never a hit, it survives as a teaching piece: arrangers from the 1940s onward cite it as the point where section writing became supple. Henderson's reputation has been rebuilt slowly, partly by scholars working out which of 'his' charts were actually written by his brother Horace or by Benny Carter. On this one the hand is generally agreed to be his own.","Listen to the saxophones alone and ignore everything else; the record is a demonstration of what four reeds can do when they breathe together. Then compare Milhaud's La creation du monde, a European composer writing jazz down a decade earlier, to hear what notation captures and what it cannot."],"chick-webb-stompin-at-the-savoy":["Three minutes at a comfortable dancing tempo, with a melody so well made that it stopped sounding like a band number and became a standard. Edgar Sampson, the band's alto player, wrote and arranged it; the reeds carry the tune, the brass punch the corners, and Webb drives from behind with a snap on the rim and a rolling press on the toms. Nobody shows off. It is a working dance chart played by the best band in Harlem.","Recorded in New York in 1934 by the house band of the Savoy Ballroom on Lenox Avenue, the largest integrated dance floor in America, where two bandstands meant the music never stopped. Webb, left small and in pain by childhood spinal tuberculosis, played a kit built to reach him and had held the room since 1931, beating every visiting orchestra that came to challenge him. The tune is named after the building it was made in.","The credit reads Sampson, Goodman and Webb, with a lyric by Andy Razaf, but the writing is Sampson's; the other names arrived with the publishing deal. Listen to Webb's bass drum, used for accents rather than for time, the opposite of every white swing drummer of the period, and to the way he leaves the last beat of a phrase empty so the band falls into the next one. Krupa said openly that he had copied him.","It became one of the most recorded tunes of the era and is now better known through Goodman's and Ella Fitzgerald's versions than through Webb's own. Webb died in 1939 at thirty-four, before the LP could preserve him properly, so his reputation rests on a handful of Decca sides and the testimony of other drummers. Critics rediscovering him almost always arrive by way of Krupa and Buddy Rich.","Listen for the drums, mixed low and doing everything. Then hear Goodman's version for the tune played faster and cleaner, and Ella's for what a singer finds in it. Webb's 'Harlem Congo' shows the same band with the leash off, which is the other half of the picture."],"chick-webb-harlem-congo":["Under three minutes at a hard, fast tempo, and the best surviving evidence of what Chick Webb's band sounded like when it was trying to destroy an opponent. The brass play in tight stacked harmony, the saxophones answer, and every few bars Webb fills the gap with a drum break that is short, exact and placed so the band lands on the other side without a wobble. The last chorus is a shouted riff over drumming that never once loses the beat.","Recorded for Decca in New York in late 1937, months after Webb's band and Benny Goodman's played a public battle at the Savoy Ballroom in front of a crowd that filled the building and spilled into Lenox Avenue. Webb was the house drummer in a room where bands were judged by dancers rather than by critics, and where losing meant losing the job. He was already seriously ill. The record was made to sell a reputation earned live.","Listen to how Webb ends his breaks. A drum break is easy to start and very hard to finish; he consistently places the last stroke a fraction early, so the band's re-entry feels like a door opening rather than a rescue. Because of his size he played a kit built to reach him, with custom pedals. What sounds like sheer power is almost entirely placement.","Never a hit, and rarely anthologised beside the ballads that made the band's name, it is the track drummers name when asked why Webb mattered. The Goodman battle has hardened into folklore with both camps claiming victory; the contemporary Black press mostly gave it to Webb. His early death and thin recorded legacy mean the whole argument rests on a few sides like this one.","Follow the breaks and keep counting the beats through them; the tempo never moves. Then hear Krupa on 'Sing, Sing, Sing' from the same year for the showier, slower and far more famous version of the same idea. Any clean Decca transfer will do, since the drums are what the remastering is for."],"artie-shaw-begin-the-beguine":["Three minutes of a Cole Porter show tune that nobody had been able to make work. Jerry Gray's arrangement strips the long, wandering melody back to its skeleton and sets it over a soft Latin-tinged pulse; the saxophones state it, Shaw's clarinet floats above the section in its clear upper register, and muted brass answer before the clarinet takes it home. Nothing is fast and nothing is loud, and the song's extraordinary length passes completely unnoticed.","Recorded for Bluebird in New York in July 1938. Shaw had just rebuilt his band after abandoning an earlier group built around a string quartet, and this was meant to be the throwaway side; 'Indian Love Call' on the flip was expected to be the hit. Porter's song had flopped in the 1935 show Jubilee and was considered unsingable. The record sold in the millions and made Shaw, overnight, the only real rival Goodman had.","Porter's melody runs to more than a hundred bars, three times the normal length of a popular song, which is precisely why singers stumbled on it. Gray's solution was to stop treating it as a song at all and treat it as an instrumental theme, cutting the words out of the equation. Listen to the rhythm too: it is not a beguine. The Caribbean pattern Porter asked for has been smoothed into a swing a ballroom could actually dance to.","It made Shaw rich and he spent thirty years resenting it, walking out on his band in 1939 and calling his own audience morons in print. Critics then and since have divided over whether the record is a triumph of arrangement or a dilution of Porter; Porter himself was reportedly delighted. It remains one of the best-selling records of the era and the line in every obituary Shaw ever received.","Listen for the clarinet's entrance high above the saxophones, the sound Shaw is remembered for and cooler than Goodman's. Then find a sung version to feel how much melody Gray had to tame. Ella's Cole Porter Songbook, twenty years later, gives the composer's side of the argument back."],"artie-shaw-star-dust":["Three minutes, and among the most admired band records of the era, chiefly for three solos in a row. Billy Butterfield's trumpet states Hoagy Carmichael's melody, warm and unhurried; Shaw's clarinet takes the middle; Jack Jenney plays a trombone chorus that climbs to a held high note musicians still talk about. Behind them sits a string section, unusual in a swing band, and a rhythm section that barely moves. It ends quietly, on a clarinet note suspended over the strings.","Recorded for Victor in Hollywood in October 1940. Shaw had quit the music business the year before, gone to Mexico, and come back with a new orchestra that added strings and a French horn, which his fans regarded as a betrayal and he regarded as the entire point. Carmichael's tune was already a decade old and had been recorded hundreds of times. Shaw's arrangement treats it as a ballad to be listened to rather than a number to dance to.","Jenney's trombone chorus rises to a note near the top of the instrument's range and holds it without a break in the tone, which is why trombonists learn this solo before they learn most others. Listen to what the strings actually do underneath: nothing melodic, only sustained chords, so they function as a cushion rather than a voice. Shaw had learned from the failure of his 1936 string-quartet band that strings in jazz must stay out of the way.","It sold well and is regularly voted the finest version of a song with hundreds of rivals. The strings remain the argument: to jazz purists they are the moment the swing band began softening into the easy-listening orchestras of the 1950s, and Shaw is often blamed for a trend he only accelerated. His own view, stated repeatedly and at length, was that the purists had never understood what he was attempting.","Play it late and quiet. Count the three solos and pick a favourite; most listeners choose Jenney. Then compare Louis Armstrong's 1931 recording of the same song for the opposite approach, and Ravel's Pavane for the same nostalgia treated as a colour rather than a mood."],"hampton-flying-home":["Three minutes, a riff tune at a medium-fast tempo, and one saxophone solo that changed popular music. Lionel Hampton's big band states the theme in unison; then Illinois Jacquet plays two choruses of tenor saxophone that abandon melody altogether for repeated high notes, squeals and honks, building to a shriek the band answers with a shout chorus. Hampton's vibraphone frames the whole thing. The performance is designed to make a room lose control, and it does.","Recorded for Decca in New York in 1942. Hampton had written the tune with Benny Goodman in 1939 for the Goodman Sextet, where Charlie Christian played it on electric guitar; leaving to form his own big band, Hampton took it with him. Jacquet was barely twenty. The record sold steadily for a decade, was played at every dance the band worked, and became the number audiences demanded before it had been announced.","What Jacquet plays is not a jazz solo in the 1942 sense. It has almost no harmonic movement; it repeats one high note in rhythm, over and over, and takes its effect from sheer physical persistence. Every rhythm-and-blues tenor player of the following fifteen years, and through them every rock and roll saxophonist, is copying this chorus. Listen for the moment the band stops arranging and simply riffs behind him.","Jazz critics disliked it and called it exhibitionism; the same solo was, within a few years, the standard by which honking tenor players were judged, and Jacquet spent a career both proud of it and boxed in by it. Historians now treat the record as one of the clearest bridges from swing to rhythm and blues, and Hampton's band as the place where the transition happened first.","Listen for the exact moment the solo stops developing and starts repeating; that is the invention, not a failure of imagination. Then hear the 1939 Goodman Sextet version with Charlie Christian for the same tune as chamber music. Any decent Decca transfer works. The point is the volume."],"billie-holiday-summertime":["Three minutes, a small pick-up group, and the first jazz singer to treat Gershwin's lullaby as a blues. Holiday enters after a short instrumental introduction, sings a single chorus almost without ornament, and stays behind the beat throughout, so the words arrive a fraction later than you expect them. Bunny Berigan's trumpet answers her phrases. There is no drama and no rubato; the whole effect comes from timing, and from a voice with a small range used exactly.","Recorded in New York in July 1936, a year after Porgy and Bess had closed at a loss on Broadway. Holiday was twenty-one, working the clubs of 52nd Street and Harlem, and recording for the jukebox trade under contracts that paid a session fee and nothing afterwards. Songs were handed to the musicians in the studio and read down in a few takes. Artie Shaw, not yet a bandleader of any consequence, played clarinet on the date.","Listen to where the words fall. Gershwin wrote a slow, even melody; Holiday delays almost every phrase and then catches up, which turns a lullaby into something withheld. She also flattens the tune's leaps, staying inside a narrow band of pitches, a limitation she converted into a method. This is often described as the first popular hit the song ever had, two years before it became a standard.","It sold well enough to help establish her, and the small-group sides she made in these years are now regarded as one of the great runs in recorded music. At the time they were disposable jukebox product and she was paid accordingly, with no royalty on any of them. Critics still argue over whether her late, damaged voice or this young light one is the real Holiday; the answer usually depends on the listener's age.","Play it beside a sung version from the opera and hear the difference between a soprano's line and a jazz singer's. Then listen only to her entrances, counting four and noticing that she is always late. Ella's later reading of the same song is brighter and squarer, which tells you exactly what Holiday is doing."],"billie-holiday-strange-fruit":["Three minutes, and for most of them almost nothing happens. A muted trumpet plays a long sour introduction over piano; then Holiday sings twelve lines about a lynching, twice through, with no repeated chorus and no rise in volume anywhere. Frankie Newton's small band holds sustained chords and stays out of the way. She ends on the word 'crop', held and then simply dropped. There is no applause on the record and there was none in the room.","Abel Meeropol, a Jewish schoolteacher in the Bronx who published as Lewis Allan, wrote it as a poem after seeing a photograph of a lynching. Holiday began performing it in 1939 at Cafe Society in Greenwich Village, the first integrated nightclub in New York, where it closed every set: the waiters stopped serving, the lights went down to a single spot on her face, and nothing followed it. Columbia refused to record it, so Milt Gabler issued it on his small Commodore label.","Listen to the introduction, which takes most of the first minute, an extraordinary proportion of a three-minute record and there purely to make the audience wait. The arrangement gives Holiday no instrumental support at the ends of her lines, so the silences belong to her. She never raises her voice or bends a note for effect. The only theatrical decision in the performance is the pause before the final word, and it is the reason the record works.","Banned from many radio stations, it sold steadily anyway and made Holiday a political figure she had not set out to become; the Federal Bureau of Narcotics harassed her for years afterwards, and the song is part of why. Some critics have always found it too explicit to be art, and said the accusation applies to the poem rather than the singing. Time magazine named it the song of the century in 1999.","Listen once without reading the words and once with them in front of you. Notice that she does not act: the restraint is the performance. Then hear her 1944 remake and the late versions, where the voice is rougher and the effect different. Schubert's Erlkonig is the nearest thing in another tradition, one voice, one horror, no rescue."],"billie-holiday-god-bless-the-child":["Three minutes in an unusual shape: a sung passage that states a proverb, then a refrain that keeps circling back to the title phrase. Holiday's voice is at its lightest and most controlled here, the band a small swing group with a gentle two-beat feel, the tempo slow enough to let her sit behind every phrase. The melody moves within a narrow compass and turns on one rising interval on the word 'child'. The solos are short. The record belongs entirely to the singer.","Recorded for Okeh in New York in May 1941. Holiday and the lyricist Arthur Herzog Jr. wrote it together; her account was that it came out of an argument with her mother about money, and that the phrase 'God bless the child that's got his own' was something she had said in anger and then remembered. She was twenty-six, at the height of her technical control, and had just become a headline act rather than somebody's band singer.","The song is not a blues and not quite a standard thirty-two-bar popular song. Its shape, a text stated and then returned to, comes from gospel and ultimately from the sermon. Listen to how Holiday sings the title differently every time it comes round, a little later, a little flatter, never with emphasis. The lyric's bitterness lives entirely in that timing; sung straight, the same words read as a hymn.","A modest seller that grew into one of the most covered songs in American music, taken up by singers from every tradition and often by people who have never heard her version. The authorship has been disputed: Herzog later claimed most of the words, Holiday's ghostwritten autobiography claims the idea, and the likeliest answer is that both are partly right. It is now the Holiday song that people who own no Holiday records can sing.","Follow the title phrase each time it returns and listen for what she changes. Then hear a gospel version to find where the tune's shape came from, and Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige for the same sermon logic written out for an orchestra. Start with the 1941 Okeh original rather than a later live reading."],"ella-fitzgerald-a-tisket-a-tasket":["Two and a half minutes of a nursery rhyme turned into a dance record. Ella Fitzgerald, twenty-one, sings a playground chant about a girl who loses her yellow basket, over Chick Webb's band, with the reeds answering her in the gaps and Webb's drums keeping a light, skipping beat. Near the end she improvises a spoken exchange with the musicians. The voice is clear, quick, perfectly in tune and completely unshadowed by anything that has happened to it.","Recorded for Decca in New York in May 1938. Fitzgerald had won an amateur night at the Apollo in 1934 and been taken on by Webb, who became her guardian. She and the band's arranger Van Alexander built the record out of a rhyme she remembered from her childhood in Yonkers. It sold enormously, became the biggest hit either of them ever had, and made her a star a year before Webb died and left her his orchestra to lead.","The joke is the arrangement's straight face. Webb's band plays it as a proper swing chart, with section writing and a real shout chorus, while the lyric is about a lost basket, and the gap between the two is what made it funny in 1938. Listen for the band members answering her in spoken asides: call and response, the oldest device in African-American music, deployed here on a nursery rhyme.","It made Fitzgerald famous and then followed her around: for a decade she was the singer of novelty numbers, and critics who admired Holiday's weight found her shallow by comparison. The Songbook records of the 1950s reversed that judgment completely. Webb's band has been undervalued by the same story, remembered as her backing group rather than as the finest swing orchestra in Harlem.","Hear it first as a piece of comic timing. Then jump twenty years to her Cole Porter Songbook and hear the same voice with all the sugar taken out. Listen for Webb behind her throughout; the drumming is far better than the song strictly requires."],"ella-fitzgerald-cole-porter-songbook":["Two LPs, thirty-two songs, close to two hours. Buddy Bregman's arrangements are light and mostly orchestral, strings and woodwinds over a rhythm section, and they stay behind the voice; there is very little improvising anywhere on the record. Fitzgerald sings the melodies as written, with the introductory verses most singers cut put back in, from 'Night and Day' and 'Begin the Beguine' to songs nobody had recorded in years. The tone is even from bottom to top and the diction needs no lyric sheet.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1956, the first release on Norman Granz's new Verve label, which he founded largely in order to record her properly. Fitzgerald had spent twenty years at Decca singing whatever the label put in front of her. Granz's idea was to treat the great American songwriters as a repertoire worth documenting whole, at exactly the moment rock and roll was about to turn that repertoire into history. Porter, still living, is said to have remarked on her diction.","The restored verses are the point of the project. Porter wrote introductory sections for most of his songs that nightclub singers routinely dropped, and hearing them changes the songs, because the verse usually sets up the joke or the regret the refrain then delivers. Listen also to how little Fitzgerald ornaments. She was the most fluent improviser of her generation and here she almost never departs from the written tune, deliberately.","It sold beyond anything else in her career, ran to seven more Songbooks across eight years, and effectively invented the idea of the Great American Songbook as a canon. Jazz critics have complained ever since that the records are too polite and that Bregman's charts are the weakest in the series. The counter-argument is that the project was preservation rather than performance, and that on those terms it succeeded completely.","Do not play it straight through. Take 'Ev'ry Time We Say Goodbye' and 'Night and Day' first, then one song you have never heard. Compare her 'Begin the Beguine' with Artie Shaw's instrumental to hear the words put back in. Schubert's Winterreise is a distant relation: songs treated as a repertoire rather than as a set of hits."],"ella-fitzgerald-mack-the-knife-berlin":["A live set of about forty minutes, recorded in one night with a quartet rather than an orchestra. The famous track comes late: Fitzgerald announces that nobody has recorded 'Mack the Knife' as a girl, then loses the words after a couple of verses, sings about having lost them, invents replacement lyrics naming Louis Armstrong and Bobby Darin, and finishes with a scat impression of Armstrong's voice that brings the hall down. The rest of the set is swinging, unfussy and beautifully sung.","Recorded at the Deutschlandhalle in Berlin in February 1960, on one of Norman Granz's package tours, fifteen years after the war and a year before the Wall. Kurt Weill's song came from The Threepenny Opera of 1928, written a few miles away, and by 1960 it had become an American pop hit by way of Bobby Darin. Fitzgerald was singing a Berlin song back to Berlin, in English, from a memory that turned out to be incomplete.","The mistake is the record. She loses the lyric partway through and never recovers it, and what follows is minutes of improvised text delivered in strict time and perfect tune; she does not drop a single beat while inventing. Listen for the moment she sings that she is making a wreck of the song. The audience laughs, and she uses the laugh as a rhythmic rest. It won a Grammy, for a song she did not know.","It became the best known live jazz vocal record of its era and the standard example of what a great singer does when everything goes wrong. Collectors note that the album has been reissued in expanded and resequenced forms and that the original LP was edited; the 1960 sequence is still what most people mean by it. Weill scholars tend to be less charmed, since almost nothing of Brecht's text survives the encounter.","Go straight to 'Mack the Knife', then back to the start of the set. Listen to the rhythm section, which never flinches through the improvised verses. Then hear the song in German on a Weill recording to find out what the words actually say; the distance between the two versions is the whole point."],"lester-young-shoe-shine-boy":["Just over three minutes by a five-piece group drawn from the Count Basie band, and the record on which a new way of playing the tenor saxophone arrives fully formed. Lester Young takes two choruses: a light, dry tone with almost no vibrato, phrases long enough to cross the barlines, and a rhythmic placement that floats slightly behind the beat. Basie plays sparse piano, Jo Jones brushes the time, Carl Smith takes a trumpet chorus. Two complete takes survive.","Recorded in Chicago in October 1936 for Vocalion under the name Jones-Smith Incorporated, because Basie's band was contractually tied elsewhere and the session had to be disguised. John Hammond organised it after hearing the Basie band on a late-night radio wire out of Kansas City. It was the first time Young had ever been recorded. He was twenty-seven, and had already been pushed out of Fletcher Henderson's band for refusing to sound like Coleman Hawkins.","Compare the two surviving takes. Young improvises entirely different choruses on each, both completely shaped, which is the evidence usually offered that he thought in paragraphs rather than in stored phrases. Listen to his vibrato, or its absence: every tenor player of the 1930s used a wide one, and Young's straight tone was heard at the time as thin and unmanly. Within ten years it was simply the sound of modern jazz.","The session sold nothing and was noticed only by musicians. Its reputation grew through the people it converted, Charlie Parker and Stan Getz and Dexter Gordon and eventually the whole cool school. The old division between Young's school and Hawkins's is one of the few genuine stylistic schisms in jazz, and this three-minute side is where the second side of it begins.","Play both takes back to back; that is the whole lesson. Then put on Hawkins's 'Body and Soul' from three years later to hear the two opposite answers to the same instrument. Listen for how long Young's phrases run before he takes a breath, and notice he never seems short of one."],"lester-young-lester-leaps-in":["Three minutes by a small group drawn from inside the Basie band, built on the chord sequence of Gershwin's 'I Got Rhythm' and on almost nothing else. After a brief riff statement, Young plays chorus after chorus, trading eight-bar phrases with Basie's piano, which answers with two or three notes at a time. The rhythm section never varies. The tune as such lasts a few seconds; the record is a demonstration that a set of chords can serve as a form.","Recorded for Vocalion in New York in September 1939 by a septet billed as Count Basie's Kansas City Seven. Small groups drawn out of big bands were a commercial format, cheaper to record and good for jukeboxes, and they gave soloists room the full orchestra could not. Gershwin's 1930 song had by then become the second most borrowed harmonic template in jazz after the blues, used so routinely that musicians simply called the practice rhythm changes.","Listen to the exchanges with Basie. The pianist answers full saxophone phrases with a handful of notes and a silence, and the silence is the substance: the Basie rhythm section was built to leave holes in exactly this way. Notice too that Young almost never lands on the obvious note at the end of a phrase, stopping a beat early or a beat late, which is why the line sounds like talking. None of it is written down.","It became the standard vehicle for jam sessions and the piece bebop players used a few years later to prove their fluency; Charlie Parker's generation built dozens of new tunes on the same chords. Contemporary critics barely covered it at all. It is now cited as a hinge record, the swing era's finest soloist playing, in effect, the first bebop set of variations on a borrowed ground.","Follow the trade-offs between saxophone and piano and count how few notes Basie plays. Then hear Gershwin's original song to find the chords underneath. After that, any bebop recording on rhythm changes will sound like a direct continuation of this one, because that is exactly what it is."],"hawkins-body-and-soul":["Three minutes in which the melody is played once, in the first few bars, and then never returns. Coleman Hawkins states a fragment of Johnny Green's song and improvises two full choruses over its chords, a big tenor tone with a wide vibrato, accompanied by a quiet rhythm section and, briefly, sustained band chords. The line climbs in long arcs, grows harmonically denser as it goes, and reaches its loudest point in the last eight bars before falling away to nothing.","Recorded for Bluebird in New York in October 1939, two months after Hawkins returned from five years in Europe to find that younger tenor players had taken his place. He had been playing the song nightly at Kelly's Stables on 52nd Street. The session was routine and the take reportedly almost an afterthought, cut because there was time left over. Victor released it expecting nothing; it became a jukebox hit, which for an instrumental with no tune in it was unheard of.","Count how often you hear the melody: once, at the start, and after that only as a shadow. That was the radical act, a popular record in which the song is a starting point rather than the content. Listen to how Hawkins builds density, beginning with long held notes and ending in runs, so the record accelerates without the tempo ever changing. The harmony he outlines in the second chorus is already most of the way to bebop.","Musicians memorised it, saxophone sections played it in harmony from transcriptions, and it remains the most analysed improvisation of the swing era. The debate has been over what it proves: whether Hawkins invented the modern jazz ballad or perfected the older style at the moment it was ending. Lester Young's admirers heard a heavy, ornate approach already going out of fashion. Both readings are defensible and the record outlives them.","Listen once for the tune, fail to find it, then listen again for the shape: quiet to loud, simple to complex, across three minutes. Then Lester Young's 'Shoe Shine Boy' for the other school entirely. Any clean transfer of the 1939 Bluebird will do; avoid the later live remakes, which are longer and much less concentrated."],"hawkins-picasso":["Under three minutes of tenor saxophone entirely alone: no piano, no bass, no drums, no second horn. Hawkins improvises a continuous line, implying the harmony by the notes he chooses and marking the beat by accent alone. The phrases are long, the tone is full, and the tempo drifts a little because there is nothing there to hold it steady. It is the first recording of an unaccompanied jazz saxophone solo, and it sounds exactly as exposed as that suggests.","Recorded in 1948 for The Jazz Scene, a lavish limited-edition album Norman Granz produced as a deluxe art object, with photographs and heavy pressings sold by subscription to people who could afford it. Granz asked the players to attempt something they could not do on an ordinary session. Hawkins, then in his mid-forties and watching bebop go past him, chose to record with nobody at all. The title nods at the painter, whose name in 1948 simply meant modern.","The difficulty is not the notes but the time. With no rhythm section, every implied barline is Hawkins's decision, and he keeps an audible pulse going purely through where he places accents and takes breaths; remove those and the piece becomes formless. Listen for the points where he lands on the harmony's strong beats. That is the skeleton, and there is nothing else holding the record upright.","It was heard at the time as an experiment and a curiosity, buried inside an expensive album few people owned. Its descendants make the case for it: Sonny Rollins's unaccompanied cadenzas, Coltrane's late solo passages, and the whole solo-saxophone recital tradition after 1960. Whether Hawkins meant to open a genre or to make a point about his own standing in a changing music is still unresolved.","Play it twice. The first time it sounds like an exercise, the second like a line. Then Bach's First Cello Suite prelude or the D minor Violin Partita for the same problem solved two hundred years earlier, one melody instrument implying a whole harmony. Try tapping a beat along with it and see how long you can keep it."],"django-minor-swing":["Three minutes by the Quintette du Hot Club de France: two rhythm guitars, a double bass, Stephane Grappelli's violin and Django Reinhardt's lead guitar, with no drums and no horns anywhere. The theme is a descending arpeggio figure over a short minor sequence, stated in unison by violin and guitar, and then everyone solos over the same few bars again and again. The rhythm guitars chop on every beat, and that chopping is the entire percussion section.","Recorded in Paris in November 1937. The Quintette had formed in 1934 around Reinhardt, a Manouche Romani guitarist raised in caravan encampments outside Paris who had lost the use of two fingers on his left hand in a fire at eighteen. The group played American swing with no drums, no piano and no trumpet, which was less a style choice than a description of who was available. It was the first European jazz that American musicians took entirely seriously.","Watch the left hand. Reinhardt fretted with two working fingers and a thumb, using the damaged third and fourth only for chords held across two strings, which is why his lines run in long single-note sweeps and why his chord voicings are so strange: they are the only ones he could physically reach. The famous rapid arpeggios exist because they can be played by sliding one hand position up the neck rather than by stretching.","The Quintette's records made Reinhardt the first non-American jazz musician of undisputed rank, and 'Minor Swing' has become the anthem of the gypsy jazz scene that grew up after his death, played in every Manouche session in Europe. Critics argue over whether that scene preserves him or embalms him. The romanticising of his biography, and the near-erasure of the Romani genocide from accounts of his war years, is a live issue in current writing.","Listen to the rhythm guitars first, because the whole style rests on that even chop. Then follow Reinhardt's solo and try to hear where he shifts position rather than stretching. Liszt's Hungarian Rhapsody and Ravel's Tzigane show what European art music made of Romani playing; this is a Romani musician making his own case, in his own time."],"django-nuages":["Three minutes, slow, and the most famous tune Reinhardt wrote. The melody drifts downward in long notes over shifting harmony, stated first by clarinet, since the wartime quintet had replaced Grappelli's violin with Hubert Rostaing, and then taken up by the guitar, which plays it with a wide singing vibrato and a slow chromatic descent that seems to lose its footing before it resolves. There is no fast playing anywhere on the record and nothing is repeated for effect.","Recorded in Paris in December 1940, six months into the German occupation. Grappelli had stayed in London when war broke out; Reinhardt, a Romani man in occupied France, remained and went on performing, protected in part by German officers who liked the music while the Nazi state was deporting and murdering Roma across Europe. The tune became something close to an unofficial anthem in Paris, requested nightly in rooms full of people who could not say why.","The title means clouds, and the harmony does what the word suggests: the melody holds while the chords underneath slide away from it, so the same notes keep changing meaning. Listen for the point where the tune repeats and the ground beneath it has moved. Reinhardt could not read music and dictated his compositions by playing them; the harmonic subtlety here came from an ear that had absorbed French concert music as much as American jazz.","It is the record that made Reinhardt a national figure in France rather than a specialist enthusiasm, and he recorded it many times afterwards without ever improving on it. Writing about his occupation years has moved away from the old story of a charmed survivor toward something more uncomfortable, acknowledging both the danger he was in and what playing for the occupier involved. The music has not been damaged by the reassessment.","Play it late and let the harmony do the work; try holding the melody in your head while the chords change underneath it. Then compare a Grappelli-era Quintette record to hear how differently the group breathes with a clarinet in place of a violin. Ravel's and Debussy's slow pieces are the nearest relatives it has."],"parker-koko":["Just under three minutes recorded for Savoy in November 1945. A scrambling unison figure for alto and trumpet, a passage of muted trumpet over drums, then Parker plays two choruses on the chord scheme of Ray Noble's 'Cherokee' at a tempo near three hundred beats a minute, phrases spilling over the bar lines. Max Roach takes a drum chorus, the opening frame returns, and it stops. The melody of 'Cherokee' is never stated.","Parker was twenty-five, four years out of Kansas City, and the after-hours experiments at Minton's and Monroe's had hardened into a style the record industry had not yet named. A musicians' union recording ban had kept it off disc until 1944. Savoy ran the date in a small New York studio; Gillespie was there, a teenaged Miles Davis played on other titles from the session, and the three-minute limit of a 78 rpm side shaped everything.","The absent tune is the point: publishers owned 'Cherokee', so stating the melody would have meant paying for it, while the chords were free to anyone. Listen to the introduction — not a head at all but a written frame, alto and trumpet in octaves, that returns at the end like a door closing. Behind Parker's solo the piano is Gillespie's, hands borrowed from the trumpet.","Older players heard chaos, and the American jazz press spent a decade fighting over whether bebop was still jazz. By the 1950s 'Ko-Ko' had become the exhibit for the defence, transcribed note for note in classrooms. Scholars now argue mostly about the session's details — who played what, and whether Davis really stood aside because the tempo was beyond him.","Play it twice: first for the shock of speed, then counting eight bars at a time so you can feel where Parker's phrases refuse to land. Hear 'Cherokee' in a swing band version afterwards and the trick becomes obvious. The Savoy masters have been reissued many times; look for a transfer that keeps the room sound rather than scrubbing it clean."],"parker-parkers-mood":["A slow blues in twelve bars, cut for Savoy in 1948 with John Lewis at the piano, Curley Russell on bass and Max Roach on drums. It opens with an unaccompanied alto call, a rising and falling fanfare answered by the piano, before Parker plays the most vocal choruses of his career: long bent notes, sudden clusters of fast ones, and silences left open. Three minutes, with no theme beyond the blues itself.","1948 found Parker at his most productive and least stable. A breakdown and six months at Camarillo State Hospital lay just behind him, and heroin was already costing him work and reputation. Savoy's studio dates were quick and cheap, three or four titles in an afternoon with little rehearsal. John Lewis, who would go on to found the Modern Jazz Quartet, brought a chamber player's restraint to music built on heat.","Listen for how few notes the final chorus contains. The singer King Pleasure later set words to Parker's solo — 'come with me, if you want to go to Kansas City' — and once you have heard that version the phrasing sounds like speech; after Parker's death in 1955 the record served as an unofficial elegy. The opening call is unaccompanied and never repeated, an announcement that belongs to no chorus.","Critics who accused bebop of being all velocity and no feeling had to account for this. It is now routinely named the finest blues performance Parker left, though reissues have shuffled the surviving takes and there is disagreement over which one the original issue used.","Start here if fast bebop leaves you cold. Follow the alto alone through the first eight bars, then notice Roach's brushes, which never once raise their voice. Compare it with the slow blues of the Kansas City bands Parker grew up hearing, and then with King Pleasure's vocal, which teaches you the phrasing by putting words under it."],"parker-with-strings":["Parker with a small string group — violins, viola, cello, harp and oboe — playing standards at ballad tempo: 'Just Friends', 'Everything Happens to Me', 'April in Paris', 'Summertime'. Jimmy Carroll's arrangements are plush and unhurried. Parker enters over them on 'Just Friends' with an eight-bar phrase that arcs up and does not come down where you expect. The band never swings hard; the alto does all the moving.","Norman Granz produced the sessions from 1949, betting that the most controversial alto player in America could sell records to people who bought light orchestral music. Parker had wanted strings behind him for years and afterwards named these among his own favourites. A ballad date with orchestra was the era's mark of arrival, the gesture Sinatra and Ellington were also making; for a bebop player it doubled as a claim on the concert hall.","Parker's ambitions ran further than this. He sought out Edgard Varèse in New York and asked, apparently seriously, to study composition with him, and he spoke of writing for larger forces. On 'Just Friends' listen to the opening flourish: it is neither the tune nor part of the arrangement but an unmeasured cadenza dropped in front of both, and it is the first thing most listeners remember.","Bebop partisans called it a sell-out and the arrangements syrupy, and the argument has never quite closed. Against it stand Parker's own verdict and the fact that these were his best-selling records by a wide margin. Recent writing treats the date less as compromise than as evidence of where he intended to go next, had he lived past thirty-four.","Ignore the strings for one hearing and follow only the alto line; then listen again for how it refuses to sit inside the arrangement's cushions. 'Just Friends' first. Afterwards try Ravel's Piano Concerto in G, where the traffic runs the other way and written orchestral music borrows jazz phrasing."],"parker-jazz-at-massey-hall":["One night's music from five bebop principals: Parker on alto, Gillespie on trumpet, Bud Powell at the piano, Charles Mingus on bass and Max Roach on drums. Six tunes — 'Perdido', 'Salt Peanuts', 'All the Things You Are', 'Wee', 'Hot House', 'A Night in Tunisia' — taken fast and played competitively, with Gillespie and Parker trading phrases that are half duel, half comedy. Around fifty minutes, audibly live and audibly unrehearsed.","Toronto, 15 May 1953, in a half-empty Massey Hall; a heavyweight title fight on television had kept the crowd away. Parker, under contract elsewhere, was billed as 'Charlie Chan' and played a white plastic Grafton alto, his own horn having been pawned. Mingus recorded the concert himself and issued it on Debut, the label he ran with Roach and one of the first artist-owned jazz labels in America.","The bass you hear was largely added afterwards: unhappy with the hall's balance, Mingus overdubbed his part in a studio, which is why it sits so far forward of everything else. The plastic saxophone is audible too once you know — thinner and more brittle than Parker's usual sound. Gillespie is said to have slipped offstage between solos to check the fight.","It has been sold for seventy years as the greatest jazz concert ever given, a claim the music does not need. The playing is superb and slightly ragged, the recording amateur. What is not in doubt is that these five never played together again, and that this is the fullest surviving live document of bebop's founders at work in front of an audience.","Start with 'Hot House', where the theme's difficulty is the joke. Notice how Powell accompanies — jabbing, irregular, sometimes simply absent. Then go back to the studio 'Ko-Ko' and hear how much a stage changes them. Later masterings have tried to fix the bass balance; the earliest Debut transfers keep the rough honesty of the tape."],"gillespie-shaw-nuff":["Three minutes cut in May 1945 by a Gillespie sextet: trumpet and Parker's alto in a unison head so fast and so exactly matched that it sounds like one instrument with two timbres, over Al Haig's piano, Curley Russell's bass and Sid Catlett's drums. A whirling written introduction, the head, solos, the head again. The theme sits on the chords of 'I Got Rhythm', the common currency of the style.","The 52nd Street clubs had been running this music for two years with nothing on record to show for it. Guild, a tiny label, got Gillespie and Parker into a studio in 1945, and the sides that resulted taught bebop to musicians in cities where nobody had ever heard it played live. The tune is named for Billy Shaw, the booking agent who put the band on the road.","The introduction is the tell. It is written out bar by bar and it is nobody's idea of a jazz riff: a knotted chromatic figure that sounds composed rather than improvised. Bebop is usually described as a soloist's music, yet its calling cards were these arranged frames. Catlett, a swing drummer a generation older than the horns, is the secret of how hard it swings.","Musicians learned these records by needle-drop, slowing the 78s to half speed. Older critics called it noise and younger ones treated it as scripture, splitting the American jazz press into mouldy figs and modernists. Nothing about the record is controversial now except the old question of how much of the style was Parker's and how much Gillespie's.","Play the head three times before going further, until your ear can separate trumpet from alto — most people cannot on a first pass. Then follow Catlett rather than the horns. Compare it with an Ellington side from the same years to hear what bebop gave up, which was the orchestra, and what it gained in return."],"gillespie-a-night-in-tunisia":["The most recognisable tune of the bebop years: an A section riding a bass figure that rocks rather than walks, a bridge that suddenly strides in four, and between them a written four-bar break for the soloist that has frightened saxophonists ever since. Gillespie's own 1946 recordings set his high, bright trumpet over it; Parker's Dial version turns the break into a famous twelve seconds of unaccompanied alto.","Gillespie wrote it around 1942 while with the Earl Hines band, when it went by the name 'Interlude'; Frank Paparelli shares the credit. Caribbean and Cuban dance music was audible all over wartime New York, and it was in Gillespie's ear years before he hired a conga player. By 1946 he was leading a big band and the tune had become his signature and his encore.","The lurch in the A section comes from a bass line that refuses to walk, rocking between two notes instead, which is why the bridge's ordinary four-to-the-bar arrives like sunlight. Listen also to what the break actually is: an unaccompanied solo over nothing at all, written into the arrangement, so that every recording of this tune gets judged on four bars.","It became a standard within five years and a test piece within ten, which is both its fortune and its problem — played so often that its strangeness has worn smooth. Debate continues over the date of composition, the original title, and how much Paparelli contributed beyond the publishing arrangement.","Hear two versions back to back, a Gillespie big-band reading and Parker's Dial take, listening only for the break. Then notice the moment the bass starts walking at the bridge; that switch is the whole design. Blakey's live Birdland version shows what a drummer can do with the same rocking figure underneath."],"gillespie-manteca":["Three minutes for big band, recorded in December 1947. It begins with bass and baritone saxophone locked into one repeated figure, then adds congas, then brass, layer on layer, until the band is playing four things at once; a written bridge in straight swing arrives like a change of weather before the layers rebuild. Chano Pozo's drums and chanted voice run through the whole of it.","Gillespie hired Pozo, a Cuban percussionist newly arrived in New York, for his big band in 1947 on Mario Bauzá's recommendation. Pozo spoke little English and Gillespie no Spanish, so they worked by singing at each other. Pozo brought the opening vamp and the rhythms; Gillespie and the arranger Gil Fuller supplied the bridge and the overall shape. Pozo was shot dead in a Harlem bar a year later.","The two halves do not belong to the same musical system, and that is the design: the vamp sections sit on a clave-based cycle that never resolves, while the bridge is ordinary thirty-two-bar swing. Gillespie said afterwards that Pozo's material had no bridge at all until he wrote one for it. Underneath the horns, listen for Pozo's chanted vocal line.","'Manteca' is usually dated as the start of Afro-Cuban jazz as a recorded style, though Machito's band had been fusing the two in New York for years and the priority argument persists. Its influence is not in doubt: Latin jazz, salsa and a great deal of later funk descend from those stacked, unresolving ostinatos.","Count the layers as they enter — bass, baritone, congas, trumpets — then wait for the bridge to knock the floor out. Follow it with Stravinsky's Rite of Spring for the same trick of piling repeated cells until they grind against each other, and with Milhaud's La création du monde for the opposite traffic, a European composer borrowing from jazz."],"thelonious-monk-round-midnight":["A ballad in a minor key whose melody keeps stepping downward and then correcting itself, harmonised with chords that never settle where a 1940s popular song would settle. Monk's own first recording, made for Blue Note in November 1947, frames it with a written horn introduction and a coda, Art Blakey's brushes underneath. Three minutes, in which the theme matters more than anything the soloists do with it.","Monk was thirty, had been house pianist at Minton's Playhouse in Harlem through the early 1940s, and was almost unrecorded. Alfred Lion signed him to Blue Note in 1947 largely on faith, and the records sold badly for a decade. The tune was older than the session: Cootie Williams recorded it in 1944 and took a composer credit, and Bernie Hanighen later added a lyric.","Nearly every version you have heard begins with an introduction Monk did not write — Gillespie's fanfare, attached to the tune in the late 1940s and now almost inseparable from it. In Monk's original the song simply starts. Notice too the bar where the melody hangs on one repeated note while the harmony slides away underneath, which is the moment the song stops being ordinary.","It is the most recorded composition by any jazz musician, a status that took twenty years to arrive. Critics in 1947 found Monk's playing clumsy and his harmony wilful; by 1964 he was on the cover of Time. The Williams credit is still disputed by scholars who believe Monk had the tune finished years before anyone else touched it.","Hear Monk's own version first, unadorned, then Miles Davis's 1956 reading with the added fanfare, and the difference will teach you what an arrangement does to a song. Listen for the descending melody in the first four bars; it is the whole piece. Monk's later solo recordings of it are slower and stranger still."],"thelonious-monk-brilliant-corners":["Five pieces for a shifting band: Sonny Rollins and Ernie Henry on saxophones, Oscar Pettiford on bass, Max Roach on drums, with Clark Terry and Paul Chambers arriving for the closing 'Bemsha Swing'. The title track alternates two tempos, the second exactly twice the first, so the theme lurches into double time and back. 'Pannonica' adds a celeste; 'Ba-lue Bolivar Ba-lues-are' is a slow blues. Around forty-four minutes.","Riverside had signed Monk in 1955 and eased him in with albums of Ellington tunes and standards; this was the first record of his own music for the label, cut in October 1956 with Orrin Keepnews producing. Monk's New York cabaret card had been revoked after a 1951 drug arrest, keeping him out of the city's clubs for years, so the bands on his records were assembled rather than road-tested.","The title track was never played through successfully. Keepnews spliced the issued performance together out of some twenty-five incomplete takes, and the seams are audible if you listen for sudden shifts in room sound. On 'Pannonica' Monk plays the piano with one hand and a celeste with the other; the tune is named for Baroness Pannonica de Koenigswarter, his patron and friend.","The sessions were notoriously tense, with the title track's written part reportedly defeating the band. Reviewers at the time were baffled; the record then became the standard answer to anyone who thought Monk a primitive. It is now read as the first fully realised Monk album, and the splice is treated as documentary evidence rather than as a scandal.","Start with 'Pannonica' for the tune, then take the title track twice: once for the tempo flip, once counting to confirm that the ratio really is exact. Roach's timpani on 'Bemsha Swing' is easy to miss. If the angles feel arbitrary, hear Bartók's Fourth Quartet and the family resemblance may suddenly click."],"thelonious-monk-monks-music":["A septet recorded in June 1957. It opens with something nobody expects on a jazz record: a Victorian hymn, 'Abide with Me', scored for four horns, lasting under a minute, with no rhythm section at all. Then come five Monk pieces, among them 'Well, You Needn't', 'Ruby, My Dear' and the closing 'Crepuscule with Nellie'. Coleman Hawkins and John Coltrane share the tenor chair. About forty minutes.","Monk's cabaret card was restored in 1957, and the Five Spot residency that followed, with Coltrane in the band, remade his reputation in a season. Hawkins had recorded Monk a decade earlier when almost nobody else would; setting the thirty-year-old Coltrane beside him was a deliberate joining of generations. Riverside were by now willing to let Monk record more or less whatever he proposed.","At one point Monk shouts 'Coltrane, Coltrane!' to cue a solo and the take was kept — listen for it in the middle of 'Well, You Needn't'. The hymn is by William Henry Monk, no relation, and Monk had reportedly loved it since childhood; hearing four horns hold those plain chords before the album's angularity begins reframes everything that follows it.","Critics were kinder to this than to Brilliant Corners, partly because Hawkins's presence certified it as jazz. Coltrane's solos here are now studied as the point where his harmonic thinking broke open under Monk's tutelage. The lasting argument is whether the hymn is a joke, a homage, or a plain statement of where Monk's harmony came from.","Play the first minute, stop, and let the hymn settle. Then go to 'Crepuscule with Nellie', a through-composed piece with no improvisation at all, written for Monk's wife. Compare Hawkins's broad, vibrato-rich tone with Coltrane's narrow driven one in the same tune; they are twenty years apart in every respect."],"thelonious-monk-solo-monk":["Monk alone at the piano: thirteen short performances, around forty minutes. Half are his own pieces, half are old popular songs — 'Dinah', 'I Should Care', 'Sweet and Lovely', 'These Foolish Things'. The left hand often plays outright stride, striding bass note and chord, while the right pulls the melody apart, flattens notes and leaves holes in it. The touch is percussive and deliberately uneven throughout.","Columbia had signed Monk in 1962 and briefly made him a famous man; Teo Macero produced, and the sessions ran across 1964 and 1965. Solo piano was where Monk had begun, in Harlem rent parties and church halls, and these records are full of the stride tradition of James P. Johnson, whom he had known as a neighbour. The cover photograph shows him dressed as a First World War aviator.","Listen to what he does with songs he did not write. In 'I Should Care' he sounds a note and holds the pedal so that its decay bleeds into the next chord — an effect impossible in a band, and the closest jazz piano comes to using silence as material. The wrong-sounding notes recur from take to take; they are not slips, they are the arrangement.","For years this was filed as minor Monk, a label record of standards. Later writers hear it as the clearest statement of his aesthetic: nothing to hide behind, the whole style audible in the touch. The long argument over whether his technique was limited or exactly calibrated is one that solo recordings settle for most listeners.","Begin with 'Dinah' for the stride, then 'Ask Me Now' for the strangeness. Notice the silences and resist the urge to fill them. If you know Satie's Gymnopédies, the shared taste for plainness and space is startling; if you know Liszt's études, this is the opposite ambition pursued with equal seriousness."],"bud-powell-un-poco-loco":["Four and a half minutes for piano trio — Powell, Curly Russell on bass, Max Roach on drums — recorded for Blue Note in May 1951. Roach plays a cowbell figure that runs almost unbroken from first bar to last, an Afro-Cuban pattern rather than a swing beat. Over it Powell states a jagged theme and then improvises in long unbroken single-note lines, hammering the keys, the left hand punching chords at unpredictable moments.","Powell was twenty-six and had already spent long stretches in psychiatric hospitals, where he was given electroconvulsive treatment; a police beating in Philadelphia in 1945 had left lasting damage. Alfred Lion kept recording him through the worst of it. Afro-Cuban rhythm was in the New York air by 1951, four years after Gillespie hired Chano Pozo, and Powell brought the idea into a piano trio, where it had not been.","Blue Note issued three takes together on the same record, which is unusual, and they are not interchangeable: the first two never find the feel, the third finds it and stays inside it. Hearing the failures beside the success is the nearest thing in jazz to reading a composer's crossings-out. Listen to Roach's cowbell, which never once drops the pattern.","Powell's reputation is complicated by the audible struggle in his later records; this one comes from the period everybody agrees about. Critics have called it the finest piano trio performance of the bebop era, and it appears regularly on lists of the century's American music. What remains argued is how far his illness shaped the intensity people hear in it.","Listen to the issued master first, then the two earlier takes, and notice how much depends on Roach settling into the groove. Follow the right hand for one chorus without trying to track the harmony. Afterwards, Gillespie's 'Manteca' shows where the rhythm came from, and Scriabin's hammered études offer a distant cousin in obsessive repetition."],"bud-powell-amazing-bud-powell-vol-1":["A Blue Note album drawn from two sessions. The 1949 date is a quintet — Fats Navarro's trumpet, a nineteen-year-old Sonny Rollins on tenor, Tommy Potter's bass, Roy Haynes's drums — playing Powell originals: 'Bouncing with Bud', 'Wail', 'Dance of the Infidels'. The 1951 date is the trio with Russell and Roach and includes 'Un Poco Loco' and 'Parisian Thoroughfare'. About forty minutes of the fastest, cleanest bebop piano on record.","Powell had no working band and few club engagements; these dates, arranged by Alfred Lion around his hospitalisations, are most of what survives of him at full power. Navarro was dying of tuberculosis and would be dead within a year of the first session. Rollins, still a teenager from Sugar Hill, was handed his first serious recording date by the pianist his whole generation had copied off records.","The 1949 quintet themes are not blowing vehicles but written pieces with bridges and codas, much closer to compositions than the borrowed-chord contrafacts that filled the era's sessions. Listen to 'Dance of the Infidels' for a theme that would stand up without any solos at all. Powell's left hand does almost nothing — a jab here and there — which is why the right hand sounds so exposed.","This is the record on which the case for Powell as the pianist of bebop rests, and it has not been seriously contested since the 1950s. The arguments are all around it: discography, take numbers, reissue sequencing, and how far Blue Note's editing shaped our picture of a man who rarely got to a studio on a good day.","Start with 'Bouncing with Bud' for the sheer flow, then 'Dance of the Infidels' for the writing. Listen for Navarro, warmer and rounder than Gillespie and less often imitated. If Powell's right hand starts to seem relentless, Chopin's Op. 10 études make the same demand of the same fingers for entirely different reasons."],"bud-powell-glass-enclosure":["Two and a half minutes for piano trio, recorded for Blue Note in August 1953 with George Duvivier on bass and Art Taylor on drums. It is not a tune with solos: it is a chain of episodes that follow one another without repeating — a grand fanfare, a scurrying passage, a chord that stalls, a fast run at the end. There is essentially no improvisation. It simply stops.","By 1953 Powell was under the informal guardianship of the man who managed Birdland and was reportedly kept confined to an apartment between engagements; the title is usually read that way. He had been in and out of institutions for eight years. In the same period he was writing pieces rather than heads, an ambition for which the jazz record business of the early 1950s had almost no room.","Count the sections: there are five or six depending how you divide them, and not one returns. Jazz almost never does this, because the form is normally a loop you go round. The ending gives the game away: a downward rush that does not cadence so much as fall off an edge, which is why the piece feels like a door shutting rather than a performance finishing.","Contemporary reviewers barely noticed it, sitting as it did among ordinary trio sides. Later writers took it up as evidence that Powell was a composer of real ambition kept from the forms he needed, and it has since been arranged for larger ensembles more than once. Whether the biographical reading of the title is accurate is not documented anywhere reliable.","Two minutes: play it four times in a row. Try to predict where each section will go and notice how often you are wrong. Then hear Ravel's Gaspard de la nuit for the same taste for brilliant, unstable piano writing that refuses to settle, and Monk's solo records for the opposite solution to the same problem."],"max-roach-we-insist-freedom-now-suite":["Five movements, some thirty-seven minutes, for Abbey Lincoln's voice, Booker Little's trumpet, Julian Priester's trombone, tenor saxophones including Coleman Hawkins, bass, Roach's drums and, in the last movements, African percussion. 'Driva' Man' is a work song in an uneven metre punctuated by a tambourine crack like a whip. 'Triptych' is Lincoln and Roach alone: prayer, then protest, in which she screams, then peace. 'All Africa' names ancestral peoples over drums.","Recorded in 1960 for the small, fearless Candid label with Nat Hentoff producing, it was conceived for the 1963 centenary of the Emancipation Proclamation and overtaken by events: the Greensboro sit-ins had begun that February, and the sleeve photograph shows three Black men at a segregated lunch counter. Oscar Brown Jr. wrote the lyrics. Roach and Lincoln were partners, and both were putting their careers at risk.","'Tears for Johannesburg' responds to the Sharpeville massacre of March 1960, in which South African police killed sixty-nine people — the suite reaches outside America deliberately and early. In 'All Africa' the exchange between Roach's kit and Michael Olatunji's hand drums is not accompaniment but conversation between two drum languages. Lincoln's scream sits in the middle of the record with nothing around it.","Some critics called it propaganda and questioned whether Lincoln could sing at all; one hostile review provoked a famous published round-table in reply. Roach lost bookings and Lincoln's recording career stalled for years afterwards. It is now read as the moment the civil rights movement entered jazz as content rather than atmosphere, and as a direct ancestor of the free jazz that followed.","Do not sample it; play it through in order, because the sequence is the argument. 'Driva' Man' first, with the tambourine. Brace yourself for 'Triptych'. Then hear Rollins's Freedom Suite from two years earlier to measure how fast the temperature rose, and Blakey's Free for All for the same fury without any words."],"max-roach-drums-unlimited":["An Atlantic album that alternates quintet tracks with three pieces for unaccompanied drum kit. 'The Drum Also Waltzes' is in three, a lilting figure built from tom-toms and bass drum that develops like a theme rather than showing off. 'For Big Sid' salutes the swing drummer Sidney Catlett. The solo pieces have shape, contrast and proper endings; they are compositions for a set of drums, not breaks between choruses. About forty minutes.","By the mid-1960s Roach had spent a decade arguing that the drum kit was an instrument of pitch and melody rather than a timekeeper, and that Black American music should be heard as art with a history. He had co-led the Clifford Brown quintet, made the Freedom Now Suite, and taken his politics into print. Atlantic gave him room to put unaccompanied drumming on a commercial LP.","Listen to the tuning. Roach pitches his toms so that the solo pieces move between recognisable high and low intervals, which is why 'The Drum Also Waltzes' has a tune you can hum afterwards. Listen too for the dynamic restraint: much of this is quiet, and the crescendos are structural rather than climactic, arriving where a composer would place them.","Unaccompanied drum records were a hard sell in 1966 and remain a minority taste, but musicians took the point, and almost every subsequent solo percussion record in jazz descends from these tracks. Critics divided between those who heard composition and those who heard a demonstration of technique; the first view has largely won.","Take 'The Drum Also Waltzes' first and count it in three. Notice how few cymbals there are. Then hear Varèse's Ionisation, written thirty years earlier for percussion alone in an entirely different tradition, and the shared problem becomes obvious: how do you make a piece out of sound without fixed pitch?"],"clifford-brown-and-max-roach":["The first album by the quintet Brown and Roach co-led: trumpet, Harold Land's tenor, Richie Powell's piano, George Morrow's bass and Roach's drums, recorded across 1954. Seven tracks including 'Joy Spring' and 'Daahoud', both Brown's, plus Duke Jordan's 'Jordu' and Bud Powell's 'Parisian Thoroughfare'. Brown's tone is round and unhurried at any speed; the arrangements are tight, with worked-out introductions and endings around every solo.","Hard bop is often dated from this band. After bebop's speed and the cool school's restraint, Brown and Roach put the heat back without giving up the writing, and the formula — two horns, strong themes, blues and gospel inflections, a drummer who leads from behind — set the pattern for a decade of Blue Note and Prestige records. Richie Powell was Bud Powell's younger brother.","'Parisian Thoroughfare' opens with the band imitating city traffic, horns and a whistle, before the tune begins — a piece of theatre inherited from Bud Powell's solo version of his own piece. Listen to how Brown begins his solos: almost always mid-phrase, as though he had been playing before the tape started. Land, the least famous man here, is the quiet reason the ensemble blends.","Brown was twenty-three and already spoken of as Gillespie's successor; he neither drank nor used drugs, which mattered enormously to a generation watching Parker die. He and Richie Powell were killed in a car crash on the Pennsylvania Turnpike in June 1956, and the critical literature has never quite escaped the elegiac mode. On the record alone, the ensemble discipline is what impresses.","'Joy Spring' first — the tune is a small model of how to write for two horns. Follow Roach behind the soloists rather than during his own breaks; he is arranging in real time. Then hear the Jazz Messengers live at Birdland from the same year for the rougher, hotter version of the same idea."],"clifford-brown-study-in-brown":["The same quintet a year on, recorded for EmArcy in February 1955 and playing faster and harder. It opens with 'Cherokee' at a tempo that invites comparison with Parker, and includes 'Sandu', a slow blues, 'George's Dilemma' with its Latin undertow, and a closing 'Take the \"A\" Train'. Brown's playing is clean at every speed, full-toned down low, and he ends phrases where a singer would need to breathe.","The band had been touring for a year and sounds like it. EmArcy, Mercury's jazz imprint, was recording them regularly, and hard bop was becoming a commercial proposition rather than a rumour among musicians. Brown's sound — warm, centred, without the flamboyant high notes of the Gillespie school — was being copied by trumpet players across the country before he turned twenty-four.","'Cherokee' is the test and the joke at once: the same chord sequence Parker used for 'Ko-Ko' ten years earlier, now played with the melody restored and at a tempo that makes restoring it much harder. Listen to Brown's articulation in the fast passages — every note is tongued and separate, where most trumpeters slur in order to survive.","For decades this and its companion album were what people meant by hard bop. Reception at the time was strong and it never went out of print; after Brown's death his discography acquired a weight a four-year recording career could not otherwise carry. Trumpeters still argue over whether anyone since has matched the evenness of his attack.","Play 'Cherokee' and then Parker's 'Ko-Ko' back to back: the same chords, two different eras of technique. Then 'Sandu' to hear Brown slow, which is where the tone tells most. Look for EmArcy masterings that leave the trumpet unbrightened; over-equalised reissues damage the very sound he was famous for."],"art-blakey-a-night-at-birdland":["A live quintet date from February 1954 at Birdland: Clifford Brown, Lou Donaldson's alto, Horace Silver's piano, Curly Russell's bass and Blakey's drums, issued by Blue Note across two volumes, around eighty minutes in all. The repertoire is bebop — 'A Night in Tunisia', 'Now's the Time', 'Confirmation' — but the delivery is louder, bluesier and far more physical. Blakey's press roll before a soloist's entry works like a shove in the back.","Blakey was thirty-four, a swing-trained drummer who had come up with Billy Eckstine's band, and the name Jazz Messengers was not yet fixed to the group. Birdland on Broadway was the music's showcase room, and recording live in a nightclub was still unusual and technically awkward. The club's diminutive master of ceremonies, Pee Wee Marquette, introduces the band and his patter was left on the record.","Listen to the audience. You can hear tables, glasses and conversation, and the band plays over and into it, which is precisely the point: this is dance-hall energy applied to bebop's repertoire. Blakey's trademark is audible several times — a rapid closing and reopening of the hi-hat on the second and fourth beat that pushes the band rather than merely keeping time.","Released as two LPs that have never been out of print, it is often cited as the first hard bop record, though Silver's own dates and the Brown–Roach quintet have equal claims. Brown's death two years later gives it the same posthumous charge as his studio work. Later reissues have restored extra takes and Marquette's full announcements.","Start with 'A Night in Tunisia' and hear what Blakey does with the rocking bass figure. Follow Silver's accompaniment, sparser and funkier than a bebop pianist's. Then compare the studio polish of the Brown–Roach records to hear how much a room adds. Seek reissues taken from the original tapes rather than early electronic reprocessing."],"art-blakey-moanin":["Six tracks, forty minutes, recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's Hackensack studio in October 1958 by the Jazz Messengers line-up of Lee Morgan, Benny Golson, Bobby Timmons, Jymie Merritt and Blakey. The title piece opens with Timmons's piano playing a four-bar gospel phrase to which the horns answer 'amen' — call and response lifted straight out of church. 'Blues March' walks in from a parade; 'Along Came Betty' is Golson's long-lined ballad.","Blakey had rebuilt the band around Golson as music director, and the record turned the Jazz Messengers into an institution and a finishing school — Morgan was twenty. Blue Note's engineer Van Gelder had by then perfected the drum sound that defined the label. The gospel feeling was deliberate and commercial: hard bop was reaching back into the Black church at exactly the moment rock and roll was doing the same.","The album was issued untitled, with only the band's name on the cover; it became 'Moanin'' because that is what people asked for in record shops. Listen for Golson's writing across the other tracks — 'The Drum Thunder Suite' is a three-part feature that gives Blakey composed material rather than a solo spot, something almost nobody was doing for drummers in 1958.","It sold, especially in France and Japan, and turned the Messengers into a touring franchise that ran until Blakey's death in 1990. Purists then and since have complained that the gospel turn was a formula; the counter-argument is that the formula produced Morgan, Shorter, Hubbard, Hancock and thirty years of working musicians who could read, write and lead.","The first thirty seconds decide it for most people. Then listen to 'Blues March' for what a drummer trained on parade rudiments does with a backbeat. Follow it with Silver's 'The Preacher' from three years earlier, the same idea in embryo, and with Copland's Simple Gifts for another composer mining a plain hymn tune."],"art-blakey-free-for-all":["Thirty-five minutes recorded in February 1964 by the sextet Messengers: Freddie Hubbard, Curtis Fuller's trombone, Wayne Shorter's tenor, Cedar Walton's piano, Reggie Workman's bass and Blakey. The title track, Shorter's, is played at a pitch of controlled violence — cymbals washing over everything, horns shrieking in the ensembles, soloists pushed rather than supported. 'Pensativa', which closes the record, sounds almost weightless by comparison.","Shorter had been music director for four years and was writing pieces that stretched the band's harmony towards where he would take Miles Davis's quintet the following year. The session falls between Coltrane's most searching Impulse records and the first wave of free jazz; Blakey, twenty years older than his sidemen, was audibly refusing to be left behind. Van Gelder had by then moved the studio to Englewood Cliffs.","Listen to what Blakey does with the ride cymbal on the title track: for long stretches he abandons the swing pattern altogether and simply roars, which is a free jazz gesture sitting inside a hard bop record. Shorter's theme is built from a single interval turned over and over; once you hear it in the head you will find it inside every solo that follows.","Reviewers found it ferocious and some found it ugly. It sat in the shadow of Moanin' for years and is now the Messengers album musicians name first, largely because Shorter's writing here is the missing link between his Blue Note albums and his work with Davis. How close Blakey came to free jazz, and whether he wanted to, is still argued.","Play the title track loud; quiet playback destroys it. Try counting the first sixteen bars, then give up and follow Blakey instead. Afterwards, Roach's Freedom Now Suite shows the same energy given an explicit politics, and Stravinsky's Rite of Spring the same appetite for pounded, unresolved rhythm."],"horace-silver-the-preacher":["Four minutes of quintet blues recorded for Blue Note in February 1955 by Kenny Dorham, Hank Mobley, Silver, Doug Watkins and Art Blakey. The theme is a simple singable riff played in harmony by trumpet and tenor, its shape close to the old song 'Show Me the Way to Go Home', and the band delivers it with a hand-clapping lilt. Silver's piano solo is spare, riff-based and rhythmically stubborn, with almost no fast runs.","The session was billed as Horace Silver and the Jazz Messengers, the co-operative group that would shortly become Blakey's band. Silver was twenty-six, from Norwalk, Connecticut, the son of a Cape Verdean immigrant. Bebop had made jazz a listener's music; Silver was reintroducing the parts a dancer or a churchgoer would recognise, at a moment when rhythm and blues was outselling jazz everywhere in America.","Alfred Lion reportedly thought the tune too old-fashioned to record and had to be talked round, with Blakey usually cast as the advocate in the retellings. Listen to Silver's left hand: it plays almost nothing but one repeated rhythmic figure, which is the whole trick, and the reason pianists ever since have been able to imitate the style badly.","It was a hit by jazz standards and gave a name to what became soul jazz. The condescension was immediate — funky, crowd-pleasing, backward-looking — and never entirely went away, even as the record's descendants filled the Blue Note catalogue for fifteen years. Silver's defenders point out that the simplicity here is composed, not fallen into.","Listen once for the tune, which you will be humming afterwards, then once for the left hand alone. Notice that nobody plays anything technically difficult. Then hear 'Moanin'' from 1958 for the same idea with a church organ's authority, and Monk's Music for the hymn showing at the root of it."],"horace-silver-song-for-my-father":["A Blue Note album whose title track runs six minutes over a bass figure Silver brought home from Brazil: a bossa nova pulse rather than a swing beat, with the piano stating the melody in bare octaves before Carmell Jones's trumpet and Joe Henderson's tenor take it up. The rest of the record mixes that band with the previous quintet. About forty minutes, warm and unhurried from end to end.","The cover photograph is of Silver's father, John Tavares Silva, a Cape Verdean who emigrated to Connecticut and played violin, guitar and mandolin in a folk band. Silver had toured Brazil and come back with the rhythm; Cape Verdean music, Brazilian music and the Black American church all sit inside the same tune. Blue Note by this point was selling jazz records to people who bought little else.","The bass vamp had a long afterlife: Steely Dan built 'Rikki Don't Lose That Number' on it a decade later and said so openly. Listen also to the piano's opening statement, played in bare octaves with no chords at all — Silver withholds the harmony for eight bars, so the tune arrives undressed and only afterwards gets its clothes.","It became Blue Note's best-selling album of its era and Silver's signature, which flattened his reputation into that of a groove writer. Later assessments have restored the composer: the harmonic writing across the rest of the record is subtler than the hit suggests. Any argument about hard bop's commercial turn eventually arrives at this record.","Let the title track's first minute pass before deciding anything; the tune only opens out at the trumpet's entry. Then listen to Henderson, the most modern player on the date. Compare it with Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras for a different meeting of Brazilian melody with a formal tradition from elsewhere."],"sonny-rollins-saxophone-colossus":["Five tracks, forty minutes, recorded for Prestige in June 1956 with Tommy Flanagan's piano, Doug Watkins's bass and Max Roach's drums — no second horn, so the tenor carries everything. 'St. Thomas' is a calypso; 'Moritat' is the tune from Brecht and Weill's Threepenny Opera; 'Blue 7' is a slow blues, eleven minutes long, built almost entirely out of one three-note shape turned over and over.","Rollins was twenty-five and had just come off a year in Chicago getting clean; he had already recorded with Miles Davis, Monk and the Brown–Roach quintet. Prestige worked fast and cheap — Van Gelder's Hackensack studio, one afternoon, minimal rehearsal, no second takes to speak of. New York tenor playing at that moment was an open contest, and Rollins won it with this record.","'St. Thomas' is not an invention: Rollins learned the tune as a nursery song from his mother, who came from the Virgin Islands, and it has an older life in Caribbean folk music. On 'Blue 7', listen for the small figure he plays in the first chorus and then count how often it returns, inverted, stretched and compressed; the whole solo grows out of it.","Gunther Schuller's 1958 essay on 'Blue 7' argued that Rollins improvised thematically, developing motifs the way a composer would, and made the record an exhibit in the case for jazz as art music. Rollins later said the analysis made him self-conscious for years afterwards. Some writers now suspect Schuller over-read a performance that was at least partly instinct.","'St. Thomas' first for pleasure, then 'Blue 7' with Schuller's claim in mind — try to catch the motif coming back. Compare 'Moritat' with a German cabaret performance of the same song to hear what jazz does to it. Any decent transfer of the Prestige master will serve; the recording is plain and close."],"sonny-rollins-way-out-west":["A trio record — tenor, Ray Brown's bass, Shelly Manne's drums, no piano — made for Contemporary in March 1957. With no chords behind him Rollins has to imply the harmony himself, and the resulting space is enormous. The repertoire is a joke told with a straight face: 'I'm an Old Cowhand' and 'Wagon Wheels', cowboy songs played as hard bop, alongside Ellington's 'Solitude'. About forty minutes.","Rollins was in Los Angeles on tour and the session ran through the early hours after a club date, which is part of why the playing has such a loose, late feel. Contemporary's engineer Roy DuNann got a famously clear sound in a small room. William Claxton's cover photograph shows Rollins in the Mojave in a Stetson and gun belt holding his tenor, a Black New Yorker claiming the iconography of the western.","The piano-less trio was not a novelty but a strategy: with nobody stating the chords, Rollins could stretch or skip them, and the long solos here come as close to thinking aloud as recorded jazz gets. Listen to Manne playing a clip-clop figure on woodblock under 'I'm an Old Cowhand' with a completely straight face; the humour is real and the swing is unaffected by it.","The cover has generated as much commentary as the music, read variously as parody, as a claim on American mythology, and as a record company's idea of a gimmick. Musically it is now standard study for anyone learning to play without a chordal instrument behind them, and DuNann's sound remains a benchmark among audio enthusiasts.","Start with 'I'm an Old Cowhand' and enjoy the absurdity, then stay for the third chorus, when it stops being funny. Listen for how rarely Rollins plays the obvious note at the end of a phrase. Original Contemporary pressings and their careful reissues are worth the search; the sound is part of the record."],"sonny-rollins-freedom-suite":["A Riverside album whose first side is a single nineteen-minute piece for tenor, Oscar Pettiford's bass and Max Roach's drums, again with no piano. It moves through several themes and tempos without a written score's rigidity — a waltz, a march, a long slow section — and returns to its opening idea at the close. The remaining tracks are standards. The suite makes its argument by development, not by words.","Rollins wrote a sleeve note that did the talking: America's culture, he observed, is saturated with the contributions of the Black American, who is nonetheless denied his freedom. In 1958 that was an unusual thing to print on a jazz record. Riverside issued it, then later reissued the album under a different title with the note dropped and the suite buried — a reversal Rollins did not authorise.","The music itself carries no programme and no lyrics, which was deliberate: Rollins wanted the claim to rest on the quality of the playing rather than on illustration. Listen for the return of the opening theme nearly twenty minutes later, slowed and thickened. Pettiford, who died three years afterwards, is doing far more than keeping time; in a piano-less trio the bass is the harmony.","It is now recognised as the first extended jazz statement of the civil rights era, more than a year before Roach's Freedom Now Suite and five before Coltrane's 'Alabama'. At the time the retitling attracted little comment, which is itself part of the story. Critics still debate whether a wordless suite can carry an explicit political claim, or whether the sleeve note does the work.","Give the suite its nineteen minutes in one sitting. Follow Pettiford rather than Rollins for a chorus and you will hear the harmony being invented underneath. Then play Roach's Freedom Now Suite, which answers it with voices, and Copland's Appalachian Spring for the official American pastoral that this record declines to join."],"cannonball-adderley-somethin-else":["A Blue Note quintet date from March 1958: Adderley's alto, Miles Davis's muted trumpet, Hank Jones's piano, Sam Jones's bass and Art Blakey's drums. It opens with 'Autumn Leaves' — a bell-like piano figure, then the trumpet entering alone and slow, one of the most quoted introductions in the music. 'Love for Sale' and the loping blues 'One for Daddy-O' follow. Around forty-five minutes, unhurried and dark-toned.","Adderley had joined Davis's band the year before, and on this date the roles are politely reversed: the album is Adderley's, the presence is Davis's, and Davis was under contract elsewhere, so the sleeve is careful about how it says so. Blue Note rarely got Davis at all in these years. Alfred Lion paid for rehearsal, which is audible in how worked-out the arrangements sound.","At the end of 'One for Daddy-O' a voice asks, 'Is that what you wanted, Alfred?' — Davis addressing Alfred Lion, and the take was kept. Listen also to the contrast the whole record is built on: Adderley's alto bright, fast and blues-drenched, Davis's muted trumpet spare and behind the beat, the tracks sequenced so that the two never quite meet.","It has sold steadily for decades and remains a favourite entry point to jazz, which has made some critics wary of it as dinner-party music. The counter-case is that 'Autumn Leaves' here is a lesson in how much can be drawn out of a standard, and that Blakey's playing is far more attentive than the album's reputation for smoothness suggests.","The first two minutes of 'Autumn Leaves' will decide it for you. Listen for the piano's four-note figure, which never stops turning. Then hear Adderley on his own later records, where the joy is much louder, and Dexter Gordon's Go for the same relaxed authority on a bigger horn."],"cannonball-adderley-mercy-mercy-mercy":["Five minutes of gospel in a rolling six-eight, written by the band's Austrian pianist Joe Zawinul and played on a Wurlitzer electric piano whose reedy, slightly distorted sound was still new in jazz. Nat Adderley's cornet and Cannonball's alto state a plain hymn-like tune; the alto solo builds by repeating phrases rather than running the changes; an audience claps and shouts throughout. It ends on an amen.","Recorded in 1966 in a Los Angeles studio with an invited audience and drinks laid on, then released as a live album from a club that did not exist as billed. Zawinul had been with the Adderleys for five years and would go on to co-found Weather Report; the electric keyboard here was a step in that direction. Soul music was outselling jazz, and this was jazz answering in kind.","The staged audience is the open secret, and it works, because the shouts arrive in the right places — the room knew what it was there for. Listen to Zawinul's left hand, which plays a rocking figure closer to a gospel organist's than a jazz pianist's, and to Cannonball's spoken introduction, which frames the tune as a sermon before a note is played.","It was a genuine pop hit, played on radio stations that never otherwise touched jazz, and covered within months by singers and organ trios. Critics divided over whether this was jazz reaching an audience or leaving the room, the same argument that attached to Silver and to the boogaloo records of the period. Zawinul's later career complicates the easy dismissal.","Listen first for the electric piano's timbre, which is the reason the record sounds like 1966 and nothing else. Then follow the alto solo and notice how little it moves harmonically. Pair it with 'The Preacher' and 'The Sidewinder' to hear a decade of jazz reaching for the same congregation."],"dexter-gordon-go":["A quartet album recorded for Blue Note in August 1962 with Sonny Clark's piano, Butch Warren's bass and Billy Higgins's drums. Gordon's tenor is huge, dry and placed just behind the beat, so that every phrase seems to arrive late and still land right. 'Cheese Cake' opens at a medium swing; 'I Guess I'll Hang My Tears Out to Dry' is a ballad played almost without vibrato. Six tracks, forty minutes, nothing hurried.","Gordon had been among the first tenor players to translate Parker's language onto the bigger horn in the 1940s, then lost most of the 1950s to heroin and prison. Alfred Lion brought him back with a run of Blue Note albums of which this is the best known. Within weeks of the session Gordon left for Europe and settled in Copenhagen for fourteen years, part of a wider expatriation of Black American musicians.","Listen for the quotations. Gordon habitually dropped fragments of other songs into his solos — a nursery tune, a show melody, an anthem — placed so that they comment on the lyric of whatever he is playing. Listen too for how far behind Higgins's cymbal his phrases sit; written down the notes would look early, and the delay is entirely deliberate.","The Blue Note run rebuilt a reputation the 1950s had wrecked, and Go is the album named first. Gordon returned to America in 1976 to a hero's reception, then took the lead role in the film Round Midnight and was nominated for an Academy Award. The persistent critical question is how much of his 1940s priority was later credited to other players.","Start with 'Cheese Cake' and deliberately listen late — try to locate the beat while he plays around it. Then take the ballad, where the tone does everything and technique does nothing. Van Gelder's recording puts the tenor very close; on a good system it is almost too present, which is exactly the point."],"lee-morgan-the-sidewinder":["The title track runs ten minutes over a twenty-four-bar boogaloo: a strutting bass line, Billy Higgins's backbeat, and a riff that Morgan's trumpet and Joe Henderson's tenor play in harmony until it lodges in your head. Barry Harris's piano and Bob Cranshaw's bass hold the groove without ornament. The rest of the album, recorded in December 1963, is straighter hard bop, and none of it sounds like filler.","Morgan was twenty-five and had already been a star with Gillespie's big band at eighteen and with Blakey's Messengers, then lost two years to heroin. Blue Note wanted a strong opening track and got a hit instead. Soul and rhythm and blues were taking jazz's audience away; the boogaloo, a dance rhythm with a backbeat, was the label's answer, and this record turned it into a formula.","The tune is twenty-four bars long, not the blues' twelve or the standard's thirty-two, and that extra length is why the groove seems to keep turning over instead of resolving. Some of the record's sales had nothing to do with jazz at all: the title track was used in a car advertisement broadcast during the 1965 World Series.","It charted, an almost unheard-of thing for a Blue Note album, and the label spent the next four years asking its artists for another sidewinder — a demand several of them resented, Morgan included. Critics have used it ever since as the example of jazz's mid-1960s commercial turn. Morgan was shot dead at a New York club in February 1972, at thirty-three.","Give the title track its full ten minutes; the groove is the composition and it needs the length to work. Listen to Higgins, who never varies and never drags. Then hear 'Song for My Father' and 'Mercy, Mercy, Mercy' alongside it, and Morgan's own Search for the New Land for what he did when nobody was asking for a hit."],"miles-davis-birth-of-the-cool":["The sides gathered here were cut by a nine-piece band: trumpet, trombone, French horn, tuba, alto and baritone saxophone over piano, bass and drums. The horn and the tuba are the point — they give the group a soft, wide middle register no bebop combo had. Tempos sit back, solos are short, and the written passages matter as much as the improvising. Recognise it by the becalmed, hymn-like sound of “Moon Dreams”.","Miles Davis was twenty-two and had just left Charlie Parker's quintet. The band grew out of the arranger Gil Evans's basement flat on West 55th Street in Manhattan, where Evans, Gerry Mulligan, John Lewis and John Carisi argued over voicings. It played two weeks at the Royal Roost in 1948 and then almost nowhere else. Capitol recorded it in three sessions across 1949 and 1950, issued the results as 78s, and only gathered them onto an LP in 1957 with the title that stuck.","Listen for the French horn and tuba doubling the saxophones an octave apart. Evans learned that thickened unison from Claude Thornhill's dance band, where he had arranged before the war; the nonet is essentially Thornhill's orchestra shrunk to the smallest size that keeps the blend. Notice too how rarely the drums announce anything. They shade rather than punctuate, which is half of what the word cool came to mean.","The records sold poorly and were treated as curiosities until the LP appeared and gave a name to a style already spreading from California. Critics have argued ever since about whether cool was Davis's invention or Evans's, and whether these sessions launched West Coast jazz or merely got the credit for it. Mulligan spent years insisting the band had been a collective. The influence itself is not in doubt.","Start with “Boplicity” — Evans's arrangement, credited to Cleo Henry, Davis's mother — and hear how the phrase lengths refuse to land where you expect. Then put on a Parker record from the same years and notice what has been removed: the heat, the extremes of register, the sense of a race. The 1998 Capitol edition adds the surviving Royal Roost broadcasts."],"miles-davis-miles-ahead":["Davis plays flugelhorn throughout — rounder and warmer than his trumpet — in front of nineteen players whose ranks include French horns, tuba, bass clarinet and flutes but no saxophone section to speak of. Gil Evans wrote every note around him and segued the ten pieces together, so the record plays as one unbroken half hour. The textures shift constantly under a single unhurried solo voice; the effect is closer to a concerto than to a big band date.","Davis had signed to Columbia and wanted the nonet sound at full size. He and Evans had not worked together in seven years. The repertoire is deliberately unbookish: a song by Léo Delibes, Kurt Weill's “My Ship”, Dave Brubeck's “The Duke”, Ahmad Jamal's “New Rhumba”. Columbia's engineers assembled the master from many takes, a freedom that magnetic tape had only lately made cheap, and one Evans exploited hard.","The joins. Several of the famous transitions are edits rather than performances, and once you know it you can hear the acoustic of the room shift under the splice. Listen also to Davis's entrances: he almost never begins a phrase on a downbeat, so the orchestra seems to arrive first and he steps in late, a habit that makes a written score sound improvised.","Some critics heard luxury and called it mood music. Others took it as proof that a jazz soloist could carry a large written score without turning into a concerto soloist. The original sleeve, showing a white woman and a child on a sailing boat, angered Davis and was replaced on later pressings. The 1996 reissue restored much of the unedited session material and made the construction audible.","Play it whole and unshuffled — the segues are the argument. “The Maids of Cadiz” makes the case in two minutes: a nineteenth-century French salon song rebuilt for flugelhorn and low brass. Then hear Sketches of Spain, which takes the method further than anyone expected. The mono mix keeps Davis inside the band rather than in front of it."],"miles-davis-ascenseur-pour-lechafaud":["Barely half an hour of music for Louis Malle's thriller, and most of it is one trumpet wandering over a vamp that hardly moves. Davis leads a pick-up group of French players plus the expatriate American drummer Kenny Clarke: tenor saxophone, piano, bass, brushes. There are no themes in the usual sense, only a mood, a walking bass and long silences between phrases. The main title, over Jeanne Moreau walking the boulevards at night, is the sound everything after it copies.","Davis was in Paris in December 1957 at the end of a European tour. Malle, twenty-five and making his first feature, screened the film in the studio and asked the band to play to it. They worked through the night with the images running, using sketched harmonies rather than scores. French audiences already treated Davis as an artist rather than an entertainer, and the film's success at home did much to make jazz the default sound of screen menace.","Listen for how little the harmony moves. Long stretches sit on a single minor chord with the bass rocking between two notes — a year before the modal experiments of Milestones and Kind of Blue. The pictures left no room for chord changes, so you can hear the discovery arriving under duress rather than as a theory. Clarke's brushes never once raise their voice.","The soundtrack was praised at once in France and largely ignored in America, where it appeared piecemeal. It is now read as the hinge between Davis's Gil Evans records and his modal period, though writers disagree about how deliberate the harmonic simplicity was. Later reissues added alternate takes that show the band feeling its way, which some listeners find more revealing than the finished cues.","Watch the opening ten minutes of the film first, then hear the music alone; it loses none of the atmosphere and gains an ache. Follow the drummer rather than the trumpet for one pass. Compare the Gerry Mulligan quartet's pianoless records for another cool sound made largely out of space."],"miles-davis-milestones":["The one studio album by the sextet with both Cannonball Adderley's alto and John Coltrane's tenor beside Davis, over Red Garland, Paul Chambers and Philly Joe Jones. Most of it is fast, hard-swinging bebop. The title track is not: it turns on two scales, one bright and one darker, held for long stretches while the horns play a simple rising figure. Adderley's whoop and Coltrane's crowded runs make the two saxophones instantly distinguishable.","1958 was the year Davis's quintet became a sextet and the band worked almost continuously. Davis had been listening around the edges of jazz — to Ahmad Jamal's use of space, to George Russell's argument that scales could replace chord sequences. Columbia gave him a budget and left him alone. Garland walked out during the sessions after a disagreement, which is why Davis himself plays the piano on one track.","On the title track, count how long each of the two scales lasts: sixteen bars apiece, an eternity by bebop standards, and the reason the soloists sound like they are floating rather than running. Notice that Coltrane and Adderley solve the new problem in opposite ways — Coltrane fills the space with notes, Adderley sings through it and leaves it open.","Reviewers heard a strong hard bop record and mostly missed the significance of the title track; only after Kind of Blue did writers go back and find the method already working here. The name has caused confusion ever since, because Davis had recorded a different tune called “Milestones” in 1947. Arguments persist about how much of the modal idea belongs to Russell rather than to Davis.","Play “Milestones” and then “So What” back to back — the same discovery a year apart, one still restless, one completely calm. Listen for Philly Joe Jones's rim shot on the second beat, the signature that dates the band to the minute. Then try Cannonball Adderley's own records from the same months, with Davis as sideman."],"miles-davis-porgy-and-bess":["Gil Evans took numbers from George Gershwin's opera and rewrote them for flugelhorn, trumpet and a nineteen-piece orchestra thick with flutes, French horns and tuba. There is no singing; Davis carries every vocal line. “Summertime” becomes a short riff tolled behind him like a bell, and “I Loves You, Porgy” is played with so little vibrato that it sounds spoken. “Prayer (Oh Doctor Jesus)” is the wildest thing here, closer to a field holler than to an aria.","The opera was twenty-three years old and freshly contentious: a Hollywood film was in production, and Black critics were increasingly uneasy about a white composer's Catfish Row. Davis and Evans said little about that argument and simply removed the libretto. Columbia's 30th Street Studio, a converted church, gave Evans the reverberant space his low woodwind writing needed. By now the two men were working almost as a single composer.","On “Summertime” Evans hands the orchestra a two-bar figure and repeats it roughly a dozen times without variation while Davis improvises above it — pattern-and-drone thinking a year before Kind of Blue made it explicit. Listen for the moment the figure drops out entirely and the trumpet is left alone in the church acoustic, with nothing underneath at all.","Reviews were strong and it sold well. Later critics have asked harder questions about what is lost when an opera about a Black community loses its words. Others answer that the wordlessness is the point, returning Gershwin's melodies to the blues idiom they were borrowed from in the first place. Evans's scores were long thought lost and have been only partly reconstructed for concert use.","Hear Gershwin's “Summertime” sung first — any great version will do — then this one, and notice that Davis plays almost none of the melody's ornaments. “Gone”, a drum feature for Philly Joe Jones, is the shock in the middle of the record. Seek the stereo remaster; mono flattens Evans's careful separation of flutes and horns."],"miles-davis-kind-of-blue":["Five pieces, two sessions, one sextet: Davis, John Coltrane, Cannonball Adderley, Bill Evans (Wynton Kelly on one track), Paul Chambers and Jimmy Cobb. “So What” alternates two scales a semitone apart. “All Blues” is a slow blues in six over a shivering piano figure. “Blue in Green” is a ten-bar loop that never resolves, and “Flamenco Sketches” gives each soloist a series of scales to move through at will. Nothing hurries; the bass introduces almost everything.","Davis brought sketches rather than parts to Columbia's 30th Street Studio in March and April 1959, and the band read them down, most pieces captured in one or two takes. Bill Evans, recently out of the group, was brought back for the date, and his Debussy-and-Ravel touch is audible in the voicings. Jazz was losing its dance-hall audience to rock and roll and finding a new one in college rooms and hi-fi shops.","Evans's liner note compares the method to Japanese brush painting, where a line cannot be corrected once drawn. Listen closer to “Flamenco Sketches”: the last scale each soloist reaches is the Spanish-sounding one, and each man decides for himself how long to stay there. The original pressings also run very slightly sharp, because one of the three-track machines ran fractionally slow — corrected only in the 1990s.","Praised on release, it became the best-selling jazz album ever made and a shorthand for good taste, which has provoked a backlash of its own: the complaint that it now gets played as furniture. The authorship of “Blue in Green” is disputed. Bill Evans said he wrote it, most scholars agree, and the credit still reads otherwise. Nobody disputes what the record did to everyone who came after it.","Listen to “So What” twice: once for Davis, once for Jimmy Cobb's cymbal, which is the record's pulse and its weather. Then follow Coltrane from here to A Love Supreme and hear where the scales took him. Any pressing from the corrected remaster onward is fine; earlier ones are a shade too bright and too fast."],"miles-davis-sketches-of-spain":["The long centrepiece is the slow movement of Joaquín Rodrigo's Concierto de Aranjuez, with Davis's flugelhorn in the guitar's place and Gil Evans's orchestra — harp, castanets, low woodwind, snare drums — standing in for the strings. Around it sit “Will o' the Wisp” from Falla's El amor brujo, a funeral-procession “Saeta” in which Davis imitates the unaccompanied Andalusian street chant, and “Solea”, a long flamenco lament laid over a march.","Davis heard a recording of the Concierto in 1959 and began buying Spanish folk records by the armful; Evans studied flamenco forms for months before writing a note. The sessions ran long and over budget, with classical session players who found the rhythms hard to place. Spain itself was closed under Franco and neither man had been there, so the record is an act of imagination assembled in a New York studio.","In “Saeta” the snare drums of the procession approach from a distance, halt, and start again: the band is staging a Holy Week street ritual indoors, with Davis as the singer who interrupts it from a balcony. Listen too for how often Evans doubles the trumpet at the octave with a bass clarinet or flute, so the soloist never quite sounds alone even at his most exposed.","Classical critics objected to the liberties taken with Rodrigo, who was reportedly unhappy; jazz critics complained there was hardly any improvising. Both were right and both missed the point, and the album outsold nearly everything Evans ever wrote. Later writers have debated homage against appropriation, and recent scholarship tends to place it in the long line of Spanish tinge running through jazz from its beginnings.","Play Rodrigo's Adagio first, on guitar, then this — the same melody, the same key, an utterly different silence around it. Listen for the moment near the end of “Solea” when the orchestra drops away and the trumpet keeps going. Then hear Falla's El amor brujo for the source of the record's darkest colours."],"gil-evans-out-of-the-cool":["Evans's own band, without Miles Davis in front of it. Five long tracks for around fifteen players, weighted heavily towards low brass and woodwind, with guitar in the rhythm section. “La Nevada” is the heart of it: fifteen minutes built on a single riff that Evans cues and re-cues, letting soloists enter while the texture thickens and thins like weather. The writing leaves large holes on purpose and the band fills them differently each chorus.","Impulse! was a new label with money and ambition, and Evans, then nearing fifty, finally had a record under his own name and his own control. He had spent a decade as the invisible hand behind Davis's orchestral albums and had a reputation as the slowest writer in New York. The programme mixes his own pieces with Kurt Weill's “Bilbao Song” and George Russell's “Stratusphunk” — a fair map of where his ear was pointing.","“La Nevada” was not fully written down. Evans brought in a riff and shaped the performance from the piano as it happened, cueing entrances with a nod, which is why the ensemble sometimes arrives raggedly and why nobody troubled to fix it. Listen for the bass trombone and tuba sustaining under everything: the bottom of an Evans band never stops breathing.","This is the Evans album musicians name when they want to argue that he was a composer rather than an arranger. Critics of the day filed it beside the Davis collaborations; later writers have treated it as the more radical record, because the looseness anticipates the open forms of the decade that followed. The alternate takes released since show how much was decided in the room.","Give “La Nevada” its full length with headphones and follow one instrument — the guitar is the easiest — all the way through. Then go back to Miles Ahead and hear the same hand writing for a soloist instead of an orchestra. The Impulse! stereo mix spreads the winds wide and is worth seeking."],"bill-evans-peace-piece":["Seven minutes of solo piano over a two-chord figure in the left hand that never changes and never stops. The right hand begins with plain, slow melody and drifts steadily further from the key, thickening into clusters and bell-like dissonance before withdrawing again to where it began. There is no theme, no head, no solo in the ordinary sense — only a rocking left hand and a mind wandering above it.","Evans recorded it in December 1958 at the end of a day's work on his second Riverside album. The ostinato came from a vamp he had been playing behind Leonard Bernstein's song “Some Other Time”, which the trio had tried and abandoned. He was twenty-nine, months away from joining Miles Davis's sextet, and his conservatory training — he had played Ravel and Debussy seriously — was audible to everyone who heard him.","The performance is improvised in one take and unedited. Follow the left hand: it is the same two chords from beginning to end, so everything that sounds like harmonic motion is happening above a floor that does not move. Near the middle the right hand reaches notes belonging to no key at all, and the left simply keeps rocking underneath, which is what makes the dissonance feel calm rather than tense.","It was a filler track that became the piece students learn first. Critics have debated whether to call it jazz at all, and Evans rarely played it live, saying it could not be repeated. Its lineage is argued over too: Satie and Debussy are the names usually offered, though the Bernstein vamp is documented and much closer. Minimalist composers have claimed it retrospectively.","Play it in the dark and resist the urge to wait for a tune. Then hear Satie's first Gymnopédie and notice the shared trick: a slow, unvarying accompaniment that makes anything laid over it weightless. From here Kind of Blue is six months away and sounds inevitable."],"bill-evans-portrait-in-jazz":["The first record by the trio of Evans, the bassist Scott LaFaro and the drummer Paul Motian, and the one where the format changes. LaFaro does not walk behind the piano: he plays counter-melodies in the same register, answering phrases and sometimes starting them. Motian uses brushes and mallets more than sticks. The programme is standards — two takes of “Autumn Leaves”, “Someday My Prince Will Come” — plus “Blue in Green”, carried over from Kind of Blue.","Evans had left Miles Davis's band after eight months, exhausted and uneasy in the spotlight. Riverside's Orrin Keepnews gave him a trio date at the very end of 1959. LaFaro was twenty-three and had the technique to play lines rather than roots; Motian was willing to abandon timekeeping altogether. The three had worked the material out in rooms rather than on the stand, which is why a first album sounds like a fifth.","Listen to the two versions of “Autumn Leaves” one after the other: in one LaFaro plays melody under the piano, in the other he stays out of the way. The trio is auditioning its own principle in public. Notice too how often Evans leaves the root out of a chord entirely, trusting the bass to supply it or to decline, which is why the harmony seems to hang in the air.","Reviewers liked it; musicians rebuilt their bands around it. Every piano trio since has had to decide whether to take LaFaro's freedom or refuse it. The lasting argument is whether Evans's introversion was temperament or manner, and whether the classical touch thins the swing — a charge most pianists now consider answered by the playing itself.","Start with “Autumn Leaves” and listen only to the bass for one chorus, then play it again and listen only to the drums. Compare any Red Garland trio record of the fifties for the older division of labour. The Riverside stereo mix separates the three players cleanly, which helps the first time through."],"bill-evans-sunday-at-the-village-vanguard":["Recorded live in a Greenwich Village basement on a Sunday afternoon and evening, with the audience audible throughout — glasses, conversation, the occasional laugh. The selection favours Scott LaFaro: his two compositions, “Gloria's Step” and “Jade Visions”, both appear, and the mix keeps his bass well forward. “Alice in Wonderland” shows the trio's habit of letting the tempo breathe rather than hold. It is a quiet record made in a noisy room.","The trio had been playing together for about eighteen months and had recorded twice in the studio. Orrin Keepnews brought a recording truck to the Village Vanguard on 25 June 1961 and taped five sets. Eleven days later LaFaro was killed in a car crash in upstate New York, aged twenty-five. Evans stopped playing for months, and Riverside drew two albums from the tapes: this one and Waltz for Debby.","“Gloria's Step” is incomplete as recorded, because the tape machine dropped out for a few seconds during the first take; the released version patches the gap. Listen also to the audience. On the quietest numbers the room noticeably drops its voice and then resumes between tunes — the band is not playing over the crowd but pulling it down to its own volume.","Released months after LaFaro's death, it was received as a memorial, and critics have argued ever since about how much of its standing belongs to the story. The consensus holds that both albums from the date are among the finest live jazz recordings made. Complete editions of all five sets appeared later and confirmed how consistent the playing was across the whole day.","Begin with “Gloria's Step” and follow the bass as a lead instrument; it takes a minute to stop hearing it as accompaniment. Then play Waltz for Debby, from the same afternoon. If the music takes hold, the complete Vanguard sessions are worth the length — the between-tune talk is part of it."],"brubeck-time-out":["Seven original pieces by the Dave Brubeck Quartet, every one in a metre jazz had avoided: nine beats to the bar, five, six, three against four. Paul Desmond's alto saxophone is dry and almost vibratoless, Brubeck's piano blocky and chord-heavy, Eugene Wright's bass steady as a metronome, Joe Morello's drums quick and light. The contrast between Desmond's floating lines and Brubeck's hammered chords is the group's whole character, audible within ten seconds.","Brubeck had studied composition with Darius Milhaud at Mills College in California, who told him not to abandon jazz for the concert hall. In 1958 the quartet toured Eurasia for the US State Department, and Brubeck heard Turkish street musicians playing a rhythm of nine beats grouped unevenly. Columbia was nervous: an album of originals in odd metres behind an abstract painting looked like commercial suicide. It became one of the first jazz albums to sell a million copies.","The nine beats of “Blue Rondo à la Turk” are grouped two-two-two-three, the lopsided version Brubeck heard in the street rather than the tidy three-three-three a Western composer would write. Listen also to Wright's bass on the trickiest tracks: he plays almost nothing but roots on the strong beats, the anchor that lets everyone else float. Brubeck cancelled bookings rather than tour the segregated South without him.","Critics of the day accused Brubeck of stiffness, and of being a white musician handed a magazine cover that better players deserved — a charge he accepted with visible discomfort, saying Ellington should have had it first. The metres were dismissed as gimmicks. Sixty years on the record is still in print, still the entry point for new listeners, and the rhythmic argument has been thoroughly won.","Try to clap along to “Blue Rondo à la Turk” and fail, then feel the long beat underneath and stop counting. “Take Five” is the hit, but “Three to Get Ready” is the charmer, alternating waltz and march bar by bar. Compare Bartók's Bulgarian dances for the same lopsided rhythms collected in another tradition."],"brubeck-take-five":["Five beats to the bar, grouped three then two, over a vamp of two chords that lasts the entire piece. Paul Desmond wrote it and plays the tune, a thin dry alto line floating above Brubeck's rocking piano figure. The middle is given over to Joe Morello's drum solo, which keeps the five going while playing across it. The single edit runs well under four minutes; the music never modulates, never resolves, and simply stops.","Desmond wrote the tune at Brubeck's request for something in five, building it from figures Morello had been playing at soundchecks. Columbia released it as a single in 1959 without much hope, and it charted twice, becoming the money that funded the quartet for the rest of its working life. Desmond later left his composer royalties to the American Red Cross, which continues to receive them.","The famous piano vamp is the accompaniment, not the tune. Brubeck plays it unchanged from first bar to last, so the piece has no harmonic motion at all — exactly the discovery Miles Davis was making in the same year. Listen to Morello's solo with the vamp in mind: the piano holds the five while the drums pretend to lose it and never quite do.","It made the quartet famous and left Desmond, who wrote it quickly, faintly embarrassed. Purists called it a novelty, and its long afterlife in films, adverts and lifts has made it hard to hear freshly. Musicians still use it as the first lesson in odd metre, and drummers still argue about whether Morello's solo is played in five or in ten.","Count one-two-three, one-two right through and the strangeness turns into a groove. Notice that Desmond never once raises his volume. Then hear one of the quartet's live versions from the early sixties, which run twice as long and show what the studio take was holding back."],"brubeck-blue-rondo-a-la-turk":["It opens with the piano hammering nine beats to the bar in an uneven pattern — short, short, short, long — at a speed that sounds impossible, with the saxophone doubling it. Then, without warning, the band drops into a slow, ordinary blues in four, and for the rest of the piece the two worlds alternate, the nine swinging back like a door. The improvising all happens in the blues; the nine is the frame around it.","Brubeck heard the rhythm from street musicians in Istanbul during the quartet's 1958 State Department tour and asked where it came from; the answer, as he liked to tell it, was that it was as natural to them as the blues was to him. The title glances at Mozart's Rondo alla Turca, which is neither in nine nor especially Turkish. The piece opens Time Out and announces exactly what the album intends.","The nine divides two-two-two-three, and the joins are where the trick lives: listen for how the band gets out of the nine and into the four, which happens on a single shared beat with no signpost at all. Brubeck also shifts the long unit to different places in the bar in later choruses, so the pattern keeps tilting under the same tempo.","This was the track that convinced sceptics the odd metres were musical rather than arithmetical, and it remains the harder of Time Out's two famous pieces to play. Critics have since noted the irony of a Californian borrowing an Istanbul street rhythm and naming it after Mozart's fantasy of Turkey, though Brubeck's own account of the encounter is warmer than the title suggests.","Listen for the switch into the blues about a minute in — the moment the room relaxes. Then compare Bartók's Romanian and Bulgarian dances, written out of the same kind of field encounter decades earlier, and Mozart's Turkish rondo, which is what this sort of borrowing used to sound like."],"chet-baker-chet-baker-sings":["Baker sings standards in a light, almost boyish tenor with no vibrato, no ornament and very little volume, then plays a trumpet solo that sounds like the same voice with the words removed. “My Funny Valentine”, “But Not for Me”, “Let's Get Lost”, “I Fall in Love Too Easily”. The band behind him — piano, bass, drums — stays quiet enough that you hear breath. Nothing is dramatised; the effect is of someone singing to himself close to a microphone.","Baker was twenty-four, had just left Gerry Mulligan's quartet, and was being sold by Pacific Jazz as the photogenic face of West Coast jazz. Singing was the label's commercial idea and it worked: the record outsold his instrumental albums, was expanded two years later, and fixed his public image for life. Los Angeles studios in the mid-fifties were cheap and close-miked, so the intimacy is partly a technology. This voice could not have filled a room.","Listen to where he breathes. Baker phrases as a trumpeter, holding a line past the point a trained singer would take air, then snatching a breath in an odd place, which is why the delivery sounds unguarded rather than styled. He also keeps almost entirely to one narrow register, transposing songs downward to stay in it, so the melodies come out flattened and conversational.","Singers were scathing about the pitch and the absence of technique; the public did not care, and the record has never been out of print. Queer and feminist readings have since been drawn to the androgyny of the voice. Baker's later life — heroin, prison, a broken jaw, a fatal fall in Amsterdam in 1988 — has coloured every rehearing, sometimes at the music's expense.","Start with “But Not for Me” and hear how the trumpet solo answers the vocal in identical phrasing. Then hear Frank Sinatra sing “My Funny Valentine” and measure the distance between craft and confession. The mono Pacific Jazz mix is the one to look for; later stereo remixes push the voice too far forward."],"chet-baker-my-funny-valentine":["Rodgers and Hart's song played by the Gerry Mulligan Quartet — trumpet, baritone saxophone, bass and drums, and no piano at all. Baker states the melody almost straight, in the middle of the horn, with a tone so unshaded it could be a flugelhorn. Mulligan does not take a solo; he plays a countermelody underneath, so the two horns move like two singers. Without a piano there is no chord to lean on, and the harmony is implied between them.","Mulligan formed the quartet in Los Angeles in 1952, partly because the club he was playing had no piano worth using. Baker, then unknown and barely able to read music, won the trumpet chair at an audition. Pacific Jazz was a new label run out of a record shop, and the West Coast scene it documented was cheap to record, beautifully photographed and very quickly turned into a brand.","Count the bars supported by nothing but bass and brushes: the absent piano is not a subtraction but a structural decision, forcing the two horns to supply the harmony between them. Listen for Mulligan's line in the second half, which is not a harmony part but a separate tune — the arrangement is closer to two-part Baroque counterpoint than to a jazz head.","The record made Baker famous within months and won him magazine polls over trumpeters with far more technique, which caused lasting resentment and the charge that West Coast jazz was a white, photogenic dilution of bebop. Later critics have managed to separate the marketing from the music, and the performance is now taught as the model of what a pianoless group can do.","Listen for the countermelody rather than the tune; once you hear Mulligan's line as an equal, the record changes shape. Then hear Baker sing it two years later, and after that Miles Davis's long, furious live version from 1964, which takes the same song somewhere else entirely."],"gerry-mulligan-walkin-shoes":["A relaxed medium-tempo original for the pianoless quartet: Mulligan's baritone saxophone, Chet Baker's trumpet, bass and brushed drums. The tune ambles, as the title promises. The two horns state it in loose harmony, then trade and overlap so that the accompaniment is always the other horn. Mulligan's baritone is light and agile, played high in its range, nothing like the honking bottom-of-the-band instrument of the swing era.","Mulligan had arrived in California from New York with a reputation as an arranger — several of the Birth of the Cool charts were his — and almost no money. The quartet's residency at the Haig, a small club on Wilshire Boulevard, turned him into a bandleader. Pacific Jazz recorded the group cheaply and often. A narcotics arrest in 1953 broke up the band after barely a year of work.","Listen to how the two horns divide the labour in the final chorus: whoever is not playing the melody is playing a bass line or a riff, so four players cover the work of six. The drummer plays brushes almost throughout and tunes his drums high, which keeps the bottom of the sound clear for the baritone to move around in.","The quartet's records sold enormously and became the emblem of West Coast cool, which later critics treated as a suspiciously comfortable style: polite, white, well recorded. Mulligan spent decades objecting that his band swung hard and that the geography was an accident of where the work was. Heard now, through decent reissues, the playing is springier than the reputation.","Follow the baritone saxophone alone and notice how rarely it descends. Then hear the same quartet's “My Funny Valentine” for the ballad version of the idea, and Mulligan's later Concert Jazz Band for what he did once he had a full brass section again."],"lee-konitz-subconscious-lee":["A new melody written over the chord sequence of Cole Porter's “What Is This Thing Called Love”, played by Konitz's alto and Billy Bauer's guitar in unison, with Lennie Tristano at the piano. The line is long, even and almost without accent — no bebop stabs, no obvious phrase endings — and Konitz's tone is pale and vibratoless, deliberately unlike Charlie Parker's. The rhythm section keeps time and does very little else.","Tristano, a blind pianist from Chicago, ran what amounted to a private conservatory in New York, where students learned to sing solos from records before playing them and were forbidden to lean on familiar phrases. Konitz was his most gifted pupil and the one alto player of the period who could be heard without thinking of Parker. These Prestige sides are the school's manifesto, recorded in the same year as Miles Davis's nonet.","The theme is composed, not improvised, yet it sounds like a solo — which is exactly Tristano's principle, that a written line should be indistinguishable from a good improvisation. Listen for the accents, or rather their absence: Konitz places weight almost nowhere, so the ear cannot locate the bar lines and the melody seems to have started before the record did.","Tristano's circle was admired and disliked in roughly equal measure; the playing was called cerebral, bloodless, a white intellectual's jazz. Konitz outlived the argument by seventy years of playing and is now heard as one of the few genuinely independent voices of his generation. Scholars also note that the group recorded free improvisations in 1949, a decade before Ornette Coleman.","Listen to the unison head twice before following the solos; getting the shape of that line into your ear is the whole lesson. Then hear Parker on the same chord sequence for the heat Konitz was deliberately avoiding. Konitz's album Motion, from 1961, stretches the approach across a whole record."],"stan-getz-focus":["Seven movements for string orchestra by the arranger Eddie Sauter, with Getz's tenor saxophone improvising over them. There is no written solo part at all: Sauter composed the strings complete and left Getz to find his own way through. The writing is nervy and contrapuntal rather than lush — no cushion of sustained chords — so the saxophone has to argue with the strings instead of floating on them. Drums join for one movement only.","Getz had returned from three years in Scandinavia and was between styles, his cool-toned manner out of fashion beside hard bop. Verve's Creed Taylor paired him with Sauter, a swing-era arranger with serious compositional ambitions. Records of a jazz soloist with strings were usually commercial sweetening; this one inverted the formula by making the strings the difficult element and the saxophone the free one.","The movement “I'm Late, I'm Late” is built on a scurrying string figure openly indebted to Bartók's fast night-music, and Getz enters chasing it rather than riding on it. Listen for the passages where he stops playing entirely: the score stands up perfectly well alone, which is why the record feels composed rather than accompanied, and why his silences carry weight.","Getz called it his favourite among his own records, and it is the one critics reach for when defending third stream against the charge of compromise. It sold modestly. Later assessments give more credit to Sauter, whose name is rarely on the spine; the argument now is whether the album belongs to the arranger or the soloist, which is precisely the question the music poses.","Play “I'm Late, I'm Late” first and hear the saxophone catch up. Then put on Bartók's Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta for the sound world Sauter was working in. Nothing else in Getz's discography sounds like this, so hear it before the bossa nova records rather than after them."],"stan-getz-getz-gilberto":["Eight songs, most of them by Antônio Carlos Jobim, who plays the piano. João Gilberto sings in Portuguese and plays the guitar pattern that defines bossa nova, a syncopated figure that lands almost nowhere you expect. Getz's tenor answers him in long, warm phrases. On two tracks Gilberto's wife Astrud sings the English words in an untrained, almost affectless voice. Everything is quiet, and the quietness is the style rather than the mood.","Recorded in New York in March 1963 and held back a year by Verve. Bossa nova had reached the United States through Getz's earlier Jazz Samba, and Brazil was exporting the music deliberately as the sound of a modern, optimistic country — a year before the 1964 military coup ended that story. Astrud Gilberto had never recorded before; she was in the studio because she spoke English. The single, cut down from the album track, became a worldwide hit.","Listen to Gilberto's right hand. The guitar plays a two-bar pattern that contradicts his own singing, so voice and accompaniment are constantly a fraction apart — the tension that makes bossa nova feel as though it is leaning. Notice too that Getz never plays loudly enough to break the room; his volume is set by the guitar rather than the other way round.","It won the Grammy for album of the year, almost unheard of for a jazz record, and turned bossa nova into a lounge cliché within months. Brazilian critics have long been ambivalent about the version of their music that travelled. Astrud Gilberto was paid a session fee and no royalties, a fact that has shadowed the record ever since; her plain delivery is now widely credited as the reason it worked.","Hear “Corcovado” before “The Girl from Ipanema” — the same recipe without the familiarity. Listen for the moment Getz enters on each track, always after the voice and never over it. Then go to Gilberto's own Brazilian records, where the guitar is louder and the saxophone is not there at all."],"modern-jazz-quartet-django":["John Lewis's elegy for the guitarist Django Reinhardt, who had died the year before. A slow, dark theme in vibraphone and piano, stated in unison, gives way to solos over a different and faster sequence, and the theme returns at the end like a frame closing. Milt Jackson's vibraphone is the singing voice; Lewis's piano is spare, almost prim, placing two notes where another pianist would use ten. The bass carries the funeral tread throughout.","The quartet had grown out of Dizzy Gillespie's big band rhythm section and decided, unusually, to behave like a chamber ensemble: dinner jackets, concert halls, printed programmes, no patter from the stage. Lewis, who had studied music formally in New York, wrote most of the repertoire and set the group's manner; Jackson, a blues player to his fingertips, supplied the heat. The tension between the two men is the band.","The solo section is not the theme's chord sequence. Lewis wrote a separate structure with shifting held bass notes to improvise over, so the piece has two harmonic worlds rather than one — an idea borrowed from composed music and almost unheard of in a jazz standard. Listen for the bass holding a single note under long stretches of the solos while the harmony moves above it.","Musicians adopted it immediately and it became one of the most recorded jazz compositions of the fifties. The quartet's formality drew fire: critics called them stiff, and some heard the concert dress as a bid for respectability on white terms. Lewis argued that jazz deserved the same conditions as any other music, and Jackson's periodic frustration with the discipline is part of the group's history.","Listen for the moment the slow theme returns at the end and reframes everything that happened in between. Then hear the same group's Third Stream Music for where the formality led. A Reinhardt record afterwards makes the dedication land — nothing in the tune imitates him, which is the tact of it."],"modern-jazz-quartet-third-stream-music":["The quartet plays with two guests: the Jimmy Giuffre Three, a drummerless group of reed, guitar and bass, and the Beaux Arts String Quartet. The pieces, by John Lewis, Giuffre and Gunther Schuller, alternate written passages for the combined ensembles with improvised sections for the jazz players. The sound is unusual — vibraphone against violins, clarinet against a jazz rhythm section — and the seams between written and improvised are deliberately left audible.","Schuller had used the phrase third stream in a lecture in 1957 to describe a music that was neither jazz nor classical but fed by both, and by 1960 the idea had enough currency to sell a record. Atlantic backed it. The Modern Jazz Quartet was the obvious vehicle: a jazz group already touring concert halls, already playing fugues, already trusted by classical promoters and resented by some jazz writers for exactly that.","Listen to the string players' rhythm. Classically trained musicians in 1960 rarely swung, and the writing mostly declines to ask them to, giving them sustained lines and counterpoint while the jazz musicians supply the pulse. That division of labour is the honest solution to the problem the album sets itself, and where the two groups do meet rhythmically you can hear the effort it costs.","The reception set the pattern for third stream in general: classical critics found the writing thin, jazz critics found the strings a hindrance, and both accused the project of being a theory rather than a music. Schuller argued the case for the rest of his life. Later listeners, less invested in the border, tend to hear a curious and often lovely record that nobody quite followed up.","Start with the Giuffre pieces, where the blend is easiest, before the string tracks. Listen for Milt Jackson's vibraphone, equally at home in both settings, which is the proof that the experiment was possible at all. Then compare Schuller's Transformation and Getz's Focus for two other answers to the same question."],"john-coltrane-my-favorite-things":["Thirteen minutes on a waltz from a Broadway musical, played by Coltrane on soprano saxophone — an instrument almost nobody in modern jazz had touched — over McCoy Tyner's stacked piano chords and Elvin Jones's rolling drums. The tune appears at the start and at the end. In between, the band alternates between two chords for chorus after chorus, the piano hammering, the soprano circling a small group of notes until they stop sounding like a tune at all.","Coltrane had left Miles Davis's group in 1960 and formed his own quartet, and Atlantic wanted something that would sell. Rodgers and Hammerstein's show had opened on Broadway in 1959, so the song was current and familiar to everyone. The soprano saxophone came to Coltrane partly through his study of Sidney Bechet; its nasal, reedy cry, closer to a shawm than to a saxophone, became his second voice almost overnight.","The waltz never stops. Underneath everything, Jones keeps three beats to the bar while playing patterns that suggest four and six at once, so the music feels rooted and unmoored together. Listen for the switch between the minor and major versions of the same two-chord vamp: Coltrane uses it as a light switch, and the return to minor is the darkest moment each time it happens.","An edited version became a hit and Coltrane's calling card, and he played the piece for the rest of his life in versions that grew steadily longer and wilder until the tune was barely present. Critics who loved this recording often recoiled from the later ones. The soprano revival it set off changed the instrumentation of jazz for a generation.","Listen to the piano's left hand, which plays two chords for ten minutes and never tires of them. Then find a late live version from 1965 or 1966 to hear where the idea went. The original album is worth having whole, but this track is the reason it exists."],"john-coltrane-africa-brass":["Coltrane's first record for Impulse! and his only large-ensemble date of the period: the quartet plus a brass group of French horns, euphonium, trombones and tuba, scored mainly by Eric Dolphy from Coltrane's sketches. “Africa” runs sixteen minutes over a bass pattern played by two bassists at once, the brass entering in slow slabs behind the tenor. “Greensleeves” turns the English tune into a modal waltz, and “Blues Minor” is the short hard closer.","Impulse! signed Coltrane in 1961 with a promise of freedom and a budget, and he spent it immediately on brass. He had been listening to recordings of African drumming and reading about non-Western music, an interest that would dominate his last years and is already organising this record. Dolphy, a close friend, did the scoring and conducted, though the label credited him grudgingly at the time.","The brass writing barely modulates. Dolphy voices the horns in dense, static chords that work as colour rather than as harmony, so the ensemble behaves like an enormous drone — the opposite of a big band's usual job. Listen for the two basses on “Africa”: one holds the pattern while the other departs from it, which is why the floor of the music seems to move.","Reviewers divided between those who heard a new orchestral jazz and those who found it monotonous, a complaint that followed Coltrane from here on. The sessions produced more music than one album could hold, and later editions have shuffled versions of “Africa” and “Greensleeves” in and out. Dolphy's contribution is valued far more highly now than it was in 1961.","Put on “Africa” and stop waiting for a chord change; the interest is in the layering, not the motion. Follow the tuba. Then hear Gil Evans's orchestras for the other way of writing low brass behind a soloist, and A Love Supreme for where this drone eventually goes."],"john-coltrane-ballads":["Eight songs, none longer than five minutes, played by the quartet — Coltrane, McCoy Tyner, Jimmy Garrison, Elvin Jones — almost entirely in the melody. There are solos, but they stay close to the tune and to the middle of the horn. Coltrane's tone here is broad and unhurried, with none of the vertical scrambling of the preceding years. “Say It (Over and Over Again)”, “You Don't Know What Love Is”, “Nancy (With the Laughing Face)”.","Coltrane's live playing in 1961 and 1962 had drawn accusations of anti-jazz from critics who found the long modal explorations shapeless. Impulse!'s Bob Thiele scheduled a run of approachable records in response: this one, an album with Duke Ellington, another with the singer Johnny Hartman. A frequently repeated story holds that a damaged mouthpiece made slow playing easier that season. It is plausible and not firmly documented.","Listen to Elvin Jones, who does not play a straightforward ballad beat anywhere on the record. Under the calmest melodies he keeps a shifting, triplet-heavy undertow going at very low volume, so the quiet is never static. Tyner meanwhile voices his chords in fourths rather than thirds — the sound of the quartet's most radical records, applied to songs your grandparents knew.","It was dismissed at the time as a commercial retreat and has since become one of Coltrane's best-loved albums, the one non-specialists own. The argument about whether it represents rest or capitulation continues, and the mouthpiece story is part of that argument. Nothing in the playing sounds reluctant, which is the strongest evidence against the capitulation reading.","Play it late, quietly and straight through; it is short. Listen for the drummer under the melody rather than the saxophone on top of it. Then go to A Love Supreme, recorded two years later by the same four men, and hear how far one sound can travel."],"john-coltrane-a-love-supreme":["A suite in four parts — “Acknowledgement”, “Resolution”, “Pursuance”, “Psalm” — played by the quartet in about thirty-three minutes. A four-note bass figure opens it and runs through everything; near the end of the first part Coltrane sings the words of the title over that figure, many times over. “Pursuance” is fast and hard, with a long Elvin Jones solo. “Psalm” has no theme and almost no pulse: the saxophone recites.","Recorded in one evening in December 1964 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in Englewood Cliffs, New Jersey. Coltrane had stopped using heroin in 1957 after what he described as a spiritual awakening, and the suite is the thanksgiving he had been promising ever since. He wrote a poem and printed it in the sleeve alongside a signed letter to the listener. The record appeared as the civil rights movement entered its most dangerous years.","“Psalm” is the poem. Coltrane plays it phrase by phrase, one saxophone phrase per line of text, so the movement is a wordless recitation you can follow with the sleeve in your hand, right down to the closing amen. Listen also to the opening bass figure: Coltrane transposes it through key after key in the first part, refusing to let it settle before the voices enter.","It sold in numbers no Coltrane album had reached and remains the record that carries his name to people who own no other jazz. Some critics were uneasy with the explicit religion; others have argued that the devotional frame made the increasingly free playing of his last years legible. A sextet version and a live Antibes performance, both released much later, have changed how scholars read the composition.","Read the poem while “Psalm” plays; one hearing turns the movement inside out. Start with “Acknowledgement” for the bass figure, which is the whole suite in four notes. Then compare Messiaen's Quartet for the End of Time for another sacred piece built on slow, static devotion rather than argument."],"george-russell-all-about-rosie":["Three connected movements for a jazz orchestra of about fifteen players, lasting eleven minutes, all derived from a single short phrase — a Black children's game song from Alabama. The first movement is fast and contrapuntal, the second slow and dissonant, the third a headlong swing over which Bill Evans plays the piano solo that made his name. The writing is dense and the counterpoint real, yet the band never stops sounding like a jazz band.","Brandeis University commissioned six pieces in 1957 for its Festival of the Creative Arts, three from jazz composers and three from concert composers, and had them all recorded by one mixed ensemble. Russell had spent years developing a theory of scales — the Lydian Chromatic Concept — arguing that jazz harmony should be built from modes rather than from chord sequences. Miles Davis knew the theory, and Kind of Blue was two years away.","The theme is only a few notes long. Russell derives every melody, countermelody and bass line across all three movements from it, in the way a symphonic composer would treat a motif, while the rhythm section swings unbothered underneath. Listen for the phrase inverted and slowed in the second movement, where it becomes almost unrecognisable, then hammered out plainly in the third.","The Brandeis commissions were meant to prove that jazz composition could stand in a concert hall, and this is the piece critics agreed had done it. Russell's reputation rests on the theory as much as on the music, and there is a long argument about how much of the modal turn belongs to him rather than to Davis. Bill Evans's solo has been transcribed and studied for decades.","Go straight to the third movement for the Evans solo, then start again from the beginning to hear how the material was assembled. Follow the little phrase; once you have it, the whole piece opens. Then hear Milestones, where the same thinking arrives in a band of six."],"schuller-transformation":["Eight minutes for eleven players. It opens as strict twelve-note chamber music — pointillist, pulseless, single notes passed between winds, vibraphone and piano in the manner of Webern. A drum beat enters almost imperceptibly, then a walking bass, and within two minutes the same material is being swung by a jazz band with Bill Evans at the piano. Near the end the process reverses and the music dissolves back into isolated notes and silence.","Schuller was a French horn player who had recorded with Miles Davis's nonet and played in the Metropolitan Opera orchestra, which made him unusually qualified to argue that the two musics could meet. He coined the term third stream in a lecture at Brandeis University in 1957, the same year the university commissioned this piece for its Festival of the Creative Arts. Serialism was then the prestige language of American academic composition.","The transformation is literal and audible if you follow the drummer. The jazz does not arrive as a new section; it is smuggled in one element at a time — first a cymbal, then a bass note on the beat, then a piano voicing — so there is no bar where the style changes, only a few where you notice that it has. The twelve-note row survives the crossing intact.","It remains the clearest demonstration of what Schuller meant, and the usual exhibit for both sides of the argument: proof of concept to his defenders, a laboratory experiment to critics who thought jazz needed no legitimising. Schuller went on to write a two-volume history of jazz that changed the field's scholarship. The piece is performed rarely, which says more about programming than about the music.","Listen once without knowing when the jazz begins and try to mark the moment; you will be wrong. Then follow the piano through the whole span. Afterwards hear Webern's miniatures for the opening language, and All About Rosie, written for the same festival, for the journey made in the other direction."],"jimmy-giuffre-train-and-the-river":["Five minutes for a trio with no drummer and no piano: Giuffre's clarinet, low and woody in its bottom register, with guitar and bass. The piece alternates two ideas — a rocking blues figure that is the river and a chugging, syncopated one that is the train — and never states a beat outright. The pulse is carried by the guitar's strumming and by implication, which leaves the music unhurried and slightly weightless.","Giuffre had been an arranger for Woody Herman's band before moving to California and stripping his own music down to almost nothing. In the mid-fifties he was preoccupied with folk song, country blues and the idea that a jazz group could dispense with drums entirely, and Atlantic let him try it. The trio's performance at the 1958 Newport Jazz Festival was filmed for Jazz on a Summer's Day, which is how most people first met the piece.","The clarinet stays in its dark bottom octave — the hollow register that classical composers reserve for menace and that swing-era jazz clarinettists almost never visited. Listen for how Giuffre articulates: he tongues very little, letting notes slur into one another, which is a country fiddler's phrasing transferred to a reed instrument.","Critics of the day used it as evidence that West Coast jazz had gone soft, and Giuffre's later career — increasingly abstract, eventually free, and commercially disastrous — was read as a warning. Reassessment came late. His 1961 trio with Paul Bley and Steve Swallow is now treated as a landmark of quiet free improvisation, and this piece is the friendly door into that body of work.","Find the clip from Jazz on a Summer's Day and watch the players listen to each other; the missing drummer becomes visible. Then hear Giuffre's Free Fall from 1962 for the same instincts taken to the edge. Compare Copland's American vein for the folk colour in the writing."],"ahmad-jamal-poinciana":["Eight minutes on an old popular song, recorded live in a Chicago cocktail lounge by a trio of piano, bass and drums. Vernel Fournier plays a repeating figure on tom-toms and closed hi-hat that never varies and never swings in the ordinary way; Israel Crosby's bass holds a two-note pattern. Jamal plays very little, leaving bars of silence, then fills the space suddenly with a cascade. The tension between the unchanging groove and the unpredictable piano is the whole performance.","Jamal had worked Chicago rooms for years with a trio built on discipline, rehearsed relentlessly and instructed to play less rather than more. The album, recorded at the Pershing Lounge in January 1958, sold in enormous numbers and stayed on the charts for years, which was unheard of for a jazz piano trio. Miles Davis had been listening since the early fifties and told his own pianists to study Jamal's use of space.","The drum pattern is the tune's memory. Fournier keeps it going through the piano's silences, so nothing ever collapses when Jamal stops playing — the groove carries the form, not the soloist. Listen for the two or three moments when he plays the melody plainly; they land like arrivals because he has withheld it for so long.","Critics of the fifties often filed Jamal as a cocktail pianist, a judgment that took decades to reverse and that says more about the prestige of density in jazz criticism than about the playing. Davis's public admiration helped, as did the hip-hop producers who sampled his records heavily from the 1990s. He is now routinely named among the most influential pianists of the century.","Listen to the drums alone for the first chorus and nothing else. Then hear Milestones, where the same patience becomes modal jazz in a larger band. If it takes hold, the complete Pershing sets show a trio that could keep this up all night without raising its voice."],"ornette-coleman-the-shape-of-jazz-to-come":["Six Coleman tunes played by a quartet with no piano: Coleman's plastic alto, Don Cherry's pocket cornet, Charlie Haden's bass and Billy Higgins's drums. Each piece opens with a tight, often lopsided theme played in unison, then the solos head off without a fixed chord sequence underneath, following the melody's mood instead. The opener, Lonely Woman, is the track everyone knows: a slow, keening lament from the horns floating above bass and drums that race along at double speed.","Coleman had spent the 1950s in Los Angeles, laughed off bandstands and working as a lift operator while he studied theory books. Contemporary Records gave him two albums; then John Lewis of the Modern Jazz Quartet sent him to the Lenox School of Jazz in summer 1959 and to Atlantic, where Nesuhi Ertegun produced this session in Hollywood that May. The title was a label's boast, and that November the quartet opened at New York's Five Spot to fascinated, furious crowds.","Without a piano nobody is laying down chords, so the bass is free to move with the soloist rather than beneath him. Listen to Haden on Lonely Woman: he strums a repeating figure that almost suggests a key, then abandons it the moment Coleman drifts. The horns bend pitch deliberately, playing slightly sharp or flat for expression, which Coleman argued was a truer intonation for the human voice than the tempered scale.","The Five Spot run split New York: Leonard Bernstein was reported enthusiastic, while trumpeter Roy Eldridge and others suspected a put-on. Critics argued for years over whether Coleman could play. Within a decade the question had dissolved; Lonely Woman became a standard, recorded by the Modern Jazz Quartet among many others. It is now heard less as a revolution than as blues-rooted, singing music whose freedom lies in form, not noise.","Start with Lonely Woman and hold on to the two speeds at once, slow horns over a galloping rhythm section. Then play Congeniality and notice how the theme stops and starts like speech. Compare Miles Davis's Kind of Blue from the same year, which loosened harmony more politely. The original Atlantic stereo mix separates the four players cleanly; any faithful remaster will do."],"ornette-coleman-free-jazz":["One continuous improvisation of about 37 minutes by two quartets playing at once, one in each stereo channel. On the left, Coleman, Don Cherry, Scott LaFaro and Billy Higgins; on the right, Eric Dolphy on bass clarinet, Freddie Hubbard, Charlie Haden and Ed Blackwell. Short written fanfares mark the joins, and each player takes a turn in the spotlight while the other seven respond, argue and comment around him. The texture is dense, busy and surprisingly cheerful.","Recorded in a single New York day in December 1960 and issued the following year, a year after the Five Spot uproar had made Coleman the most debated musician in jazz. Stereo was still a novelty in jazz releases, and the double quartet used it as structure. The gatefold sleeve had a window cut into it showing a Jackson Pollock painting, White Light, making an explicit claim of kinship with Abstract Expressionism.","The notion that nothing was planned is wrong. The ensemble passages between solos are composed, the soloists appear in a set order, and a shorter first take of the same plan, about seventeen minutes long, survives and was later issued on the Atlantic album Twins. Listen for the bassists: LaFaro plays fast and melodic on the left while Haden stays low and grounded on the right.","Down Beat ran two reviews side by side, one maximum rating and one refusing to rate it at all, which neatly summarised the reaction. Its title named a movement, which Coleman came to find limiting. Musicians who dismissed it in 1961 were recording collective blowing sessions by 1965. Later listeners are often surprised by how swinging and good-humoured it is next to the noisier records it inspired.","Use headphones and follow one channel at a time: listen through once to the left quartet, then to the right. Notice how the horns briefly echo a soloist's phrase before scattering. Then hear Coltrane's Ascension, which took the idea to a bigger, hotter band, and Stockhausen's Gruppen, a composed piece for three orchestras that also treats space as form."],"ornette-coleman-at-the-golden-circle":["Two volumes recorded live in a Stockholm club by a trio: Coleman, David Izenzon on bass and Charles Moffett on drums. Most of it is Coleman's alto at its most lyrical, singing long lines over a bass that bows and swoops like a second voice. Then he picks up the violin and trumpet, instruments he had taught himself, and the sound changes completely: violin sawed in rapid, scraping strokes, trumpet in splintered bursts, colour and energy rather than conventional technique.","After 1962 Coleman withdrew from performing, partly in a dispute over fees, and spent the time practising violin and trumpet. He resurfaced in Europe in 1965, where audiences received him more warmly than New York's club owners, and Blue Note, by then open to the avant-garde, recorded the trio over two December nights at the Gyllene Cirkeln in Stockholm. It was his first Blue Note release.","Izenzon was trained in classical bowing and plays arco much of the time, so the bass often sounds like a cello in counterpoint with the saxophone rather than a timekeeper. On the violin tracks, notice that Coleman ignores conventional bowing altogether, sawing in rapid strokes that treat the instrument as a source of texture. Moffett keeps a crisp, almost march-like snare underneath.","Down Beat readers and European critics hailed the return, and the trio is widely held to be the best small group of Coleman's middle years. The violin and trumpet divided opinion: trained players heard incompetence, while others heard a musician refusing to let technique dictate sound. The Blue Note engineering is admired, and the two volumes are among the most approachable ways into his music.","Begin with the alto ballads on the first volume and notice how tuneful they are. Then put on a violin track and hear it as texture, like a painter changing brushes. Faces and Places shows the trio at full running speed. Compare Cecil Taylor's piano records of the same year to hear two very different ideas of freedom."],"ornette-coleman-dancing-in-your-head":["Two long variations on one short, bouncing, folk-like theme, played by Coleman's electric band Prime Time: two electric guitars, electric bass and drums weaving independent lines under his alto. The tune repeats over and over while everything around it shifts, so the effect is part dance record, part mosaic. A short final track, Midnight Sunrise, is something else: Coleman playing with the Master Musicians of Jajouka in the Moroccan hills, reeds and drums in a fierce ritual drone.","The theme comes from Skies of America, Coleman's 1972 piece for his quartet and the London Symphony Orchestra. By the mid-1970s he had been developing a theory he called harmolodics, in which every player may treat the melody as the root of their own line. He had visited Jajouka in 1973 with the writer Robert Palmer, and A&M's jazz imprint Horizon released the album as funk and electric jazz were both commercially strong.","Harmolodics is audible here if you listen to the guitars separately: each is effectively soloing on the theme in a different place at the same moment, rather than one playing chords behind the other. The theme itself never modulates in any conventional sense; what shifts is everything the other players build against it. On Midnight Sunrise the clarinet heard alongside Coleman is Palmer's.","Jazz critics were puzzled by the repetition and the backbeat; rock critics, and later the New York punk and no wave scene, embraced it. It fed directly into James Blood Ulmer's and Ronald Shannon Jackson's bands and the downtown funk of the late 1970s. Debate continues over whether harmolodics is a coherent theory or a poetic name for Coleman's instincts; the record's energy is not in dispute.","Let the theme lodge in your head in the first minute, then stop following Coleman and follow one guitar instead. Play loud. Afterwards try Midnight Sunrise on its own, then Steve Reich's Drumming, which builds different cycles from repetition. The CD reissue keeps both variations intact."],"coltrane-ascension":["A single forty-minute piece for eleven players. Roaring full-ensemble passages, in which horns overlap on a few loose scale shapes until the sound becomes a thick, surging mass, alternate with solos by Coltrane, Freddie Hubbard, Pharoah Sanders, Archie Shepp, Marion Brown, John Tchicai and Dewey Johnson, each backed by McCoy Tyner, bassists Jimmy Garrison and Art Davis and Elvin Jones. It opens with a brief five-note motif, then the dam breaks.","Recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in New Jersey in June 1965, six months after A Love Supreme had made Coltrane jazz's most admired figure. He had been listening closely to the younger free players in New York and brought several of them in, most for the first time on a major label. Impulse! issued it early in 1966; it marks the end of the classic quartet's world, though they are all present.","Two complete takes were made, and both were released under the same title at different times, as Edition I and Edition II, after Coltrane changed his mind about which he preferred; their solo orders differ. Listen to the ensemble sections: the players are not improvising freely but moving through a sequence of agreed chords and scales at their own speed, which is why the mass seems to shift gradually, like weather.","Reactions in 1966 ranged from reverence to accusations that Coltrane had abandoned music for noise. Tyner and Jones, uneasy in the new direction, left within the year. It is now seen as the pivot of Coltrane's late period and a charter for large-group free jazz, from Europe's orchestras to the Jazz Composers Orchestra. Many listeners still find the recording harder to love than to admire.","Listen in one sitting and loud, treating the ensemble roars as landscape and the solos as figures walking through it. Notice how each soloist is framed by Tyner's rolling chords. Then compare Ornette Coleman's Free Jazz, which is sparer, and Penderecki's Threnody, a notated work of the same decade that also turns clusters of sound into drama."],"coltrane-meditations":["A continuous five-part suite, The Father and the Son and the Holy Ghost, Compassion, Love, Consequences and Serenity, for a sextet with two tenors, Coltrane and Pharoah Sanders, and two drummers, Elvin Jones and Rashied Ali. The first section is a howling storm, Sanders shrieking in the upper register; then the music calms into Compassion and a tender bass-led Love before rising again and ending in a quiet, hymn-like Serenity. The arc is an act of devotion.","Recorded in November 1965, the last studio album with McCoy Tyner and Elvin Jones, both of whom left within months. Coltrane was increasingly absorbed in religious reading, Christian, Hindu and Sufi, and in the idea that sound itself could heal. A quartet recording of the same suite, made two months earlier, surfaced years after his death as First Meditations; comparing the two shows what the extra horn and drummer added.","Listen to the drums: Jones plays in pulse and swing while Ali plays in washes of cymbal with no fixed beat, and the two kits argue across the stereo image. Love begins with Jimmy Garrison's bass alone, a calm after the storm, and Tyner's piano here is at its most hymn-like; the suite quietly looks back while moving on.","Many listeners found Sanders's screams unbearable at the time, and some of Coltrane's quartet admirers still do. Later assessments put Meditations beside A Love Supreme as a spiritual statement, but a rawer, more exposed one. The existence of First Meditations has fuelled debate over whether the quartet or the sextet version is the better realisation.","Treat it as one piece and listen through without skipping the opening storm; the peace of Love only lands if you have been through it. Then hear First Meditations for quartet. For contrast with a composed spiritual arc, try Messiaen's Quartet for the End of Time, written in a prison camp and also ending in praise."],"coltrane-live-at-the-village-vanguard-again":["Two long performances from a May 1966 night at the Vanguard by Coltrane's last band: Pharoah Sanders, Alice Coltrane on piano, Jimmy Garrison, Rashied Ali and a percussionist. Side one is Naima, his gentle 1959 ballad, turned into a slow-burning prayer. Side two opens with a long, strummed solo introduction by Garrison, then My Favorite Things, the Rodgers and Hammerstein waltz that had been his hit, stretched and shattered into something ecstatic and nearly unrecognisable.","Five years earlier Coltrane had made his name at this same Greenwich Village club with Eric Dolphy; the title's Again! invites comparison. Tyner and Jones had gone, replaced by his wife Alice and the pulse-free drummer Ali. Coltrane was still filling rooms, but many older fans walked out. Impulse! released the album later that year, one of the few official live records from the final band.","Hear how the old tunes are used: Coltrane states the waltz theme of My Favorite Things on soprano almost exactly as on the 1961 record, then leaves it far behind, so the familiar melody works as a doorway rather than a destination. Garrison's introduction is played with chords and strums like a flamenco guitarist; it is one of the great bass solos on record.","Sanders's screaming contributions divided reviewers, and many listeners still regard this band's recordings as an ordeal. Others hear the most honest version of Coltrane's search, a band that trusted intensity over polish. Its reputation has risen steadily with the posthumous flood of late-period tapes, and it is the most accessible introduction to that period.","Start with the Garrison introduction on side two and let it lead you into My Favorite Things. Then play the 1961 studio My Favorite Things back to back to hear how far the same song travelled in five years. Naima rewards the same comparison with its 1959 original on Giant Steps."],"coltrane-interstellar-space":["Four duets for tenor saxophone and drums, named for planets: Mars, Venus, Jupiter and Saturn. Each starts with Coltrane shaking a set of sleigh bells before he states a short theme and launches into long, torrential improvisation while Rashied Ali plays waves of cymbal and tom with no fixed beat. Venus is gentler; Mars is ferocious. With nobody else present, you hear every breath, overblown shriek and fast rippling run in close focus.","Recorded at Van Gelder's studio in February 1967, five months before Coltrane died of liver cancer at 40. He was playing little in public by then. The tapes stayed in the vault until Impulse! released them in 1974; later reissues added further duets from the session. The saxophone-and-drums format was almost unknown in jazz at the time and became a staple of free improvisation afterwards.","The sleigh bells are the clue to structure: each time they sound, a new section is beginning, so the bells frame improvisations that would otherwise have no audible edges. Listen too for how often Coltrane, amid the storm, returns to a short melodic cell and turns it over, like a preacher repeating a phrase. Ali never plays a steady beat, yet the pulse is always felt.","Released posthumously to a jazz world that had moved toward fusion, it was received as a relic. Its standing has grown since the 1990s; many now hear it as the purest document of late Coltrane, free of any debate about his sidemen. Some listeners still find the undifferentiated intensity exhausting; others call it his last great record.","Start with Venus, the calmest, then take on Mars. Listen to the drums as much as the saxophone. The planet titles invite a comparison with Holst's The Planets; hear Holst's Mars after Coltrane's and notice two utterly different ideas of force. Then compare Peter Brötzmann's duets and trios, which built on this format."],"cecil-taylor-unit-structures":["Four pieces for a septet built around Taylor's piano: Eddie Gale's trumpet, Jimmy Lyons's alto, Ken McIntyre on alto, oboe and bass clarinet, two bassists, Henry Grimes and Alan Silva, and Andrew Cyrille's drums. The music moves in blocks, bursts of composed material handed from player to player, then sprawling improvisation. Taylor's piano is the engine: clusters struck with fists and forearms, fast runs, sudden silences. It sounds less like a band soloing on tunes than an orchestra thinking aloud.","Taylor had studied at the New England Conservatory and admired Bartók, Stravinsky and Ellington as much as Monk. For a decade he had struggled for work in New York, sometimes washing dishes. Blue Note, under Alfred Lion, recorded him in May 1966 at Van Gelder's studio, the first of two albums for the label. Taylor wrote his own liner notes, a dense prose poem about sound and structure.","Notice the two bassists: one bows high and the other plucks low, so they occupy different registers rather than doubling. The title refers to Taylor's method of building pieces from small units, cells passed round the band and recombined, which is why fragments you heard in the opening Steps keep resurfacing in later tracks. The oboe is rare in jazz and gives some passages a chamber-music colour.","Reviewers in 1966 were baffled or awed; LeRoi Jones and a handful of others championed Taylor as the music's most original mind. The record is now regarded as one of the high points of 1960s free jazz and the clearest early statement of Taylor's compositional thinking. Debate lingers over how much of it is written; the answer seems to be more than it sounds.","Try Enter Evening first, the quietest track, with oboe and bowed bass. Then go back to Steps and listen for the moments when the whole band snaps together on a composed figure. Compare Bartók's Fourth String Quartet, whose clusters and cell-building Taylor admired, and Elliott Carter's Double Concerto, a notated cousin in density."],"cecil-taylor-conquistador":["Two side-long pieces, Conquistador and With (Exit), for a sextet: Bill Dixon on trumpet, Jimmy Lyons on alto, basses from Henry Grimes and Alan Silva, and Andrew Cyrille on drums. It is more open and flowing than Unit Structures: long, wave-like build-ups, Taylor's piano rushing in torrents, Lyons threading bebop-derived lines through the storm, and Dixon's trumpet playing soft, smeared, breathy tones. The title track rises and falls in great surges for eighteen minutes.","Taylor's second and last Blue Note album, recorded that October at Van Gelder's studio. Bill Dixon, a trumpeter and organiser, had led the October Revolution in Jazz concerts of 1964 and the short-lived Jazz Composers Guild, efforts to give avant-garde players control of their own work. Blue Note was taking risks on the new music while also scoring hits with Lee Morgan and Horace Silver.","Dixon's trumpet is the odd colour here: he avoids brassy attacks and plays in low, breathy smears, almost like a muted voice, which sets up a very different conversation with the piano. Listen for Lyons, who stayed with Taylor for over twenty years; his alto keeps a thread of Charlie Parker running through music otherwise far from bebop. The CD reissue adds an alternate take of With (Exit).","Less discussed than Unit Structures at first, it has gained ground as the more immediately exciting of the two Blue Notes, with some critics preferring its sweep. Taylor then went years without a steady record deal, working in universities and Europe, so these two albums stood for a long time as his fullest studio statements.","Start at the beginning of the title track and ride the build-ups rather than trying to follow individual notes. Follow Lyons's alto as your guide. Then hear Unit Structures to compare Taylor's tighter and looser modes, and Ornette Coleman's Golden Circle trio from the same year for a different free idiom."],"cecil-taylor-silent-tongues":["A solo piano concert recorded at the Montreux Jazz Festival in July 1974: a five-part suite followed by encores. Taylor plays with astonishing physical force, arpeggios crashing up the keyboard, clusters hammered with the flat of the hand, thunderous bass rumbles, and then passages of hesitant, almost tender single notes. The movements have titles, Abyss, Petals and Filaments, Jitney, Crossing, After All, and the whole plays like one long argument that keeps returning to its opening material.","In the early 1970s Taylor taught at the University of Wisconsin and Antioch College, and his solo concerts had become events in Europe and Japan. Alan Bates's Freedom label, distributed in the United States by Arista, issued the Montreux set in 1975. Solo piano was newly fashionable in jazz thanks to Keith Jarrett's ECM concerts, which offered a very different model: flowing, tonal, meditative.","Listen for short figures from the opening of Abyss: Taylor keeps returning to his opening material, transformed, so the suite coheres like a composed work. Taylor is often called a percussive pianist, but notice his pedalling, which blurs clusters into halos of resonance; the piano rings like a gong in places, recalling Cage's and Cowell's interest in the instrument as a sound source.","Widely considered one of the finest solo records of the 1970s, it won Taylor wide European acclaim. Critics who had dismissed his music as chaotic found the solo format made his logic easier to hear. Comparisons with Jarrett's Köln Concert, recorded six months later, became a standard way to frame two possibilities for solo improvisation.","Try it in one sitting, loud, with no distractions, and listen for the returning material. Then hear the softest encore. For a composed counterweight, hear Scriabin's Fifth Sonata, all ecstatic flights, or Feldman's Triadic Memories, whose extreme quiet makes the opposite case for the piano."],"albert-ayler-spiritual-unity":["A trio of Ayler's tenor, Gary Peacock's bass and Sunny Murray's drums, in four pieces: Ghosts: First Variation, The Wizard, Spirits and Ghosts: Second Variation. Ayler's sound is huge, with a wide, trembling vibrato like an old gospel singer or a brass band at a funeral. He states simple, almost childlike melodies, then tears them apart in screams and honks. Peacock plays fast, free lines and Murray plays no beat at all, just a shimmer of cymbals.","Recorded in New York in July 1964 for Bernard Stollman's new ESP-Disk label, which paid little but let musicians record as they wished. Ayler, from Cleveland, had developed his sound playing in army bands and in Scandinavia, where he found more listeners than at home. The title is a statement of belief; he spoke of his music as spiritual in the most literal sense.","Ghosts is built on a tune that sounds like a hymn or a folk song, and it appears twice, opening and closing the record in two variations, a frame most listeners miss because the middle is so wild. Listen to Murray's drums: the cymbals hiss constantly and the bass drum marks accents rather than time, a sound he developed with Cecil Taylor that removes any sense of a regular beat.","It was barely reviewed at first, but musicians heard it; Coltrane reportedly championed Ayler and asked that he play at his funeral in 1967. The record is now regarded as a landmark of free jazz, some saying the first to fully abandon pulse and chord. Critics still argue about Ayler's intonation and his later turn toward rhythm and blues, but this trio is held in near-universal respect.","Put on Ghosts: First Variation and hum the tune along; it is catchier than its reputation suggests. Then listen to the whole record and let the vibrato wash over you. Compare Bessie Smith's St. Louis Blues and a New Orleans funeral march; Ayler is closer to those than to bebop."],"albert-ayler-love-cry":["Short, march-like and hymn-like tunes, most under five minutes, played by Ayler with his brother Donald on trumpet, Alan Silva on bass, Milford Graves on drums and, on several tracks, Call Cobbs on harpsichord. The harpsichord's tinny jangle is the surprise, making the music sound at times like a Baroque parlour invaded by a brass band. Ayler sings wordlessly on the title track, and revisits old themes, Ghosts and Bells, in compact new versions.","Ayler signed to Impulse! after Coltrane's intervention, and this was his third album for the label, recorded in 1967 and 1968. His music was moving toward shorter, more melodic forms and, on the next records, toward rhythm and blues and rock. He was found drowned in New York's East River in November 1970, aged 34; the circumstances remain unclear.","Notice how Ayler compresses his earlier pieces: the new Ghosts takes the tune of Spiritual Unity and plays it with trumpet in unison, almost like a Salvation Army band. Graves is one of the most original drummers of the era, playing flurries of tom-toms that sound like hand drums. Cobbs's harpsichord gives a church-hall or parlour feel that Ayler clearly wanted.","It sold modestly and was eclipsed in critical attention by his ESP records and by the controversial rock-leaning New Grass that followed. Later listeners often find it the most approachable Ayler, concise and tuneful, while purists prefer the extended trio work. It is a good record for hearing how thoroughly Ayler's music was rooted in melody.","Play Ghosts here and then the version on Spiritual Unity to hear the same tune tightened into a march. Then the title track for Ayler's voice. Compare Ives's Central Park in the Dark, which also layers a street band, parlour music and dissonance."],"sun-ra-jazz-in-silhouette":["A Chicago big band record that sounds, at first, like sophisticated late-1950s hard bop: swinging charts, tight saxophone sections, John Gilmore's muscular tenor. But the textures are strange, full of flutes, percussion, exotic modes and unexpected dissonance. The centrepiece, Ancient Aiethopia, is a long, mysterious piece with chanting, flute and a nearly static pulse, looking toward Egypt and Ethiopia. Enlightenment, a lilting tune Sun Ra kept playing for decades, opens the album.","Sun Ra, born Herman Blount in Alabama, had reinvented himself in Chicago as a visitor from Saturn and built a big band, the Arkestra, which rehearsed obsessively. He and his partner Alton Abraham ran their own label, Saturn, pressing records in small runs and selling them at gigs, one of the first artist-owned jazz labels. This was recorded in 1958 and released around 1959.","Listen to Ancient Aiethopia for the moment the band begins chanting and the trumpet plays a tense, snake-charmer line: this was years before modal or Afrocentric jazz became fashionable. Sun Ra was reading widely in Egyptology and esoteric history, and his spellings of titles were often deliberate. The saxophonists Gilmore, Marshall Allen and Pat Patrick stayed with him for decades, a stability almost unknown in jazz.","Barely distributed at the time, it was known mostly to Chicago audiences. Later reissues brought it wide attention, and it is now often recommended as the best entry point to Sun Ra, proof that the space-age eccentric was a superb arranger in the Ellington and Fletcher Henderson line.","Start with Enlightenment, a tune Sun Ra's bands played for the rest of his life, then Ancient Aiethopia, and notice how the same saxophone section that swings so crisply can turn to incantation. Hear Ellington's Far East Suite afterwards, a later big band travelogue that makes a telling comparison. A modern CD reissue sounds far cleaner than the original Saturn pressings."],"sun-ra-the-magic-city":["Side one is a single 27-minute collective improvisation, The Magic City, for the Arkestra. It begins with Sun Ra's electric keyboard, spacey and warbling, then passes through sections for small combinations of instruments, flutes and piccolos in eerie clusters, saxophones in wild counterpoint, piano runs, near silence. There is no beat or tune to hold on to, but it is carefully shaped, with dynamics rising and falling as if Sun Ra were conducting the band by hand.","Recorded in New York in 1965, where the Arkestra had moved in 1961 and lived communally, rehearsing daily. Sun Ra's hometown, Birmingham, Alabama, was nicknamed the Magic City; in 1963 it had been the scene of the church bombing that killed four girls. Released on Saturn around 1966, the album joined a burst of New York free jazz including the Heliocentric Worlds records on ESP.","Listen for Sun Ra directing: sudden changes of texture happen too cleanly to be accidental, and he is known to have used hand signals to cue sections. The title is ambiguous, part tribute to the city of his birth, part futurist vision of a city yet to be built. The piccolo passages recall Varèse more than anything in jazz.","Initially obscure, like most Saturn releases, it is now regarded as one of the most fully realised of Sun Ra's free recordings, and admired by improvisers for its control of large-group texture. Some listeners prefer the tighter, swinging Arkestra; for others this is the band at its most visionary.","Listen in the dark, in one sitting, and treat it like a film score without a film. Notice how often only three or four players are sounding at once. Then hear Varèse's Arcana or Ligeti's Atmosphères, composed works of shifting orchestral texture, and Coltrane's Ascension, made the same year with a very different density."],"sun-ra-space-is-the-place":["The title track fills side one, over twenty minutes of a simple chanted refrain, space is the place, sung by June Tyson and the Arkestra over a loping rhythm, while saxophones, synthesiser and percussion swirl and erupt around it. It is part gospel call-and-response, part cosmic manifesto, part party. Side two mixes shorter pieces: Discipline 27-II with its chanted invocations, and more conventional Arkestra material, all threaded with Sun Ra's electronic keyboards.","Released in 1973 on Blue Thumb, a rock-oriented label, one of Sun Ra's rare mainstream deals. He had lectured at Berkeley in 1971 on 'The Black Man in the Cosmos', and was shooting a film of the same name, Space Is the Place, released in 1974, in which he lands in Oakland to offer Black Americans passage to another planet. The Arkestra by now performed in costume with dancers and light shows.","Behind the chant, listen for Sun Ra's electronic keyboards: he was among the earliest jazz musicians to use synthesisers, and the burbling electronic sounds are an essential part of the cosmic effect. The lyric is simple but pointed: in the film and the song, space is a metaphor for escape from American racism, the idea later named Afrofuturism.","Sold modestly but became a touchstone for later Afrofuturist artists, from Parliament-Funkadelic's space mythology to hip-hop and electronic musicians. Jazz critics sometimes treated Sun Ra as a novelty; the reappraisal since the 1990s sees him as a serious conceptual artist whose theatre and music are inseparable. The film has become a cult classic.","Let the chant carry you and notice how the horns erupt and subside around it. Then watch the film if you can. For kin, listen to Art Ensemble of Chicago's Les Stances à Sophie and Pharoah Sanders's Karma; for contrast, Stockhausen's Stimmung, a very different ritual chant built on overtones."],"archie-shepp-fire-music":["A mix of originals and standards played by Shepp's sextet with brass and alto. Hambone opens with a raucous, swaggering theme drawn from children's street rhymes; Los Olvidados, the forgotten ones, is a portrait of abandoned city youth. Malcolm, Malcolm – Semper Malcolm, for trio, sets Shepp's own spoken poem over bass and drums, before his tenor wails. Then comes Ellington's Prelude to a Kiss, played with fruity, old-fashioned warmth, and The Girl from Ipanema, the pop hit of the moment, taken apart.","Shepp, a playwright and poet as well as a saxophonist, was one of the most outspoken figures of the new Black music. Coltrane had helped get him signed to Impulse!, and this, recorded in two sessions in February and March 1965, was one of his first albums for the label. Malcolm X was assassinated in New York on 21 February, between the sessions; the elegy was recorded a few weeks later.","Listen to the choice of material. Shepp's sound, breathy, with a wide vibrato like Ben Webster, and his affectionate Ellington show he saw himself inside the jazz tradition, not outside it. The Girl from Ipanema is a political gesture too: the most commercial song of 1964, broken open. The Latin semper, always, in the elegy's title makes the same claim as the poem: that Malcolm endures.","Received as a manifesto, praised by LeRoi Jones and others as an expression of Black nationalist anger in music, and attacked by more conservative critics. It remains Shepp's most celebrated album. The range, from street rhyme to Ellington, is now seen as its point: a claim to the whole of African American music.","Start with Malcolm, Malcolm – Semper Malcolm for the poem and the saxophone cry after it. Then Prelude to a Kiss, to hear how lovingly Shepp plays a ballad. Compare Ellington's own version of the tune and Schoenberg's A Survivor from Warsaw, another spoken testimony over music."],"archie-shepp-attica-blues":["A big band record with strings, singers and a funky rhythm section, moving from the pounding soul of the title track through gospel, blues, ballads and a spoken invocation. Steam is a lament for a young cousin of Shepp's killed in street violence; Blues for Brother George Jackson mourns the prisoner and author shot at San Quentin; Quiet Dawn is sung by a child. It is closer to Marvin Gaye's What's Going On than to Fire Music, but free-jazz horn cries break through.","In September 1971 prisoners at Attica Correctional Facility in New York rebelled; the state's assault to retake the prison left dozens dead, prisoners and hostages. Shepp, by then teaching as well as performing, recorded this response in early 1972 with a large ensemble including strings. Soul, funk and protest music were at their commercial height, and Impulse! was releasing ambitious, political records.","The spoken Invocation is delivered by William Kunstler, the radical lawyer who had tried to negotiate at Attica. Listen for how Shepp uses the strings: sometimes lush like a soul record, sometimes as a sombre chorale. The album tracks a single emotional arc, from rage through mourning to the quiet hope of the final child's song.","Initially seen by some jazz critics as a sell-out to soul, it has since become a favourite of DJs, rare-groove collectors and hip-hop producers, and is now regarded as one of the key protest albums of its time. Shepp revived it in live performances in the 2010s, finding it newly relevant.","Start with the title track and turn it up; notice the free horn solo breaking through the groove. Then listen straight through in order to feel the arc. Compare Shepp's Fire Music to hear how his anger changed form, and Sun Ra's Space Is the Place for another answer to the same America."],"eric-dolphy-out-to-lunch":["Five Dolphy compositions for a quintet with no piano: Freddie Hubbard's trumpet, Bobby Hutcherson's vibraphone, Richard Davis's bass and the teenage Tony Williams on drums. Dolphy plays bass clarinet, flute and alto, all with huge leaps between registers, like speech gone angular. The vibes' ringing chords replace the piano's, and the rhythm section breaks up time, bars stretching and contracting. Hat and Beard, a portrait of Thelonious Monk, lurches in nine; Gazzelloni is a tribute to an Italian flautist.","Recorded at Van Gelder's studio in February 1964. Dolphy had played with Mingus, Coltrane and Ornette Coleman, and also worked with Gunther Schuller's third-stream projects and performed contemporary classical flute music. He was about to leave for Europe with Mingus's band. He died in Berlin that June, aged 36, from undiagnosed diabetes, before the album came out.","Gazzelloni is named after Severino Gazzelloni, the flautist of the Darmstadt avant-garde for whom Berio wrote his Sequenza I, a clue to how closely Dolphy followed new classical music. Listen at the end of the title track for a hesitant, stuttering ensemble figure that deliberately fails to resolve. Tony Williams was eighteen and in Miles Davis's quintet; his ride cymbal floats rather than swings.","Posthumous release lent it the aura of a last testament. It is now considered one of the finest records in the Blue Note catalogue and a model of avant-garde jazz that is composed and structured rather than open-ended. Its blend of freedom and precision has been admired by later composers and improvisers from Anthony Braxton to Steve Coleman.","Start with Hat and Beard, and hear how the vibes and bass clarinet play off each other. Then the title track. Afterwards try Berio's Sequenza III, whose leaping vocal line has kinship with Dolphy's playing, and Monk's own recordings to hear the man in the portrait."],"eric-dolphy-at-the-five-spot":["Live recordings from a residency at New York's Five Spot by a quintet co-led by Dolphy and the young trumpeter Booker Little, with Mal Waldron on piano, Richard Davis on bass and Ed Blackwell on drums. Waldron's Fire Waltz opens with a dark, rolling three-beat groove; Little's Bee Vamp twists through odd accents; Dolphy's The Prophet is long and searching. Dolphy's alto and bass clarinet leap wildly while Little plays lines of unusual lyricism.","The quintet played the Five Spot for two weeks in July 1961, and Prestige recorded one night, issuing it on its New Jazz subsidiary in volumes. The club had become the centre of New York's avant-garde since Ornette Coleman's debut there in 1959. Booker Little, only 23, died of uraemia that October, making the recording a memorial almost immediately.","Listen to Little: he often bends toward dissonant intervals and deliberately sour notes, playing inside the harmony yet stretching it, a counterweight to Dolphy's leaps. Waldron's piano is the anchor, repeating left-hand patterns hypnotically. Notice Blackwell's drums, New Orleans parade rhythms folded into modern time, keeping the long pieces dancing.","Regarded as one of the great live jazz albums, a perfect balance between hard bop's drive and the avant-garde's freedoms. Some later listeners regard the Dolphy-Little quintet as a road not taken, cut short by Little's death; with Dolphy's own death three years later the record has doubled as elegy.","Put on Fire Waltz first and let Waldron's groove settle you in, then listen to how differently Dolphy and Little solo over it. Compare Ornette Coleman's Shape of Jazz to Come, which removed the piano; here the piano stays but the solos slip its grip."],"roscoe-mitchell-sound":["Three pieces by a Chicago sextet led by Roscoe Mitchell with Lester Bowie on trumpet and Malachi Favors on bass. The long title track is quiet and sparse: long silences, single notes, harmonica wheezes, bicycle horns and whistles, recorder squeaks, then sudden explosions of sound. Ornette is a warmer, swinging tribute; The Little Suite is a playful sequence of small textures. What you hear most is space, instruments speaking one at a time, with pauses as important as notes.","Recorded in August 1966, the first album by members of the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians, founded in Chicago in 1965 around Muhal Richard Abrams. Bob Koester's Delmark, a blues and traditional jazz label, took the risk. The AACM insisted on original music, self-organised concerts and education, a response both to the collapse of the club scene and to the civil rights movement.","Listen to how often a sound is followed by silence: Mitchell was thinking about sound as an event in space, closer to Cage or Feldman than to Coltrane's continuous intensity. The small instruments, bells, horns, whistles, which became an AACM trademark, are used for colour and humour. The piece called Ornette sounds nothing like free jazz noise; it is a gentle, affectionate homage.","Initially ignored beyond Chicago, it is now seen as the first document of the AACM aesthetic, a turning point that took free jazz away from fire and fury toward space, colour and structure. Critics like John Litweiler and scholars like George Lewis have established it as one of the key records of 1960s American experimental music.","Listen in a quiet room and do not turn the volume up to compensate for the silences; they are the music. Follow the small instruments as characters. Then compare Cage's 4′33″ and Feldman's Rothko Chapel, and the Art Ensemble of Chicago's People in Sorrow, which grew from this band."],"muhal-richard-abrams-levels-and-degrees-of-light":["Abrams's first album as a leader, three pieces featuring AACM members including Anthony Braxton and violinist Leroy Jenkins. The opening title piece begins with a wordless soprano over shimmering vibraphone, floating and slow, then gradually thickens. My Thoughts Are My Future – Now and Forever is a fierce ensemble workout. The Bird Song sets spoken poetry and bird-like sounds against free improvisation. The whole record mixes the delicate and the ferocious, with Abrams's own piano moving from gospel to cluster.","Abrams had led the Experimental Band in Chicago since 1961, a workshop for new compositions, and in 1965 co-founded the AACM, whose members studied theory, organised concerts and taught in the South Side community. Delmark released this in 1968. The album appeared at a time when AACM members were starting to be heard beyond Chicago; within a year several would move to Paris.","Listen to the use of voice: the soprano Penelope Taylor sings wordlessly, like an instrument, rather than singing a text, a colour almost unknown in jazz at the time. The Bird Song, with its imitations of birds and its poet, is closer to Messiaen's bird-inspired music than to anything in bebop, and shows how widely the AACM's idea of jazz ranged.","Rarely reviewed at the time and hard to find for years, it has since been championed as one of the foundational AACM statements and Abrams as the movement's intellectual centre. George Lewis's history of the AACM, A Power Stronger Than Itself, placed it at the heart of that story. Its mix of composition and improvisation now looks prophetic.","Start with the title track and let the voice and vibes set the mood. Then hear The Bird Song. Compare Oliveros's Sonic Meditations and Messiaen's bird music, and Roscoe Mitchell's Sound for the AACM's other founding sound."],"anthony-braxton-for-alto":["A double album of unaccompanied alto saxophone, eight pieces each dedicated to someone: the composer John Cage, the pianist Cecil Taylor and others, friends and fellow musicians among them. Each piece explores one idea: rapid-fire staccato, long tones, multiphonics that produce two notes at once, squeals, key clicks, fragments of melody. Some are gentle and lyrical, others relentlessly harsh. Without accompaniment every breath and click is exposed, and each piece has the character of a study.","Recorded in Chicago in 1968 and released by Delmark in 1969, when Braxton was 23 and a member of the AACM. An entire album of solo saxophone had essentially no precedent; Coleman Hawkins's short Picasso of 1948 was the famous earlier solo. Braxton, also a chess player and student of philosophy, was developing a system of diagrams and numbered compositions that would govern his whole career.","The dedication to Cage is not incidental: Braxton admired Cage, Stockhausen and Schoenberg as much as Coltrane, and approached solo improvisation as composition, with each piece built on a chosen language of sounds. Listen for how each track isolates one technique. His later titles would be diagrams rather than words, already implicit here.","Initially seen by many as an extreme experiment and by some jazz critics as not jazz at all, it has become a landmark; it established solo saxophone as a genre, followed by Steve Lacy, Evan Parker and others. Braxton's relationship to jazz, which he both belongs to and refuses, remains a lively debate.","Choose one piece, perhaps the Cecil Taylor dedication, and listen to it twice; it gets easier. Then hear the Cage dedication and think about how silence and sound work. Compare Berio's Sequenza III, a composed solo virtuoso piece, and Debussy's Syrinx for unaccompanied flute."],"art-ensemble-of-chicago-people-in-sorrow":["A single piece in two parts, about forty minutes, by Lester Bowie, Roscoe Mitchell, Joseph Jarman and Malachi Favors. It begins almost silently, with bells, gongs and soft flute, and grows slowly over many minutes, like a procession approaching, into a keening lament of horns and then an anguished climax, before fading back into quiet percussion. Every member plays many instruments, including gongs, whistles, sirens and bells, and the sound is lamenting and ceremonial rather than improvised in the bebop sense.","In 1969 the group moved to France, where free jazz had found a keener audience than in the United States, and recorded this piece that summer. The drummer Don Moye had not yet joined, so the four horn players shared the percussion. The group's motto, Great Black Music, Ancient to the Future, captured their aim to embrace the whole African diaspora. The album was reissued by Nessa in the US.","Listen to the first ten minutes very quietly: the members are playing small percussion and silence, and a slow, sustained lament emerges, much closer to ritual than jazz. The group wore face paint and costumes onstage, and the title's sorrow has often been heard as mourning for Black America after the assassinations of 1968. Notice Favors's bowed bass, a steady voice under the chaos.","Widely acclaimed in Europe, it became the group's most admired early work and a key example of AACM compositional thinking. Critics praised its patience and structure, qualities rarely credited to free jazz at the time. For many listeners it remains the group's finest record, and among the quietest, subtlest documents of late-1960s free jazz; some find its slow opening a test of patience.","Listen in one sitting, in quiet surroundings, and wait: the arc takes time. Notice the moment the horns first combine. Compare Feldman's Rothko Chapel, another slow ritual of mourning, and Penderecki's Threnody for the opposite, instant anguish."],"art-ensemble-of-chicago-les-stances-a-sophie":["The soundtrack to a French film, and the Art Ensemble's most accessible record. Thème de Yoyo, the opening, is a thumping funk vamp sung by Fontella Bass, the soul singer of Rescue Me, with lyrics about a woman's body; the horns then erupt into free jazz. The rest of the album moves between atmospheric film cues, a lovely ballad-like Thème de Céline, and freer passages, with drums from Don Moye, now a full member.","Made in Paris in 1970 for Moshé Mizrahi's film Les Stances à Sophie, a feminist-inflected drama from a novel by Christiane Rochefort. The group was living in France, playing regularly and recording for several labels. Bass, then married to Lester Bowie, was singing with them. The soundtrack was issued by Pathé and was hard to find for decades.","Listen to how the groove of Thème de Yoyo is interrupted by free-jazz explosions, then comes back: the collision of dance music and the avant-garde would become a model for later groups. The saxophone solos on the funk vamp are wild, but Malachi Favors's bassline never budges. The film itself is rarely seen now; the soundtrack has outlived it.","Unknown to most jazz fans for years, it was rediscovered by rare-groove DJs and hip-hop crate-diggers; Thème de Yoyo is now one of the group's best-known pieces. Critics count it as a proof that the AACM's freedom could embrace funk and soul, not only abstraction.","Put on Thème de Yoyo loud and move. Then hear the quieter cues. Compare Ornette Coleman's Dancing in Your Head, which made funk and freedom meet from the other side, and Milhaud's Création du monde, a much earlier fusion of jazz and the concert hall."],"alice-coltrane-journey-in-satchidananda":["Five pieces built on drones and repeated bass figures. In the title track, a tanpura hums throughout, Cecil McBee repeats a short, hypnotic bass line, and Alice Coltrane's harp pours over them in rippling glissandi while Pharoah Sanders's soprano saxophone sings long, ornamented lines, and Rashied Ali keeps a gentle pulse. Shiva-Loka and Stopover Bombay continue the mood; Isis and Osiris, recorded live, adds oud and Charlie Haden's bass. The harp is the sound people remember.","Alice Coltrane, pianist and harpist, had been in John Coltrane's last band and was grieving his 1967 death. She became a devotee of Swami Satchidananda, the Indian guru who opened the Woodstock festival, and travelled to India in 1970. This album, recorded that year and released in 1971, made her spiritual practice her music. Impulse! was by then the home of what came to be called spiritual jazz.","The tanpura is played by a musician credited only as Tulsi, and it tunes the whole album: its drone is the tonal centre that every other instrument leans on, just as in Hindustani classical music. Listen to how Alice's harp does not play chords like a pianist but sweeps, a technique partly her own. The title word joins three Sanskrit terms for being, consciousness and bliss.","Received quietly and sometimes condescendingly as the work of Coltrane's widow, it has been thoroughly reappraised, especially since the 2000s, as a founding album of spiritual jazz and a touchstone for ambient, electronic and new-jazz musicians. Her role as composer and bandleader is now fully recognised.","Start with the title track and let the bass figure and tanpura settle in before listening for the harp. Then hear Ravi Shankar's Raga Mala and La Monte Young's Well-Tuned Piano, other drone-based musics that made their way into the West. For the Coltrane lineage, hear Meditations."],"alice-coltrane-universal-consciousness":["Alice Coltrane on harp and Wurlitzer organ with a small string section, bassist Jimmy Garrison and drummers Jack DeJohnette, Rashied Ali and Clifford Jarvis. The organ is the surprise: swirling, bending, almost psychedelic, played with pitch-bending effects. The strings play slow, glowing lines. The six pieces include Battle at Armageddon, a fierce organ and drums duet, the devotional Hare Krishna and Sita Ram, and the title track, where harp, strings and organ build to a kind of cosmic surge.","Recorded in 1971, this marked Alice Coltrane's growing ambition as composer and arranger. She wrote for strings with help from Ornette Coleman, who transcribed her ideas; she had begun to compose by playing on the organ and singing lines. She was deepening her Hindu practice, and would later found a Vedantic centre and ashram in California.","Listen to how the organ pitches slide: she used the instrument in ways drawn from Indian vocal ornament, bending notes rather than hitting them cleanly. The strings are written with Coleman's help, which explains their slightly angular, unexpected voicings. Battle at Armageddon, a savage duet with Ali, shows her as a forceful improviser, not only a devotional one.","Barely noticed at release and long out of print, it has become one of her most admired albums, often cited by later musicians and critics as her finest. It is now seen as a bold synthesis of jazz, Indian devotion and Western art-music orchestration. Some listeners still find the organ's wild pitch-bending hard going; others hear it as the record's boldest idea.","Start with the title track and let the organ swirl. Then hear Battle at Armageddon for the contrast. Compare Messiaen's organ music, especially La Nativité du Seigneur, and Scriabin's Prometheus, both mystic visions in colour and harmony."],"pharoah-sanders-karma":["Almost all of it is The Creator Has a Master Plan, a 32-minute piece that starts with a peaceful, rising theme over a two-chord bass figure, then Leon Thomas sings a simple hymn of love and faith, and his yodelling vocal cries become part of the texture. In the middle Sanders's tenor erupts into a shrieking, overblown storm before the calm returns. French horn, flute and piano add colour. A short second piece, Colors, is gentler.","Sanders had played with John Coltrane from 1965 until Coltrane's death. This, his third album as leader, recorded in February 1969, built on Coltrane's A Love Supreme but made it more direct, more like a gospel or pop song stretched out. Leon Thomas, a singer in Count Basie's orbit, had developed a yodelling technique he linked to African Pygmy song.","Listen to the bass line: it keeps a short, repeating figure for most of the piece, a debt to Coltrane's A Love Supreme and to Indian drones. Thomas's yodel is the most surprising sound on the record; he said it came from hearing Central African music. Notice that the saxophone storm and the peaceful theme are the same piece; the peace frames the chaos.","It became a surprising success, played on FM radio and embraced by the counterculture. It is now regarded as one of the founding records of spiritual jazz and has been sampled and covered widely. Critics once debated whether its accessibility was a betrayal of free jazz; the consensus now celebrates it.","Listen straight through, letting the theme lodge before the storm hits; notice how relieved you feel when it returns. Compare Coltrane's Meditations and Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda. For a classical cousin, hear Terry Riley's In C."],"brotzmann-machine-gun":["An octet with Brötzmann, Evan Parker and Willem Breuker on saxophones, Fred Van Hove on piano, two bassists and two drummers, Han Bennink and Sven-Åke Johansson, blasting at full volume. The opening title track hits like artillery: repeated saxophone attacks, pounding drums, screaming, collectively improvised. Then the din breaks into sections where smaller groups play, with brief written riffs that pull everyone together again. It is loud, dense and relentless, with a surprisingly clear structure.","Recorded in Bremen in May 1968 and released on Brötzmann's own label, later reissued by FMP, the Berlin label he helped found. The title refers to the Vietnam War, and to Don Cherry calling Brötzmann's saxophone a machine gun. The record came out of the student protests and a European generation's search for a jazz that owed nothing to American models or to German post-war conformity.","Listen for the composed parts: short riffs and signals that bring the band together after the chaos, showing that the fury has a plan. The doubled basses and drums echo the double quartet of Ornette's Free Jazz. Notice Van Hove's piano, often inaudible in the roar, but pounding clusters in the quieter sections.","It became the founding document of European free improvisation and is often cited by rock and noise musicians as an influence. Many listeners still find it too much; its supporters consider it one of the most important records of 1960s Europe. It has been reissued repeatedly, often with alternate takes.","Listen at volume in one sitting, and use the composed riffs as landmarks: each time the band snaps together on one, a new section begins. Notice Bennink's drumming, theatrical and funny even at full tilt. Then compare Coltrane's Ascension, its direct ancestor, and Penderecki's Threnody, a notated work that also turned mass sound into protest."],"don-cherry-complete-communion":["Two side-long suites, Complete Communion and Elephantasy, each chaining four themes together, by a quartet of Cherry's cornet, the Argentine Gato Barbieri on tenor, Henry Grimes on bass and Ed Blackwell on drums. Themes appear, are improvised on, and give way to the next, so each side flows like one long song. Cherry's cornet is light and playful; Barbieri's tenor is hoarse and passionate; Blackwell's drums are melodic and dancing, rooted in New Orleans.","Recorded on Christmas Eve 1965 at Van Gelder's studio, Cherry's first album for Blue Note, after years as Ornette Coleman's partner and time in Europe with Albert Ayler and his own groups. Cherry would soon settle in Sweden and move toward what he called organic music, drawing on Indian, African and Turkish music, a pioneer of what would later be called world music.","Listen to how the themes are linked: the suite form, borrowed perhaps from Ellington as much as from Ornette, lets improvisations flow into new tunes without stopping. Blackwell's drumming draws on New Orleans street beats. Cherry's cornet is small and bright, with half-valved smears, a sound he used to shape melodies almost like a singer.","Received well at the time, it is now regarded as one of the most joyful and structured of 1960s free jazz records, an example of free jazz that sings. It is often seen as a bridge between Ornette's quartet and Cherry's later world-music explorations. Barbieri, soon famous for the Last Tango in Paris score, is heard here at his rawest.","Listen to one side straight through and notice when a new theme begins. Compare Ornette's Shape of Jazz to Come, where Cherry began, and Ellington's suites. Then hear Cherry's later records, with their Indian and African elements."],"miles-davis-filles-de-kilimanjaro":["Five long pieces with French titles, played by Miles Davis's second great quintet at the moment it began to plug in. Herbie Hancock and later Chick Corea play Fender Rhodes electric piano, Ron Carter and then Dave Holland play bass, Tony Williams drums, Wayne Shorter tenor. The grooves are slower and straighter than bebop, closer to soul records, and the tunes are built from short riffs repeated until they turn into landscapes. The sound you remember is the soft, bell-like buzz of the electric piano under the trumpet.","Recorded in two sessions in June and September 1968, the album is a hinge: the acoustic quintet that had made Miles Smiles and Nefertiti was breaking up, and Davis was listening hard to James Brown, Sly Stone and Jimi Hendrix. The second session brought in Corea and Holland, the players who would carry him into In a Silent Way. Columbia was pressing him to reach the young audience that was buying rock albums, and Davis wanted that audience too.","The woman on the cover is Betty Mabry, the funk singer who married Davis in 1968 and introduced him to Hendrix and Sly Stone; \"Mademoiselle Mabry\" is named for her, and its opening borrows the chord shape of Hendrix's \"The Wind Cries Mary\", slowed almost to stillness. Listen for the change of personnel between tracks: the bass on the Holland pieces is rounder and more insistent, a first hint of the vamping bass lines to come.","Often overlooked, squeezed between the celebrated acoustic quintet records and the electric revolution of 1969, the album was received politely rather than excitedly. Later listeners have promoted it as the true transition record, the place where the fusion idea is audible before it was a style. Arguments continue over whether it is the last quintet album or the first electric one; the honest answer is both.","Start with \"Mademoiselle Mabry\", sixteen unhurried minutes, and let the rhythm section's patience set the pace. Then play \"Frelon Brun\" for the busier, rock-tinged drumming of Tony Williams. Follow with Nefertiti from the year before to hear what changed, and In a Silent Way from the year after to hear where it went."],"miles-davis-in-a-silent-way":["Two sides, each around twenty minutes. Side one, \"Shhh/Peaceful\", rides a light, ticking hi-hat groove under a cloud of three electric keyboards, with John McLaughlin's guitar picking out single notes. Side two frames the funkier \"It's About That Time\" with Joe Zawinul's hymn-like \"In a Silent Way\", heard at the start and again at the end. Nothing hurries; solos drift in and out of a haze. The sound is of a jazz band holding its breath.","Recorded in a single day in February 1969 in New York, with Hancock, Corea and Zawinul on keyboards and the English guitarist John McLaughlin, who had arrived in America days before. Davis had heard rock musicians building whole pieces from one chord, and he stripped Zawinul's tune of its original changes, leaving a single sustained tonality. Producer Teo Macero then shaped hours of tape into two sides with a razor blade.","The symmetry of side two is an editing trick: Macero copied the \"In a Silent Way\" section and spliced it onto both ends, so the same performance is heard twice. Side one also repeats a large block of music. The album is in part a composition made in the edit suite, like musique concrète, and it quietly pioneered the studio-built jazz record that Bitches Brew would make explicit.","Jazz critics were split: some heard a lazy abandonment of swing and harmony, others a new kind of beauty. Rock critics loved it at once. Its reputation has only grown, and it is now often preferred to the louder Bitches Brew as the most perfectly made of the electric records. The argument over Macero's edits, whether they are sacrilege or authorship, has largely settled in favour of authorship.","Listen in the dark, at moderate volume, from start to finish. Notice how the drums never get louder than a whisper for the first side, and wait for Davis's trumpet to enter the title theme, plain and almost vibrato-free. Then compare Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air from the same year: another American music made of sustained drones and patient repetition."],"miles-davis-bitches-brew":["A double album of six long pieces for a band twice the normal size: two or three drummers, electric and acoustic bass together, three electric pianos, guitar, soprano saxophone and Bennie Maupin's dark bass clarinet. Grooves churn for twenty minutes at a time, dense and murky, while Davis's trumpet, often drenched in echo, cuts across the top. The title track opens with a stark, spaced call and response before the band boils over. It sounds like jazz heard through the walls of a rock club.","Recorded over three days in August 1969 in New York, the week after Woodstock. Davis gave the musicians sketches, a bass line, a mood, and let the tapes roll; producer Teo Macero then assembled the album from the takes. Columbia marketed it to the rock audience, Davis played the Fillmore halls opening for rock bands, and the record became his first to sell half a million copies, with Mati Klarwein's psychedelic cover painting in every student bedroom.","\"Pharaoh's Dance\" is stitched together from many short fragments, some loops repeated verbatim; the famous stops and restarts are edits, not band cues. Macero also added tape echo to the trumpet after the fact. Listen to the stereo picture: the two drummers and two keyboard players are often split left and right, so the album works almost like two bands arguing in real time.","It split the jazz world. Many older critics and musicians called it a sell-out or a noise; younger listeners heard the future. Its commercial success made fusion a genre and launched the bands of nearly everyone on the sessions: Weather Report, Return to Forever, Mahavishnu Orchestra. Debate now centres less on legitimacy than on credit, since so much of the form belongs to Macero's editing.","Start with \"Spanish Key\" or \"Miles Runs the Voodoo Down\", the most tightly grooved pieces, before tackling the half-hour title track and \"Pharaoh's Dance\". Pick one player and follow them all the way through, the bass clarinet is a good guide. Hear it loud. Then go back to In a Silent Way to hear how much quieter the same idea began."],"miles-davis-a-tribute-to-jack-johnson":["Two sides, \"Right Off\" and \"Yesternow\", each around twenty-five minutes. The first is Davis's most openly rock record: John McLaughlin's guitar chops out a relentless shuffle, Michael Henderson's bass locks with Billy Cobham's drums, and Davis plays one of the longest, most ferocious trumpet solos of his life over the top. The second side slows into a brooding, spacious bass figure and ends with an actor's voice speaking as the boxer.","Made as the soundtrack for Bill Cayton's documentary about Jack Johnson, the first Black heavyweight champion, whose defiance of white America in the 1910s Davis saw as his own. Davis was a keen amateur boxer. The music was drawn chiefly from a session in April 1970 and edited by Teo Macero, with material from other dates folded in. It was the first time Davis worked with the funk bassist Michael Henderson, who had played for Stevie Wonder.","\"Right Off\" reportedly began while Davis was in the control room: McLaughlin started noodling a shuffle in E, the others joined, and Davis ran out and began playing. Herbie Hancock, who happened to drop by, plays a cheap Farfisa organ. The bass figure under much of \"Yesternow\" is taken from James Brown's \"Say It Loud, I'm Black and I'm Proud\". The closing speech is voiced by Brock Peters.","Barely promoted on release, it sold poorly and was overshadowed by Bitches Brew. Its reputation rose steadily among guitarists, who treat McLaughlin's playing here as a founding document of jazz-rock, and among rock critics, who often rank it Davis's best electric album. The 2003 box set of the complete sessions showed how much editing lay behind it and sharpened the debate about what the finished work really is.","Play \"Right Off\" loud and wait: Davis's entry about two minutes in, after a long guitar groove, is one of the great moments of electric jazz. Notice how the band stops and restarts on a signal. Then listen to James Brown's \"Say It Loud\" and hear the bass line come back as a meditation on \"Yesternow\"."],"miles-davis-on-the-corner":["Four sides of dense, repetitive funk: a single bass riff hammered for minutes, several drummers and percussionists interlocking, tabla and electric sitar buzzing inside the rhythm, wah-wah guitar and keyboards stabbing in and out. Davis plays trumpet through a wah-wah pedal, in short bursts, as one voice in the mix rather than the soloist on top. There are almost no tunes; the groove is the composition. The feeling is of a crowded street heard from inside.","Recorded in June and July 1972 in New York, and designed, Davis said, to reach young Black listeners who had abandoned jazz for James Brown and Sly Stone. The English arranger Paul Buckmaster had introduced him to the music of Karlheinz Stockhausen, and the album grew from the idea of layered, looping parts. Columbia released it with cartoon street characters by Corky McCoy on the cover and, at first, no list of the musicians at all.","The Indian instruments are not decoration: Badal Roy's tabla often carries the pulse while the drum kit plays around it, a borrowing of how Hindustani music layers rhythm cycles. Listen to how the tape was edited into repeating blocks, much as Stockhausen and later hip-hop producers built pieces from loops. The absent credits were deliberate: Davis wanted the band heard as one anonymous machine.","It was savaged on release, called monotonous and a cynical grab for a pop audience, and it sold badly. Few jazz records have been rehabilitated so completely: post-punk bands, drum and bass producers and hip-hop beatmakers adopted it, and it is now heard as a prophecy of loop-based music. Some jazz critics still regard it as a dead end; many more now call it Davis's boldest record.","Start with the opening medley and give it ten minutes; the point arrives only once the repetition stops sounding repetitive. Follow the tabla, then the bass, then Davis's wah-wah trumpet as just another colour. Compare it with Steve Reich's Drumming from the year before, another American work of interlocking patterns, and with Stockhausen's Mantra."],"miles-davis-agharta":["A double album recorded at an afternoon concert: four sides of continuous, roaring electric music with no gaps between numbers. Pete Cosey's guitar, fed through fuzz, wah and synthesisers, howls and squeals; Reggie Lucas plays tight rhythm guitar; Michael Henderson and Al Foster keep a heavy funk pulse; James Mtume's percussion never stops. Sonny Fortune adds sax and flute. Davis plays wah-wah trumpet and stabs at an organ, directing the band with hand signals. The sound is closer to Hendrix than to bebop.","Recorded on 1 February 1975 at Festival Hall in Osaka during a Japanese tour; the evening concert that day became the companion album Pangaea. Davis was in poor health, in pain from a hip condition and ulcers, and playing through it. Within months he stopped performing altogether and did not record again until 1980. The album was released first in Japan, where his electric music had a loyal audience.","Listen for how the music changes gear: Davis signals shifts with a raised hand or an organ chord, and the whole band swerves at once, a live version of the edits Teo Macero once made with tape. Cosey's guitar often sounds like electronic music, a whole wall of noise rather than notes; he used a bank of effects pedals and an EMS synthesiser. The title names a mythical underground city.","American critics largely dismissed it as chaotic noise from a declining star. Its standing changed in the 1990s, when noise-rock and experimental guitarists championed Cosey and when the loops of electronic dance music made its repetition sound modern. Many now rank Agharta and Pangaea as the summit of Davis's electric bands; others hear the sound of a musician burning out.","Put on the first side, \"Prelude\", and let the first twenty minutes settle the scale of the thing. Track Cosey's guitar solo as it moves from blues to pure texture. Then compare the dense layers of On the Corner, built in the studio, with the same idea here done live, without a net."],"tony-williams-emergency":["A double album by a trio of drums, guitar and Hammond organ, played at rock volume with jazz speed. Tony Williams drives everything with a flood of cymbals and tom-toms, John McLaughlin plays fast, distorted guitar lines, and Larry Young's organ supplies both bass and swirling chords. Williams sings, tunelessly and bravely, on a few tracks. The pieces range from blistering jams to the eerie, slow \"Vashkar\" by Carla Bley. The recording itself is raw, overloaded and loud.","Recorded in New York in May 1969, three months before Bitches Brew. Williams, still in his early twenties, had spent six years as Miles Davis's drummer and wanted to play the kind of music he heard from Cream and Jimi Hendrix. He left Davis to form Lifetime, taking McLaughlin, who had just arrived from London. Polydor, a label associated with rock, released it as a double LP.","The distortion is not all intentional: the recording was badly engineered, and the band disliked the sound. Yet that grit became part of the record's legend and its influence on later noise and rock musicians. Listen past it to Young's organ bass lines, played with his feet and left hand, which let the trio sound like a full band.","It sold poorly and confused both jazz and rock audiences. Among musicians it had an outsized influence: many regard it, rather than Bitches Brew, as the first true jazz-rock record. McLaughlin's playing here led directly to the Mahavishnu Orchestra. Critics now place it among the essential fusion documents, while still wishing for a better-sounding master.","Begin with the title track and simply hold on. Then focus on Williams's cymbal work: he plays the ride cymbal with the precision of a jazz drummer while hitting the drums like a rock drummer. Compare with Cream's live recordings from 1968 and with Mahavishnu's The Inner Mounting Flame two years later."],"weather-report-mysterious-traveller":["Seven pieces that move from the long, hypnotic \"Nubian Sundance\", with wordless voices and a crowd audible behind the band, to the springy funk of \"Cucumber Slumber\" and the tender duet \"Blackthorn Rose\" for Wayne Shorter's soprano and Joe Zawinul's piano. Zawinul's synthesisers act like an orchestra, painting backdrops and sound effects. The group had become a composed band rather than a jam band: parts are arranged, solos short, and the whole record sounds like a series of short films.","Weather Report was founded in 1970 by Zawinul and Shorter, both veterans of Miles Davis's early electric bands, with a manifesto that everyone solos and no one solos. By 1974 Zawinul was steering the band toward groove and orchestration. New bassist Alphonso Johnson brought a funkier feel. Zawinul had embraced the ARP synthesiser, then a new, expensive instrument, and was using it as a composing tool rather than a novelty.","\"Nubian Sundance\" sounds as if it were recorded live, but the audience noise was reportedly overdubbed onto a studio performance, a trick to capture the energy of the band's concerts. The title track begins with a rushing electronic sound and a single low gong-like note. Listen for how often Shorter plays only a few notes, then disappears: his restraint is the band's secret colour.","Well received at the time and a strong seller for a jazz album, it has lived in the shadow of Heavy Weather. Critics who prefer the band's early, freer records sometimes see it as the moment they became too polished; others hear the best balance of adventure and groove. The turn toward tight, composed grooves set the course for the band's later hits.","Start with \"Cucumber Slumber\" for the groove, then go back to the opening track for the wider picture. Pay attention to Zawinul's synthesiser as orchestration, colours that function like horns or strings. Compare with In a Silent Way, where both Zawinul and Shorter played five years earlier."],"weather-report-heavy-weather":["Eight tracks, opening with \"Birdland\", Joe Zawinul's joyful tribute to the New York jazz club, which builds from a low synthesiser bass line and Jaco Pastorius's bass harmonics to a hummable big-band shout. \"A Remark You Made\" is a slow, aching ballad for Wayne Shorter's tenor; Pastorius's \"Teen Town\" puts his fretless bass up front, playing the melody at speed. The sound is bright and clear, synthesisers and saxophone blending into a single colour.","By 1977 the band had been transformed by Pastorius, a young Florida bassist who told Zawinul he was the greatest bass player in the world and proceeded to prove it. With Alex Acuña on drums and Manolo Badrena on percussion, the band recorded in Los Angeles. Synthesiser technology had jumped forward, and Zawinul used new polyphonic instruments that allowed chords, not just single lines.","In \"Birdland\" Pastorius plays the high melody in bass harmonics, touching the strings lightly so they ring like a bell; many listeners assume it is a keyboard. Pastorius also plays the drums on \"Teen Town\". \"Birdland\" is built like a big-band chart without a big band: several separate tunes stacked up, each one returning, the last a shout chorus worthy of Count Basie.","It became Weather Report's best-selling album and one of the best-selling jazz records of the decade, and \"Birdland\" was quickly covered by Maynard Ferguson and, with words, by the Manhattan Transfer. Some critics grumbled that the band had gone pop, and that Shorter was sidelined. Most now hear the album as fusion at its most melodic and exact, and the Zawinul–Pastorius partnership at its height.","Start with \"Birdland\" and count how many separate themes it has before the final shout; then listen to \"A Remark You Made\" for the tenderness beneath the bravado. Notice the fretless bass, which slides like a voice. Follow with the album Jaco Pastorius from the year before and the Manhattan Transfer's vocal \"Birdland\"."],"hancock-maiden-voyage":["A five-track suite about the sea. The title track floats on a repeated rhythmic figure and hovering chords that never quite settle, while Freddie Hubbard's trumpet and George Coleman's tenor state a calm, open melody. \"The Eye of the Hurricane\" is fast and churning, \"Little One\" a slow meditation, \"Survival of the Fittest\" nearly free, and \"Dolphin Dance\" a gentle, harmonically winding close. Hancock's piano is light-fingered and full of space.","Recorded in March 1965 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in New Jersey for Blue Note. Hancock, just twenty-four, was the pianist in Miles Davis's quintet, and he brought along Davis's bassist Ron Carter and drummer Tony Williams. Jazz musicians were then exploring modes, scales used in place of chord sequences, after Kind of Blue; Hancock took that idea and made it into a coloured, programmatic album.","The title tune's chords are suspended, the kind that ask a question and leave it unanswered, which is why the music seems to bob on water rather than move forward. Hancock reportedly first wrote the vamp for a television commercial. Listen to Tony Williams on \"Survival of the Fittest\": the drummer shifts from time-keeping to free commentary, the tune dissolving into sound effects of a storm.","Received well at the time, it has become one of the most loved Blue Note albums, and both \"Maiden Voyage\" and \"Dolphin Dance\" are core standards taught in every jazz school. Critics sometimes debate whether it is truly a concept album or a well-sequenced blowing date. Few doubt that it defined the harmonically sophisticated post-bop sound of the mid-1960s.","Begin with the title track and hear the pattern of the bass and piano rocking under the melody. Then \"Dolphin Dance\" for Hancock's way of changing key every few bars without ever sounding lost. Compare with Debussy's La Mer, another portrait of the sea made from colour rather than tune."],"hancock-head-hunters":["Four long tracks of tight, playful funk. \"Chameleon\" opens with a synthesiser bass line, gurgling and fat, that anyone who hears it remembers; the band locks into a groove for fifteen minutes and then changes into a different piece halfway through. \"Watermelon Man\" reinvents Hancock's early hit with bottles, whistles and a slow, loping beat. \"Sly\" salutes Sly Stone; \"Vein Melter\" is dreamy and slow. Clavinet, Rhodes piano and synthesisers replace horns as the main colour.","Recorded in San Francisco in 1973. Hancock had spent several years leading the Mwandishi band, whose spacey experimental music had lost money and audiences. Newly committed to Nichiren Buddhism, he decided to make music that would move bodies and reach people, and he formed a new band of funk-minded players. The newly affordable synthesisers made by ARP gave him the bass sound he wanted.","The opening of \"Watermelon Man\" is percussionist Bill Summers blowing across the top of a beer bottle, imitating the hindewhu, a whistle-and-voice technique of the Ba-Benzélé people of central Africa that Hancock had heard on a field recording. The \"Chameleon\" bass line is played by Hancock himself on a synthesiser, not by the bassist. The album's funk became a source of samples for decades of hip-hop.","It became the best-selling jazz album of its time, crossing into pop and R&B charts. Jazz purists attacked it as a commercial betrayal; funk and soul audiences loved it. The debate softened as its influence on hip-hop, electronic dance music and jazz musicians of every generation became obvious. It is now usually named alongside Bitches Brew as a foundation of fusion.","Start with \"Chameleon\", ride the groove, and notice the moment around the middle when it turns into a new, slower piece with a Rhodes solo. Then listen to the original 1962 \"Watermelon Man\" and hear the tune taken apart. Compare it with James Brown's early seventies records for the rhythm section's discipline."],"hancock-rockit":["A single built from machines: a hard, clapping drum-machine beat, a stuttering synthesiser riff, and the scratching of a vinyl record by the DJ Grand Mixer D.ST, pushed back and forth under the needle so that it becomes a percussion instrument. Hancock's keyboards add hooks and chords. There is no singing and almost no melody in the ordinary sense; the track works by texture, repetition and surprise. The scratch is the thing people remember.","Hancock made Rockit with the New York producers Bill Laswell and Michael Beinhorn of the band Material, who were connected to the downtown hip-hop and art scenes. Hancock, a lifelong gadget enthusiast, was fascinated by early computers and samplers. The song's video by Godley and Creme, full of jerking robot sculptures, ran constantly on the new MTV and won several of its first video awards.","For many listeners outside New York this was the first time they heard scratching, and Grand Mixer D.ST's contribution was placed front and centre, credited on the record: a DJ treated as a featured soloist on a major-label jazz musician's single. Listen to how the scratches answer the synthesiser like a horn in a call-and-response. It also won the Grammy for best R&B instrumental performance.","A hit worldwide, it baffled jazz critics and delighted breakdancers. Hip-hop historians now credit it with spreading turntablism internationally, and generations of DJs cite it as their first inspiration. Some have argued about whether Hancock or Laswell and D.ST deserve the credit for its sound; most agree it was a genuine collaboration and a bridge between jazz, electro and hip-hop.","Listen for the first scratch, and then count how many different sounds D.ST gets from one record. Compare the beat with Afrika Bambaataa's \"Planet Rock\" from the year before, and go back to Head Hunters to hear the same musician's funk ten years earlier. The original extended mix is worth finding."],"corea-now-he-sings-now-he-sobs":["A piano trio record of five pieces, played with crisp attack and sudden changes of direction. Chick Corea's piano is percussive and clear, full of fast runs and chords struck like a drum; Miroslav Vitouš's bass is agile and melodic; Roy Haynes's drums crackle and comment. \"Matrix\" is a bright, angular blues; \"Steps – What Was\" moves from free improvisation into a Spanish-tinged theme; the title track is lyrical and searching. The trio sounds like three equal voices in conversation.","Recorded in New York in March 1968, before Corea joined Miles Davis's band. Corea had played Latin music with Mongo Santamaría and Willie Bobo, and the influence of Bill Evans and McCoy Tyner was in the air, but here he found his own crisp, bright style. The piano trio was being rethought after Bill Evans made the bass and drums equal partners.","The titles come from Corea's reading of a translation of the I Ching, the ancient Chinese book of divination. Listen to \"Steps – What Was\": the Spanish theme near the end prefigures \"Spain\", one of his most famous compositions four years later. Roy Haynes, then in his forties, had played with Charlie Parker; his snare comments sound as fresh as the young players around him.","Not a big seller at first, it became one of the most influential piano trio albums of the late 1960s; pianists study \"Matrix\" and the trio's interplay as a model. Reissues added extra tracks from the sessions, sparking mild debate over the original sequence. Corea himself returned to the trio with Vitouš and Haynes in later decades.","Start with \"Matrix\" and listen to Haynes's snare drum talking back to the piano. Then \"Now He Sings, Now He Sobs\" for a gentler side. Compare with Bartók's Mikrokosmos, whose crisp, percussive piano writing Corea admired, and with Bill Evans's trio records for another model of equal partnership."],"corea-return-to-forever":["Four pieces bathed in light. The title track opens with Flora Purim's wordless voice floating over Chick Corea's Fender Rhodes, then breaks into a Latin groove; \"Crystal Silence\" is a still, bell-like ballad; \"What Game Shall We Play Today\" is a light Brazilian song; \"Sometime Ago – La Fiesta\" moves from a sung reverie into a blazing Spanish-flavoured workout. Joe Farrell's flute weaves through, Stanley Clarke's bass sings, and Airto Moreira's percussion keeps everything shimmering.","Recorded in New York in February 1972 for Manfred Eicher's young German label ECM, and released first in Europe. Corea had just left both Miles Davis and the free-jazz group Circle, and wanted music that communicated directly. The Brazilian musicians Airto and Flora Purim brought samba and bossa nova into the band, part of a wave of Brazilian musicians then working in American jazz.","The band had no name when this was recorded; the album title became the band's name, and Return to Forever went on to become one of the loudest fusion bands of the 1970s, a long way from this gentle beginning. Listen to \"La Fiesta\": its flamenco-style chord sequence and hand-clapped rhythm show Corea's Spanish ancestry and his ear for Spanish classical music.","Warmly received, it became a touchstone for a lyrical, Latin-inflected fusion. \"Crystal Silence\" and \"La Fiesta\" became standards, and the former a favourite duet for Corea with vibraphonist Gary Burton. Critics who prefer the band's acoustic origins sometimes rank this above its later, heavier albums; others find it slight. Its clarity and warmth still win listeners.","Begin with \"La Fiesta\", the most exciting track, then go back to the title track and let the flute and voice carry you. Notice how the Rhodes piano chimes rather than buzzes. Compare with Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5, another fusion of Brazilian and European music for a floating soprano voice."],"mahavishnu-orchestra-the-inner-mounting-flame":["Eight tracks of fiercely virtuosic, high-volume music in unusual rhythms. John McLaughlin's electric guitar and Jerry Goodman's electric violin trade phrases at blinding speed; Jan Hammer's keyboards add distortion and colour; Billy Cobham's drums are thunderous and exact. \"Meeting of the Spirits\" opens with a massive, ringing chord pattern; \"A Lotus on Irish Streams\" is a delicate acoustic trio; \"The Dance of Maya\" sets a slow blues shuffle against an odd-metre riff.","McLaughlin, an English guitarist who had played on In a Silent Way and Bitches Brew, formed the band in New York in 1971. He was a disciple of the spiritual teacher Sri Chinmoy, who gave him the name Mahavishnu; the music reflects his study of Indian rhythm and his devotion. Rock audiences were ready for long instrumental virtuosity after Cream and Hendrix, and the band became a concert draw.","\"The Dance of Maya\" runs a riff in ten beats and then, without warning, turns into a plain twelve-bar blues shuffle, so the two rhythms seem to argue: illusion, maya, against the ordinary world. Many rhythms borrow from Indian music's long cycles. \"You Know You Know\", a quiet, spacious track, was later sampled by Massive Attack on their first album.","An immediate success with rock audiences and musicians, it established fusion as music for virtuosos. Jazz critics sometimes found it overheated and short on swing. Its influence on progressive rock and later metal guitarists is enormous; many prog bands cite the band as a model. Opinion now splits between those who hear exhilaration and those who hear excess.","Start with \"Meeting of the Spirits\" and feel the weight of the first chord pattern. Then listen to \"The Dance of Maya\" and count the rhythm until the shuffle arrives. Compare with the machine-like energy of Stravinsky's Rite of Spring, another music of odd, driving metres, and with McLaughlin's acoustic group Shakti."],"mahavishnu-orchestra-birds-of-fire":["Ten tracks, shorter and tighter than the first album. The title track opens with a gong and builds a hard, odd-metre riff on guitar and violin that climbs and climbs. \"Miles Beyond\" is a funky tribute to Miles Davis; \"Thousand Island Park\" is a gentle acoustic piece; \"One Word\" ends with a long drum feature for Billy Cobham. Jan Hammer's newly acquired Moog synthesiser adds sounds that rival the guitar. The band sounds like a single engine.","Recorded in 1972 at the height of the band's fame. It became one of the biggest-selling jazz-rock albums of its era and reached the upper reaches of the American pop album chart, unusual for instrumental music. The band toured relentlessly with rock acts, and internal tensions over credit and composing were already building; the original lineup split later in 1973.","Listen to how the title track's riff is built: guitar and violin play in unison, then split into close harmony, then compete, all in a metre that refuses to settle into four. Hammer's Moog often imitates the guitar's distortion, so it is hard to tell which instrument is playing. The drum solo in \"One Word\" is composed around cues, not simply improvised.","A commercial peak, it was praised at the time for its power and precision. Later critics sometimes find it colder than The Inner Mounting Flame; others consider it the band's best-played album. Its influence on rock guitarists and on the jazz-rock of the late seventies is widely acknowledged, and the title track remains a benchmark for guitar-violin unison.","Start with the title track and listen for the gong opening. Then \"Thousand Island Park\" as relief. Compare its driving metres with Stravinsky's Rite of Spring and its unison fury with Bartók's Miraculous Mandarin."],"return-to-forever-romantic-warrior":["Six instrumental pieces on a medieval-fantasy theme, played by a quartet of virtuosos: Chick Corea on keyboards and synthesisers, Al Di Meola on guitars, Stanley Clarke on bass and Lenny White on drums. \"Medieval Overture\" opens with fanfare-like synthesiser brass; the title track is played on acoustic instruments, a gentle ballad turned into a quest; the closing \"Duel of the Jester and the Tyrant\" is a long, twisting composed showpiece of unison runs and sudden changes of mood.","Recorded in early 1976 at Caribou Ranch in the Colorado mountains, a studio used by rock bands, and released by Columbia. Return to Forever had turned from the soft Latin band of 1972 into a loud electric quartet competing with Mahavishnu and with progressive rock groups such as Yes and Emerson, Lake and Palmer. Polyphonic and portable synthesisers let Corea imitate brass and strings on stage.","Each member contributed writing: Stanley Clarke's \"The Magician\" and Lenny White's \"Sorceress\" sit beside Corea's pieces, which is one reason the album sounds like a suite of characters rather than a string of solos. Listen to how much of \"Duel of the Jester and the Tyrant\" is written out note for note: fusion here behaves like chamber music, with improvisation squeezed into short windows.","It became the band's best-selling album and a favourite of musicians, while many jazz critics mocked its sword-and-sorcery imagery and its closeness to progressive rock. Admirers point to the precision of the writing and playing; detractors hear fusion at its most grandiose. It remains a rite of passage for young guitarists and bassists.","Start with the acoustic title track, then \"Duel of the Jester and the Tyrant\" for the full machine. Follow a single instrument, Clarke's bass is the clearest guide. Compare with the medieval music it imagines, Machaut's songs, and with Stravinsky's Soldier's Tale, another tale of characters told by a small virtuoso band."],"jaco-pastorius-jaco-pastorius":["The debut album of the bassist who rewrote the instrument. It opens with \"Donna Lee\", the bebop line from Charlie Parker's circle, played at speed on fretless electric bass with only Don Alias's congas. \"Portrait of Tracy\" is built almost entirely from harmonics, ringing like a music box. \"Come On, Come Over\" is hard funk with vocals by Sam and Dave; \"Continuum\" is a slow, singing ballad. Herbie Hancock, Wayne Shorter and the Brecker brothers guest.","Pastorius, a young musician from Florida who had played in local R&B and pop bands, was produced by Bobby Colomby of Blood, Sweat and Tears for Epic. He had pulled the frets out of his Fender Jazz bass himself and filled the slots with wood, creating a voice-like sliding tone. The album was released just as he joined Weather Report, which quickly made him famous.","The whole of \"Portrait of Tracy\" uses harmonics, notes produced by lightly touching the string at precise points, so the bass sounds like a bell or a high keyboard. It is written for his first wife, Tracy. On \"Opus Pocus\" Othello Molineaux's steel drums join Wayne Shorter's soprano: a Caribbean colour Pastorius loved from his Florida years.","Received with astonishment by musicians, it transformed the electric bass from a supporting instrument into a solo voice, and bass players still treat it as a textbook. Critics sometimes note that the album is uneven, a showcase jumping between styles. Pastorius's later struggles with illness and his early death in 1987 have given the record a poignant weight.","Start with \"Donna Lee\" and remind yourself the bass is the only melodic instrument. Then \"Portrait of Tracy\" with headphones for the harmonics. Compare with Weather Report's Heavy Weather and Pat Metheny's Bright Size Life, both of which he played on."],"metheny-bright-size-life":["A trio of guitar, fretless bass and drums playing eight light, open, singing pieces. Pat Metheny's guitar has a warm, clean tone and a melodic, almost folk-like way of phrasing. Jaco Pastorius plays counter-melodies rather than bass lines, and Bob Moses's drums are loose and responsive. The title track is bright and springy; \"Unity Village\" rolls; \"Midwestern Nights Dream\" is quiet and wide. It closes with a medley of Ornette Coleman tunes.","Recorded in December 1975 in Ludwigsburg, Germany, for ECM, when Metheny was only twenty-one and teaching at Berklee after playing with vibraphonist Gary Burton. Metheny came from Lee's Summit, Missouri, and wanted a sound that reflected the open landscapes of the Midwest, not New York. ECM's clear, spacious recording style suited that ambition.","Listen to the chords on the title track: Metheny often plays open voicings, with strings ringing together like a folk guitar, which gives the music its brightness. The Ornette Coleman medley at the end is a clue: Metheny admired Coleman's melodic freedom, and would later record Song X with him. Pastorius here plays mostly in the upper register, like a second guitar.","Modestly successful on release, it grew into one of the most admired guitar trio records in jazz, a model for students of melodic improvisation. Some critics saw ECM's sound as too polished; others heard a genuine new pastoral voice in American jazz. It is now routinely listed among the essential guitar albums.","Start with the title track and hum along; the melodies are singable. Then \"Midwestern Nights Dream\" for the landscape. Compare with Copland's Appalachian Spring, another American music of wide open space, and with Jaco Pastorius's own album from the same year."],"metheny-as-falls-wichita-so-falls-wichita-falls":["The first side is a single twenty-minute title piece, a slow-building sound picture of synthesiser drones, voices, distant crowd sounds and a repeating melodic cell that grows into something vast and ominous. The second side holds shorter songs: \"Ozark\" is a bouncing piano piece, \"September Fifteenth\" a slow elegy, \"It's for You\" joyful, and \"Estupenda Graça\" a meditation on \"Amazing Grace\". Naná Vasconcelos's berimbau and voice add colour throughout.","Made by Pat Metheny with his keyboard partner Lyle Mays and the Brazilian percussionist Naná Vasconcelos, and released on ECM in 1981. Polyphonic synthesisers were becoming available to musicians, and Metheny and Mays used them to create textures closer to minimalism and film score than to jazz. The album is credited to Metheny and Mays together.","\"September Fifteenth\" is dedicated to the pianist Bill Evans, who died on 15 September 1980; Mays's piano voicings deliberately echo Evans's style. In the title piece, listen for the long ostinato underneath the drama: like much minimalist music, it holds still while the colours around it shift, which is why its crescendo feels so large.","Something of a cult favourite, it is admired by fans of ambient and minimalist music as well as jazz listeners. Critics have described the title track as a precursor of ambient and post-rock; others find it too close to film music. It has stayed in print and is often cited by musicians outside jazz.","Put on the title track with headphones and do not skip; the build takes time. Then play \"September Fifteenth\" and think of Bill Evans. Compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians and Philip Glass's Music in Twelve Parts, other slow-building works of the same decade."],"jarrett-belonging":["Six pieces by Keith Jarrett's so-called European Quartet: Jan Garbarek's saxophone, cool and piercing, over Jarrett's piano, Palle Danielsson's bass and Jon Christensen's drums. \"Spiral Dance\" is short and bouncing; \"Blossom\" is spacious and lyrical; \"'Long as You Know You're Living Yours\" is a warm, gospel-tinged groove; the title piece is a brief hymn. The feel is airy, with long notes, open space and a melody-first approach, and Garbarek's tone is the signature.","Recorded in Oslo in April 1974 for ECM, the German label whose producer Manfred Eicher had a vision of clean, spacious sound. Jarrett was also leading an American quartet with Dewey Redman and Charlie Haden at the time, a fiercer, more blues-rooted group. The European Quartet gave him a different outlet: Nordic players who were drawn to folk song and open space.","Listen to how often Christensen plays around the beat rather than on it, with cymbal colour and wash instead of steady time; this is the Scandinavian drum approach that became part of the ECM sound. Jarrett's piano on \"'Long as You Know\" slips into gospel phrasing, a reminder of his American roots even in the most European of settings.","Warmly reviewed at the time, it has become one of the most admired ECM records and a favourite among fans who find the label's sound at its most balanced here. Some critics found the music too cool, too European; others see it as the start of a distinct Nordic jazz tradition. The quartet's later albums, including Nude Ants, have added to its standing.","Start with \"'Long as You Know You're Living Yours\" for the groove, then \"Blossom\" for the space. Notice how Garbarek holds long notes with little vibrato. Compare with Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel, another ECM-era music of stillness, and with The Köln Concert from the next year."],"jarrett-the-koln-concert":["A solo piano concert, entirely improvised, released as four sections across a double album. The first part moves from a simple, rocking figure to long passages over two repeated left-hand chords, with melodies that sound like folk songs or hymns. The later parts range from gospel to rhapsody to hushed lyricism. Jarrett hums, stamps and groans. What is recognisable is the rolling left-hand ostinato under the singing right hand.","Performed on 24 January 1975 at the Cologne Opera House, organised by a seventeen-year-old promoter, Vera Brandes. Jarrett arrived exhausted from travelling, with back pain, to find the wrong instrument: a small, worn piano with weak bass and brittle top notes. He nearly refused to play. The concert was recorded for ECM, which had built a reputation for clear, intimate piano recordings.","The opening notes are said to echo the chime used in the opera house to summon audiences back to their seats, which drew a laugh. Listen to how Jarrett avoids the piano's harsh upper register and leans on the middle and repeated bass figures: much of the famous sound came from working around a bad instrument. For years he declined requests to publish a transcription.","It became the best-selling solo piano recording in jazz and one of ECM's biggest records, running into millions of copies. Some critics dismissed it as new-age wallpaper; others hailed it as a new kind of spontaneous composition. Jarrett himself grew weary of its fame. Scholars now discuss it alongside minimalism and Romantic piano improvisation as well as jazz.","Start with Part I and give it half an hour, following the repeated left hand. Then listen for the moments where a melody seems to arrive from nowhere. Compare with Satie's Gymnopédies for stillness, Glass's Mad Rush for repetition, and Jarrett's own recording of Bach's Goldberg Variations for his classical side."],"shorter-speak-no-evil":["Six compositions, dark and elegant, played by Wayne Shorter's tenor saxophone with Freddie Hubbard's trumpet, Herbie Hancock, Ron Carter and Elvin Jones. \"Witch Hunt\" opens with a stark horn fanfare; \"Fee-Fi-Fo-Fum\" borrows from fairy tale; \"Dance Cadaverous\" is a slow, eerie waltz; \"Infant Eyes\" is a ballad of great tenderness. The tunes are short, harmonically unusual and haunting, and Shorter's saxophone has a dry, slightly veiled tone that makes them sound like riddles.","Recorded on Christmas Eve 1964 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio and released in 1966. Shorter had just joined Miles Davis's quintet after years with Art Blakey's Jazz Messengers, and he was writing a stream of compositions for both. The mid-sixties were full of anxiety, the civil rights struggle and the Vietnam war, and the record's imagery of witches, spells and fairy-tale threats has often been read against that background.","The chord progressions move by unexpected steps, so a tune seems to drift into another key and then slip back; this is why jazz students find Shorter tunes hard to improvise on and so easy to remember. Elvin Jones, John Coltrane's drummer, plays with the Davis rhythm section of Hancock and Carter, joining two of jazz's great bands on one record. The cover shows Shorter's wife at the time.","Admired from the start, it has become one of the most cherished albums in the Blue Note catalogue, and \"Infant Eyes\" a widely played standard. Critics consider it the peak of Shorter's Blue Note period and a model of post-bop composition. Debate turns on whether it is the best of his albums of this time or merely the most famous.","Start with \"Infant Eyes\" and hear how few notes Shorter needs. Then \"Witch Hunt\" for the fanfare. Compare with Hancock's Maiden Voyage, recorded months later with almost the same band, and with Bartók's Sixth String Quartet for another music of dark, questioning harmony."],"shorter-native-dancer":["Nine songs made with the Brazilian singer Milton Nascimento, whose soaring falsetto opens the album on \"Ponta de Areia\". Shorter's soprano saxophone circles the voice; Herbie Hancock plays piano and electric keyboards; Brazilian musicians provide gentle, rocking grooves. The music moves between Nascimento's songs and Shorter's own pieces, such as \"Beauty and the Beast\" and \"Diana\". The overall sound is light, warm and melancholy, closer to song than to fusion's usual volume.","Recorded in Burbank, California, in 1974, while Shorter was also a member of Weather Report. He had fallen in love with Nascimento's music, from the Minas Gerais movement known as the Clube da Esquina, and invited him to America. Brazilian music had been familiar in jazz since bossa nova, but Nascimento's songs were stranger and more hymn-like, and this album introduced him to many American listeners.","\"Ponta de Areia\" is a song about a railway line in Minas Gerais that was shut down, leaving its towns isolated: its sad optimism is political as well as personal. Listen to the way Shorter plays under and around Nascimento's voice rather than above it, a deliberate humility. Some of the album's songs had already appeared on Nascimento's own records in Portuguese.","Little noticed by jazz critics at first, it became one of Shorter's best-loved records and a founding document of Brazilian-jazz fusion. Critics now see it as far ahead of the idea of world music. Its songs have been covered by many singers, and it made Nascimento a name among jazz musicians.","Start with \"Ponta de Areia\" and let the voice carry you. Then \"Beauty and the Beast\" for Shorter's own writing. Compare with Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 for another Brazilian voice over light accompaniment, and Esperanza Spalding's Chamber Music Society, which continues the conversation."],"shorter-emanon":["A three-disc album in two parts. The first disc sets Wayne Shorter's quartet, with Danilo Pérez, John Patitucci and Brian Blade, against the Orpheus Chamber Orchestra in four works including \"Pegasus\" and \"Prometheus Unbound\", orchestral colour and improvisation braided together. The other two discs capture the quartet live in London, playing long, shape-shifting pieces where themes appear only in fragments. Shorter's saxophone, now spare and oracular, speaks in short phrases.","Released in 2018, when Shorter was in his mid-eighties, it came packaged with a graphic novel written by Shorter with Monica Sly and illustrated by Randy DuBurke, about a philosopher-hero who travels between worlds. Shorter had long been fascinated by science fiction and comics, and by the idea of music as philosophy. The quartet had played together since 2000, developing a style of collective improvisation without set solos.","Emanon is \"no name\" spelt backwards, a title Dizzy Gillespie had used for a bebop tune in the forties, and Shorter meant it as a statement about a hero with no fixed identity. Listen in the orchestral pieces for how written passages for strings and winds hand over to the quartet mid-phrase, so it is hard to tell where composition ends and improvisation begins.","Widely praised and awarded a Grammy for best jazz instrumental album, it was hailed as a late-life summation. Some critics found the orchestral pieces more successful than the live discs; others the reverse. It stands as one of the most ambitious jazz-orchestra projects of recent years.","Start with the orchestral disc and \"Pegasus\"; listen for the moment the quartet takes over from the strings. Then dip into the live discs. Compare with Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige, Gershwin's Rhapsody in Blue and Kamasi Washington's The Epic."],"mehldau-songs-art-of-the-trio-vol-3":["Ten tracks of a piano trio thinking in counterpoint. Brad Mehldau's left hand is as busy and melodic as his right, so tunes often seem to be played by two pianists. The programme mixes originals such as \"Song-Song\" and \"Unrequited\" with standards like \"Bewitched, Bothered and Bewildered\" and two rock songs: Radiohead's \"Exit Music (For a Film)\" and Nick Drake's \"River Man\". Larry Grenadier's bass and Jorge Rossy's brushes are soft and supple.","Recorded in 1998 for Warner Bros., the third in a series of trio albums that made Mehldau the most influential jazz pianist of his generation. He grew up playing both jazz and classical music, and was open to rock songs at a time when many jazz musicians treated them with suspicion. The album appeared only a year after Radiohead's OK Computer.","Mehldau's two hands often play independent lines, like a Bach invention or a Brahms intermezzo, and he has spoken about the influence of German Romantic piano music; the last track is even called \"Sehnsucht\", longing. Listen to \"Exit Music\": he keeps the song's slow build and dark harmony but lets the left hand carry a counter-melody the original never had.","Widely praised, it made Mehldau's version of \"Exit Music\" a new standard and helped legitimise rock songs as jazz material. Some critics found his style too introspective or Romantic; many more regarded it as a fresh direction for the piano trio. The album is now a staple for pianists studying independence of hands.","Start with \"Exit Music (For a Film)\", then compare it with Radiohead's original. Listen to the left hand. Follow with Brahms's Intermezzo Op. 118 No. 2 and Schumann's Kreisleriana for the Romantic roots, and The Köln Concert for Jarrett's different kind of piano storytelling."],"kamasi-washington-the-epic":["Three discs and nearly three hours: a ten-piece band with two drummers, two basses and keyboards, plus a string orchestra and a choir. Kamasi Washington's tenor saxophone plays long, spiritual, searching solos over big grooves and wordless choral swells. The record ranges from the soaring \"Change of the Guard\" to a lush arrangement of Debussy's \"Clair de Lune\" and \"Malcolm's Theme\", which includes Ossie Davis's eulogy for Malcolm X. The sound is huge, warm and ecstatic.","Washington and his West Coast Get Down collective of Los Angeles musicians had played together since childhood. He had just played on Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly, and the album was released by Flying Lotus's electronic label Brainfeeder. Both records appeared the same spring, at a time of protests over police killings, and they helped bring jazz back to a young audience.","Listen to the \"Clair de Lune\" arrangement: Debussy's piano piece is rescored for sax, choir and band, and the famous melody appears in a slow, swaying groove that nods to 1970s soul. The two bassists, one acoustic and one electric (Thundercat), and the two drummers are spread across the stereo field. The spirit of Alice and John Coltrane runs underneath.","Hailed as a landmark of jazz's revival and a crossover success, it topped many year-end lists. Some critics found its length and grandeur excessive, and argued that it looked back to 1970s spiritual jazz rather than forward. Its influence on the new wave of jazz festivals and audiences is widely acknowledged.","Start with \"Change of the Guard\" and ride the build. Then \"Clair de Lune\", comparing it with Debussy's piano original. Don't try to take in all three discs at once; treat it like a trilogy. Follow with Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly for the hip-hop side of the same scene."],"esperanza-spalding-chamber-music-society":["Eleven tracks mixing jazz trio, string trio and voice. Esperanza Spalding sings and plays double bass, often both at once, in melodies that leap and bend. The opening \"Little Fly\" sets William Blake's poem to a spare string accompaniment; \"Chacarera\" draws on Argentine folk rhythm; \"Apple Blossom\" features Milton Nascimento. The sound is intimate, like a salon concert, with strings used for colour and counterpoint rather than lush backing.","Released in 2010, it was Spalding's third album and part of a planned pair with the later Radio Music Society. She grew up in Portland, Oregon, trained on violin as a child, and studied and taught at Berklee. The album's title points to the European chamber tradition, and she arranged the string parts with a sense of classical intimacy.","\"Little Fly\" sets Blake's \"The Fly\" from Songs of Experience, and the strings imitate the fluttering insect. Listen for how often Spalding's bass line and vocal line diverge, a feat of coordination most musicians cannot manage. The Nascimento duet links her to the Brazilian thread that runs back through Wayne Shorter's Native Dancer.","Well received, it became famous when Spalding won the Grammy for Best New Artist in 2011, the first jazz musician to do so, beating Justin Bieber and prompting a storm of online comment. Some critics found the album too gentle; others praised its independence from both pop and jazz formulas.","Start with \"Little Fly\" and read Blake's poem first. Then \"Chacarera\" for the rhythm. Compare with Ravel's String Quartet for the string colours and Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 for voice and strings, and with Native Dancer for the Nascimento link."],"robert-glasper-black-radio":["Twelve tracks where jazz musicians play R&B and hip-hop as their own music. The Robert Glasper Experiment, with Casey Benjamin on sax and vocoder, Derrick Hodge on bass and Chris Dave on drums, backs a series of singers and rappers: Erykah Badu on a slow \"Afro Blue\", Lalah Hathaway on Sade's \"Cherish the Day\", Yasiin Bey on the title track, and a vocoder version of Nirvana's \"Smells Like Teen Spirit\". The drums play like a sampled beat, subtly off the grid.","Released on Blue Note in 2012, it grew from Glasper's double life as a jazz pianist and as a sideman for hip-hop and neo-soul artists. Chris Dave's drumming reflects the influence of J Dilla's loose, dragging beats, played by hand. The album came at a moment when young jazz musicians grew up with hip-hop as their first music, and it announced a new mainstream.","The title, from Yasiin Bey, refers to an airliner's black box: the thing that survives the crash, as Black American music survives. Listen to the drums: Chris Dave places beats slightly early or late, imitating producers who shifted samples by hand off the grid. \"Afro Blue\" was a Mongo Santamaría tune later sung by Abbey Lincoln and played by Coltrane.","It won the Grammy for Best R&B Album, which surprised jazz and R&B listeners alike, and sparked debate over whether it was jazz at all. Glasper argued that the category mattered less than the conversation. It is now seen as a turning point in the merging of jazz and hip-hop.","Start with \"Afro Blue\" with Erykah Badu, then \"Cherish the Day\". Notice Chris Dave's drumming. Compare with Head Hunters for an earlier jazz-funk crossover, and Kamasi Washington's The Epic for another side of the same generation."],"gogo-penguin-v2-0":["An acoustic piano trio that plays like electronic music. Chris Illingworth's piano repeats short, crystalline patterns; Nick Blacka's double bass locks into tight, bowed or plucked riffs; Rob Turner's drums imitate the skittering breakbeats of drum and bass. Pieces such as \"Murmuration\", \"Garden Dog Barbecue\" and \"Hopopono\" build by accumulation, like dance tracks, with melancholy chords floating over furious rhythm. The sound is clean, precise and cinematic.","Released by the Manchester label Gondwana in 2014, it was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, rare for a jazz trio. The three musicians grew up on electronic artists such as Aphex Twin and Squarepusher as much as jazz, and studied at Manchester's music colleges. Their approach reflects a generation for whom programmed beats were as natural as swing.","Listen to Turner's drums on \"Garden Dog Barbecue\": he plays patterns designed to sound like edited, looped breakbeats, humanly reproducing a machine. The piano parts owe more to Steve Reich and Philip Glass than to bebop, and the trio later wrote and performed their own live score for Godfrey Reggio's film Koyaanisqatsi, replacing Glass's.","Warmly reviewed and lifted by the Mercury nomination, it brought new listeners to jazz. Some critics argued the music had little to do with jazz; others praised its synthesis of minimalism, electronica and the piano trio. It helped define a new British jazz scene alongside groups in London and Manchester.","Start with \"Hopopono\" and follow the drums. Compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians and Philip Glass's Glassworks for the pattern-building, and with Brad Mehldau for another modern trio. Listen loud, like a dance record."],"charley-patton-pony-blues":["Three minutes of one man doing the work of a band. Patton sets a hard, uneven stomp on the low strings, slaps the guitar body between phrases, and sings in a cracked bark so far back in the throat that half the words arrive as sound rather than language. The verses run on the usual twelve-bar frame but he stretches or clips bars at will, so the beat keeps stumbling and recovering. Between vocal lines the guitar answers in short bent figures, as though two people were talking.","Recorded in June 1929 at Paramount's session in Richmond, Indiana, after the Jackson record dealer H. C. Speir sent Patton north. He was already the best-known musician on the Dockery Farms circuit in the Mississippi Delta, playing plantation dances, picnics and juke joints for a mixed Black and white audience. Paramount was chasing the race-record boom Bessie Smith had opened six years earlier, and had discovered that a single man with a guitar cost far less to record than a band.","Listen for how little of the rhythm comes from strumming. Patton hits the strings, damps them, then knocks the top of the instrument, so the pulse is partly percussion on wood. The song is also a template rather than a text: the same melody and stomp carry a dozen other Patton titles, and several lines here turn up, barely altered, in records made by Howlin' Wolf twenty-five years later.","Paramount's shellac was notoriously poor and surviving copies hiss badly, which kept Patton's reputation behind Robert Johnson's for decades; the 1929 sides only became widely audible with post-war reissues and modern transfers. Scholars now treat him as the earliest documented Delta stylist rather than an inventor working alone, since he clearly inherited a local tradition. Arguments continue over how much of the murk is Paramount's fault and how much is Patton's deliberate roughness.","Play it twice: once for the words, which you will not entirely catch, and once ignoring them, following only the guitar's replies. Then put on Son House's 'My Black Mama' from a year later and hear the same engine driven harder. For a way in, find a remastered transfer rather than a raw disc rip — the difference in what you can hear is large."],"charley-patton-high-water-everywhere":["A two-part record, one song split across both sides of a disc, and the nearest thing the country blues has to reportage. Patton names Delta towns one after another — Sumner, Leland, Greenville, Rosedale — as the water takes them, and the guitar hammers a single insistent figure underneath, broken by slapped percussion on the body of the instrument. His voice climbs in the second part until it is closer to shouting than singing. There is no chorus and no resolution; the flood simply keeps coming.","The Great Mississippi Flood of 1927 broke the levees and put a region the size of a small country under water, displacing hundreds of thousands, most of them Black sharecroppers, many of them held in refugee camps and made to work on the levees at gunpoint. Patton recorded this in October 1929, two years after, for Paramount. It was one of the first commercial records to treat a disaster as news rather than as metaphor, and it helped push the Great Migration story onto shellac.","Follow the geography. The towns are listed in a route that moves the singer north and east ahead of the water, so the record is a map of an actual escape. Notice too that Patton drums on the guitar hardest exactly where the words describe the levee breaking — the instrument is doing the flood while the voice does the reporting.","Long treated as folklore, the record is now read by historians as testimony, cross-checked against relief records and newspaper accounts of the camps. Its authority rests on Patton having been in the Delta when it happened. Debate continues over whether the second part's near-shout is exhaustion at the end of a long session or a deliberate rise — the two parts were almost certainly cut back to back.","Hear both parts in order and without a gap; split across two sides, they were designed as one arc. Keep a map of the Mississippi Delta open and follow the town names. Then listen to Memphis Minnie's 'When the Levee Breaks', made the same year about the same flood, and notice how differently a duo handles the subject."],"charley-patton-down-the-dirt-road-blues":["A travelling song, and one of the clearest recordings of Patton's guitar hand. The bass strings walk a repeating figure while the treble strings snap out a high answering phrase; between them the voice growls verses about leaving, about a country where every day feels like a wrong day, about going where he has never been. The tempo sits slightly faster than a walk. Patton interrupts himself with spoken asides, half-audible, addressed to nobody in the room.","Cut at the same 1929 Paramount sessions as 'Pony Blues'. Patton spent his life moving between Delta plantations, and mobility for a Black sharecropper in Mississippi was both the one available freedom and a legal hazard: vagrancy laws and debt peonage made leaving a plantation dangerous. The record was also released under the alternative title 'Over the Sea Blues', a reminder of how loosely Paramount handled the material it bought.","Count the beats and you will keep losing the thread: Patton adds or drops a half-bar wherever the words need room, a habit that vanished once blues bands had to play together. The spoken interjections are the other clue — juke-joint practice, where the singer kept talking to the dancers, preserved by accident on a studio disc.","Once dismissed as a lesser cousin of 'Pony Blues' because it shares much of its material, the song is now valued precisely for that: it shows a working musician recycling a frame rather than composing fixed songs. Transcribers still disagree about several lines, and the two issued titles have caused decades of discographical confusion.","Ignore the words for one pass and follow only the low strings; the walking figure is the ancestor of a thousand boogie bass lines. Then listen for the asides. Pair it with Florence Price's First Symphony, written in the same decade from the same Southern material, and hear what happens when the idiom is scored for an orchestra."],"son-house-my-black-mama":["Two parts on two sides of one disc, and a single unbroken argument. House plays bottleneck slide in an open tuning, so the guitar answers every vocal line with a whining, voice-like figure that slides up to its note rather than arriving at it. The singing is loud and unrelenting, closer to a preacher at full volume than to crooning. Part two turns to a death — a telegram, a body, a coffin — and the guitar's answers get shorter and harder as it goes.","Recorded in May 1930 at Paramount's Grafton, Wisconsin studio, on a trip Charley Patton arranged: Patton, House, Willie Brown and the pianist Louise Johnson drove up together. House had been ordained as a Baptist preacher and had served time at Parchman Farm; he came to the blues late and never stopped treating it as the devil's competition for the same congregation. The sides sold almost nothing — the Depression had gutted the race-record market within months.","Part two contains the material that House later rebuilt, nearly line for line, as 'Death Letter Blues' thirty-five years on. Hearing the two together is like watching a photograph taken twice. Listen also to how the slide never quite lands on a fixed pitch: House lets it hover a fraction under, which is exactly what gives the blues note its ache.","Paramount pressed so few copies that for decades the record existed mainly as a rumour among collectors; the surviving copies are worn almost to noise. Its status rose sharply when House was rediscovered in 1964 and could demonstrate what the crackle had hidden. Scholars now treat these 1930 sides as the missing link between Patton's stomp and Robert Johnson's tighter, more written songs.","Start with part two, where the story is, then go back for part one. Listen for the exact moment the slide enters after a sung line — House almost never lets voice and guitar sound together. Follow it immediately with his 1965 'Death Letter Blues' to hear the same song at a different speed and a different age."],"son-house-preachin-the-blues":["House announces that he is going to get religion, join the Baptist church, and be a preacher so he will not have to work — and then spends two record sides demolishing the idea. The slide guitar hammers a single repeating figure at a hard, driving tempo; the voice rides over it in long shouted phrases with the cadence and the breath control of an actual sermon. The joke turns bitter as it goes: the church, the deacon, the sister in the congregation all come in for it.","Also from the Grafton sessions of May 1930. House had genuinely preached, and the conflict between pulpit and juke joint was the central fact of his life rather than a pose; he gave up the blues more than once. In the Delta of 1930 the two institutions competed for the same Saturday-night and Sunday-morning crowd, and a bluesman in church was a scandal. Paramount, chasing sales, seems to have liked the provocation.","The song's structure is a sermon's, not a ballad's: House states his text, repeats it, elaborates, then returns to it louder. Listen for the places where he stops singing and simply declaims over the guitar. Bessie Smith had recorded a different song of the same title in 1927, which makes House's version a rejoinder as much as a title.","Later writers took the song as evidence of a sacred–secular split in Black Southern music; more recent scholarship reads it the other way, as proof that the two shared a single delivery and a single audience. Its reputation grew enormously after 1964, when House performed a version on film and the sermon-like construction became visible as well as audible.","Listen for breath. House phrases exactly as a preacher does, pushing to the end of a line and then snatching air. Compare it with Blind Willie Johnson's 'John the Revelator', which uses the same guitar tradition for the opposite purpose, and with Gershwin's 'It Ain't Necessarily So', where the same doubt is set on Broadway."],"son-house-death-letter-blues":["A letter arrives saying the woman he loves is dead; he takes the train, finds her laid out on the cooling board, and cannot make himself look at her face. House sings it at the top of his voice with a steel-bodied guitar answering in slide figures that bend and scrape. The tempo lurches — he speeds up when the words press and stops outright between verses — and near the end he abandons the chords entirely and just beats the strings. Four minutes, no chorus, no consolation.","Recorded in 1965 for Columbia after three young collectors tracked House down in Rochester, New York in 1964; he had not played seriously in twenty years and had to be taught his own records back. The folk revival was at its height, Newport was booking rediscovered Delta singers alongside Bob Dylan, and a white college audience was hearing this music live for the first time. House was sixty-three and drinking heavily; the playing is unsteady and all the more frightening for it.","The song is a rebuild of part two of his 1930 'My Black Mama', slowed and expanded. Listen for the bar where he loses the count and simply waits — the guitar drops out, the voice keeps going, and the silence is the most exposed moment on the record. The White Stripes' 2000 version copies that hesitation deliberately.","Some critics hold that the 1965 recordings are diminished next to the 1930 sides and the Lomax field tapes; others argue the age and the unsteadiness are the point, and that this is the definitive performance. What is not disputed is its reach: it moved through the British blues bands into the garage rock revival, and is now probably the most covered Delta blues after Robert Johnson's.","Start here if you start anywhere in the Delta blues — it is the most immediately overwhelming of these records and the sound quality is modern. Watch the surviving film of House performing rather than only listening; the physical effort explains the sound. Then go back to 'My Black Mama' and hear the same song thirty-five years younger."],"robert-johnson-cross-road-blues":["Under three minutes, and one of the tightest things in the country blues. Johnson plays slide in an open tuning, keeping a thumping bass with his thumb while the bottleneck cries above it, so the record sounds like two guitars and a foot. The words are plain and terrifying in their plainness: he kneels at a crossroads asking for mercy, tries to flag a ride, and the sun goes down with nobody stopping. A Black man caught outside after dark in 1930s Mississippi needed no supernatural explanation.","Recorded in San Antonio in November 1936, in a makeshift studio set up in the Gunter Hotel by the producer Don Law for the American Record Corporation. Johnson was twenty-five, a travelling musician who had already absorbed Son House, Charley Patton and Lonnie Johnson, plus whatever was on the jukeboxes. He reportedly played facing the corner of the room, which engineers took for shyness and modern ears hear as a man using the wall as a reflector.","The devil is not in the lyric. Every supernatural reading of this song was added later, largely after 1966 writing built a legend from a remark of Son House's; what the words actually describe is sundown law and the danger of being stranded. Listen instead to the guitar's timing: Johnson pushes the slide figure a fraction ahead of the thumb, and that tiny disagreement is what makes the record twitch.","The record sold modestly in 1937 and was almost forgotten until Columbia's 1961 reissue. Cream's 1968 rewrite, 'Crossroads', made it a rock standard and simultaneously buried the original's meaning under guitar-hero mythology. Scholarship since the 1990s has worked hard to separate Johnson the working musician from the legend, and this song has been the main battleground.","Listen once for the thumb alone; it never stops, and everything else is hung on it. Then compare Cream's version and notice what a full band has to add and what it loses. Seek a transfer made from a clean shellac copy at the correct speed — several early reissues ran the discs fast, which raised Johnson's voice."],"robert-johnson-hellhound-on-my-trail":["The most unsettled of Johnson's records and the least regular. He keeps blurring the bar lines, so the song seems to hurry and then hang back; the guitar figure is high, thin and repeated like a nervous tic. The voice climbs into a near-falsetto on the word 'trail'. Images accumulate without a story: rain falling on his trail, hot ashes, a woman sprinkling powder round his door, leaves shaking on the tree. Three minutes that never settle into a groove.","Recorded in Dallas in June 1937, at the second and last of Johnson's two sets of sessions; he was dead fourteen months later, near Greenwood, Mississippi, aged twenty-seven. The imagery comes from hoodoo practice widespread in the Delta — goofer dust sprinkled at a threshold was an ordinary act, not a metaphor — and from the older song tradition of Skip James, whose 'Devil Got My Woman' Johnson clearly knew.","The melody and much of the harmonic shape are lifted from Skip James's 1931 'Devil Got My Woman', a record almost nobody owned; Johnson must have heard it and kept it. Listen also for how rarely he repeats a bar count. This is one of the few blues recordings where the irregularity is plainly deliberate rather than the residue of dance-hall habit.","Often described as the moment the blues becomes interior rather than social, which is probably too neat: the imagery is communal, not private. Its reputation was made by the 1961 reissue and by writers who heard modern dread in it. Performance scholars argue over the pitch — if Johnson's discs were cut slightly slow, the famous high vocal would have been a shade lower in the room.","Do not try to tap along; let the bar lines go. Listen for the guitar's high repeated figure and how it refuses to resolve. Then play Skip James's 'Devil Got My Woman' and hear where it came from, and Schubert's Winterreise for another traveller walking away from a house with something behind him."],"robert-johnson-love-in-vain":["A farewell at a railway station, told in four short verses and almost no adjectives: he carries her suitcase, the train pulls out, two lights on the back of it — one blue, one red — and then he is standing there. The guitar keeps a slow, unhurried walking pattern with none of the agitation of his faster records. At the end the words run out and he hums the last verse, which is the most expressive thing on the record.","From the June 1937 Dallas sessions, the last Johnson recorded. It was issued in 1939, after his death. Its skeleton comes from a much older song associated with Leroy Carr, the polished Indianapolis singer-pianist whose records Johnson studied; the borrowing is typical, and shows how far a Delta musician's listening reached beyond the Delta by the late 1930s. The railroad departure was by then a standard blues situation and a literal one for a migrating population.","The hummed final verse is not an afterthought. Johnson hums an entire chorus in place of words, which nobody had made a habit of on record; it makes the silence of the platform audible. Listen also to the two lights on the train, blue and red — the lyric gives the colours and lets you supply the meaning, which is why the image has survived.","The Rolling Stones recorded it in 1969, credited it oddly, and turned it into one of the most familiar blues in rock; a generation met Johnson through that version. Critics divide over whether the Stones' country-rock reading honours or flattens it. Johnson's own take is now generally reckoned his most controlled performance and the best evidence against the wild-man legend.","Wait for the hum; everything before it is preparation. Listen for how steady the guitar stays while the words fall apart. Then hear the Rolling Stones' version, and Son House's 'Death Letter Blues', where a train also carries the singer towards a loss he cannot fix."],"robert-johnson-king-of-the-delta-blues-singers":["Sixteen of Johnson's twenty-nine recorded songs, drawn from the 1936 and 1937 sessions and sequenced onto one long-playing record: 'Cross Road Blues', 'Hellhound on My Trail', 'Come On in My Kitchen', 'Preachin' Blues', 'Walkin' Blues', 'If I Had Possession Over Judgment Day'. Heard in a row rather than one side at a time, the range is the surprise — dance numbers, comic songs and hoodoo nightmares by the same man. The sleeve carried a painting of a faceless figure, because no photograph was known.","Assembled by Frank Driggs for Columbia in 1961 from masters the company had owned since it absorbed Johnson's original label. Almost nobody outside a small circle of collectors had heard these songs; the 78s were rare and Johnson had been dead twenty-three years. The album landed in the middle of the American folk revival and reached Greenwich Village and, within a few years, London, where Keith Richards, Eric Clapton and Brian Jones treated it as scripture.","The faceless cover is the whole story of the reissue: the compilers had no image of Johnson and filled the gap with mystery, which is how the legend hardened. Listen also to the false starts and studio talk left on some takes — Don Law's voice, room noise, Johnson counting himself in — the few seconds that show a working session rather than a haunting.","Arguably the most influential reissue ever released: it created the British blues boom, and with it the guitar culture of the next forty years. Later scholarship has pushed back hard, arguing that the album's framing — king, mystery, devil — distorted a musician who was one of many and not the most famous in his own lifetime. The complete recordings issued in 1990 largely replaced it, and sold over a million copies.","Hear it in the 1961 running order at least once; the sequencing is part of what hit London so hard. Then move to the complete set for the alternate takes, which show how precisely Johnson repeated himself. Compare Gershwin's Porgy and Bess, which imagined the same South for Broadway two years before these sessions."],"blind-lemon-jefferson-see-that-my-grave-is-kept-clean":["A man arranging his own funeral in advance. The verses ask for two white horses, count the church bells, and describe the coffin sound as the body goes down; each one is answered by a guitar that stops keeping time and plays a slow tolling figure on the bass strings. Jefferson's voice is high and conversational, almost light, which makes the subject stranger. The record is short, plain, and closer to a hymn's pacing than to a dance blues.","Recorded for Paramount in Chicago in 1927. Jefferson had been the label's biggest seller for two years, the first Black male country blues singer to move records in quantity, and Paramount had him cutting sides at speed. He had also recorded religious material under a pseudonym, and this song sits deliberately between the two catalogues: not a spiritual, but shaped like one. He died in Chicago in the winter of 1929, the circumstances never firmly established.","The guitar imitates two things in turn — the church bell, struck on a single low string, and the hollow knock of earth on a coffin lid. Once you hear the imitation you cannot unhear it. The song also moves in and out of a twelve-bar shape; Jefferson simply stops counting when the bell figure needs longer, a freedom that the accompanying-band blues of the next decade lost.","Remembered as much through its descendants as itself: Bob Dylan recorded it on his 1962 debut, and it has been covered continually since. Folklorists have argued over whether it is Jefferson's composition or his arrangement of a much older Southern funeral song; the honest answer is probably both. Its influence on the sung death-lament in American music is hard to overstate.","Listen for the bell — a single repeated low note between verses — and count how many times it strikes. Then hear Dylan's 1962 version, and Dowland's 'Flow My Tears' from three centuries earlier, where a lute does something not unlike what this guitar does between the lines."],"blind-lemon-jefferson-black-snake-moan":["A comic, filthy, entirely deadpan blues about a black snake that has got into the singer's room, crawled in his bed, and will not be found. Jefferson delivers it slowly and innocently while the guitar does the opposite: long single-string runs slither up and down between the vocal lines, refusing to settle into a strum. The joke is carried by the instrument, not the voice, and the pauses are as long as the phrases.","One of Jefferson's first big sellers for Paramount, cut in Chicago around 1926, when the label was still working out that a blind street singer from Dallas could outsell its jazz bands. The double entendre was standard commercial practice in the race-record market, and the censors of the day had little grip on records sold mostly by mail order and in Southern furniture stores. Jefferson's fee per side was small; Paramount's profit was not.","Listen to how the guitar fills. Jefferson never plays a steady accompaniment; he stops dead at the end of each sung line and then improvises a run of unpredictable length, so the record has no fixed metre at all. That freedom — voice and guitar taking turns rather than sharing a pulse — is the single most copied feature of his style, audible later in T-Bone Walker and B.B. King.","Long treated as a novelty beside 'See That My Grave Is Kept Clean', it has been reassessed as the clearest surviving demonstration of Jefferson's guitar technique, which no contemporary matched for speed or unpredictability. Its title has had a long second life in film and song. Disputes persist about the recording date, since Paramount's files are incomplete.","Follow only the guitar for one listen; the runs are the reason musicians studied this record. Then note how far the accompaniment is from the strummed backing you may expect of 1920s blues. Pair it with Charley Patton's 'Pony Blues' from three years later for the difference between Texas and the Delta."],"mississippi-john-hurt-avalon-blues":["A homesick song written on a trip north, and one of the sweetest-sounding records in the country blues. Hurt picks a steady alternating bass with his thumb while two fingers lay a melody over it, so the guitar is playing a duet with itself; nothing is hurried and nothing is loud. The voice is soft, slightly behind the beat, and never pushes. New York is a good town, he says, but it is not Avalon — and Avalon is a hamlet in the Mississippi hills of perhaps a few dozen people.","Recorded for OKeh in New York in December 1928, on the second of two trips the label paid for after a local fiddler recommended him. Hurt was a farmhand in his mid-thirties who played at country dances for Black and white neighbours alike; he was not a professional and did not become one. OKeh's race-record series collapsed with the Depression, the sides sold poorly, and Hurt went home and farmed for thirty-five more years.","The song named the singer. In 1963 the collector Tom Hoskins took the line 'Avalon, my home town' literally, found Avalon on an old atlas, drove there and knocked on doors until he found Hurt alive. Very few records have ever retrieved their own maker. Listen too for the thumb: it never varies, which is what lets the melody float so freely above it.","Almost unheard until Harry Smith included Hurt on the 1952 Anthology of American Folk Music, which put him in front of the revival generation. Since the rediscovery, critics have argued over whether his gentleness makes him marginal to the blues or shows how narrow the standard definition is; the second view has largely won, and 'songster' is now the usual word.","Listen with headphones for the separation between thumb and fingers — it is easy to miss how hard this is to play. Then hear the 1966 recordings of the same songs by a man in his seventies, and Dvořák's New World Largo, another outsider's song about a home that is somewhere else."],"mississippi-john-hurt-stack-o-lee-blues":["The American murder ballad, told by the mildest voice imaginable. Stack O' Lee shoots Billy Lyons over a Stetson hat, is arrested, tried and hanged; Hurt narrates all of it in the same unhurried, almost apologetic tone he uses for love songs, over a rolling three-finger guitar pattern that never changes pace. The gap between the violence and the delivery is the record. The refrain — that bad man, cruel Stack O' Lee — arrives each time like a shrug.","Recorded for OKeh in New York in December 1928. The song descends from a real killing in St. Louis in 1895 and had been travelling orally for thirty years by the time Hurt cut it; dozens of versions existed, from ragtime piano to prison work chants. Hurt's is a ballad version — verses telling a story in order — rather than a blues, which is one reason he is best described as a songster rather than a bluesman.","Notice that the guitar never dramatises anything. Where a blues singer would break the pattern at the gunshot, Hurt keeps picking exactly as before, and the steadiness makes the story colder. Listen also for how the accompaniment implies a bass line, a chord and a melody at once with three fingers — the technique young guitarists in the 1960s spent years transcribing.","Harry Smith placed it on the Anthology of American Folk Music in 1952, where it became one of the revival's foundational tracks and a standard for every folk guitarist who followed. Folklorists have used the song's many versions as a case study in how an American ballad mutates. Hurt's remains the most widely learned, largely because it is the most playable.","Follow the story first — it is unusually clear — then listen again only to the guitar and try to separate the three parts. Compare Copland's Billy the Kid, which turns another American badman into a concert score, and note how differently the two handle a killing."],"mississippi-john-hurt-today":["A man in his seventies, recorded properly for the first time, playing the repertoire he had carried since before the First World War: blues, ballads, spirituals, rags and one or two new songs. The guitar is the same alternating-thumb pattern as in 1928, a little slower and considerably clearer; the voice has thinned into something almost spoken. Because it is a studio album rather than a set of three-minute sides, the songs breathe, and you hear the room, the fingers and the occasional chuckle.","Made for Vanguard in 1966, three years after Hurt was found in Avalon, Mississippi and two Newport Folk Festival appearances later. The folk revival had created an audience — largely young, largely white, largely urban — for exactly this, and labels were recording rediscovered Southern musicians as fast as they could be located. Hurt died in November 1966, months after the sessions; it was the last record he completed.","Compare any song here with its 1928 OKeh original and the tempos are nearly identical. Thirty-eight years of not performing professionally changed almost nothing, which tells you the playing was habit rather than performance. Listen also for the quiet between phrases — modern tape catches the sound of his fingers leaving the strings, which shellac never could.","The rediscovery albums are sometimes dismissed as folk-revival souvenirs; this one resists the charge, because the fidelity finally matches the delicacy. Critics debate whether Hurt's late work was shaped by what his new audience wanted to hear, particularly in his choice of gentler material. His playing, at least, shows no sign of adjustment.","Begin with the familiar songs — 'Stagolee', 'Candy Man', 'Louis Collins' — then let the record run. Good headphones repay it: the point is the detail of the picking. Afterwards, hear Berio's Folk Songs for the opposite approach, a folk repertoire deliberately recomposed rather than preserved."],"skip-james-devil-got-my-woman":["Perhaps the eeriest record in the country blues. James tunes his guitar to an open minor chord, so every strum sounds hollow and unresolved, and picks a pattern that keeps circling without ever cadencing. Above it he sings in a light, unstrained falsetto — not a shout, not a growl, but a floating, almost disembodied voice, unlike anyone else on record in 1931. The words are simple jealousy: he would rather be the devil than be that woman's man, because his best friend took her.","Recorded in February 1931 at Paramount's Grafton, Wisconsin studio, at a single session that produced eighteen sides. The Depression had destroyed the race-record market; almost nothing sold, and James gave up music for the church, later becoming a minister. The tuning and the minor cast come from the area around Bentonia, Mississippi, where a small local tradition favoured that sound; James learned it from an older local player rather than inventing it.","The guitar is tuned so that an open strum gives a minor chord — an unusual choice that does most of the emotional work before a word is sung. Robert Johnson plainly knew this record: 'Hellhound on My Trail' takes its melody and shape from it, which means one of the most obscure discs of 1931 fed directly into the most famous blues of 1937.","Original copies are among the rarest 78s in existence, which shaped the record's mystique as much as its sound. Its standing rose steeply after James was rediscovered in 1964 and it reached a wider audience through the revival. Scholars now debate how much of the Bentonia sound was a genuine local school and how much was two or three men who knew each other.","Listen first for the tuning: strum-shaped chords that sound sad before anything happens. Then for the falsetto, which sits oddly high above the guitar. Follow it with Robert Johnson's 'Hellhound on My Trail' to hear the inheritance, and Dowland's 'Semper Dowland, semper dolens' for another musician who made a career out of one shade of misery."],"skip-james-hard-time-killing-floor-blues":["A Depression song made from almost nothing: the same hollow minor tuning, a guitar figure that repeats and never arrives anywhere, and that thin high voice reporting that hard times are here and everywhere you go, that people are drifting from door to door and cannot find a heaven. There is no anger in the delivery and no self-pity. James sings it slowly and evenly, as though describing weather, which is exactly what makes it hard to listen to twice in a row.","From the same February 1931 Grafton session. The timing is almost unbearable: James recorded a song about mass destitution into a market that destitution was destroying, for a label that would be out of business within a year. Cotton had collapsed, Mississippi tenant farmers were being turned off the land, and the Southern migration north was accelerating. Paramount pressed few copies and sold fewer.","There is no chorus and no refrain — each verse simply follows the last, so the song has no shape that promises an end. Listen for the guitar's refusal to resolve: the figure returns to the same unfinished place every time, which is the technical reason the record feels like a closing door. The phrase 'killing floor', from the slaughterhouse, was ordinary industrial language before the blues made it a metaphor.","Barely heard for seventy years, it reached a mass audience in 2000 when it appeared on the soundtrack of the Coen brothers' O Brother, Where Art Thou?. That exposure has divided opinion: some hear a Depression document finally given its due, others a period mood cue. Most critics now rank it with 'Devil Got My Woman' as James's finest surviving side.","Play it in a quiet room; it does not survive being background. Listen for how few notes the guitar actually uses. Then compare Son House's 'Death Letter Blues', which meets desolation with volume, and Dowland's 'In Darkness Let Me Dwell', which meets it with the same stillness three hundred years earlier."],"skip-james-im-so-glad":["The one fast record in the 1931 session, and a display piece. James picks a rapid, rippling figure that runs continuously under the vocal, never pausing for breath, while he sings a lyric of about a dozen words repeated in shifting order: glad, tired, weary, don't know what to do. The effect is of someone spinning in place. It is not a twelve-bar blues at all but a short circling form, closer to a rag, played at a speed that most guitarists still find awkward.","Recorded at Grafton in February 1931. James built the song on a sentimental popular tune of the 1920s, reharmonised and driven far beyond its original speed — the sort of transformation that shows how much commercial sheet music a Mississippi musician had absorbed. Like everything from the session, it sold almost nothing, and James was working as a labourer and later as a minister within a couple of years.","The words barely change, which throws all the attention onto the right hand. Listen for the moment the picking pattern shifts register and climbs; James does it without altering tempo, which is the difficult part. The lyric's circular repetition — the same four states shuffled — is a formal device, not a shortage of material.","Cream recorded it on their 1966 debut and again live, and the royalties reportedly helped pay James's medical bills in the last years of his life — one of the few cases where the revival's borrowing returned money to its source. Critics disagree about whether Cream's version has anything to do with the original beyond the tune. James's take remains the technical benchmark.","Count nothing; just follow the right hand and notice that it never rests. Then hear Cream's version for the distance a rock trio travels from a solo guitar. Pair it with Mississippi John Hurt's picking for the gentler use of the same three-finger technique."],"blind-willie-johnson-dark-was-the-night-cold-was-the-ground":["Three minutes with no words at all. Johnson hums and moans a melody while a bottleneck slide answers each phrase, sliding up to its notes from below and never quite arriving cleanly; guitar and voice trade lines like two people in the same grief. The underlying tune is an eighteenth-century English hymn about Christ's agony in Gethsemane, but nothing here is sung — the text is assumed, and its absence is the point. The dynamic barely changes from beginning to end.","Recorded in Dallas in December 1927, at Johnson's first session for Columbia. He was a street evangelist, not a nightclub musician, and sang on sidewalks with a tin cup wired to his guitar; the record company came to Texas rather than the other way around. Wordless moaning had a place in Southern Black worship — the congregation's sound when language runs out — so the record documents a practice, not an experiment.","The slide is played with a pocket knife rather than a bottleneck, which is why the tone scrapes. Listen for how Johnson tunes his humming slightly against the guitar so that voice and slide are never quite in unison — the beating between them is the record's whole texture. In 1977 the track was placed on the Voyager Golden Record and is now travelling out of the solar system.","Ry Cooder called it the most soulful piece on any American record and built his 1984 Paris, Texas score on its sound, which has made it the standard shorthand for desolate American landscape in film. Musicologists point out that it is technically a hymn arrangement, and argue over whether calling it 'the blues' at all is a category error. Nobody argues about its effect.","Listen in the dark, once, without doing anything else. Notice that you cannot tell where the voice stops and the guitar begins. Then hear Ives's The Unanswered Question, another American piece built from one line calling into silence, and Dowland's 'In Darkness Let Me Dwell'."],"blind-willie-johnson-john-the-revelator":["Call and response, stripped to two people. Johnson roars the question — who's that writing? — in a manufactured bass growl, and a woman's clear voice answers, John the Revelator, over a hard chopped guitar rhythm. The verses run through the Book of Revelation and the seven seals in fragments; there is no narrative, only the alternation, repeated until it becomes physical. The guitar keeps a blunt, almost percussive strum and stays out of the way.","Recorded in Atlanta in April 1930, at the last of Johnson's Columbia sessions. The second voice is probably his wife, Willie B. Harris, though the attribution has never been settled from the paperwork. The form — one leader, one responder, no choir — is the smallest possible unit of the congregational singing Johnson had grown up inside. Within months the Depression ended his recording career; he went on preaching on Texas streets until his death in 1945.","The growl is not his speaking voice. Johnson used a false bass for performance, and on the few sides where he sings in his natural register the difference is startling — the roar is a costume, adopted to carry over street noise. Listen also to the woman's part: she is not harmonising but answering, which keeps the record a conversation rather than a duet.","Endlessly covered, from gospel quartets to Depeche Mode and the White Stripes, usually with the call-and-response intact because nothing else will carry the song. Scholarship on Johnson has largely been a matter of recovering basic facts — his birth date, his wife's identity, the manner of his death — from almost no documents. The music, unusually, has never needed rehabilitation.","Listen for the two voices separately, then for the gap between them; the record lives in that gap. Play it after Son House's 'Preachin' the Blues' for the sacred answer to the same question. Tippett's A Child of Our Time, which set spirituals inside an oratorio, shows a third route out of the same tradition."],"lead-belly-goodnight-irene":["A waltz, which is the first surprise: three beats to the bar, gently rocked on a twelve-string guitar tuned low, with a chorus so easy that anyone can join it after one hearing. The second surprise is the verses, which are about a broken marriage, a woman's family, laudanum, and the singer's plan to jump in the river and drown. Lead Belly sings all of it in the same warm, declamatory voice, so the sweetness and the despair arrive together and neither cancels the other.","First recorded in 1933 by John and Alan Lomax on portable equipment at Louisiana's Angola prison, where Ledbetter was serving a sentence; the Lomaxes were collecting for the Library of Congress and believed prisons preserved older songs. Lead Belly did not write it — versions circulated in the South well before — but he made it his, and carried it north when the Lomaxes brought him out as a living exhibit, a role he resented and used.","The verses vary between every recorded take he made; the chorus never does. That is how an oral repertoire actually behaves, and it is visible here across a decade of sessions. Listen too for the twelve-string's low tuning, several steps down, which gives the bass strings their loose boom and let one guitar fill a room without amplification.","The Weavers' smoothed-out 1950 version, with strings and no laudanum, was the biggest-selling record in America that year — six months after Lead Belly died, poor, of ALS. The episode is the standard case study in who profits from folk material. Critics now generally treat his own recordings as the real thing and the hit as a sanitised cover.","Hear a Lomax-era recording before the Weavers' version, and notice which verses disappear. Count the three beats and feel how unusual a waltz is in this repertoire. Then compare Copland's Appalachian Spring, made from the same impulse to fix American vernacular material in place."],"lead-belly-midnight-special":["A prison song driven hard. The twelve-string hammers a rolling bass figure that does not let up, and Lead Belly half-sings, half-shouts verses about Miss Rosie coming to the gate, the sheriff, the Houston jail — and the train whose headlight sweeps across the cell blocks at midnight. The chorus asks the light to shine on him, which folklore read as a wish for release. It is the most physically forceful thing in his repertoire and the clearest demonstration of what one twelve-string can do.","Recorded by John and Alan Lomax in Louisiana in 1934 during their prison-farm collecting trips. The song was old and communal — versions had been noted among Texas convicts years before — and Lead Belly, who had served time in both Texas and Louisiana, sang it from inside the tradition rather than about it. The Lomaxes' portable disc cutter, heavy and temperamental, is why any of this survives at all.","Listen to the guitar rather than the words: the bass strings play a repeating pattern that behaves exactly like a boogie-woogie pianist's left hand, which is where the barrelhouse and the country blues meet. The 'special' is a named train, not a metaphor; several Southern prisons lay close enough to a line for a passing headlight to reach the windows.","It became a standard twice over — first in the folk revival, then in 1969 when Creedence Clearwater Revival turned it into a rock hit, by which point almost no listener connected it to a Louisiana prison farm. Folklorists have argued for decades over the headlight's meaning, with no agreement. The Lomaxes' methods, and their control of the copyrights, remain contested.","Play it loud enough to hear the low strings buzz. Notice how he pushes ahead of the beat on the chorus. Then hear Creedence's version for what electricity adds and subtracts, and Copland's Billy the Kid for the concert-hall version of the same American outlaw material."],"lead-belly-where-did-you-sleep-last-night":["Also known as 'Black Girl' and, further back, as 'In the Pines'. A woman is asked where she slept; she answers, in the pines, where the sun never shines, shivering the whole night. Somewhere in the same song a man's head is found under a train wheel. The two halves are never connected, and Lead Belly does not connect them. He sings it slowly, with long gaps, the twelve-string ringing out between phrases, and holds the word 'shiver' until it breaks.","Recorded in New York in 1944, by which time Lead Belly had spent a decade as a professional performer in the city, sharing stages with Woody Guthrie and Pete Seeger and finding the folk left a more congenial audience than the concert societies that first booked him. The song itself is far older, an Appalachian ballad documented in the 1870s and shared across Black and white Southern traditions long before any recording.","The song is a fragment collection, not a narrative — the head under the train and the night in the pines come from different older songs and were fused by oral transmission. That is why it never resolves, and why it unsettles. Listen for Lead Belly's pauses: he lets the guitar ring into silence between lines, which almost no commercial record of 1944 would have permitted.","Nirvana's 1993 performance on MTV Unplugged introduced it to millions who had never heard of Lead Belly, and Kurt Cobain's closing scream is now, for many listeners, the song's defining sound. Folklorists have traced dozens of variants under three main titles. Debate continues over whether the modern rock reading imports a violence the older versions leave ambiguous.","Listen for the silences, which are as composed as the notes. Then play Nirvana's version back to back and hear how little had to change. Berio's Folk Songs offers the opposite treatment — the same kind of material deliberately rewritten rather than handed on."],"memphis-minnie-when-the-levee-breaks":["Two guitars locked together and two voices trading lines. Minnie and Kansas Joe McCoy play interlocking parts — one keeping a bright steady figure, the other answering in the gaps — with a precision that sounds rehearsed rather than jammed. The words are a flood evacuation from the inside: mean old levee, the singer's home washed down, packing to leave, the thought of going to Chicago and the knowledge that Chicago is nobody's home. No chorus, no rescue, six plain verses.","Recorded in 1929, two years after the Great Mississippi Flood, which had displaced hundreds of thousands and pushed the Great Migration into its second wave. Minnie had come from Walls, Mississippi to Memphis; she and McCoy, then married, were discovered playing in a barbershop and taken to New York to record. The record's subject was still live news, and its politics — Chicago as the only exit — were the actual choice facing its audience.","Listen to how the two guitars divide the labour: neither plays a complete part, and it is only together that the accompaniment makes sense. That interlocking is the country blues moving towards a band sound years before amplification made bands compulsory. Led Zeppelin's 1971 rewrite kept the words and the title and threw away everything else, including both guitar parts.","Overshadowed for decades by the Led Zeppelin version, which is the one most listeners know and which credits Minnie only after litigation-era corrections. Reassessment came with the broader recovery of women in the blues, and Minnie is now generally ranked among the finest guitarists of her generation, not merely the finest woman. Her authorship of the lyric is no longer questioned.","Try to follow one guitar at a time; they are easier to hear apart with headphones. Then play Charley Patton's 'High Water Everywhere' about the same flood, and the Led Zeppelin version, and notice which of the three still sounds like a report from the water's edge."],"memphis-minnie-me-and-my-chauffeur-blues":["Minnie's biggest seller, and a record you can hear the 1950s coming through. She sings about wanting her chauffeur to drive her and nobody else, in a flat, amused voice that leaves the double meaning to the listener, and plays a clean, fast electric lead over a small band's steady shuffle. The solo is the point: single-note lines, bent and phrased like speech, delivered with a confidence that most electric guitarists would not reach for another decade.","Recorded in Chicago in 1941 for OKeh, with her partner Ernest Lawlars. Minnie had been in Chicago for years and had adapted completely to the tight, bass-and-drums studio style the Bluebird and Columbia sessions had standardised. The electric guitar was barely five years old as a commercial instrument; she took it up early and without fuss. Within a decade this sound, amplified further, would be Chicago blues.","Listen to the phrasing of the guitar solo rather than the notes: she breathes between figures exactly as she does when singing, so the instrument answers the vocal in the old country-blues way even though the setting is a modern band. The chauffeur was also a straightforward image of status — a Black woman in 1941 singing about having a driver is making a claim as well as a joke.","A commercial hit in its day, later sidelined by a critical taste that preferred the acoustic country blues to the Chicago studio sound. That preference has been steadily dismantled, and Minnie's electric work is now heard as a direct bridge to Muddy Waters and Chuck Berry. Her standing as a guitarist, rather than a singer who happened to play, took even longer to establish.","Go straight to the guitar solo, then rewind and hear how the vocal phrases the same way. Compare Blind Lemon Jefferson's runs from fifteen years earlier to see what she inherited. Then hear Ellington's Harlem for another portrait of Black urban confidence in the same decade."],"bessie-smith-downhearted-blues":["Voice and piano, nothing else, and the voice does not need anything else. Smith takes a slow twelve-bar song about a woman who has been mistreated once too often and delivers it with enormous, unhurried tone, bending single syllables across two or three pitches before letting them land. The piano stays out of the way, filling the gaps at the end of each line. The last verse turns: she has got the world in a jug and the stopper in her hand, and the threat is entirely credible.","Recorded in February 1923 in New York, Smith's first issued side for Columbia after other labels had turned her down. The song was written by Alberta Hunter and Lovie Austin, two Black women already established in the business. It sold in numbers nobody had forecast and rescued Columbia's finances; within two years the race-record market was a major industry, and the demand it created is what sent scouts to Mississippi looking for Charley Patton.","Listen to the ends of lines. Smith habitually finishes a phrase early and leaves a beat of silence before the piano answers, so the record breathes in a way that band accompaniments would later smooth out. Note also the acoustic recording process: no microphone, everything sung into a horn, which is why the voice sounds close and the piano distant.","The commercial event that defined the classic blues era and, indirectly, funded the country blues recordings that followed. Later critics, chasing authenticity, downgraded the theatre blues as commercial and elevated the Delta singers; that hierarchy has been thoroughly reversed, and Smith's influence on jazz and later on soul singing is now taken as the more consequential line.","Listen to one verse, then sing the same line yourself and hear how much she moves inside a single note. The acoustic sound is thin — let your ear adjust rather than reaching for a later record. Then hear Gershwin's 'Summertime', written a dozen years on by a man who had listened hard to singers like her."],"bessie-smith-st-louis-blues":["The meeting of two traditions on one disc: Smith's voice and Louis Armstrong's cornet, with a reed organ droning underneath instead of a piano. Every line she sings, he answers — not accompanying but replying, sometimes agreeing, sometimes contradicting. W. C. Handy's 1914 song has a tango-flavoured middle strain, which the organ turns solemn and churchy. Nothing is hurried; the whole record is two soloists taking turns and listening to each other, which was not yet a common way to make a record.","Recorded in New York in January 1925 with the twenty-three-year-old Armstrong, who had just arrived from Chicago and was months from his own Hot Five sessions. Handy's song was already the most published blues in America, a Tin Pan Alley property performed by white dance bands. Putting it in the hands of the two greatest Black improvisers then on record was a corrective, and it was Columbia's newly adopted electrical recording era that let their tone survive.","The reed organ is the oddity. It gives the record a harmonium's wheeze — a parlour and chapel sound, not a nightclub one — and turns Handy's dance number into something closer to a lament. Listen also to how Armstrong never plays over her: he waits for the end of every line, which is call and response taken straight out of church and into the studio.","Generally regarded as the definitive recording of the most recorded blues song of all, and as the clearest demonstration of what blues singing gave jazz. Handy's own view of blues authorship — he wrote down and copyrighted material he collected — has been argued over ever since. The record itself has never been seriously challenged.","Follow the cornet alone for one pass and treat the voice as its accompaniment; the record works both ways round. Then compare a white dance-band version of the same song from the same decade. Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige, eighteen years later, is where this conversation goes next."],"bessie-smith-nobody-knows-you-when-youre-down-and-out":["A song about money and friendship, sung with no self-pity at all. Smith had it, spent it, lost it, and reports that the people who drank her liquor are nowhere to be found; she also warns, evenly, that she may be back on top one day. A small band with horns backs her in a slow, almost stately tempo. It is not a twelve-bar blues but a thirty-two-bar popular song, and she sings it like one, holding the long notes and letting the horns fill behind her.","Recorded in New York in May 1929, five months before the Wall Street crash, which gave the lyric a second and much larger life. Jimmy Cox had written it in 1923. Smith's own career was already sliding: the theatre circuits that had made her were closing, talking pictures were taking their audiences, and Columbia dropped her in 1931. She died in 1937 after a car crash in Mississippi, aged forty-three.","Notice that she never sounds beaten. The warning in the final verse — that fortune turns — is delivered with more weight than any of the complaint, which is the reverse of how most singers have since read the song. Listen too for the small band's restraint: the horns answer between phrases and stay silent while she sings, the same discipline Armstrong showed four years earlier.","The Depression made it a standard, and Eric Clapton's 1970 acoustic version made it a rock one; most later readings treat it as a lament, which Smith's does not. Her death, and John Hammond's widely repeated claim about a segregated hospital, became a story of its own — later research has substantially complicated it, and Edward Albee turned the legend into a play.","Listen for the last verse and how her tone changes; everything before it is setup. Compare Clapton's version for what the song loses when it becomes purely sad. Then hear Weill's Threepenny Opera, written the year before, for another performer's clear-eyed account of who has money and who does not."],"ma-rainey-see-see-rider-blues":["The song that fixed a floating folk blues in one shape. Rainey sings heavily and slightly behind the beat about a rider who has gone, and then — calmly, in the last verse — about buying a pistol and shooting him. Behind her sits an exceptional small band: a cornet answering each line, trombone and clarinet weaving around it, piano keeping the floor. The tempo is slow enough to feel like weight rather than pulse. Her voice is rougher and more countrified than the polished singers who followed her.","Recorded in New York in October 1924 for Paramount, with Louis Armstrong on cornet and Fletcher Henderson at the piano — the best jazz players available, put behind a singer who had spent twenty years in Southern tent shows. Rainey had been performing the blues since around 1902, long before anyone recorded it, and she brought the rural idiom into the theatre and then into the studio essentially intact.","Listen for how far behind the beat she sits. Rainey lets the band start the bar and arrives late almost every time, which creates the drag that makes the record feel heavy; it is a tent-show technique for holding a crowd's attention. Armstrong, meanwhile, never fills the same space twice — his answers get shorter as the record goes on.","The song became one of the most recorded in American music, under several spellings, travelling through jazz, western swing and rock. Rainey's own version was long treated as a historical curiosity because of Paramount's poor sound; critical opinion now places her above most of her successors for rhythmic authority. August Wilson's play about a later session has done a good deal to restore her name.","Set a steady beat with your hand and hear how consistently she lands after it. Then follow Armstrong's cornet for a second pass. Compare Bessie Smith's 'St. Louis Blues' from a few months later, with the same cornet player and a completely different singer's approach."],"ma-rainey-prove-it-on-me-blues":["A jug band backs her — washboard, kazoo, guitar, the sound of a street corner rather than a theatre — while Rainey sings about going out with a crowd of friends who must have been women, since she does not like men, and wearing a collar and a tie. The refrain is a dare: they say she does it, but nobody can prove it on her. She sings it lightly, almost teasingly, and the cheap rattling band makes the whole thing sound like gossip set to music.","Recorded for Paramount in 1928. Rainey had been arrested in Chicago some years earlier after a party involving women in her chorus, a story that circulated in the business. Paramount advertised the record with a drawing of a woman in a man's jacket courting two women while a policeman watches from across the street — one of the most openly queer pieces of commercial art in 1920s America, and one the label clearly thought would sell.","The jug-band accompaniment is a choice, not a budget. Rainey could command the best jazz players in Chicago; putting a washboard and kazoo behind a lyric this pointed frames it as folk talk rather than a theatrical number, which is exactly what lets it get away with the subject. Listen for how the kazoo mocks the vocal line at the end of each verse.","Ignored for half a century, then recovered by scholars of Black women's blues as one of the earliest first-person queer statements in recorded American music; Angela Davis's work on Rainey and Smith put it at the centre of the argument. Debate continues over how much is autobiography and how much is stage persona, and whether that distinction meant much to Rainey.","Read the lyric before you listen — the diction is old and the pressing is rough. Then listen for her tone, which is amused rather than defiant. Pair it with Weill's Threepenny Opera for another 1920s stage voice that says the unsayable and lets the audience decide what to do with it."],"muddy-waters-i-cant-be-satisfied":["Just two musicians: Muddy Waters singing and playing slide on an electric guitar, and Big Crawford slapping an upright bass behind him. The song is a Delta blues about a restless man ready to leave, but the sound is new. The bottleneck buzzes and rings through the amplifier with a bite no acoustic guitar could give, and the bass pushes the beat forward so the whole record swings like a dance tune rather than a porch lament.","Muddy had been recorded on Stovall Plantation in Mississippi by Alan Lomax and John Work for the Library of Congress in 1941 and 1942, and had moved to Chicago in 1943, driving a truck by day and playing house parties at night. Leonard and Phil Chess, Polish-Jewish immigrants who ran a South Side club, had started the Aristocrat label. The record was cut in 1948 and reportedly sold out its first pressing in Chicago within a day, to migrants who heard home in it.","This is a remake. Muddy had sung a version of the same song for Lomax on Stovall Plantation, played on an acoustic guitar in the old style. Comparing the two is like watching the Great Migration happen in six years: the melody and words barely change, but the electric slide cuts sharper, the tempo quickens, and the bass turns a solitary field song into something a city crowd can move to.","Leonard Chess reportedly doubted the record would sell, since it sounded so rural; its immediate success in Chicago's Black neighbourhoods changed his mind and set the direction of the label. Historians now treat it as the hinge between the Delta and the Chicago blues. What is still argued is how much of the new sound was invention and how much simply the necessity of being heard over a noisy tavern crowd.","Start by listening only to the slide: every sung line is answered by a sliding phrase, as if a second voice were agreeing. Then play Son House's 'My Black Mama' and hear where it came from. Seek a Chess anthology of Muddy's early sides; the 1941 Library of Congress version makes the perfect companion."],"muddy-waters-rollin-stone":["A single voice and a single electric guitar, playing one heavy, repeating riff that barely changes for three minutes. The song imagines the singer as a catfish and ranges over wandering, desire and a restless life. There is no chord change in the usual sense: the guitar hammers the same low figure, lets it ring, and answers each line with a short sting. The effect is closer to a trance than a tune, and the amplified guitar thickens every note.","Recorded in Chicago in 1950 for Chess, the new label the Chess brothers launched that year after Aristocrat, it was among the first records issued under the Chess name. The song is Muddy's version of a widely shared Mississippi piece usually called 'Catfish Blues', recorded by many Delta singers. By 1950 Muddy was already playing with a full band in clubs, but Chess still preferred to record him nearly alone, as on his first hits.","The name travelled further than the song. A young Brian Jones, needing a band name for a gig in London in 1962, is said to have looked at a Muddy Waters record and taken 'Rollin' Stone' from it; the magazine Rolling Stone took its name partly from the same source. Listen, too, for how Muddy slightly delays the riff's return, making the listener lean forward.","A modest regional seller on release, it has grown in reputation as the purest document of Muddy's early electric style before the band took over. Critics note that it is less an original composition than a local standard made definitive. Some later listeners find it monotonous; most hear the monotony as the point, the Mississippi drone that John Lee Hooker and Howlin' Wolf also built on.","Turn it up and let the riff settle into your body before listening to the words. Then hear John Lee Hooker's 'Boogie Chillen'', recorded two years earlier, for a different take on the one-chord trance. The Chess box sets and 'The Anthology' collection carry clean transfers."],"muddy-waters-hoochie-coochie-man":["The full Chicago band at its peak: Muddy singing, Little Walter's amplified harmonica, Otis Spann on piano, Jimmy Rogers on second guitar, Willie Dixon on bass and Fred Below on drums. The record is built on a stop-time riff: the band plays a short, heavy five-note figure, then falls silent, leaving Muddy's voice alone to make another boast about his birth, his charms and his power. Then the band crashes back in for the refrain.","Willie Dixon, Chess's house bassist and songwriter, wrote the song and reportedly taught it to Muddy in a club washroom during an intermission; Muddy tried it that same night and the crowd demanded it again. It was recorded in Chicago in January 1954 and became one of his biggest sellers. The hoodoo imagery, gypsy prophecy, black cat bone, mojo, was drawn from Southern folk magic well known to his migrant audience.","That stop-time riff is the whole architecture of later hard rock: a band hitting together and then leaving a hole. Listen to Little Walter's harmonica fills between the vocal lines; he is not simply decorating but answering, like a second singer. And notice that the song's supernatural swagger is played half as comedy; Muddy smiles through the boasts, and live audiences laughed along.","It made Dixon Chess's key writer and Muddy the undisputed king of the South Side. The riff and the boast format spawned direct answers within a year: Bo Diddley's 'I'm a Man', then Muddy's own 'Mannish Boy'. British bands of the 1960s covered it constantly, and it is now read as a founding text of the rock persona. Some scholars caution against treating its hoodoo as authentic folk religion rather than theatre.","Count the silences: every time the band stops, feel how long Muddy holds the space. Then listen for Otis Spann's piano rippling quietly underneath. Afterwards play Mannish Boy to hear the answer-song. The Chess single remaster in 'The Anthology' is ideal; avoid the thinner reprocessed stereo versions."],"muddy-waters-mannish-boy":["A single stop-time riff repeated for the whole song, with no chord changes at all. Muddy declares, over and over, that he is a grown man, spelling the word out letter by letter while voices in the studio whoop and shout agreement. There is almost no melody; the song is rhythm, voice and attitude, a call-and-response between the singer and a crowd, with harmonica wailing in the gaps.","This was the third move in a Chicago conversation. Willie Dixon's 'Hoochie Coochie Man' (1954) inspired Bo Diddley's 'I'm a Man' (1955) on Chess's Checker label, and Muddy answered Bo within months with this, recorded in May 1955. The spelled-out word echoes the everyday insult of Black men being called 'boy' in the Jim Crow South; the claim to manhood carried a charge beyond sexual boast.","The best-known version is not this one. In 1977 Muddy recut the song for the album Hard Again, produced by the Texas guitarist Johnny Winter, whose excited shouts are all over it; that take was later used in films and advertisements, so many listeners have never heard the tighter 1955 original. Compare the two to hear the same song as a young man's challenge and an old man's victory lap.","Seen at first as a novelty answer record, it has become one of Muddy's defining songs, largely through the 1977 remake and through its use in film. Critics debate whether its power lies in the riff or in the social meaning of the word being spelled out. Its credit, shared between Muddy, Mel London and Bo Diddley, reflects how freely Chicago musicians borrowed from one another.","Listen first for the shouts behind the voice; they turn the record into a sermon with a congregation. Then play the 1977 Hard Again version and hear Muddy in full command at sixty-two. A double bill with Hoochie Coochie Man shows how a riff can be stripped down until only its bones remain."],"muddy-waters-at-newport-1960":["A live set by Muddy's touring band at the Newport Jazz Festival, with Otis Spann on piano and James Cotton on harmonica. The record opens with a fast, driving band sound and peaks with 'Got My Mojo Working', played once, then again at a crowd's insistence, faster and wilder, with Muddy visibly dancing. It closes with Spann singing a blues farewell to the festival. Short, loud, joyful: a club band playing to a lawn of white jazz fans for the first time.","Recorded on 3 July 1960 in Newport, Rhode Island. The festival, a genteel jazz event, had just been shaken by rioting crowds, and the city ended the event early; Muddy's set was among the last. The closing 'Goodbye Newport Blues' had words written on the spot by the poet Langston Hughes. The album came out as the folk revival was starting to look for 'authentic' Black music among college audiences.","'Got My Mojo Working' was not Muddy's song; it had been recorded first in 1956 by the singer Ann Cole, and Muddy reworked it. The second, encore performance is the one to listen to: the band accelerates, Cotton's harmonica duels with Muddy, and you can hear the moment the crowd stops being polite. Spann, whose piano holds the whole set together, is barely credited on the original sleeve.","It was among the first live blues albums and one of the first to reach a mainly white audience, and it circulated in Britain among the young players who would form the Rolling Stones and the Yardbirds. Critics have argued over whether Newport marked Muddy's discovery or his packaging for outsiders. Few argue about the performance itself, which remains the best document of his band on stage.","Go straight to the two versions of 'Got My Mojo Working' back to back and hear what an audience does to a band. Then return to the start and follow Spann's piano rather than the singer. Newer reissues add material and correct the sound; seek the expanded edition if possible."],"muddy-waters-folk-singer":["Muddy unplugs. Nine songs played on acoustic guitar, with a young Buddy Guy on second guitar, Willie Dixon on bass and a whisper of drums. The tempos are slow, the room is quiet, and every detail of the voice and fingers is audible: the rasp of the slide on the strings, the thump of a thumb on the bass notes. It sounds like a return to Mississippi, made with Chicago's precision.","Recorded at Chess studios in September 1963 and released in 1964, at the height of the folk revival, when white college audiences prized acoustic blues as authentic and electric blues as commercial. Chess, never shy of marketing, packaged Muddy accordingly. The engineer Ron Malo captured the small group with unusual clarity, and the record was designed as much for hi-fi listeners as for jukeboxes.","Buddy Guy, who would become one of the loudest guitarists in Chicago, plays here with extraordinary restraint, answering Muddy in soft, precise figures. Listen for the way the two guitars never double each other: one keeps time, the other comments. And listen to the silences between phrases, far longer than any Muddy single; the album is built from space.","Some listeners in the 1960s saw it as a cynical costume change for the folk market, and there is truth in the packaging. But the performances have outlasted the marketing, and the album is now widely regarded as one of the finest acoustic blues records of its decade, prized for recorded sound as much as for singing. It rewards comparison with his 1941 plantation recordings.","Listen in a quiet room, ideally on good speakers or headphones; the sound itself is half the pleasure. Start with the long, slow songs and notice how little happens and how much that little carries. Then play Son House's 1965 'Death Letter Blues' for the Delta original of this bare style."],"howlin-wolf-moanin-at-midnight":["The record opens with Wolf humming a low, wordless moan, deep in his chest, before the band enters on a single droning riff. Willie Johnson's guitar is loud and distorted, the drums thud, and Wolf alternates between singing and blowing raw harmonica. The song, about a restless man woken in the night by trouble, is almost all atmosphere: one chord, a menacing pulse, and a voice like gravel in a bucket.","Recorded in Memphis in 1951 at Sam Phillips's Memphis Recording Service, three years before Phillips recorded Elvis Presley. Phillips leased the masters to Chess in Chicago; meanwhile Ike Turner took Wolf to record for the rival Modern/RPM label, and the two companies fought over him until Chess won and Wolf moved north in 1952. Wolf was then a radio personality in West Memphis, Arkansas, and over forty.","Listen to the guitar tone: Willie Johnson's overdriven sound, probably from a small amplifier pushed past its limit, is one of the earliest heavily distorted guitars on record. Its B-side, 'How Many More Years', gave Led Zeppelin the frame for 'How Many More Times' in 1969. Sam Phillips later said that finding Howlin' Wolf mattered more to him than any other discovery, Elvis included.","It was a hit on the rhythm-and-blues market and launched Wolf's career. Today it is admired as a missing link between the Delta drone of Charley Patton, from whom Wolf learned, and the heavy riffs of the 1960s. Its crudeness, once a reason for dismissal, is now heard as a raw power the more polished Chicago studios would partly tame.","Start with the opening hum; it tells you everything. Then follow the guitar and notice how it growls rather than rings. Play Charley Patton's 'Pony Blues' next to hear where Wolf's voice came from. Collections of the Memphis Sun recordings give the rawest sound."],"howlin-wolf-smokestack-lightnin":["One chord, one circling guitar riff from Hubert Sumlin, and a groove that rocks like a train on a long straight track. Wolf sings loosely connected images of a train at night and a lover gone, and between lines he throws back his head and lets out a falsetto howl, the sound that gave him his name. There is no conventional blues progression at all; the song simply rolls forward until it stops.","Recorded in Chicago in 1956 for Chess, after Wolf had built a steady band there. He had been singing versions of the song since the 1930s in Mississippi, and it grew from the Delta style of Charley Patton and Tommy Johnson, whose falsetto yodel Wolf adapted into his howl. Sumlin, a young guitarist Wolf treated almost as a son, gave the band its angular, unpredictable sound.","The howl is borrowed and transformed: Tommy Johnson's 1920s falsetto and even Jimmie Rodgers's country yodel lie behind it; Wolf said he wanted to yodel like Rodgers but it came out as a howl. Listen to how Sumlin's riff subtly changes each time round, never quite repeating, so the drone never goes dead.","A rhythm-and-blues hit in 1956, it became one of the most covered blues songs of the 1960s rock boom, recorded by the Yardbirds, the Animals and many more. Critics treat it as proof that the modal, one-chord Delta blues could survive in the city. Wolf's own performances remain unrivalled; most covers sound polite beside them.","Listen for the howl and count how many times he uses it; he rations it. Then focus on Sumlin's guitar and hear the small variations. Play a Charley Patton record straight after, then a Yardbirds version, for the full journey. Any good Chess compilation will do."],"howlin-wolf-howlin-wolf-rocking-chair":["A dozen singles from 1959 to 1962, most written by Willie Dixon, gathered on an LP whose cover shows only an acoustic guitar leaning on a rocking chair. It holds 'Spoonful', 'Back Door Man', 'Little Red Rooster', 'Wang Dang Doodle', 'Shake for Me' and 'Down in the Bottom'. The band is tight and heavy, Hubert Sumlin's guitar darts and stabs, and Wolf's voice ranges from a purr to a roar, sometimes in one line.","By the late 1950s Wolf and Muddy Waters were bitter rivals at Chess, and Dixon, who wrote for both, fed Wolf some of his strongest material. Chess assembled the album from singles for the growing LP market in 1962, the year before British bands began to discover these songs. Hubert Sumlin, now mature, had become the most original guitarist in Chicago.","Wolf reportedly disliked several of these Dixon songs, 'Wang Dang Doodle' especially, calling it old-fashioned; you can hear him slightly ironic in the delivery. The one-chord 'Spoonful' is almost entirely riff and voice, the song as ritual. Notice too Sumlin's solo on 'Shake for Me', squeezed and quirky, which young guitarists copied note by note.","The album became a bible for British bands: the Rolling Stones took 'Little Red Rooster' to the top of the British chart in 1964, Cream turned 'Spoonful' into long improvisations, and the Doors recorded 'Back Door Man'. It regularly appears in lists of the greatest albums of any kind. Debate continues over how fairly Dixon and Chess credited and paid the performers who made these songs famous.","Start with 'Spoonful' and let the riff hypnotise you, then play 'Shake for Me' for Sumlin. Afterwards hear Cream's live 'Spoonful' to see how far the rock bands stretched it. Any remaster of the original Chess LP; the mono mixes hit hardest."],"howlin-wolf-killing-floor":["A fast, bouncing riff from Hubert Sumlin, doubled by bass and horns, drives this song about a man who regrets not leaving a bad relationship sooner. Unlike Wolf's hypnotic one-chord pieces, this is taut and quick, almost a soul record in its rhythm, with the band snapping on every accent. Wolf sings with controlled fury, and Sumlin's jagged fills cut across the beat.","Recorded in Chicago in 1964, when soul music was reshaping Black radio and Chess was pushing its blues artists toward sharper, more danceable arrangements. Wolf wrote the song himself. The phrase 'killing floor', meaning the slaughterhouse floor in the Chicago stockyards, was also a Delta figure of speech for rock bottom, used in Skip James's 1931 'Hard Time Killing Floor Blues'.","Jimi Hendrix opened his famous Monterey Pop Festival set in 1967 with this song, played at double speed, and Led Zeppelin reworked it as 'The Lemon Song' in 1969 without credit; a settlement later added Wolf's name. Listen for how Sumlin's riff stays the same while the chords move beneath it, a trick rock bands would lean on for decades.","Less celebrated at first than Wolf's 1950s classics, it gained stature through the rock covers, especially Hendrix's. Critics now hear it as proof that Wolf and Sumlin could modernise without dilution. The Led Zeppelin episode is often cited in the long argument about white rock bands profiting from Black blues writers.","Listen for the riff and try humming along; it is simple and restless. Then hear Hendrix's Monterey version and Zeppelin's 'The Lemon Song' to see how far it travelled. Skip James's 1931 record completes the lineage of the phrase."],"willie-dixon-i-am-the-blues":["The songwriter sings his own songbook. Willie Dixon, with a small Chicago band, performs nine of the songs he wrote for others: 'Back Door Man', 'I Can't Quit You Baby', 'The Seventh Son', 'Spoonful', 'I Ain't Superstitious', 'You Shook Me', 'Hoochie Coochie Man', 'The Little Red Rooster' and 'The Same Thing'. His voice is warm, rounded and knowing, the voice of a storyteller more than a shouter, and the arrangements are relaxed.","By 1970 the Chess era was ending and Dixon's songs were earning money for British and American rock bands: Led Zeppelin had recorded 'You Shook Me' and 'I Can't Quit You Baby' on their 1969 debut. Columbia, a major label, invited Dixon to record his own versions. He was also touring Europe with the Chicago Blues All-Stars and beginning his long campaign for songwriters' rights.","The album's title is a claim of authorship in a business that had often denied it. Listen to how Dixon sings 'Hoochie Coochie Man' not as a boast but almost as a smiling folk tale; the performer's swagger was always a role he wrote for someone else. His delivery reveals the gospel quartet training of his youth in every phrase.","Dixon was never regarded as a great singer, and some critics find these versions pale beside Muddy's and Wolf's. But the album is valued as a composer's statement, and its title has become shorthand for his place in history. In 1985 Dixon settled with Led Zeppelin over 'Whole Lotta Love', which borrowed from his 'You Need Love'; the album sits at the start of that reckoning.","Play each song beside the famous version: Muddy for 'Hoochie Coochie Man', Wolf for 'Spoonful' and 'Back Door Man', Otis Rush for 'I Can't Quit You Baby'. The contrast shows what a writer hears in his own song. Start with 'The Seventh Son'."],"little-walter-juke":["An instrumental for amplified harmonica and Muddy Waters's band, fast and swinging. Walter plays the harmonica like a jazz saxophone: riffing, bending notes, sliding into long, fat, distorted tones that seem too big for such a small instrument. The guitars chop a boogie rhythm underneath, and the drums shuffle. There is no singing and no need for it; the harmonica does all the talking.","Recorded in Chicago in May 1952 at the end of a Muddy Waters session, using Muddy's band, it was issued on Chess's new Checker label and went to the top of the rhythm-and-blues chart, a rare feat for a harmonica instrumental. Walter left Muddy's band almost at once to lead his own group. The record was a theme he had used to open the band's club sets.","The sound is the invention. Walter cupped the harmonica tightly against a cheap microphone meant for public-address systems and ran it into a guitar amplifier, pushing it into distortion; the cupping lets him shape the tone like a trumpet mute. Listen to the long held notes near the end, bent and swelling, which no acoustic harmonica could sustain.","'Juke' made Walter a star and changed the harmonica for good; almost every later blues and rock harmonica player, from James Cotton to Paul Butterfield, followed his amplified route. Critics sometimes rank him among the most original instrumentalists in any postwar American music, jazz included. The record's success also showed Chess that instrumentals and side musicians could sell.","Listen once for the harmonica tone alone; it sounds less like a mouth organ than a horn section. Then notice how Walter phrases in short riffs like a swing saxophonist. Follow with 'My Babe' for his singing, and with Junior Wells for the next generation."],"little-walter-my-babe":["A light, bouncing song about a woman who will not stand for any nonsense, sung by Little Walter over a stop-and-go band groove, with his harmonica answering each line. It is short, cheerful and catchy, closer to rhythm and blues than to the heavy Chicago sound, and it shows Walter as a relaxed, likable singer as well as a harmonica innovator.","Willie Dixon wrote it, and it gave Walter a number-one rhythm-and-blues hit in 1955. The tune is taken from the gospel song 'This Train (Is Bound for Glory)', which Sister Rosetta Tharpe had recorded, and turning sacred songs into secular ones was standard practice in the 1950s, as Ray Charles was doing at the same moment.","Once you know the gospel original, you cannot unhear it: the melody of the sacred train song has simply been given new words. Listen for the stop-time breaks, where the band cuts out and Walter's voice or harmonica fills the silence, the same device Dixon used in 'Hoochie Coochie Man'. Walter reportedly resisted recording it; Dixon persisted.","It was Walter's biggest vocal hit and was widely covered, by Ricky Nelson, Elvis Presley in concert and many British groups. Blues purists sometimes rank it below his instrumentals as too pop. It is now valued as an example of the sacred-to-secular exchange at the root of rhythm and blues and soul.","Listen to Sister Rosetta Tharpe's 'This Train' first, then this. Then notice how Walter's harmonica acts as a second vocal line. Pair with 'Juke' to hear both sides of his talent."],"elmore-james-dust-my-broom":["It opens with the most famous slide-guitar figure in the blues: a rolling, triplet run up the neck, hammered out on an electric guitar at full volume. Elmore James sings about leaving a lover and heading out at dawn, his voice strained and urgent, and repeats the slide riff at the start of each verse. The harmonica of Sonny Boy Williamson II wails behind him. The whole record sounds like it is about to boil over.","Recorded in August 1951 in Jackson, Mississippi, for Trumpet Records, a small label run by Lillian McMurry. James had long played Robert Johnson's 1936 'I Believe I'll Dust My Broom' in juke joints. It became a hit in 1952, and he soon moved to Chicago, recording the riff in many variants for different labels for the rest of his career.","The riff is Robert Johnson's, but the sound is new. Elmore, a radio repairman, modified his amplifiers and pickups to make them louder and dirtier, and the resulting distortion is part of the composition. Listen for the pounding rhythm on beat four, and for how the slide notes buzz and break up.","The record made James a star in the blues market and fixed the riff for generations: Brian Jones, Duane Allman and Jeremy Spencer of Fleetwood Mac all learned it; Spencer built a stage persona around it. Some critics find James repetitive, since he rerecorded the figure so often. Most hear it as a signature as distinct as any in music.","Play Robert Johnson's 1936 version first, then this; hear an acoustic idea turn electric. Next, listen to the slide opening again and notice how much volume and grit change its emotional meaning. Compilations of his Trumpet and Chicago sides are easy to find."],"elmore-james-the-sky-is-crying":["A slow blues in which rain streaming down a street becomes the image of a broken heart. Elmore James's slide guitar weeps rather than rolls here, with long, bent notes swelling behind the voice, and his singing is at its most anguished. A saxophone section breathes softly beneath. It is short and simple, and it proves he was far more than a one-riff player.","Recorded in Chicago in November 1959 for Bobby Robinson's Fire label, based in New York, and released in 1960. James was in failing health from heart disease and moving between Chicago, Mississippi and New York sessions. The song reportedly came from an actual downpour during the session. He died in 1963, aged forty-five.","The slide tone here is warmer and more vocal than in 'Dust My Broom'; listen for how he drags the slide slowly into each note rather than striking it. The simple words carry real grief when you know how ill James was when he sang them. The song's structure is the standard twelve-bar blues, used with no decoration at all.","It was a rhythm-and-blues hit and has become a standard, recorded by Albert King, Stevie Ray Vaughan, George Harrison and Eric Clapton among others. Critics regard it as James's finest slow blues. Discussions of his late work often mourn how little recording time he had with good bands before his early death.","Listen for the first slide phrase and the way it seems to weep. Then hear Stevie Ray Vaughan's version for a rock guitarist's approach. Play it after 'Dust My Broom' to hear both sides of Elmore: the whirlwind and the storm cloud."],"bb-king-three-oclock-blues":["A slow blues about a sleepless man at three in the morning, wondering where his woman has gone. B.B. King's guitar opens with a short, clear, single-note phrase and answers each vocal line with another: not chords but melody, sung on the strings. A small horn section and piano provide a soft bed. His voice is young and pleading, full of gospel inflection.","The song was written and first recorded by the West Coast bluesman Lowell Fulson in 1948. King, then a disc jockey on WDIA, the Memphis radio station programmed for a Black audience, recorded it in Memphis in 1951 for the Bihari brothers' RPM label, reportedly in a makeshift studio at a YMCA. It went to the top of the rhythm-and-blues chart in early 1952 and launched his touring career.","Listen for King's left-hand vibrato, still a little shy here: the note trembles as he shakes the string. It was his attempt to imitate the slide-guitar sound of his cousin Bukka White, which he could never master, so he made his fingers do what the slide did. That substitution became the most imitated sound in blues guitar.","It made King a national figure in the rhythm-and-blues market; for the next decade he toured hundreds of nights a year. Critics now see it as the foundation of his style: single-string, vocal, sparing. The debt to T-Bone Walker and to jazz guitar is widely acknowledged, as is the Delta influence through Bukka White.","Listen to how few notes King plays and how long he lets each hang. Then hear Lowell Fulson's original for contrast. Move on to Live at the Regal to hear what this young man became."],"bb-king-live-at-the-regal":["B.B. King and his touring band on a single night before a Black Chicago audience. The set moves without pause from fast shuffles to slow blues, King's guitar Lucille darting between vocal lines, the horns punching accents, and the crowd shrieking, laughing and answering throughout. Songs include 'Every Day I Have the Blues', 'Sweet Little Angel', 'It's My Own Fault' and 'How Blue Can You Get?', whose punchline sets off the loudest roar on the record.","Recorded on 21 November 1964 at the Regal Theater on Chicago's South Side, a grand venue on the Black entertainment circuit, and released by ABC in 1965. King was then playing chitlin' circuit dates almost nightly for Black audiences and had not yet been embraced by white rock fans. The album caught him in his natural element, before the Fillmore and Las Vegas years.","Listen to the audience as an instrument. King times his pauses to their reactions, holding a guitar phrase until the screaming peaks, then sliding into the next line. On 'How Blue Can You Get?' he builds a list of grievances to a comic final line, and the band holds a silence just long enough for it to land. It is showmanship as precise as any stand-up comedian's.","Sales were modest at first, but guitarists passed it around: Eric Clapton, Mike Bloomfield and others have named it essential study. It is now routinely listed among the greatest live albums in any genre. Some critics prefer the rougher 1971 Live in Cook County Jail, but Regal remains the reference for King at his peak.","Listen straight through, as a concert, with the volume up. Pay attention to how short King's guitar solos are and how the crowd responds to single notes. Then play Muddy at Newport for a contrasting live audience."],"bb-king-the-thrill-is-gone":["A slow minor-key blues about the calm, final end of a love affair, set not in a nightclub but in a cool, spacious studio sound with a string section. King's guitar is at its most expressive: short phrases, long bends, that trembling vibrato. The strings enter quietly and swell. It is the most elegant record of his career, as close to pop balladry as to the blues.","Written and first recorded in 1951 by Roy Hawkins and Rick Darnell, the song was recut by King in 1969 for the album Completely Well, produced by Bill Szymczyk, with strings arranged by Bert de Coteaux. King was now crossing to white rock audiences through Fillmore concerts, and the record crossed to the pop charts and won a Grammy.","The strings were added after the band was recorded, and King reportedly did not expect them. Listen to how they enter only in the later part, doubling the melancholy rather than sweetening it. The minor key is unusual for King; it gives the song a coolness quite unlike his usual gospel warmth.","It became King's signature song and his biggest crossover hit. Purists grumbled about the strings, but the record brought him to a mass audience that sustained him for four decades. It is now considered among the finest blues recordings of the rock era, and a model of how orchestral colour can serve the blues.","Listen for the moment the strings come in; notice how they change the song's colour without changing its shape. Then listen to the original 1951 Roy Hawkins version. The album cut, over five minutes, is preferable to the edited single."],"albert-king-born-under-a-bad-sign":["Albert King's guitar over the Stax house band: Booker T. and the MG's with the Memphis Horns. The title song rides a sinuous bass-and-horns riff, and King tells of a lifetime of bad luck. His guitar enters with enormous bends, a note pushed up until it screams, and his soft voice contrasts with the ferocity of his playing. The album also includes 'Crosscut Saw', 'The Hunter', 'Personal Manager' and 'As the Years Go Passing By'.","Recorded at Stax in Memphis in 1966 and 1967 and assembled as an album in 1967. Stax, an integrated soul label, set King's blues over tight Southern soul arrangements, a new sound for the blues. The title song was written by Booker T. Jones and William Bell. King was then in his forties, a heavy-set guitarist who played a Flying V left-handed, upside down.","King's strings were set for a right-handed player, but he held the guitar left-handed, so his thinnest string sat on top. This lets him pull strings down rather than push up, bending them farther than anyone else. Listen for bends that go past the note you expect. Eric Clapton's solo in Cream's 'Strange Brew' (1967) copies King's phrasing almost note for note.","Cream recorded 'Born Under a Bad Sign' in 1968, and the album spread through rock guitar circles. It is now regarded as one of the most important electric blues albums, and as a model of blues meeting soul. Critics note that the arrangement's tightness, sometimes seen as confining, is what gives King's big bends their shock.","Start with the title song and focus on the first guitar bend. Then hear Cream's version, and then Clapton's 'Strange Brew' solo, to recognise King's phrases in rock. Follow with Stevie Ray Vaughan to hear the next generation."],"albert-king-live-wire-blues-power":["Albert King in concert, stretching out. Long instrumentals and slow blues give his guitar space to develop ideas across many choruses. On the title piece he talks to the audience between phrases, explaining what the blues is and why it matters, and the guitar answers. The band is smaller and looser than on the Stax studio records, and the atmosphere is intimate despite the volume.","Recorded in June 1968 at the Fillmore Auditorium in San Francisco, booked by the promoter Bill Graham, who put blues artists on bills alongside psychedelic rock bands. The audience was largely young and white; King was introduced to them as a master, and he responded with extended solos suited to the rock-concert setting. Released by Stax later that year.","Listen to how King builds a solo in the long instrumentals: he repeats a short phrase, bending it a little further each time, until tension becomes almost unbearable. Rock guitarists of the Fillmore era learned that approach here. Also notice his quiet talking between phrases; he is teaching the room.","A strong seller for Stax, it confirmed King's standing with rock audiences. Some critics find the long solos indulgent compared with his tight studio sides, while guitar players tend to prize them for exactly that reason. It is now seen as a key document of the moment blues met psychedelia on its own terms.","Begin with 'Blues Power' and follow the spoken asides as well as the guitar. Then try one of the long instrumentals and count how many times a phrase is repeated before it changes. Compare with B.B. King's Live at the Regal for a very different audience."],"freddie-king-hide-away":["A bright, bouncing guitar instrumental in a shuffle rhythm, built from several catchy sections strung together like a medley: a stop-time opening, a walking-bass boogie, and snatches of other tunes. Freddie King's guitar rings hard and clear, attacked with picks on thumb and finger. It is short, jaunty and made to dance to, with not a vocal in sight.","Recorded in Cincinnati in 1960 for Federal, part of Syd Nathan's King Records, and released in 1961. King adapted it from a tune played by the Chicago slide guitarist Hound Dog Taylor and named it after Mel's Hide Away Lounge on the West Side. Instrumentals were in vogue, and the record crossed over to the pop chart.","Listen for the quoted snippets: part of the tune borrows the riff of Henry Mancini's television theme 'Peter Gunn', turned into a blues. The stop-time opening became a test piece for young guitarists; Eric Clapton recorded it as 'Hideaway' with John Mayall's Bluesbreakers in 1966, his guitar sound on that album widely regarded as a turning point for British rock guitar.","A hit in its day, it became a standard of every bar band and blues jam. Critics see it as the start of a line of blues-rock guitar instrumentals, and as proof that King deserves a place beside B.B. and Albert King as one of the 'three Kings' of electric blues guitar. It is sometimes dismissed as a novelty, but guitarists never have.","Listen for each new section and try to hear where one tune ends and the next begins. Then play Clapton's 1966 version with Mayall to hear how a British player absorbed it. Follow with 'Have You Ever Loved a Woman' for King's singing."],"freddie-king-have-you-ever-loved-a-woman":["A slow blues about loving a woman who belongs to one's best friend, sung with gospel force. Freddie King's voice rises and breaks, and his guitar fills each space with stinging, ringing notes. The arrangement is spare: piano, bass and drums hold the slow pulse, and the guitar provides all the drama. It is short and intense, with an emotional climax that arrives far sooner than a listener expects.","Written by Billy Myles and recorded in 1960 for Federal in Cincinnati, it was among King's first singles. He was then working in Chicago clubs, where Chess had declined to sign him, reportedly finding his guitar too close to B.B. King's. Federal gave him the chance to record both instrumentals and vocal blues.","The song's situation, loving your best friend's wife, is exactly the plot Eric Clapton was living in 1970, and he recorded it with Derek and the Dominos on the album Layla, alongside songs written for Pattie Boyd. Knowing that, listen to how King's version feels less like confession and more like story; the personal meaning was added later.","Less famous than 'Hide Away', it is now considered one of King's finest vocal performances. Clapton's recording kept it alive, and critics note it as an example of British rockers transforming a Chicago blues into autobiography. King's own later live versions, stretched out and louder, are also prized.","Listen first for the singing: the break in King's voice is as powerful as any guitar. Then play Clapton's version on Layla for a rock treatment. King's live versions from the 1970s show how he himself grew the song."],"buddy-guy-first-time-i-met-the-blues":["A slow blues in which the blues itself is personified as a figure who comes walking through the woods toward the singer. Buddy Guy's voice rises to a piercing scream, and his guitar snaps and stings between lines, loud and nervy, with notes that jump out of the mix. The band plays quietly behind him, making his outbursts even more startling.","The song was written by the pianist Little Brother Montgomery and recorded by him in 1936. Guy, newly arrived in Chicago from Louisiana, cut it for Chess in 1960, early in a period when the label used him mostly as a session guitarist. The West Side of Chicago was then producing a younger, more aggressive blues style than Muddy Waters's South Side sound.","Listen to the extreme dynamics: Guy shifts from a whisper to a shriek with no warning, both in voice and guitar. This was his stage style too, which Chess found hard to capture. Hendrix, who saw Guy play in the 1960s, adopted much of that theatricality. The personification of the blues as a stranger walking up is old Delta imagery, from Montgomery's generation.","It sold modestly but became a touchstone among guitarists. Critics now see it as an early glimpse of the intensity that British and American rock players would copy. Guy has said that Chess wanted him to play quietly; this record shows what happened when he did not.","Turn it up and listen for the sudden scream in the voice and the answering sting of the guitar. Then play Little Brother Montgomery's 1936 version for the piano original. Guy's later live versions go even further."],"buddy-guy-damn-right-ive-got-the-blues":["A big, polished blues-rock album, with Buddy Guy's guitar and voice front and centre and guest appearances by Eric Clapton, Jeff Beck and Mark Knopfler. The title song is a declaration of identity; other tracks range from slow blues to rock-edged shuffles. Guy's guitar is louder and wilder than ever, full of screaming bends and sudden leaps, and his voice has aged into a rough, commanding instrument.","By the late 1980s Guy was running his own club in Chicago but had not made a major-label album in years. The British label Silvertone signed him, and producer John Porter built the album around his reputation among rock guitarists. It won a Grammy and began a late-career revival that lasted three decades, making Guy the public face of the Chicago blues after Muddy's death.","Listen to how the guests play differently from Guy: Clapton cleaner and more controlled, Beck more abstract, Knopfler cool and precise. Guy is the one who takes the most risks. The album's opening track, the title song, quotes the attitude of his early Chess records, but now he is the elder, not the upstart.","Commercially his biggest success, it was welcomed as long-overdue recognition. Some blues critics found the rock production too glossy; others argued it was the only way to reach a new audience. Guy's revival is now credited with keeping Chicago blues visible into the twenty-first century.","Start with the title track, then play one of the guest duets and listen to how guitarists of different generations speak. Then return to 'First Time I Met the Blues' from 1960 to hear the same voice three decades younger."],"junior-wells-hoodoo-man-blues":["Junior Wells's quartet, with Buddy Guy on guitar, playing the kind of set they played nightly in Chicago clubs. Harmonica and voice lead; Guy's guitar is supple and inventive; bass and drums groove with a loose, funky feel influenced by James Brown. Songs include 'Snatch It Back and Hold It', 'Hoodoo Man Blues' and 'Good Morning Little Schoolgirl'. The sound is warm and immediate, as if you were at a table near the stage.","Recorded in September 1965 for Delmark, a small Chicago label run by Bob Koester that aimed at blues and jazz fans rather than jukeboxes. It was among the first Chicago blues albums made as albums, capturing a working club band rather than assembling singles. Guy, under contract to Chess, was credited under a pseudonym, 'Friendly Chap'.","Listen to Guy's guitar playing rhythm figures that sound more like soul music than blues, a sign of the times. Also notice how Wells's harmonica often sounds amplified only faintly: the album's engineers aimed for the room sound rather than the hard distortion of Little Walter's records. The pseudonym on the sleeve fooled nobody.","It became Delmark's best-selling album and a model for later blues records, and critics regard it as one of the finest Chicago albums of its decade. Its informal, club-set approach influenced how blues would be recorded for the new album-buying audience.","Start with 'Snatch It Back and Hold It' for the groove, then follow Guy's guitar through a slow number. Compare with Little Walter's 'Juke' to hear how the harmonica's role shifted in thirteen years."],"etta-james-at-last":["Etta James's first album mixes lush, string-backed versions of pop standards with blues and rhythm-and-blues numbers. The title song, a 1940s ballad, floats on sweeping strings while her voice moves from husky warmth to a controlled cry. Other tracks include 'Stormy Weather', 'A Sunday Kind of Love', Willie Dixon's 'I Just Want to Make Love to You' and the blues ballad 'All I Could Do Was Cry'. The contrasts are the point: a blues singer in evening dress.","Leonard Chess signed James in 1960 and placed her on the Argo label, pairing her with the arranger Riley Hampton's string charts to reach both Black and white audiences. The title song, by Harry Warren and Mack Gordon, came from the 1942 film Orchestra Wives. The album was released in late 1960 and yielded several singles.","Listen to how James phrases the ballads like a blues singer: she lags behind the beat, bends notes and adds small growls, even over the most polished strings. On the Dixon song, she transforms a swaggering male boast into a woman's frank declaration, changing its meaning without changing a word.","Received as a strong debut, it gained status over the decades, and the title song became a wedding standard and her signature. Critics argue about whether the strings sweeten her too much; most now hear the tension between the raw voice and the smooth arrangement as the record's secret.","Start with the title song, then jump to the Dixon number for a completely different Etta. Listen to how the voice cuts through the strings. Then play 'I'd Rather Go Blind' to hear where she went next."],"etta-james-id-rather-go-blind":["A slow Southern soul ballad about watching a lover walk away with someone else. The band plays a gentle, rocking six-beat feel with warm organ and a horn section; the guitar picks out quiet phrases. Etta James sings with extraordinary restraint at first, building to a raw, pleading climax. It is soul in form and blues in feeling.","Recorded in 1967 at FAME Studios in Muscle Shoals, Alabama, with Rick Hall producing and the studio's house musicians. It appeared as the B-side of 'Tell Mama' on Chess's Cadet label. Credited to Ellington Jordan and Billy Foster, it was, James later said, partly her own work, but she gave her share of credit to Foster.","Listen for how little James does in the first verse: almost no vibrato, very soft. Then notice the moment near the end where she lets the voice crack wide open. The Muscle Shoals rhythm section plays almost nothing, which leaves her space. The song was a B-side; the A-side was the hit, but the B-side outlived it.","It became one of the most admired soul ballads of the 1960s, covered by Chicken Shack with Christine Perfect (later McVie) in 1969, by Rod Stewart and many others. Critics consider it one of James's finest performances. The authorship story has become part of the song's legend.","Listen for the build from whisper to cry. Then hear Chicken Shack's British version from 1969. Compare with 'At Last' to hear James without strings, standing on a soul band's bare floor."],"koko-taylor-wang-dang-doodle":["A rowdy party song listing a crowd of colourful characters invited to an all-night gathering. Koko Taylor sings with a rough, powerful growl, pushing the band. The groove is a steady stomp; harmonica and guitar punctuate each line. It is loud, funny and irresistible, with Taylor's voice commanding it all.","Willie Dixon wrote it and first had Howlin' Wolf record it in 1960; Wolf reportedly disliked it. Dixon, producing Taylor at Chess in 1966, gave it to her, and it became a rhythm-and-blues hit and her signature. Taylor had been cleaning houses and singing in South Side clubs. Few women had led a hard Chicago band on record.","The characters named in the song are drawn from old Southern party lore, and Dixon reportedly based the idea on a traditional song about a wild all-night gathering. Listen to how Taylor's version turns a mild male performance into a commanding female one; she sounds like the host, not a guest.","It made Taylor the leading female voice of Chicago blues, a title she kept for four decades. Critics see it as a key document of women claiming the loudest blues style. Some compare it with Wolf's version and find hers more convincing, which rarely happens with Wolf covers.","Play Howlin' Wolf's 1960 version first, from the 'Rocking Chair' album, then Taylor's 1966 Checker single, and listen to how she takes command where Wolf sounds half-amused. Notice the harmonica jabbing between her lines and the band's steady, stomping beat. Then move to her Alligator Records albums of the 1970s and 1980s, where the same growl leads her own touring band at full power."],"john-lee-hooker-boogie-chillen":["Just John Lee Hooker, an amplified guitar and his stomping foot. The guitar plays a single, relentless boogie figure on one chord, and Hooker half-talks, half-sings about arriving in Detroit, walking down Hastings Street and hearing the music in the clubs. The rhythm is loose and irregular, bars stretched and shortened as he pleases, yet the groove never loses its grip.","Recorded in Detroit in 1948 by the producer Bernard Besman and released by Modern Records in Los Angeles, it became a major rhythm-and-blues hit. Hooker had come north from Mississippi, working in the car factories. Hastings Street was the heart of Detroit's Black entertainment district, later demolished for a freeway.","The boogie figure comes from Hooker's stepfather, Will Moore, a Mississippi guitarist; Hooker said he learned the style from him. Listen to the foot: it is recorded almost as loud as the guitar, part drum, part percussion, and it holds the time more steadily than the guitar does. The song's irregular bars would baffle any band trying to follow him.","It made Hooker a star and inspired countless imitations. Canned Heat and ZZ Top built songs on its groove. Critics now see it as a root of rock and roll's riff-based style, and as proof that Delta modal blues could flourish in the industrial North.","Listen to the foot first; then the guitar; then the voice. Notice how each is slightly independent. Then play Muddy's 'Rollin' Stone' for a Chicago counterpart, and a Canned Heat boogie for the rock inheritance."],"john-lee-hooker-boom-boom":["A tight, danceable stop-time song in which Hooker says he wants to take someone home. The band hits short accents together, then stops, leaving Hooker's voice and guitar alone. There is a sharp, bright horn section, a steady beat and a catchy hook. It is one of Hooker's most structured recordings, closer to rhythm and blues than to his solo boogies.","Recorded in Chicago in 1961 and released by Vee-Jay in 1962. Vee-Jay was a Black-owned Chicago label, home to Jimmy Reed, and soon to issue the Beatles' first American records. Hooker had spent the 1950s recording solo or with small groups for many labels; here he fronted a full band with horns, reportedly including members of Motown's house band, the Funk Brothers. The single crossed over to pop radio and reached white teenage listeners at the start of the blues revival.","Listen for the stop-time breaks and how Hooker times his lines against the silences. Also notice that the song stays in regular bars, unlike his earlier records; the band clearly insisted. The British group the Animals recorded it in 1964, and their version, with its heavy organ, introduced it to many listeners.","It became one of Hooker's best-known songs and has been used in film and advertising. Critics sometimes find it lightweight beside his solo boogies, but it shows how he could adapt to the band format. Its crossover success helped establish him with British bands, who toured and recorded with him.","Listen first to the stop-time structure and count the gaps where the band falls silent and Hooker's guitar answers alone. Then play the Animals' 1964 version, with Alan Price's organ, to hear how a British band heard it. Finally go back to 'Boogie Chillen'' and notice how differently Hooker breathes when a disciplined band holds him to regular bars. The original Vee-Jay mono single is the version to seek."],"spiritual-go-down-moses":["A slow, heavy call to Pharaoh: the leader tells the story of Moses in Egypt, and every verse closes on the command ‘Let my people go’, sung by all. The tune stays low and narrow, circling a minor-sounding scale, and rises only on that refrain, so the demand lands like a blow. There is no chorus in the hymn-book sense — just the same four words hammered home verse after verse, often twenty verses in a traditional telling.","It first reached print in 1861 as ‘The Song of the Contrabands’, taken down by the chaplain Lewis Lockwood from escaped slaves sheltering behind Union lines at Fort Monroe, Virginia — the first spiritual published with its music. For enslaved people the Exodus was not an allegory at a distance: Egypt was the South, Pharaoh the master, and Harriet Tubman was called ‘Moses’ by those she led north. The Fisk Jubilee Singers made it a concert staple after 1871.","The earliest sheet music dressed it in a genteel piano arrangement with a major-key feel; the minor, modal colour everyone now hears came later, from singers rather than editors. Listen to how the refrain falls a whole step at its end rather than resolving upward — a cadence closer to a field holler than a hymn. That falling close is also why the tune sits so naturally in a blues singer's mouth.","White collectors in the 1860s debated whether songs like this were ‘music’ at all or crude imitations of Methodist hymns; Black scholars from W. E. B. Du Bois, who called the spirituals ‘sorrow songs’, onward answered by treating them as the nation's great folk art. Paul Robeson made it his signature in the 1920s and 30s, and Louis Armstrong's 1958 recording with a choir brought it to jazz audiences.","Start with Paul Robeson's bass, where the refrain sounds like an order rather than a plea; then hear Tippett's choral setting in A Child of Our Time, which keeps the tune plain inside modern harmony. Notice how few notes the melody uses — the power is in the repetition. Then hear Armstrong's version, which swings it without softening it."],"spiritual-steal-away":["The quietest of the great spirituals. A gentle refrain — ‘Steal away, steal away, steal away to Jesus’ — alternates with verses in which ‘my Lord calls me, he calls me by the thunder’ and ‘the trumpet sounds within my soul’. The melody moves in small steps and long held notes and is almost always sung softly, as if overheard. It lasts barely three minutes, but its hush makes it one of the most recognisable songs of the American nineteenth century.","It appeared in the first songbook published for the Fisk Jubilee Singers in 1872 and was one of their most requested pieces on tours of the North and of Britain. A tradition, impossible to prove, links it to Nat Turner's revolt of 1831 or to secret gatherings in the woods; another credits it, with ‘Swing Low’, to Wallace Willis, a freedman in the Choctaw Nation of Indian Territory.","The double meaning is the point: ‘steal away’ is both to die and go to God and to slip out of the quarters to a hidden prayer meeting, or north. Listen for the thunder and trumpet in the verses — images of the Last Judgment that are, sung softly, a promise of rescue rather than a threat. The contrast between loud words and a whispered tune is the song's secret.","Nineteenth-century listeners praised its ‘plaintive’ tenderness and missed its irony; twentieth-century scholars, from Sterling Brown to Lawrence Levine, read it as a key text for the coded meanings of the spirituals, while more cautious historians warn that most ‘escape code’ stories were attached later. Tippett's choice of it to end Part One of A Child of Our Time made it known to European concert audiences.","Hear it first sung very quietly by a small ensemble or a single voice, and notice how the refrain's third ‘steal away’ reaches upward like a door opening. Then compare Tippett's version, where a soprano floats over the choir. Mahalia Jackson's slow reading shows how a gospel singer stretches it."],"spiritual-nobody-knows-the-trouble-ive-seen":["A lament that turns on itself: the refrain sings ‘Nobody knows the trouble I've seen, nobody knows but Jesus’, then ends — astonishingly — ‘Glory, hallelujah!’ The tune is built on a five-note scale, rocking gently in a slow walking rhythm, and the verses (‘Sometimes I'm up, sometimes I'm down’) are short and personal. It is the spiritual as a private confession, and the sudden hallelujah is the moment a first-time listener remembers.","A version, ‘Nobody Knows the Trouble I've Had’, was printed in Slave Songs of the United States (1867), the first collection of the genre, gathered by Northern teachers among freedpeople on the Sea Islands of South Carolina during the Civil War. The Fisk Jubilee Singers carried it to Europe, and by the early twentieth century it was sung in recital, in vaudeville and by church choirs across the country.","The ‘Glory, hallelujah’ at the end of a song about suffering is not a happy ending tacked on: it is the characteristic double motion of the spirituals, grief and praise in one breath. Listen for the five-note scale — the same pentatonic shape Dvořák heard in these songs and used in his ‘American’ Quartet and New World Symphony.","It became one of the most arranged spirituals, sometimes smothered in sentimental harmonies. Marian Anderson's plain recordings restored its dignity; Louis Armstrong's 1938 recording with a choir and his later versions made it a jazz standard. Tippett placed it at the heart of A Child of Our Time, and it remains a test of whether a singer can keep it simple.","Listen for the turn to ‘Glory, hallelujah’ and ask whether the singer makes it triumphant or weary — the best versions make it both. Marian Anderson's recording is the classic; Armstrong's shows the melody's swing. Then hear Tippett's choral setting and notice how he leaves the tune almost untouched."],"spiritual-sometimes-i-feel-like-a-motherless-child":["Three lines and a sigh: ‘Sometimes I feel like a motherless child, a long ways from home’, repeated, then ‘a long ways from home’ once more, falling. Later verses — ‘Sometimes I feel like I'm almost gone’ — sink further. The melody is modal and bare, with long notes on ‘motherless’ and ‘home’ that invite the singer to bend and moan. It is the darkest of the well-known spirituals, and it offers no hallelujah.","Its image comes straight from slavery, in which children were routinely sold away from their mothers. The Fisk Jubilee Singers sang it on their tours in the 1870s, and it entered published collections in the following decades. It became a recital piece in Harry T. Burleigh's generation, a protest song in the civil-rights years, and a staple of folk revival singers such as Odetta.","Many versions end with ‘true believer’ or a turn to heaven, but the oldest-sounding performances simply stop on ‘a long ways from home’, unresolved. Richie Havens's opening set at Woodstock in 1969 grew, famously improvised, into ‘Freedom’ on top of this tune — a whole generation heard the spiritual there without knowing its name.","Du Bois singled out the ‘sorrow songs’ as the truest American music, and this is the text most often quoted to show why. Some twentieth-century arrangers rendered it lush and slow; singers from Paul Robeson to Mahalia Jackson and Odetta preferred it stark. It is now one of the spirituals most often heard in films and at memorials.","Seek an unaccompanied or near-unaccompanied version first — Paul Robeson, Odetta or a folk singer — and listen for the moan on ‘long’. Then hear Richie Havens's ‘Freedom’ to hear it turned into a frantic guitar chant. Compare with ‘Nobody Knows’, which escapes into praise; this one does not."],"spiritual-were-you-there":["A Passion meditation in four questions: ‘Were you there when they crucified my Lord? … when they nailed him to the tree? … when they pierced him in the side? … when they laid him in the tomb?’ Each verse halts on a drawn-out ‘Oh!’ followed by ‘Sometimes it causes me to tremble, tremble, tremble.’ The trembling is sung — the word is stretched and shaken on repeated notes. The song asks the listener to stand at the cross as a witness.","Sung in the nineteenth century, it reached print in 1899 in a collection of plantation hymns, and in 1940 it entered the hymnal of the Episcopal Church, often said to be the first spiritual in a major white denominational hymnal. It is now sung in Good Friday services across denominations worldwide and was a favourite of Marian Anderson and Johnny Cash.","The question ‘Were you there?’ collapses time: the enslaved singer claims to have been present at the crucifixion, identifying her suffering with Christ's. Listen for the long ‘Oh!’ that breaks each verse — a written-down trace of the congregational moan, the wordless groan of the Black church that later reappears in blues and gospel singing.","Its adoption by white churches has been praised as recognition and questioned as appropriation that softened its meaning; recent scholarship reads it alongside lynching, when the ‘tree’ was not metaphorical. Musically it is admired for its structure — question, cry, tremble — as much as for its melody.","Hear Marian Anderson's recording, then a congregation singing it in a Good Friday service, and notice how the ‘tremble’ is made physical. Compare it with Bach's St Matthew Passion, whose chorales also make the listener a witness to the crucifixion, by very different means."],"spiritual-wade-in-the-water":["A baptism song with a rolling, insistent beat: ‘Wade in the water, wade in the water, children, God's gonna trouble the water.’ The verses call up the Red Sea and the pool of Bethesda, where an angel ‘troubled’ the water and the first to step in was healed. The tune is minor-tinged and repetitive, made for moving bodies — a procession down to the river — and it swings naturally.","It was printed in 1901 in New Jubilee Songs, edited at Fisk University by John Wesley Work II and his brother Frederick. Its setting is the outdoor river baptism of the rural South. In the twentieth century it became a gospel quartet number, a civil-rights anthem, and the centrepiece of Alvin Ailey's dance Revelations (1960).","A popular story makes it Harriet Tubman's coded instruction to escaping slaves to take to the water and lose the dogs; historians find no nineteenth-century evidence for this, and the legend is itself a twentieth-century creation. The ‘troubled water’ is from the Gospel of John — and it is the phrase Paul Simon echoed, via gospel quartets, in ‘Bridge over Troubled Water’.","Few spirituals have crossed genres so freely: the Ramsey Lewis Trio's jazz version was a hit in 1966, and funk, soul and film soundtracks have borrowed its groove. Some purists regret the loss of its baptismal meaning; others see its mobility as proof of its vitality.","Start with a church or quartet version and clap on the offbeat. Then watch or hear the ‘Wade in the Water’ section of Ailey's Revelations, and compare Ramsey Lewis's jazz trio, where the tune becomes a vamp. Listen for how the ‘trouble the water’ line rises."],"spiritual-joshua-fit-the-battle-of-jericho":["The most exuberant of the spirituals: ‘Joshua fit the battle of Jericho, and the walls came tumbling down.’ The melody runs in a minor key but at a quick, driving tempo, the words tumble in syncopation, and the verses retell the siege — the rams' horns, the marching, the shout. It is a narrative song for a crowd, meant to be sung with clapping and to end in a roar.","Sung during slavery and collected after emancipation, it was among the songs that jubilee choirs and later quartets made showpieces. For singers who could not fight openly, the story of an army bringing down walls by marching, trumpeting and shouting had obvious force. The Golden Gate Quartet and Mahalia Jackson both recorded it, and it was a staple of school choirs by mid-century.","‘Fit’ is the old Southern past tense of ‘fight’, and editors who ‘corrected’ it to ‘fought’ killed the rhythm. Listen for the shout itself: in many versions the walls fall on a sudden unison cry or a dropped beat, reproducing in sound the moment the Israelites shouted. It is a spiritual built on noise as a weapon.","Its minor-key swing made it a favourite for jazz and folk groups; some mid-century arrangements turned it into a novelty. Scholars link its triumphant tone to the ‘ring shout’, the circle dance of West African origin that survived in Black worship.","Hear Mahalia Jackson or a quartet version, then a big choir, and listen for where the ‘tumbling down’ falls in the bar. Compare it with ‘Motherless Child’: the same people made both, and the range between them is the range of the spirituals."],"fisk-jubilee-singers-swing-low-sweet-chariot":["Four men singing a hymn-like harmony, unhurried and exactly blended: ‘Swing low, sweet chariot, coming for to carry me home.’ The refrain falls and rises in a gentle arc built on the pentatonic scale, and the verses — ‘I looked over Jordan and what did I see’ — are sung by a lead voice answered by the group. The recording's surface hiss and the singers' formal diction preserve the concert style the Fisk singers invented in the 1870s.","The song is usually credited to Wallace Willis, a freedman in the Choctaw Nation of Indian Territory, whose songs reached the Fisk Jubilee Singers through a missionary around 1871. The first Fisk troupe sang it across the North, in Britain and before Queen Victoria, raising money to build Jubilee Hall. The Fisk University Jubilee Quartet, led by John Wesley Work II, recorded it for Victor in the first years of acoustic recording, among the earliest recordings of African American sacred music.","Dvořák, who heard spirituals from Harry T. Burleigh in New York, gave the solo flute in the first movement of the New World Symphony a theme that almost quotes this tune. Listen also to the recording's blend: the quartet sings as a choir in miniature, with little of the swing or improvised ornament of later gospel quartets — the price and the achievement of making folk song ‘respectable’.","The Fisk approach was debated from the start: some Black listeners felt concert harmonisation betrayed the congregational style, and Zora Neale Hurston later attacked ‘glee-club’ spirituals as a false front. Yet the Fisk recordings are now treasured as documents, and the song itself has become a national anthem of sorts, sung at funerals and even, in England, on rugby terraces.","Listen past the surface noise to the balance of the four voices and the evenness of the tempo. Then hear the Golden Gate Quartet's swinging jubilee sound and hear what changed in thirty years. Dvořák's first-movement flute tune makes the perfect companion."],"burleigh-deep-river":["A spiritual turned art song. The voice sings the old melody — ‘Deep river, my home is over Jordan; deep river, Lord, I want to cross over into camp-ground’ — in long, arching phrases that leap an octave on ‘deep’, while the piano supplies rich, slow-moving harmony with chromatic inner voices, like a Brahms or Dvořák song. It lasts about three minutes and is sung slowly, with a hush in the middle verse about the ‘gospel feast’.","Burleigh, the Erie-born baritone who had sung spirituals to Dvořák in the 1890s, published this setting in 1916. It was the first spiritual arranged as a concert song for solo voice and piano to become widely popular, and it opened the recital stage to the repertory: Roland Hayes, Marian Anderson and Paul Robeson built programmes around such settings. Samuel Coleridge-Taylor had already arranged the tune for piano and for violin.","Burleigh's first version was for chorus; he revised it several times, and the solo version's slower, more luxuriant harmony is what made it famous. Listen for how the piano holds back on the octave leap and then answers it — Burleigh keeps the tune exactly as sung and puts all his art into the accompaniment. Tippett closed A Child of Our Time with this spiritual.","Welcomed by white critics as proof that spirituals belonged in the recital hall, it was later criticised — by Zora Neale Hurston among others — for Europeanising a congregational music. Today it is seen as a founding document of a distinctive Black art-song tradition, which continued through Hall Johnson, Florence Price and Margaret Bonds.","Hear Marian Anderson or Paul Robeson for the classic reading, then a modern recital singer such as Jessye Norman. Listen to the piano rather than the voice for once, and notice how it prepares each high note. Compare with the unaccompanied spirituals in this collection to hear what was gained and lost."],"dorsey-its-tight-like-that":["Two men having fun: Tampa Red's bright slide guitar and Georgia Tom Dorsey's rolling piano over a bouncing medium tempo, with a sly verse-and-refrain lyric — ‘It's tight like that, beedle um bum’ — full of double meanings. It is short, repetitive and infectious, closer to a party record than to the Delta blues, and it launched a craze for good-humoured, suggestive ‘hokum’ blues.","Thomas A. Dorsey had been Ma Rainey's pianist and bandleader in Chicago. He and the guitarist Tampa Red recorded this for Vocalion in 1928, and it sold heavily, spawning sequels and imitators. Its royalties arrived at the same moment as Dorsey's religious crisis and turn to church music — the man who would father gospel was, that year, the hottest blues writer in Chicago.","The piano rhythm and blues harmony you hear here are exactly what Dorsey carried into his gospel songs a few years later; only the words changed. That is why church elders initially barred his gospel music as ‘the devil's music’. Listen also for Tampa Red's slide, clean and precise rather than raw — the ‘Guitar Wizard’ who shaped Chicago electric blues.","Hokum was long dismissed by blues historians as trivial novelty next to the Delta masters; later writers recognised its commercial importance and its role in shaping urban blues and early rhythm and blues. Dorsey himself later preferred not to discuss his blues career.","Listen once for the jokes, then again for the piano: that walking, rocking left hand is the heartbeat of Dorsey's gospel. Put it beside ‘Take My Hand, Precious Lord’ from four years later. For contrast, hear Blind Willie Johnson, who sang sacred songs with blues guitar from the other direction."],"dorsey-take-my-hand-precious-lord":["A slow gospel song in three verses: ‘Precious Lord, take my hand, lead me on, let me stand; I am tired, I am weak, I am worn.’ The melody is simple and hymn-like, but Dorsey wrote it for gospel's slow, stretched delivery, where a singer can linger on ‘tired’ and ‘worn’, bend notes blue and build to the plea ‘lead me home’. Over a rolling piano it can take five minutes or fifteen, depending on how far the singer travels.","Dorsey wrote it in August 1932 after his wife Nettie died in childbirth while he was away singing at a revival in St Louis; their newborn son died soon after. He was by then music director at Pilgrim Baptist Church in Chicago and selling his songs as sheet music door to door. It became gospel's most famous song, the favourite of Martin Luther King, sung by Mahalia Jackson at his funeral in 1968 and by Aretha Franklin at Mahalia's in 1972.","The tune is adapted from a nineteenth-century hymn, George N. Allen's ‘Maitland’ (sung to ‘Must Jesus Bear the Cross Alone’). Dorsey's contribution is the rhythm and the gaps: the melody leaves room between phrases for the pianist's blues fills and the singer's moans — the space, not the notes, is the gospel.","It was translated into many languages and recorded by Elvis Presley and by countless soloists and choirs. Some church musicians object to the blues-laden, stretched performances that became standard; others hold that the song only lives in them. It is regarded as the foundational text of gospel song.","Start with Mahalia Jackson, whose slow tempo and vast breath set the pattern; then Aretha Franklin's teenage recording from Songs of Faith. Listen for the word ‘tired’ — every great singer makes it a small drama. Compare with ‘Were You There’ for the older congregational moan behind it."],"golden-gate-quartet-golden-gate-gospel-train":["Four voices become a steam train. The bass chugs a rhythm, the tenors whistle and moan like the engine, and the lead calls out the stations of salvation as the ‘gospel train’ pulls in. There are no instruments at all, yet it swings like a small swing band. The song is an old train-to-glory spiritual; the performance is the jubilee quartet style at its most brilliant, a three-minute showpiece of vocal mimicry and rhythm.","The quartet, from Norfolk, Virginia, recorded it in their first session for RCA's Bluebird label in 1937, and their records sold across the South. John Hammond booked them for his ‘From Spirituals to Swing’ concert at Carnegie Hall in December 1938, where they shared a bill with Count Basie and Sister Rosetta Tharpe, and they went on to radio, film and the White House.","The ‘train’ is not only a sound effect: the train as the road north and the road to heaven is one of the oldest images in Black music, shared with blues harmonica ‘train’ pieces and later with soul (‘People Get Ready’). Listen to the bass singer: he is doing the job of a string bass and a drummer at once, a technique later doo-wop groups inherited.","Jubilee quartets were sometimes dismissed by gospel purists as entertainment and by jazz critics as novelties. Later historians, notably Anthony Heilbut and Doug Seroff, placed them at the centre of Black popular music between the wars, linking barbershop, jazz and doo-wop.","Listen for each voice doing a different job: bass as rhythm section, tenors as whistle and steam, lead as preacher. Then hear the Fisk quartet's ‘Swing Low’ of 1909 to hear how much swing arrived in thirty years, and Sam Cooke's Soul Stirrers to hear where the quartet went next."],"rosetta-tharpe-rock-me":["A young woman alone with her guitar, picking a swinging, bluesy accompaniment and singing a prayer — ‘Rock me in the cradle of thy love’ — with a joy that makes it sound like a dance record. Her guitar answers every vocal line with bent notes and quick runs, and the rhythm is pure swing. Three minutes that sound, heard now, like the dawn of rock and roll.","‘Rock Me’ is Thomas A. Dorsey's gospel song ‘Hide Me in Thy Bosom’, retitled. Tharpe, raised in the Pentecostal Church of God in Christ, recorded it in her first Decca session in 1938, the year she also appeared at the Cotton Club and at John Hammond's ‘From Spirituals to Swing’ concert at Carnegie Hall. She soon recorded it again with Lucky Millinder's big band. The church was scandalised; the record-buying public was delighted.","The word ‘rock’ in the title meant being rocked in God's arms — but it already carried the other meaning in Black secular slang, and the double sense is audible in her delivery. Listen for the guitar's fills between lines: that call-and-response between voice and guitar is exactly what Chuck Berry built a career on.","Gospel purists condemned her nightclub appearances; jazz critics treated her as a novelty. Her reputation fell with the rise of rock and roll, whose male stars took her style, and she was largely forgotten until biographers and the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame (2018) restored her as a founding figure.","Listen to the guitar alone first — the swing, the bends, the confidence. Then compare the big-band version with Lucky Millinder to hear her hold her own against a whole orchestra. Put it beside Dorsey's slow gospel style to hear how she sped his songs up."],"rosetta-tharpe-strange-things-happening-every-day":["A shuffling, upbeat gospel song with a boogie-woogie piano, a walking bass and drums, over which Tharpe sings a sly sermon about hypocrites — ‘There are strange things happening every day’ — and plays a stinging electric guitar solo. It swings hard, the solo is loud and bluesy, and the whole thing sounds like rhythm and blues in everything but its words.","Recorded in 1944 in New York with the pianist Sammy Price's trio, it became a hit on the ‘race’ charts in 1945 — one of the first gospel records to cross over to a secular Black audience. Wartime America was buying jump blues and boogie-woogie; Tharpe simply played gospel in that idiom. Johnny Cash, Little Richard and Jerry Lee Lewis heard her as teenagers.","It is often proposed as the first rock-and-roll record, years ahead of the usual candidates; the claim is unprovable, but the guitar solo, the backbeat and the shuffle are all there. Listen to the lyric too: it is a piece of social commentary, mocking the ‘church folks’ who condemn others while they sin — sung by a woman her own church was criticising.","Rock historians long overlooked it; since the 1990s it has become the standard exhibit for Tharpe's influence and for gospel's role in rock and roll's birth. Some scholars caution against ‘first rock-and-roll record’ contests, preferring to see a broad field of jump blues and gospel from which rock emerged.","Wait for the guitar solo and listen to its tone and attack — then play any early Chuck Berry record. Notice the piano's boogie-woogie left hand, which links it to Dorsey's blues years. Then hear her ‘Up Above My Head’ with Marie Knight for the duet side of her art."],"rosetta-tharpe-up-above-my-head":["A joyous duet: Tharpe and Marie Knight trade lines — ‘Up above my head, I hear music in the air’ — one calling, the other answering, then joining in harmony, over a bouncing rhythm section. The tune is a simple traditional spiritual refrain, but the call-and-response between the two women builds excitement verse by verse, and Tharpe's guitar punctuates it all.","Tharpe met Marie Knight, a contralto from Florida, in 1946, and the two toured and recorded together for several years; this was one of their most popular records, released by Decca in the late 1940s. Their duets filled auditoriums and ballparks; Tharpe's wedding in 1951, staged at a Washington baseball stadium before a paying crowd, was the peak of her fame.","Listen to how the two voices differ: Knight's is darker and more restrained, Tharpe's bright and rough, and the tension between them is the drama. The call-and-response is the oldest structure in Black sacred music — the leader and congregation of the spirituals — shrunk to two singers and a band.","In its day it was a popular record on the Black charts and a staple of the duo's packed tours, though church critics grumbled that Tharpe and Knight had turned worship into showbusiness. After Knight left for secular music and Tharpe's star faded, it was remembered mainly by gospel collectors. Biographies, documentaries and her Rock and Roll Hall of Fame induction revived it; historians now debate how far such duets shaped rhythm and blues vocal groups.","Follow the two voices as if they were a conversation, noticing who leads when. Compare with ‘Strange Things’ for Tharpe's solo swagger. Then hear the Staple Singers, where a family's voices do the same call and response over a blues guitar."],"mahalia-jackson-move-on-up-a-little-higher":["A rousing song about heaven, where the singer will ‘move on up a little higher’, meet the saints and ‘drink from the fountain that never runs dry’. Issued in two parts across both sides of a 78 because it was too long for one, it builds from a steady, rocking tempo into ecstatic repetition, with Jackson's huge voice swooping over piano and organ, bending notes and riding the beat like a jazz singer.","The song is by the Memphis minister W. Herbert Brewster, gospel's greatest songwriter after Dorsey. Jackson recorded it for the small Apollo label in New York in 1947; it sold enormously, reportedly in the millions, and made her the first gospel superstar. It led to radio, a Columbia contract, a Carnegie Hall concert in 1950 and national television.","Listen for the join between the two sides: part two picks up in mid-flight, and the momentum carries over as if nothing happened. Jackson's phrasing — delaying a word, then catching up with a rush — is the rhythmic freedom she learned partly from Bessie Smith records in New Orleans, though she refused ever to sing the blues.","Its sales startled a record industry that had treated gospel as a niche, and it is universally named as the record that launched gospel's ‘golden age’. Some church musicians regretted the commercial success and the star system it created,, and traditionalists disliked her blues-tinged delivery. Jazz critics heard in Jackson a singer of Bessie Smith's stature; the argument now is less about her greatness than about how much her style owed to the blues she refused to sing.","Listen to both parts in one go, and notice how the tempo seems to rise though it hardly changes — the excitement comes from her rhythmic placement. Then hear her ‘Come Sunday’ with Duke Ellington on Black, Brown and Beige, the most unexpected setting she ever sang in."],"clara-ward-surely-god-is-able":["A slow, swaying song in a lilting triple-feel rhythm, with the Ward Singers' close, high harmony and lead singers trading phrases of rising intensity: ‘Surely, surely, he's able to carry you through.’ Piano holds everything together. The effect is of a gradual climb, each lead singer pushing higher than the last until the group is almost shouting.","Another song by W. Herbert Brewster, recorded by the Ward Singers for Savoy around 1950, it became one of the best-selling gospel records of its time. The Philadelphia group, run by Gertrude Ward with her daughter Clara as pianist and arranger, featured the extraordinary Marion Williams, whose soaring leads were a key part of the sound.","The rhythm is the secret: the swaying, rocking triple lilt, unusual in gospel then, became a staple of later gospel and soul ballads. Listen too for the alternation of leads — Clara Ward's warm voice and Marion Williams's higher, wilder one — a method that trained the young Aretha Franklin, who heard these singers in her father's church.","The Ward Singers were acclaimed for their musicianship and later criticised for glamour, gowns and wigs when they moved into nightclubs and Las Vegas. This record remains the one everyone agrees on; Marion Williams's later solo career has raised her reputation among critics above Ward's own.","Listen for the lilt and let it sway you; then pick out each lead singer as she takes over. Compare Mahalia's ‘Move On Up’ for the solo star style, and Aretha's Amazing Grace, where she sings Clara Ward's ‘How I Got Over’ with Ward in the congregation."],"soul-stirrers-jesus-gave-me-water":["A brisk, rhythmic quartet number telling the story of the woman at the well from the Gospel of John. The group sets up a steady rocking pulse and harmony; over it the young Sam Cooke floats a light, clear lead, sliding between notes and adding the little ‘whoa-oh-oh’ yodel that would become his trademark. It is sweet rather than rough, and it swings.","Recorded in Los Angeles for Art Rupe's Specialty label in 1951, it was among Cooke's first records after he replaced R. H. Harris as the Soul Stirrers' lead singer at nineteen. His good looks and ease made the group idols of young church audiences, especially young women, on the gospel circuit. Six years later he left for pop and invented soul with the same voice.","Listen for the yodel: that brief break in the voice, carried almost unchanged into ‘You Send Me’ in 1957, is the single most direct thread from church to soul music. Behind Cooke, the quartet's two-lead system, invented under Harris, lets a second voice take over when the first has built the excitement.","Gospel historians rank Cooke's Soul Stirrers records among the best quartet singing on record; some elders at the time thought his style too smooth and too flirtatious for church. His move to pop in 1957 was seen by many gospel fans as betrayal.","Focus on Cooke's voice and count the ways he decorates a phrase without straining. Then hear his later soul records, especially ‘A Change Is Gonna Come’, and hear the same voice. For the harder quartet style, compare the Five Blind Boys of Mississippi."],"five-blind-boys-of-mississippi-our-father":["The Lord's Prayer as a slow, heavy moan. The quartet hums a deep, sustained harmony; over it Archie Brownlee sings the prayer's words, stretching them, then suddenly erupts into screams and falsetto cries that crack open the calm. The contrast between the hushed background and the raw lead is the whole drama.","The Five Blind Boys of Mississippi, formed at the Piney Woods school, recorded for Don Robey's Peacock label in Houston, which also made Big Mama Thornton's ‘Hound Dog’. ‘Our Father’ became one of the best-selling gospel records of its day and a jukebox favourite beyond church audiences. This was the era of ‘hard gospel’, when quartets competed to see who could bring a congregation to its feet.","Listen to Brownlee's scream: it is controlled, not wild, placed exactly where the text peaks. Ray Charles, who listened closely to gospel quartets, used the same device in soul; James Brown took it further. What sounds like abandon is technique.","It sold strongly well beyond church audiences, turning up on jukeboxes beside rhythm and blues, and its crossover showed labels a market for gospel sounds that they soon exploited in soul. Some ministers found Brownlee's screaming theatrical rather than devout. Gospel historians such as Anthony Heilbut now rank him among the greatest quartet leads, though his death in 1960 cut short his fame, and the debt soul singers owe him is still less widely acknowledged than it should be.","Listen first to the humming quartet, then notice where Brownlee breaks free. Compare the smooth Sam Cooke with the Soul Stirrers, the opposite pole of quartet singing, and then early Ray Charles for the inheritance."],"cl-franklin-the-eagle-stirreth-her-nest":["A sermon that turns into music. Rev. C. L. Franklin begins by speaking calmly about a verse in Deuteronomy — the eagle that stirs up her nest to teach her young to fly — and gradually his speech tightens into rhythm and pitch, the ‘whooping’ chant of the Black preacher, answered by the organ and by shouts from the congregation. By the end he is singing phrases on a few pitches in a rhythmic chant as the church rises with him.","Recorded at New Bethel Baptist Church in Detroit, and issued on records distributed by Chess, this was the most famous of dozens of Franklin sermons sold nationally in the 1950s. Radio broadcasts and records made him a star preacher; he toured with gospel singers and drew huge crowds. His daughter Aretha grew up hearing him every Sunday.","The ‘whoop’ is a musical form: it moves from speech to a chanted, pitched delivery with a rhythmic pulse, often landing on repeated tones answered by the organ. Listen for the moment the organ first answers him — that is when speech becomes music. Steve Reich's speech-melody pieces begin from the same observation.","It is counted among the classic recordings of African American preaching and has been studied by folklorists and musicologists as an oral art with its own forms. Franklin's reputation was clouded by personal scandal and he was criticised in his lifetime for the commercial side of his fame, but his recorded sermons are now central to understanding how Black church music and speech feed each other.","Listen for the moment talking turns to chanting, and for the organ's first response. Then hear Aretha's Amazing Grace, where her father speaks to the congregation between songs, and listen for his rise and fall in her phrasing. Steve Reich's Different Trains makes a fascinating, secular companion."],"aretha-franklin-songs-of-faith":["A fourteen-year-old girl at the piano in her father's church, singing gospel standards live — ‘Never Grow Old’, ‘There Is a Fountain Filled with Blood’, and a long ‘Precious Lord’ in two parts. The voice is already enormous, full of bends and melisma, and the congregation responds audibly. The sound is rough, recorded on simple equipment, but the singer is unmistakably Aretha.","Recorded at New Bethel Baptist Church in Detroit in 1956, it was issued first by the small JVB label and then by Chess's Checker imprint, the outlet for her father's sermons. Aretha had been touring with her father's gospel caravan and learning from Clara Ward, Mahalia Jackson and James Cleveland, all family friends. Her mother had left and later died; the church was her world.","Listen to how she handles ‘Precious Lord’: the long, stretched phrases and sudden bursts are Clara Ward's and Mahalia's, but the pianistic sense of harmony is her own. The later reissue title, ‘The Gospel Soul of Aretha Franklin’, points at what the record reveals — soul was already present in 1956.","On release it circulated mainly among gospel listeners who already knew her father's records, and it attracted little notice when Columbia signed her in 1960 as a jazz-leaning pop singer. After her Atlantic breakthrough it was reissued and reappraised, first as a curiosity and then as an essential document of her roots. Critics hear the range and control of the later soul records already in a teenager; some find the rough church recording a barrier, others its chief virtue.","Start with ‘Precious Lord’ and listen to how far she departs from the written tune. Then jump to Amazing Grace sixteen years later, and hear how much and how little changed."],"staple-singers-uncloudy-day":["A shimmering electric guitar with heavy tremolo introduces a family singing about heaven, ‘O they tell me of an uncloudy day’. Mavis Staples, still a teenager, sings in a deep contralto, her family harmonising around her, while Pops Staples's guitar supplies a shimmering, bluesy accompaniment that sounds like nothing else in gospel. The song is an old white hymn, but the sound is Mississippi.","Recorded for Chicago's Vee-Jay label in 1956, it was the Staples' breakthrough. Pops Staples had grown up near Dockery plantation, home of Charley Patton, and brought the Delta guitar style into church music. Bob Dylan, hearing it on the radio as a boy, later described it as the most mysterious thing he had ever heard.","The hymn was written in the 1880s by Josiah K. Alwood, a white Methodist; the Staples turned it into Delta gospel. Listen to the guitar's tremolo, which makes it sound like it is under water — Pops used an amplifier effect that was then new and rare in church music.","It was a hit on the gospel circuit and made the family's name, though some church listeners found Pops's amplified guitar too close to the juke joint. Later critics came to regard it as one of the great gospel records of the 1950s and a bridge between Delta blues and gospel. Dylan's testimony drew folk and rock audiences to it; scholars still weigh how much of the Staples' sound came from the Delta and how much from Chicago's electric blues.","Listen to the guitar first, then the voices. Notice Mavis's deep voice, unusual for a teenage girl. Compare with the Delta blues of Robert Johnson and Son House to hear where the guitar came from."],"mahalia-jackson-live-at-newport-1958":["Gospel's greatest singer in front of a jazz audience in the open air, with only piano and organ behind her. The set runs from stately hymns to rocking up-tempo numbers such as ‘Didn't It Rain’, whose Noah's-flood refrain she drives like a big band, and closes with a slow, enormous ‘The Lord's Prayer’. You hear the crowd go from polite attention to shouting along — a church service improvised on a festival stage.","By 1958 Jackson was a Columbia recording artist, a television presence and a national figure; earlier that year she had recorded Black, Brown and Beige with Duke Ellington, singing his ‘Come Sunday’ and a setting of the Twenty-third Psalm. The Newport Jazz Festival was the most prestigious in the country, and it booked her on its Sunday programme. The festival was filmed for Bert Stern's documentary Jazz on a Summer's Day, where her ‘Lord's Prayer’ closes the film.","Listen to her pianist Mildred Falls, the unsung partner in every Jackson record: she follows the voice's sudden stretches and accelerations without a cue, and fills every gap with gospel's rolling left hand. And notice that Jackson, who refused ever to sing the blues or jazz standards, makes no concession to the jazz crowd — she brings the church to them.","It is counted among her finest live recordings, the best record of the physical power that studio sessions tamed. Some church people disliked seeing her at a jazz festival; jazz critics, who had long compared her to Bessie Smith, took the performance as proof that gospel belonged in any discussion of great American singing.","Start with ‘Didn't It Rain’ and listen to how she pushes the tempo against the piano; then the closing ‘Lord's Prayer’ for the opposite, pure breadth of tone. Follow with her ‘Come Sunday’ on Black, Brown and Beige, and with Aretha's Amazing Grace, which takes this live-church sound to its limit."],"swan-silvertones-mary-dont-you-weep":["A spiritual from slavery sung as a late-1950s quartet showpiece: ‘O Mary, don't you weep, don't you mourn — Pharaoh's army got drownded.’ Over a light guitar and the group's rocking harmony, the leads alternate — Claude Jeter's floating, silvery falsetto and a harder, preaching second voice — and the performance pushes past the written verses into improvised lines, each one raising the temperature. About three minutes, but it feels like a whole service.","The song itself fuses two Bible stories — Mary of Bethany weeping for her brother Lazarus, and the drowning of Pharaoh's army in the Red Sea — into one promise of deliverance, a characteristic spiritual move. The Swan Silvertones, founded among West Virginia coal miners, recorded it for Chicago's Vee-Jay label in 1959, at the height of the gospel-quartet circuit, when groups toured Black churches and auditoriums in fierce competition with one another.","Near the end comes an ad-libbed line — ‘I'll be your bridge over deep water if you trust in my name’ — which Paul Simon has acknowledged as the seed of ‘Bridge over Troubled Water’ (1970), itself later recorded by Aretha Franklin. Listen for it: a spontaneous church phrase that became one of the most-sung pop songs of its decade.","Among gospel collectors it has always been a classic of the quartet style, prized for Jeter's falsetto, which Al Green and the Temptations' Eddie Kendricks both learned from. The Simon connection has given it a second audience, and Aretha's performance of the song on Amazing Grace confirmed its place in the canon.","Listen for the handover between the two lead voices — sweet, then rough — and then wait for the ‘bridge’ line. Follow with Aretha's longer version on Amazing Grace and then Simon and Garfunkel's ‘Bridge over Troubled Water’, and you have heard a single phrase travel from church to pop."],"james-cleveland-peace-be-still":["The storm on the Sea of Galilee, told by a preacher and a hundred-voice choir. Cleveland's gravelly baritone narrates — the wind, the frightened disciples, the sleeping Christ — and the choir answers with the words ‘Peace, be still’, at first softly, then in waves that grow until the whole church is roaring, with piano and organ swelling underneath. It is slow, patient and cumulative: the storm is in the build, the calm in the choir's last held chord.","Cleveland recorded it with the Angelic Choir of the First Baptist Church of Nutley, New Jersey, directed by Lawrence Roberts, and Savoy issued it in 1963. It became one of the best-selling gospel records of the decade and made the mass choir, rather than the quartet or the solo star, the centre of gospel. Cleveland had sung as a boy in Dorsey's choir at Pilgrim Baptist and coached the young Aretha Franklin in Detroit.","Listen to how Cleveland uses the choir like an orchestra — whole sections sustain while others punch short answers, and the dynamics rise in steps rather than a smooth crescendo. His rough, almost broken voice is deliberately set against the choir's smoothness: the preacher is human, the choir is the sea and then the peace.","Gospel historians treat it as the record that opened the choir era, the model for thousands of church and community choirs and for the Gospel Music Workshop of America, which Cleveland founded later in the decade. Some quartet fans felt the mass choir swamped individual artistry; its success settled the argument commercially.","Listen once for Cleveland's storytelling and once for the choir alone, noticing how each return of ‘Peace, be still’ is louder or richer. Then hear Handel's ‘Hallelujah’ chorus for another choir used as a wave of sound, and Aretha's Amazing Grace, where Cleveland directs a choir behind her."],"staple-singers-freedom-highway":["A live album made in a Chicago church, where hymns, spirituals and new freedom songs sit side by side as if they had always belonged together. The title track, by Pops Staples, is a march: a steady, stomping beat, Pops's tremolo guitar and the family's voices — ‘Marching up freedom highway, marching each and every day.’ Mavis leads with a low, rough power, Pops speaks between songs, and the congregation answers throughout. Church service and political rally are the same event.","Recorded in 1965 at New Nazareth Church on Chicago's South Side and issued by Epic, the album grew straight out of the civil-rights movement. Pops wrote the title song for the Selma-to-Montgomery march of March 1965, after the violence on the Edmund Pettus Bridge. The family had met Martin Luther King in 1963 and resolved, in Pops's words, that if he could preach it they could sing it.","Listen for Pops's spoken introductions: he frames each song as testimony, the way a preacher lines out a hymn, and that framing is what turns the concert into a service. Musically, the freedom songs use exactly the gospel toolkit — call-and-response, the marching backbeat, the guitar's shimmering blues — which is why the movement could sing them on the road without rehearsal.","Long overshadowed by the Staples' later Stax hits, it has come to be regarded as one of the essential records of the movement; an expanded edition later restored the whole church concert, and critics now prize its unpolished live sound above many studio records of the period.","Begin with the title track and let the march rhythm settle, then listen through for the congregation's voices. Compare the Staples' earlier ‘Uncloudy Day’ for the same guitar in a quieter mood, and Margaret Bonds's Montgomery Variations, a concert-hall response to the same struggle built on spirituals."],"edwin-hawkins-singers-oh-happy-day":["A youth choir, a relaxed groove and one refrain. Edwin Hawkins's piano and a soft rhythm section set up an easy mid-tempo beat, Dorothy Combs Morrison's lead voice sings ‘Oh happy day, when Jesus washed my sins away’, and the choir echoes each phrase back, adding handclaps and harmony as the song gathers force. It never shouts the way older choir gospel does; its joy is sunny and unhurried, and it lasts about five minutes.","The Northern California State Youth Choir recorded it in 1967 in a Pentecostal church in Berkeley for a small private album meant to raise travel money. A San Francisco disc jockey began playing it, a national label picked it up, and in 1969 it was an international hit and a Grammy winner; the choir was renamed after Hawkins. This was the Bay Area of the late 1960s, where church, soul radio and the counterculture overlapped.","The words are an eighteenth-century English hymn by the Congregationalist minister Philip Doddridge, ‘O happy day, that fixed my choice’, which had long been sung to a quite different tune. Hawkins kept only the refrain idea and built it on pop-soul harmony — listen to the piano's chords, which owe more to Motown than to Dorsey.","It was one of the very few gospel records to become a mainstream pop hit, and church traditionalists objected that its soft sound and radio play diluted the message. Others hailed it as the birth of contemporary gospel, the line that runs through Andraé Crouch to Kirk Franklin; that view has prevailed.","Listen to how little the song changes and how much the choir's growing confidence does. Then hear James Cleveland's ‘Peace Be Still’ for the heavier, older choir sound, and Handel's ‘Hallelujah’ for another choral hit that crossed from church to the wider world."],"aretha-franklin-amazing-grace":["Two nights of church, on four sides of vinyl. Aretha sings and sometimes plays piano in front of a full choir and a soul rhythm section, working through gospel standards — Clara Ward's ‘How I Got Over’, ‘Mary, Don't You Weep’, ‘Precious Memories’, ‘Never Grow Old’ — plus Marvin Gaye's ‘Wholy Holy’ and Carole King's ‘You've Got a Friend’ joined to ‘Precious Lord’. At its centre is an ‘Amazing Grace’ slowed almost to a standstill, each word stretched into long, climbing melismas while the church cries out.","Recorded on 13 and 14 January 1972 at New Temple Missionary Baptist Church in Watts, Los Angeles, with Rev. James Cleveland at the piano and in charge, and the Southern California Community Choir. Aretha was the biggest soul star in the world; Atlantic's Jerry Wexler agreed to let her go home to gospel. Her father C. L. Franklin and Clara Ward sat in the congregation on the second night. Sydney Pollack filmed the sessions, but the footage could not be synchronised with the sound, and the film waited until 2018.","Listen for Cleveland's voice during the title song: he is overcome and breaks off, and the choir carries on without him. On the second night her father rises to speak, recalling hearing her sing as a child — the whole family history of this collection in a few minutes of talk. The band is Atlantic's soul players, among them Bernard Purdie on drums and Chuck Rainey on bass: the grooves are Aretha's pop records, the words are the church.","It became the best-selling record of her career and is usually named the best-selling gospel album of all time. Critics at the time treated it as a curiosity beside her hits; it now stands with the greatest live recordings in any genre, and the 2018 film, which shows the congregation weeping and dancing, drew a new generation to it.","Give ‘Amazing Grace’ its full length without interruption and follow the voice as it rises in stages. Then hear ‘How I Got Over’ for the up-tempo side. Go back to her teenage Songs of Faith, and to Mahalia's ‘Move On Up’, to hear the lineage; then watch the film."],"staple-singers-ill-take-you-there":["A four-minute vamp on almost nothing. A loping bass line with a reggae lilt, light drums and a scratch of guitar set up a groove that never changes, and Mavis Staples sings of a place where ‘ain't nobody crying, ain't nobody worried, ain't no smiling faces lying to the races’. Then the song simply opens up: she calls on the band — ‘Daddy, now, play your guitar’ — and the musicians take turns while she urges them on. The destination is never named; it could be heaven or a better America.","By 1972 the Staples had moved from gospel labels to Stax in Memphis, where the executive Al Bell, who wrote and produced the song, steered them toward ‘message music’ for the pop charts. It was cut with the Muscle Shoals Rhythm Section in Alabama and went to number one. Black radio and the post-civil-rights mood made room for songs that spoke of freedom without naming the church.","The bass line and introduction are borrowed from a Jamaican instrumental, the Harry J. All Stars' ‘The Liquidator’ (1969) — early evidence of reggae feeding back into American soul. And listen to Mavis's calls to the players: they are a preacher's calls to the congregation, the call-and-response of the spirituals transferred to a studio band.","Gospel audiences were divided about the Staples' move into secular soul, though the family always insisted the message was unchanged. The record is now counted among the essential soul singles of the 1970s and a lasting demonstration that gospel's structures could top the pop charts intact.","Let the groove settle and follow Mavis's ad-libs rather than the words. Then hear ‘Uncloudy Day’, sixteen years earlier, and notice that Pops's guitar is the constant. Compare Aretha's Amazing Grace from the same year, which took the opposite road — soul back into church."],"blind-boys-of-alabama-spirit-of-the-century":["A quartet formed in 1939 sings spirituals and gospel songs alongside numbers by Tom Waits, the Rolling Stones and Ben Harper, over a spare, swampy band of acoustic and slide guitars, harmonica, bass and drums. The voices are old and weathered, the harmonies still tight, and Clarence Fountain's lead can still shout. The track everyone remembers is ‘Amazing Grace’ sung to the tune of ‘The House of the Rising Sun’ — a hymn wearing a brothel ballad's melody.","By 2001 the Blind Boys had spent six decades on the gospel circuit, with a revival in the 1980s through the stage work The Gospel at Colonus. Peter Gabriel's Real World label paired them with blues and roots musicians, the harmonica player Charlie Musselwhite among them, for a record aimed at a secular audience that loved Americana. It won the Grammy for traditional soul gospel album and began a late run of acclaimed records.","Their version of Tom Waits's ‘Way Down in the Hole’ from this album became the opening theme of the first season of the television series The Wire (2002), so millions heard the Blind Boys every week without knowing it. And the ‘House of the Rising Sun’ tune works for ‘Amazing Grace’ because both share the same common metre — an old hymn-singers' trick.","It was widely praised and sold far beyond the gospel market; some gospel listeners regretted the secular repertory and rock musicians, while the group, which had heard such complaints since the 1950s, answered that they sang only songs whose message they could stand behind.","Begin with ‘Amazing Grace’ and notice how the minor-key tune darkens the hymn. Then go back to Blind Willie Johnson's ‘John the Revelator’ for the older blues-gospel sound this record reaches toward, and to the Five Blind Boys of Mississippi for the hard-quartet style the group grew up in."],"woody-guthrie-dust-bowl-ballads":["Two albums of 78 rpm discs, one man with a flat-picked guitar and a harmonica on a neck rack. The songs follow the migration of Oklahoma and Texas farmers driven west by drought and dust: 'The Great Dust Storm', 'Talking Dust Bowl Blues', 'Do Re Mi', 'Dust Pneumonia Blues', 'I Ain't Got No Home', 'Vigilante Man'. The centrepiece is 'Tom Joad', a ballad in two parts that retells a whole novel in verse. The voice is dry, nasal and unhurried, closer to talking than singing.","Guthrie had ridden the same road himself, from Pampa, Texas, to California, where he sang on Los Angeles radio for Okie migrants. By early 1940 he was in New York, taken up by Alan Lomax and the left-wing theatre world. Victor, a major label, recorded him that spring, hoping to catch the audience for John Steinbeck's The Grapes of Wrath and John Ford's film of it. It is often called the first concept album: a set of records designed to tell one story.","'Tom Joad' borrows its tune from the outlaw ballad 'John Hardy', and Guthrie said he wrote it after seeing Ford's film rather than reading the book, which is why it follows the movie's plot. The 'talking blues' form he uses on several sides, half-spoken over a strummed pattern, came from Chris Bouchillon's 1920s records; Dylan would lift it wholesale twenty years later.","It sold modestly, and Victor did not follow it up. Its reputation grew backward, once the folk revival of the 1950s went looking for ancestors and found in Guthrie a model of the singer as witness. Critics now treat it as a founding document of American protest song and of the album as a unified statement, though some hear the songs as sharper on the page than on these rather hurried takes.","Start with 'Talking Dust Bowl Blues' for the deadpan humour, then play 'Tom Joad' straight through, all six minutes and more, and notice how little the guitar changes while the story moves. Compare Bruce Springsteen's 'The Ghost of Tom Joad' for the long afterlife. Any Buddha or Bluebird reissue will do; the sound is thin but clear."],"woody-guthrie-library-of-congress-recordings":["Several hours of Guthrie talking and singing into a microphone for Alan Lomax, who prompts him with questions. The songs are the Dust Bowl repertory and older ballads, but the talk is the point: stories of his Oklahoma boyhood, his mother's illness, oil-boom towns, hoboing and the camps of California, told in a slow, joking drawl. Songs grow out of the conversation and fall back into it, so the whole set plays like an oral autobiography with a guitar.","Lomax, then running the Archive of American Folk Song at the Library of Congress, brought Guthrie to Washington in March 1940, weeks after hearing him in New York, and recorded him for the national collection. The discs sat in the archive for more than two decades. Elektra issued them as a three-record set in 1964, when Guthrie lay in hospital with Huntington's disease and the young revivalists who revered him had never heard him speak at length.","Listen to Lomax as much as Guthrie. He steers the talk toward hardship and social comment, and Guthrie, a born performer, obliges; the recordings are as much a portrait of what the New Deal folklorists wanted as of the man himself. The rambling asides, where Guthrie loses the thread and finds another, show how his songs were built: talk first, tune second.","Revival audiences in 1964 received the set as scripture, and it helped fix Guthrie's image as the plain-spoken Okie prophet. Later biographers note how carefully he shaped that persona; he was well read, a cartoonist and a prolific writer. The recordings remain a primary source for historians of the Depression, and the Rounder reissue restored material cut from the Elektra LPs.","Do not try to hear it at one sitting. Take one side at a time as you would a radio programme, and let the stories carry you to the songs. The Rounder CD edition is the one to seek. Pair it with Lead Belly's Last Sessions, the other great document of a folk singer explaining himself."],"woody-guthrie-this-land-is-your-land":["A plain strophic song: a chorus that claims the whole country, coast to coast, for ordinary people, and verses that describe a walker crossing forests, deserts, wheat fields and highways. Guthrie sings it at an easy walking pace over a strummed guitar, with none of the swell of an anthem. The tune is so simple that a room of schoolchildren can pick it up after one chorus, which is exactly what has happened for eighty years.","Guthrie wrote it in February 1940 in a cheap New York hotel, irritated by hearing Kate Smith's recording of Irving Berlin's 'God Bless America' everywhere. His first draft ended each verse with a sarcastic twist on Berlin's title. He recorded it for Moses Asch in April 1944, during the war, and it slowly spread through schools, summer camps and the Popular Front songbooks before becoming a second national anthem in the 1960s.","The best-known versions leave out the verses that give it teeth: one mocks a private-property sign, and another watches hungry people queuing for relief and doubts the chorus's promise. The melody is borrowed almost note for note from the Carter Family's 'When the World's on Fire', itself drawn from an old Baptist hymn.","Its gentleness has made it adaptable to almost anyone, from grade-school pageants to presidential inaugurations, and many listeners never suspect it began as a rebuttal. Folk singers since Pete Seeger have made a point of restoring the missing verses. A 2004 parody dispute revealed that the song's copyright was probably weaker than its publishers claimed, a fitting footnote for a song about ownership.","Hear Guthrie's own 1944 take first, then look up a performance that includes the private-property verse, such as Pete Seeger and Bruce Springsteen at the Lincoln Memorial in 2009. Then play Kate Smith's 'God Bless America' and hear the argument in the difference of tone: one song kneels, the other walks."],"lead-belly-last-sessions":["Hours of Huddie Ledbetter at home, singing to his twelve-string guitar and talking between songs about where each one came from: field hollers, prison work songs, children's game songs, spirituals, cowboy songs, blues, and his own topical pieces. The twelve-string rings like a small piano, with a thumping bass line under chiming upper strings. Because he is relaxed and unrushed, the songs often run far longer than on his three-minute commercial sides.","The jazz writer Frederic Ramsey Jr. recorded him in New York in the autumn of 1948 on one of the new portable magnetic tape machines, which freed the session from the three-minute limit of the disc. Lead Belly was already weakened by the motor neurone disease that killed him in December 1949. Moses Asch's Folkways issued the tapes in 1953, just as young urban musicians were starting to learn his repertory.","The spoken introductions are a masterclass in how a folk singer thinks about sources: he credits people, places and prison farms, sometimes contradicting the stories he told the Lomaxes in the 1930s. Listen to how he stamps and slaps time, and to the way he half-speaks verses before singing them, a habit from years of performing for audiences who needed the words explained.","Issued posthumously, the set became a textbook for the revival, a library of songs later recorded by the Weavers, Odetta and many others. It is also valued as one of the first long-form tape documents of any American vernacular musician. Some listeners find the talk repetitive; historians find it indispensable.","Choose a single disc and listen as if you were in the room. Notice how his guitar walks a bass line under everything. Then play the Weavers' 'Goodnight, Irene' of 1950 and hear what polish added and what it took away. The Smithsonian Folkways CD reissue is the edition to find."],"the-weavers-goodnight-irene":["A slow waltz, sung in four-part harmony by a quartet of two men and two women, with Pete Seeger's banjo and Fred Hellerman's guitar nearly buried under Gordon Jenkins's strings and background chorus. Ronnie Gilbert's alto cuts through on the chorus. It sounds like a gentle, swaying singalong for a dance hall at closing time, which is why it filled jukeboxes across the country.","The Weavers had been singing Lead Belly's song at the Village Vanguard, and Decca's Gordon Jenkins, a big-band arranger, signed them and dressed it for the pop market in 1950. It became one of the biggest hits of the year, and a wave of cover versions followed. Lead Belly had died a few months earlier, too soon to see his song reach millions. Within two years the group was blacklisted for its members' left-wing politics, and Decca dropped them.","Compare the words with Lead Belly's and you will find the darker verses gone, including one about despair. The quartet's harmonies come from union halls and summer camps; Seeger's banjo, trained on old-time records, sits under the string section like a working man at a wedding. The B-side, 'Tzena, Tzena, Tzena', an Israeli song, was also a hit.","Purists complained that the arrangement smothered the song; the pop audience did not care. Historians now see it as the moment folk song first became mass-market pop, and as a case study in the blacklist, since a group at the top of the charts could be driven off radio and television within two years. The record paid royalties to Lead Belly's widow for decades.","Play Lead Belly's own 'Irene' first, with its steady twelve-string and blunt voice, then the Weavers: listen for the moment the full chorus enters, and for Gilbert's voice on top. Then hear the Weavers' Carnegie Hall concert of 1955, where the same group sings without the strings and sounds like itself."],"harry-smith-anthology-of-american-folk-music":["Eighty-four commercial 78s from the late 1920s and early 1930s, gathered into three two-record volumes: Ballads, Social Music and Songs. Murder ballads, sacred harp singing, Cajun two-steps, jug bands, Delta blues, Baptist preaching and mountain fiddle tunes sit side by side, with Black and white performers mixed together. The voices are strange and unpolished; the old shellac hiss makes it sound like a message from a lost country.","Harry Smith, a young painter, film-maker and obsessive collector from the Pacific Northwest, had bought thousands of old records that the labels had deleted when the Depression killed the rural market. Moses Asch of Folkways agreed to issue a selection in 1952, largely ignoring copyright. At a moment of McCarthyism and bland pop, the set showed a generation of urban students an America that was violent, comic, devout and utterly unlike the one on television.","Smith's booklet summarises each song in the style of a tabloid headline and hides a private cosmology: the three volumes were linked in his mind to three of the four alchemical elements, and a fourth volume was compiled but not issued until 2000. The cover reproduces an engraving of a celestial monochord from the seventeenth-century mystic Robert Fludd.","Its influence on the revival is hard to overstate: Dylan, Baez, the New Lost City Ramblers and Dave Van Ronk all learned songs from it, and several of its forgotten performers were tracked down and recorded again in the 1960s. Critics such as Greil Marcus later read it as a secret history of America. The 1997 Smithsonian reissue won Grammys and reintroduced it to a new audience.","Start with volume one, the ballads, and follow the stories: Clarence Ashley's 'The Coo Coo Bird', Buell Kazee, the Carter Family. Then turn to the Social Music volume for the sermons and sacred singing. Read Smith's notes as you go. Seek the 1997 Smithsonian Folkways box, which reproduces his booklet."],"jean-ritchie-singing-the-traditional-songs":["A single high, clear, unornamented voice, sometimes alone and sometimes over the drone of the Appalachian dulcimer, a long, narrow fretted zither laid across the lap and strummed with a quill. The songs are ballads, love songs, play-party and religious songs handed down in her family in the Cumberland Mountains. The dulcimer holds one or two notes under the tune like a bagpipe, giving the modal melodies a sound that is ancient and plain at once.","Jean Ritchie was the youngest of fourteen children in Viper, Perry County, Kentucky, a family that sang constantly. After college she moved to New York as a social worker and was soon singing for Alan Lomax and at the city's folk gatherings. The young Elektra label made this its second release. The same year she went to Britain and Ireland on a Fulbright to trace her family's songs back to their sources.","In 1917 the English collector Cecil Sharp visited the Ritchie family and noted ballads from Jean's older sisters, so some of these songs had already passed through the same notebooks that fed Vaughan Williams's folk-song settings. Listen for the 'lonesome' vocal tone with no vibrato, and for tunes that end on a note other than the one a hymn would lead you to expect.","Received quietly, the record became a cornerstone for revival singers looking for an authentic source that was not a scratchy 78. Ritchie did more than anyone to spread the mountain dulcimer. Her family song 'Nottamun Town' later surfaced as the tune of Dylan's 'Masters of War', which led to a quiet settlement.","Listen with the lights low. Notice the dulcimer's steady drone and how the voice floats free of any beat. Then play Vaughan Williams's English Folk Song Suite or his Five Variants of Dives and Lazarus to hear the same family of tunes dressed for orchestra, and Copland's Appalachian Spring for a composer's imagined mountains."],"alan-lomax-parchman-prison-songs":["Groups of men singing as they work: a leader calls a line and the gang answers, while axes and hoes fall on the beat, so the percussion is the work itself. Some songs are slow and heavy, timed to felling trees; others are quicker, for hoeing. The voices are rough, powerful and tightly together, bending notes and holding long, wavering tones. 'Rosie' and 'Early in the Mornin'' are among the pieces a listener will not forget.","Alan Lomax, who had first visited Parchman Farm with his father John in the 1930s, returned in 1947 and 1948 to record the prisoners, most of them Black men serving time under a system that worked much like slavery. The recordings were issued a decade later on a small New York folk label, Tradition, and reached a white student audience that had never imagined such music existed.","The songs have a job: they set a pace everyone can keep, so that no one is singled out by guards for falling behind, and they carry coded complaint about sentences, women and the 'captain'. Listen for the moment when the axe strokes land a fraction behind the downbeat of the leader's line; that tiny delay is how a gang of twenty stays together.","The record was admired by folklorists and blues scholars and influenced musicians from Odetta to later blues-rock bands. Today it is also discussed for the ethics of recording captive people who could not refuse, and for how Lomax's framing shaped what outsiders heard. Reissued by Rounder as Prison Songs, it remains one of the essential documents of African American music.","Play it loud and let the rhythm of the tools take over. Pick one song and follow only the answering chorus. Then play Lead Belly's 'Midnight Special', another prison song, and Johnny Cash's At Folsom Prison, which turned the prison concert into a stage. The Rounder CD sounds best."],"odetta-sings-ballads-and-blues":["One voice, huge and dark, with a guitar strummed so hard it sounds like a drum. Odetta sings work songs, spirituals, prison songs and blues: 'Water Boy', 'Muleskinner Blues', 'Another Man Done Gone', and a spiritual medley that includes 'Oh, Freedom'. She moves from a whisper to a full, trained contralto roar within a line, and her guitar pounds out a single rhythmic pattern that drives every song.","Odetta Holmes, born in Birmingham, Alabama, and raised in Los Angeles, trained as a classical singer and worked in musical theatre before turning to folk song in the San Francisco coffeehouses. This first solo album came out on Tradition, the small New York label run by the Clancy Brothers' circle, in the year of the Montgomery bus boycott. Her songs of forced labour and defiance were heard as civil rights statements.","Listen for her 'hammer' on the guitar: she strums on the beat with the force of a work gang, which is why 'Water Boy' sounds like a prison song even without axes. Her classical training shows in the breath control on the long notes. Bob Dylan said that hearing this record in a Minneapolis shop made him trade his electric guitar for an acoustic.","The album made her a star of the revival and was widely copied. Martin Luther King Jr. is reported to have called her the queen of American folk music. Later critics note how she turned field material into concert performance, the same shift that made the revival possible, and how her authority as a Black woman gave that shift its moral weight.","Start with 'Water Boy' and let the guitar's pounding pull you in, then 'Oh, Freedom', which she sang at the March on Washington in 1963. Compare the Parchman work-song recordings for the source and the Weavers for the gentle alternative. Any Tradition reissue is fine."],"the-weavers-at-carnegie-hall":["A live concert: four voices, Pete Seeger's banjo, Fred Hellerman's guitar and an audience that sings along. The set list ranges across the world, from 'Wimoweh' (the South African song later known as 'The Lion Sleeps Tonight') to 'Kisses Sweeter Than Wine', 'Darling Corey' and songs in Hebrew and Spanish. Without the studio strings, the harmonies are close and bright, and Lee Hays's deep bass anchors the chorus.","Blacklisted and dropped by Decca, the Weavers had broken up in 1952. Their manager Harold Leventhal booked Carnegie Hall for Christmas Eve 1955 for a reunion, and it sold out. The young Vanguard label released the recording in 1957. For a generation of students, this record, not the pop hits, was the Weavers: the group as political survivors singing to their own public.","Listen to the audience. The Weavers treat the crowd as a fifth singer, lining out words and pausing for response, a technique borrowed from union meetings and churches. Seeger's shrieking falsetto on 'Wimoweh' is his imitation of Solomon Linda's 1939 Zulu recording, whose composer received almost nothing for decades.","It sold steadily for years and inspired the Kingston Trio and dozens of college groups. Critics hear in it the purest statement of the Popular Front folk ideal: a sing-along world music. Later writers have questioned the Weavers' handling of songs like 'Wimoweh' that they borrowed from other cultures, while still treating the concert as a turning point.","Put it on as a concert and listen for the moment the crowd starts to join in. Compare their studio 'Goodnight, Irene' to hear the difference between pop gloss and the real thing. Then try Pete Seeger's We Shall Overcome for the same method put to the civil rights movement."],"elizabeth-cotten-folksongs-and-instrumentals-with-guitar":["Quiet, rolling guitar pieces and a few songs sung in a soft, unassuming voice. The playing is the thing: a steady alternating bass with the thumb and a melody picked above it, sweet and unhurried, as in 'Freight Train' and the parlour-style 'Vastopol'. The songs and rags sound like music made at home for the pleasure of it, never pushing for effect.","Elizabeth Cotten, from Chapel Hill, North Carolina, had played guitar as a girl, then given it up for work and church for decades. In Washington she met the Seeger family after returning young Peggy Seeger, lost in a department store, and went to work as their housekeeper. Years later the family found her playing again, and Mike Seeger recorded her at home. She was in her sixties when this album appeared.","Cotten was left-handed and played a right-handed guitar upside down, so her thumb picks the melody and her fingers play the bass, the reverse of the normal method. This gives her bass a lighter, more melodic lilt. She wrote 'Freight Train' as a girl, around 1906; a British skiffle version in 1957 carried other people's names until she won credit back.","The album spread 'Cotten picking' through the revival; Peggy Seeger, Joan Baez, the Grateful Dead and many others took up her songs. She began touring in her seventies and won a Grammy in 1985. Critics now value her as a composer in her own right rather than a folk curiosity.","Start with 'Freight Train' and listen only to the bass, the steady thumb, then only to the melody on top. Then hear the Anthology's rural guitarists and Mississippi John Hurt's Today! for kindred fingerpicking. The Smithsonian Folkways reissue is the one to have."],"the-kingston-trio-tom-dooley":["A spoken introduction about a love triangle, then a slow, minor-key ballad of a man awaiting execution, sung by three clean-cut young men over a strummed guitar and a plucked banjo. The chorus, telling the condemned man to bow his head, is sung in close harmony and repeated until it lodges in the brain. Short, smooth and catchy, it sounds like a campfire singalong with a gallows in it.","The Kingston Trio, three college students from the San Francisco Bay Area, recorded it on their first Capitol album; a disc jockey's airplay made it a surprise number one in late 1958. It sold in the millions and set off a craze for collegiate folk groups, banjos and striped shirts. With the Weavers blacklisted, this was a politically safe folk song for suburban America.","The song is about a real killing: Tom Dula was hanged in Statesville, North Carolina, in 1868 for the murder of Laura Foster. The trio's version came from the revival's collectors, who had learned it from the North Carolina singer Frank Proffitt; Proffitt's family had sung it for generations, and he later won a share of the royalties.","Purists scorned the trio's polish and lack of politics, but their record made folk commercially viable and opened doors for Baez, Dylan and Peter, Paul and Mary. It won a Grammy in the first year of the awards, oddly in a country category. Historians now see it as the start of the revival's mass phase.","Listen once for the tune, then read the story of Tom Dula's 1868 trial and listen again: the calm, pretty harmonies sit oddly against a hanging. Next find Doc Watson's version, which he learned in his own North Carolina family, for the mountain original with its harder edge. Finish with the Weavers at Carnegie Hall to hear the political sing-along the trio carefully avoided."],"the-new-lost-city-ramblers-the-new-lost-city-ramblers":["A string band of three young New Yorkers reproducing the sound of the rural records of the 1920s and 1930s: sawing fiddle, frailing banjo, a steady guitar and nasal, high-pitched voices. The songs are old-time dance tunes, comic songs and ballads. The performances are rough on purpose, with the rhythmic drive and loose tuning of the originals.","Mike Seeger, John Cohen and Tom Paley formed the group in 1958 to play the music they had found on old 78s and Harry Smith's Anthology. Against the smooth commercial folk of the Kingston Trio, they argued for faithfulness to the originals. Folkways released their debut, and they toured colleges and festivals, often bringing the older musicians they admired onto the same stage.","Mike Seeger was the son of Ruth Crawford Seeger, the modernist composer who spent her last years transcribing folk songs for the Lomaxes, and half-brother of Pete Seeger. Listen for how they swap instruments between songs to match the lineups of particular old records, a kind of historically informed performance before the term existed in folk music.","Critics praised their scholarship and some found them stiff. Their larger achievement was to point audiences back to the older musicians, several of whom were rediscovered and recorded partly because of them. The 'old-time' revival of later decades descends directly from their example.","Pick a tune and then find its source on the Anthology to hear how close they come. Listen to the fiddle's short, bouncing bow strokes. Then hear Old Time Music at Clarence Ashley's for the real thing recorded in the 1960s."],"alan-lomax-sounds-of-the-south":["A series of LPs recorded across the American South: Black and white church singing, fife-and-drum bands, mountain fiddlers, blues guitarists, prison singers, children's game songs and banjo players. The sound is unusually vivid for field recordings, with the fiddles and voices placed in stereo space. It works as a musical atlas of the region at the end of the 1950s, before much of it vanished.","Lomax had spent most of the 1950s in Britain and Europe, partly to escape the blacklist. Back home in 1959 he set out on a 'Southern Journey' with the English singer Shirley Collins and a stereo tape machine, recording in homes, churches and prisons. Atlantic, a rhythm and blues label, issued the results in 1960, bringing the music to a far larger audience than most field recordings reached.","This trip produced the first recordings of the Mississippi hill-country guitarist Fred McDowell, who became a revival star. Listen for the fife-and-drum pieces, a tradition of Black marching music from northern Mississippi that preserves military drumming alongside African rhythmic ideas. Lomax's archive later became a sampling source; Moby's Play (1999) built several hits on his field recordings.","Hailed as a landmark in audio documentation, the series influenced a generation of musicians and scholars. Lomax's romantic framing of 'vanishing' traditions has been debated, but the recordings themselves are treated as essential. Atlantic's 1993 CD reissue brought them back into print.","Choose one disc and let it play as an unfolding landscape. Try the fife-and-drum tracks first for a sound you will not hear elsewhere, then the church singing. Pair with the Anthology for the 1920s view of the same region and Bartók's Romanian recordings for another collector's journey."],"joan-baez-joan-baez":["A young soprano with a crystal-clear, fast vibrato and a steady fingerpicked guitar. The songs are old ballads and folk songs: 'Silver Dagger', 'House of the Rising Sun', 'Mary Hamilton', 'Henry Martin', 'East Virginia', 'Wildwood Flower', and the Mexican 'El Preso Número Nueve'. Each is sung simply, with no drama, the purity of the voice doing the emotional work.","Baez, nineteen, had built a following in the Boston and Cambridge coffeehouses and made a stir at the 1959 Newport Folk Festival. Vanguard recorded this album with minimal production. It sold steadily and made her the revival's first female star; within two years she was on the cover of Time. Her mother's Scottish roots and father's Mexican heritage are both reflected in the songs.","Several songs are Child ballads, numbered in Francis James Child's nineteenth-century collection of English and Scottish ballads: 'Mary Hamilton' is Child 173, 'Henry Martin' Child 250. Their tunes reached Baez partly through American mountain versions, via singers like Jean Ritchie. Listen to how she holds the last note of a verse longer than the beat, lingering like a church singer.","Critics raved about the voice, and the album stayed on the charts for well over a year, a rare feat for a record of traditional songs with one guitar. Some later listeners found the purity cool and distant beside Odetta or Jean Ritchie. Historians now see it as the moment a middle-class audience embraced old ballads as serious art, and as the template for the female folk singer that Judy Collins and others followed.","Start with 'Silver Dagger' and notice how little the guitar does. Then try 'Mary Hamilton' and follow the story of a queen's lady hanged for infanticide. Compare the Carter Family's 'Wildwood Flower' for the guitar style she borrows, and Vaughan Williams's folk settings for the ballads' English cousins."],"pete-seeger-where-have-all-the-flowers-gone":["A slow, gentle circle of questions and answers that follows flowers to the young women who pick them, the women to the men they marry, the men to war, the soldiers to graves, and the graves back to flowers. Seeger sings it quietly over a picked accompaniment. A rueful refrain asking whether people will ever learn turns a children's chain-song into an anti-war lament.","Seeger wrote the first verses in 1955 after reading a Cossack song quoted in Mikhail Sholokhov's novel And Quiet Flows the Don, during the years he was blacklisted and called before the House Un-American Activities Committee. Joe Hickerson added verses in 1960 that closed the circle. The Kingston Trio and Peter, Paul and Mary made it a hit, and Marlene Dietrich sang it in German.","The circular form is the whole meaning: the song ends where it began, with no resolution, because war repeats. The 'flowers' of the Sholokhov source are part of an old Russian song pattern, so the song is itself a folk process: a novel quoting a folk song, a folk singer quoting the novel.","It became one of the most translated protest songs of the century and was sung across the Cold War divide. Some found it sentimental; for others its simplicity was the point. The Kingston Trio's early credit of 'traditional' caused a rights dispute that Seeger resolved amicably.","Hear Seeger's own quiet version first and follow the chain from verse to verse until it closes on itself. Then play Marlene Dietrich's German recording, a weary cabaret lament from a woman who had sung for Allied troops. Compare the Kingston Trio's bright, strummed version for how much a tempo changes a song. Finally turn to Britten's War Requiem, finished two years later, for a composer's answer to the same question."],"clarence-ashley-old-time-music-at-clarence-ashleys":["A group of neighbours from the mountains of northeastern Tennessee and western North Carolina playing old-time songs and tunes: Clarence Ashley's clawhammer banjo, Doc Watson's flatpicked guitar, a fiddle, and several voices singing ballads and hymns. The music is dense and rhythmic, the voices high and hard. It sounds like a front-porch party, which it partly was.","Ashley had recorded in the late 1920s; his 'The Coo Coo Bird' was on Harry Smith's Anthology. The folklorist Ralph Rinzler and the collector Eugene Earle found him in 1960 and recorded him with local musicians, including a blind guitarist named Doc Watson who was then playing electric guitar in a dance band. The album brought both to the Newport and college festival circuit.","Listen for Doc Watson's guitar: he had been playing rockabilly, and Rinzler persuaded him to play acoustic, flatpicking fiddle tunes at high speed. That style changed the guitar's role in bluegrass and folk. Ashley's banjo is tuned to open chords that give his songs their eerie modal sound.","The record was praised for capturing a living tradition rather than a revival copy. Watson became one of the most admired guitarists in American music. The album's documentary method, recording older musicians at home, became a model for later folklorists.","Start with a banjo song led by Ashley and listen to the clawhammer stroke, a down-picking brush that makes the banjo sound like a drum and a fiddle at once. Then find a track where Doc Watson takes the lead and notice how cleanly and fast he picks single notes. Compare Ashley's 1929 'The Coo Coo Bird' on the Anthology to hear what thirty years did to his voice, and what they did not."],"pete-seeger-turn-turn-turn":["The words are almost entirely from the third chapter of Ecclesiastes, the passage that pairs a season for every opposite: birth and death, planting and harvest, war and peace. Seeger added only the three-word refrain of the title and a closing line pleading that peace is still possible. His own recording is plain, over a strummed twelve-string guitar, with a melody that rises gently on each 'turn'.","Seeger wrote it in the late 1950s and recorded it for Columbia around 1962, while still fighting a contempt-of-Congress conviction for refusing to name names. Judy Collins recorded it in 1963 with Roger McGuinn as arranger, and McGuinn's band, the Byrds, took it to number one in late 1965 with electric twelve-string guitar and harmonies, at the height of the Vietnam escalation.","The one line Seeger added, the plea for peace at the end, turns an ancient text about acceptance into a protest. The song is also a sly argument for folk process: Seeger always said he had only arranged the words of 'the original writer', and used the song to show that old texts could be made new by singing.","The Byrds' version is far better known and made the song a folk-rock landmark. Seeger's own recording is now valued as the source and a reminder of his craft as a songwriter. It is still one of the few number-one hits drawn from the Bible.","Listen to Seeger's version with its plain strum, then the Byrds' 1965 single, and notice how Roger McGuinn's chiming electric twelve-string and the stacked harmonies turn a sermon into a pop record. Judy Collins's 1963 recording sits between the two. Then read the third chapter of Ecclesiastes aloud and hear how little Seeger had to change to make it singable."],"peter-paul-and-mary-peter-paul-and-mary":["Two men and a woman with two guitars and close, polished harmonies. The album includes 'Lemon Tree', 'If I Had a Hammer', '500 Miles' and 'Where Have All the Flowers Gone?'. Mary Travers's strong voice leads and the men's voices wrap around it. The sound is clean, warm and precise, perfectly pitched for college dormitories and suburban living rooms.","The trio was assembled in Greenwich Village by the manager Albert Grossman, who also managed Dylan, as a commercial folk group with a political edge. Warner Bros. released this debut in 1962 and it became one of the best-selling albums of the decade's first half. 'If I Had a Hammer', written by Pete Seeger and Lee Hays of the Weavers, became a civil rights song in their hands.","The arrangements are more complex than they seem: listen for how the three voices trade melody and harmony line by line, and for the counter-melodies that the guitars and voices weave. 'If I Had a Hammer' had been written in 1949 for a left-wing cause and was a flop; the trio's version made it a hit and a movement song.","Purists called them commercial, but their success carried Dylan's songs to a mass audience and gave the civil rights movement a soundtrack on the radio. They sang at the March on Washington in 1963. The album won Grammys and remains a best-selling folk record.","Play 'If I Had a Hammer' and listen for how the voices divide the verse. Then hear the Weavers at Carnegie Hall to hear what the trio learned, and Dylan's own 'Blowin' in the Wind' to see how differently a song can sound."],"bob-dylan-blowin-in-the-wind":["Three verses, each a string of questions about how long war, injustice and indifference must go on before people see them. The answer is always the same, and always withheld: it is out there, carried on the air, for anyone willing to catch it. Dylan sings it plainly, strumming a guitar, with a harmonica break between verses. It is short and simple enough that anyone can sing it.","Dylan, twenty, wrote it in April 1962 and first sang it at Gerde's Folk City in Greenwich Village. It appeared on his second album in May 1963, but Peter, Paul and Mary's cover a month later made it a hit. The song became an anthem of the civil rights movement and was sung at the March on Washington. Sam Cooke was so moved that a Black songwriter had not written it that he wrote 'A Change Is Gonna Come' in response.","The melody is borrowed from the spiritual 'No More Auction Block', which Dylan had heard in the Village, perhaps in Odetta's version. The chord pattern repeats with minimal variation, so the song's force comes from the words and the unchanging refrain. Dylan later said it was not a protest song but a song about the mystery of indifference.","It made Dylan famous and the spokesman he did not want to be. Some critics find the lyrics vague; others argue that vagueness is what made it universal. It has been recorded hundreds of times and is taught in schools as a key text of the 1960s.","Hear Dylan's own version first, rough and quick, then Peter, Paul and Mary's, which sold far more and made the song an anthem: notice how their harmonies turn a question into a hymn. Then play Sam Cooke's 'A Change Is Gonna Come' and hear one song answering another. Finally seek out a recording of the spiritual 'No More Auction Block' to hear the tune's roots in slavery."],"bob-dylan-a-hard-rains-a-gonna-fall":["Seven minutes of apocalyptic visions, sung over a steady strum. Each verse opens with a parent questioning a son about where he has been and what he has seen, and the answer is a torrent of images of a wounded world: poisoned landscapes, empty roads, crowds who cannot speak or be heard. The refrain warns of a coming deluge. The lines pile up relentlessly until the final verse becomes the son's vow to go back out and bear witness.","Dylan wrote it in the autumn of 1962 and sang it first at a Carnegie Hall hootenanny that September, weeks before the Cuban missile crisis; many listeners later assumed it was written during the crisis. It appeared on The Freewheelin' Bob Dylan in 1963. Pete Seeger called it the song that would outlive all the others from those years.","The question-and-answer frame comes straight from the old British ballad 'Lord Randal', Child 12, in which a mother asks her son where he has been, and he answers that he has been poisoned. Dylan keeps the form and replaces the story with a vision. Listen for how each answer-line starts with the same words, a litany like a preacher's.","It is often called Dylan's first great poem and was cited when he won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2016; Patti Smith sang it at the ceremony and stumbled on a verse. Critics still argue about whether it is about nuclear war or something broader.","Give it full attention once: seven minutes, no chorus to rest in, and a guitar that barely changes, so the images carry everything. Then look up Patti Smith's performance at the 2016 Nobel ceremony, where her nerves and recovery made the song new. Finally find a traditional singing of 'Lord Randal' and hear the old question-and-answer frame that Dylan borrowed and filled."],"bob-dylan-the-freewheelin-bob-dylan":["Thirteen songs, nearly all Dylan's own, sung to his guitar and harmonica. Protest anthems like 'Blowin' in the Wind' and 'Masters of War' sit beside love songs like 'Girl from the North Country' and 'Don't Think Twice, It's All Right', and comic talking blues like 'Talkin' World War III Blues'. The voice is nasal and rough, the guitar steady and simple, and the words are the main attraction.","Dylan's first album had sold poorly, and Columbia executives called him 'Hammond's folly' after John Hammond, who had signed him. This second album, recorded over a year and released in May 1963, made him a star. The cover shows Dylan and his girlfriend Suze Rotolo walking down a snowy Greenwich Village street. Her absence in Italy during the recording coloured the love songs.","Several melodies come from British folk songs Dylan learned from Martin Carthy in London in the winter of 1962–63: 'Girl from the North Country' borrows from 'Scarborough Fair', and 'Bob Dylan's Dream' from 'Lord Franklin'. 'Masters of War' uses Jean Ritchie's family song 'Nottamun Town'. Dylan had also heard an advance copy of Robert Johnson's King of the Delta Blues Singers from Hammond.","It was hailed at once and has stayed central to the singer-songwriter canon. Critics see it as the record that turned folk singers into writers of their own material. Some purists missed the traditional songs; many listeners felt a new kind of voice had arrived.","Take three songs in a row for the album's range: 'Girl from the North Country' for tenderness, 'Masters of War' for fury, 'Talkin' World War III Blues' for comedy. Then play Martin Carthy's 'Scarborough Fair' and Jean Ritchie's family songs to hear the tunes Dylan borrowed, and Guthrie's Dust Bowl Ballads for the talking-blues model. The mono mix is closest to what listeners heard in 1963."],"pete-seeger-we-shall-overcome":["A live concert recorded at Carnegie Hall in June 1963, with Seeger alone on stage with banjo and twelve-string guitar, leading an audience that becomes his choir. The programme is freedom songs and topical songs, climaxing with the title song, sung slowly by the whole hall. The effect is of a mass meeting as much as a concert, with Seeger lining out each verse before the crowd sings it back.","'We Shall Overcome' came from a hymn by the Black Methodist minister Charles Albert Tindley and was adapted by striking tobacco workers in the 1940s. Zilphia Horton taught it at the Highlander Folk School, where Seeger learned it and altered the words; Guy Carawan brought it to the student sit-in movement in 1960. The concert came months before the March on Washington, while Seeger was still banned from network television.","Seeger is credited with changing one word of the refrain, 'will' to 'shall', saying the open vowel sang better. Listen for how he slows the tempo until the song almost stops, which lets a thousand people sing together. He also sings songs by younger writers such as Malvina Reynolds and Bob Dylan, using his platform to spread them.","The album spread the song worldwide and helped make it the anthem of civil rights movements from South Africa to Northern Ireland. Some historians emphasise that Black activists, not Seeger, made the song what it became; Seeger always said the same.","Play the album in order and save the title track for last, as the concert intends: notice how the audience grows bolder with each song until the hall is singing on its own. Then hear Curtis Mayfield's 'People Get Ready' for the same hope in soul music, and Frederic Rzewski's North American Ballads, which turn protest songs of the same tradition into virtuoso concert piano pieces."],"bob-dylan-the-times-they-are-a-changin":["Ten songs, spare and dark, just voice, guitar and harmonica. The title song is a hymn-like warning to parents and politicians. 'The Lonesome Death of Hattie Carroll' tells of a Black barmaid killed by a rich white man's cane. 'Only a Pawn in Their Game' is about the murder of Medgar Evers. 'With God on Our Side' marches through American wars. The mood is grave, the voice harsh.","Dylan recorded it in the autumn of 1963, the season of the March on Washington and the Birmingham church bombing, and Columbia released it in January 1964. It is his most topical album, and his last before he turned away from protest. He had sung 'Only a Pawn in Their Game' at a voter-registration rally in Greenwood, Mississippi, that summer.","'With God on Our Side' takes its melody from Dominic Behan's 'The Patriot Game', itself from an Irish folk tune. 'Hattie Carroll' is built with a refrain that tells mourners to wait: only in the last verse, when the killer receives a six-month sentence, does Dylan tell them to weep. Listen for how he withholds the moral until then.","On release some critics found it preachy and grim after the wit of Freewheelin', and Dylan himself soon disowned the role of spokesman. Many now consider 'The Lonesome Death of Hattie Carroll' among his finest songs, a model of restraint in narrative. The title song has become a generational motto, often borrowed by the very politicians and advertisers it warned, which says something about how protest songs age.","Start with 'The Lonesome Death of Hattie Carroll' and hear the whole story before judging, noticing how the refrain shifts only in the last verse. Then the title song, sung like a hymn from a pulpit. Compare Phil Ochs's I Ain't Marching Anymore for another singing journalist, and Sam Cooke's 'A Change Is Gonna Come', released the same year, for the civil rights era heard from the other side."],"buffy-sainte-marie-its-my-way":["A startling voice, with a strong vibrato and sudden swoops into a high, piercing register, over a bold, driving guitar. The songs are her own: 'Now That the Buffalo's Gone', about the treatment of Native Americans; 'Cod'ine', about addiction; 'Universal Soldier', about who makes war. In 'Cripple Creek' she plays the mouth bow, a single-string instrument held against the mouth, which gives an eerie twang.","Sainte-Marie, raised in New England, had become a star of the Greenwich Village and Toronto coffeehouses while still in college. Vanguard released this debut in 1964. She presented herself as a Cree woman adopted in infancy, and her songs about Indigenous rights were among the first in popular music. In 2023 a CBC investigation produced evidence that she was born in Massachusetts to a white family, and her identity became the subject of intense controversy.","Listen to how she uses her voice as an instrument, with sudden changes of timbre and vibrato. 'Cod'ine' was drawn from her own experience of addiction after being prescribed the drug; it was covered by Quicksilver Messenger Service and others. The mouth bow on 'Cripple Creek' is an Appalachian and Indigenous instrument, the kind of detail she used to widen the revival's sound.","The album made her a name, and 'Universal Soldier' became an anti-war standard. Her songs about Indigenous people were celebrated as a breakthrough. The 2023 revelations complicated her legacy: some honours were withdrawn, while the songs remain in circulation and are still debated.","Start with 'Now That the Buffalo's Gone', then 'Cod'ine', and listen to how her voice changes colour, from a near-whisper to a sudden, piercing cry with a wide vibrato. Then play 'Cripple Creek' for the twang of the mouth bow. Compare Joan Baez's debut of 1960 to hear two very different female voices in the same revival, one pure and still, the other restless."],"buffy-sainte-marie-universal-soldier":["A short, sharp song over a steady guitar. The verses describe one soldier who is every soldier, of every height, age, faith and nation, fighting for whichever flag he is given. The argument is that he is the one who offers his body to war, and that without his consent the wars could not happen. The ending lays the blame on the soldier, not only on the leaders.","Sainte-Marie wrote it in the early 1960s, before the big escalation in Vietnam, and included it on her debut album. The Scottish singer Donovan made it a hit in 1965. She later said the song was kept off American radio in the Johnson years, though that is hard to document.","The song's force comes from its list form: each verse widens the soldier to include more kinds of people until he stands for everyone, and only then does the argument turn toward the listener. The guitar pattern never changes, which keeps attention on the words. Listen for how she presses slightly ahead of the beat as the list grows, building urgency without raising her voice.","Donovan's 1965 cover made it a hit in Britain and spread it across the anti-war movement. Some critics found the argument simplistic, since conscripts rarely chose to fight; others saw it as one of the sharpest songs of the era precisely because it refuses to let anyone off. It remains widely sung, though the 2023 controversy over its writer's identity now shadows discussion of her work.","Hear Sainte-Marie's own version, short and fierce, then Donovan's gentler, more wistful cover and notice how the same words shift in a softer voice. Compare Seeger's 'Where Have All the Flowers Gone?' for a lament that blames no one, and Phil Ochs's 'I Ain't Marching Anymore' for a soldier's refusal told in the first person."],"dave-van-ronk-house-of-the-rising-sun":["A gravelly, bluesy voice over a guitar that walks a slow descending bass line under ringing chords. The song is an old ballad of ruin in a New Orleans house of ill repute. Van Ronk sings it with a rough tenderness, stretching the lines and letting the guitar's minor-key progression carry the mood.","Van Ronk, the leading guitarist and teacher of the Greenwich Village folk scene, developed this arrangement around 1960, drawing on older recordings. Bob Dylan learned it from him and recorded it on his debut album in 1962 without permission; Van Ronk stopped playing it because audiences thought he was copying Dylan. The Animals then used Dylan's version for their 1964 electric hit.","The descending bass line in Van Ronk's arrangement, walking down the chords, is the core of the Animals' hit and of every rock band's version since. The song itself goes back at least to the 1930s, when Alan Lomax recorded it from Georgia Turner in Kentucky.","Van Ronk's own recording is far less known than Dylan's or the Animals', and for years he got little credit for the arrangement that made them famous. Folk historians now recognise it as the source of the song's modern form. The Coen brothers' film Inside Llewyn Davis (2013), loosely drawn from his memoir, brought new listeners to his records, and his reputation as the Village's great teacher has only grown.","Play Van Ronk, then Dylan's 1962 debut, then the Animals' 1964 single, and trace the same walking bass line through all three: acoustic, then acoustic and hurried, then electric with organ. Then listen to Joan Baez's version on her first album, which follows a different, older melody. Finally hear Van Ronk's blues records for the ragtime fingerpicking behind his reputation."],"phil-ochs-i-aint-marching-anymore":["A clear, earnest tenor over a strong strummed guitar, delivering songs that read like editorials. The title song marches through American wars from the Revolution to Hiroshima and refuses to go on. 'Draft Dodger Rag' is a comic list of excuses to avoid the draft. 'Here's to the State of Mississippi' is a scathing attack on segregation. 'That Was the President' mourns John F. Kennedy.","Ochs, a journalism student turned folk singer, called himself a 'singing journalist' and titled his first album All the News That's Fit to Sing. This second album came out on Elektra in 1965, as the Vietnam War escalated. He was more openly political than Dylan and never turned away from protest, which limited his commercial success.","The title song hides a drumbeat inside the guitar strum, a march rhythm for a song that refuses to march. Its verses move in order through American wars, from the Revolution to Hiroshima, so the song doubles as a history lesson told by one soldier who has fought them all. 'Draft Dodger Rag' works the other way, using comedy to deliver the same refusal.","Ochs never approached Dylan's fame, and critics sometimes found him too literal, more headline than poetry. That directness is now seen as his strength: his songs are still sung at protests because their meaning cannot be mistaken. His reputation has grown steadily since his death in 1976, helped by documentaries and by younger singer-songwriters who cite him.","Start with 'Draft Dodger Rag' for the humour and the rapid patter, then the title song, and notice the march hiding in the strum. 'That Was the President' shows his gentler side. Compare Dylan's The Times They Are a-Changin' for a similar approach with more poetry, and Buffy Sainte-Marie's 'Universal Soldier' for another song about who bears responsibility for war."],"bob-dylan-bringing-it-all-back-home":["Side one is electric: a band with electric guitars, bass and drums backs Dylan on 'Subterranean Homesick Blues', 'Maggie's Farm' and 'She Belongs to Me'. Side two is acoustic, with four long songs: 'Mr. Tambourine Man', 'Gates of Eden', 'It's Alright, Ma (I'm Only Bleeding)' and 'It's All Over Now, Baby Blue'. The lyrics are surreal and allusive, far from the protest songs of two years earlier.","Recorded in January 1965 and released in March, the album marked Dylan's break with the folk revival. Four months later he played electric at the Newport Folk Festival, provoking boos and a legend that Pete Seeger wanted to cut the cables with an axe. The Byrds' electric 'Mr. Tambourine Man' reached number one that summer and launched folk rock.","The acoustic side is the real farewell to folk: 'It's All Over Now, Baby Blue' reads as a goodbye to the movement. 'Maggie's Farm' is a refusal to work for someone else's cause. Listen for how the acoustic songs are longer and more complex than anything on his earlier albums.","Folk purists were dismayed by the electric side and by lyrics that no longer took sides; rock critics hailed it as a turning point. It reached the top ten in America and number one in Britain. It is now seen as the album where folk and rock merged and the modern singer-songwriter was born, with the acoustic 'It's Alright, Ma' often ranked among Dylan's greatest songs.","Play side two first: four long acoustic songs that are the true farewell to the revival. Then side one, and hear the band crash in with 'Subterranean Homesick Blues'. Compare the Byrds' electric 'Mr. Tambourine Man' of the same year, and Seeger's 'Turn! Turn! Turn!' for the folk-rock connection. The original mono mix has the most punch."],"joan-baez-diamonds-and-rust":["A reflective song over a gently rocking band: an unexpected phone call from an old lover stirs memories of a shabby New York hotel room and a winter long ago. The chorus, with its ringing high notes, weighs those memories as something both precious and corroded, the image of the title. Baez's voice is lower and warmer than in 1960.","By 1975 Baez had moved from traditional ballads to writing her own songs, and this is the best known of them. It was the title song of an album on A&M, produced by David Kershenbaum with Los Angeles session players, and her biggest commercial success. The lover it recalls is widely understood to be Bob Dylan, with whom she had a relationship in the early 1960s and who was about to invite her on his Rolling Thunder tour.","The song's images are specific and personal: the phone call, the hotel, the gift of cufflinks. Baez later said she was surprised Dylan did not recognise himself. The song compresses the folk revival's story, its idealism and its disillusion, into one relationship.","Critics praised it as her finest original song, and the album went gold. Judas Priest's heavy metal cover in 1977 showed how far the melody could travel from folk. It is now seen as a classic of the singer-songwriter era and a rare case of the revival looking back on itself with both affection and irony, from the woman who had been at its centre.","Listen first for the story, then for the chords shifting under the chorus and the high notes she holds there. Compare her 1960 debut to hear how her voice lowered and warmed in fifteen years. Then play Dylan's Freewheelin' for the young man she remembers, and Judas Priest's cover for a startling second life."],"child-ballad-56-dives-and-lazarus":["A short ballad retelling the parable from Luke: the rich man Dives feasts while the beggar Lazarus starves at his gate; both die, angels carry Lazarus to Abraham's bosom, and serpents carry Dives to hell, where he begs for a drop of water. The verses are plain and quick, with no refrain. The tune most often sung to it is a wide-ranging modal melody that rises through an octave and settles gravely, one of the most recognisable shapes in English folk song.","A ballad of the rich man and poor Lazarus was licensed to a London printer in 1557, making this one of the oldest English ballad subjects on record. Francis James Child included it as number 56 of his English and Scottish Popular Ballads (1882–1898), the Harvard professor's attempt to fix the canon of traditional narrative song. The tune now associated with it was collected in the early twentieth century by Vaughan Williams and others, in several regional variants.","The melody has had several lives under other names. Vaughan Williams set a version of it as the hymn tune Kingsfold in the English Hymnal of 1906, and the same family of tunes carries the Irish song The Star of the County Down. When you hear any of them you are hearing cousins of the one melody, bent a little by different singers and districts.","Child printed only one text of this ballad, from an eighteenth-century source, and some scholars have doubted how widely it circulated orally in Britain. Its fame now rests almost as much on the tune as on the words, largely because Vaughan Williams made it the backbone of an orchestral work in 1939. Singers argue whether the fierce moral ending should be softened; traditional versions never soften it.","Hear it first sung unaccompanied, so the tune's long arch stands clear. Then play Vaughan Williams's Five Variants of Dives and Lazarus for strings and harp and notice how he treats the melody as a hymn. Finish with a sung version of The Star of the County Down and see if you can hear the family resemblance."],"child-ballad-10-the-twa-sisters":["A jealous elder sister pushes the younger into a river and lets her drown rather than lose a suitor. The body drifts to a mill; a passing minstrel makes a harp from her breastbone and strings it with her golden hair, and at the wedding feast the harp sings on its own and names the murderess. The ballad moves in two-line verses, each followed by a nonsense or refrain line such as Binnorie, O Binnorie, which keeps coming back like water.","A broadside printed in 1656 as The Miller and the King's Daughter is the earliest English text, but the story of a singing bone that accuses its killer is found right across northern Europe, especially in Scandinavia. Child gathered more than twenty variants for number 10 of his collection. The ballad lived on in Scotland, England, Ireland and the Appalachians, where the harp motif often fell away and the miller who robs the corpse took over.","Watch the refrain. In many versions it is a Scottish place name, Binnorie, sung after every line, so the listener hears the scene of the crime again and again before the murder is even told. Pentangle's 1970 title track Cruel Sister uses a different refrain, 'lay the bent to the bonny broom', and the change makes it feel like a spell instead of a lament.","Folklorists have long used this ballad as a test case for how songs cross languages: is the English text a translation from Norse, or did the story travel by many routes at once? Child's heavily Scottish selection has also been questioned by later collectors who found vigorous English and American variants he did not print. The supernatural harp is the part singers argue over keeping.","Choose an unaccompanied Scottish version first and let the refrain lull you before the push. Then hear Pentangle's Cruel Sister for the same story in a chamber-folk setting, and an Appalachian version, often titled The Old Miller, to hear how American singers trimmed the magic away."],"child-ballad-2-the-elfin-knight":["A riddle ballad in the form of a contest. A man, in the oldest texts an elf, sets a woman impossible tasks: make a shirt without seam or needlework, wash it in a dry well. She answers with equally impossible tasks for him: plough an acre between salt water and the sea strand, reap it with a sickle of leather. The verses carry a refrain of herbs, most famously parsley, sage, rosemary and thyme, and whoever runs out of answers first loses.","The earliest printed text is a broadside of around 1670, 'A Proper New Ballad Entitled The Wind Hath Blown My Plaid Away', where the elf is a supernatural suitor to be outwitted. Over three centuries the song lost its elf and became a love-token exchange; one branch settled in the north of England as Scarborough Fair, sung with the herb refrain. Child placed it second in his collection, just after another riddle ballad.","The herb refrain is not decoration. Parsley, sage, rosemary and thyme were all plants associated with protection and fidelity, and in riddle ballads the woman's wit is her defence against being carried off. In the oldest version she wins by refusing to be trapped. Later versions that sound merely wistful have quietly dropped the danger.","Scarborough Fair became world-famous through Martin Carthy's arrangement of 1965 and then Simon and Garfunkel's recording of 1966, which borrowed Carthy's setting without credit and caused a long quarrel, patched up only decades later. Folklorists treat the whole family as one ballad; most listeners have never heard of the elf, and the song's supernatural origin is now its least known fact.","Listen for the structure of the contest: task, counter-task, refrain. Start with a traditional English Scarborough Fair, then find a text that keeps the elfin knight so you hear the menace return. Compare Martin Carthy's guitar setting with the duo's hit to hear how one arrangement travelled."],"child-ballad-12-lord-randal":["A mother questions her son, just home from hunting. Where have you been? What did you eat? What became of your hounds? Each answer ends 'mother, make my bed soon, for I'm weary wi' hunting and fain wad lie down', and bit by bit the truth comes out: his sweetheart fed him eels, the dogs that ate the leftovers died, and he is poisoned. The ballad ends with his will, leaving hell and fire to the girl.","Walter Scott printed a Scottish text in his Minstrelsy of the Scottish Border in 1803, though an Italian song on the same story, L'avvelenato, is documented earlier, and the question-and-answer form has relatives across Europe. Child gathered many versions as number 12, including English and American ones where the victim is a child poisoned by a stepmother. It remained among the most widely sung ballads in the Appalachians.","Everything is done by indirection: no one ever says 'poison' in some versions. The hounds dying is the moment the listener works it out, and a good singer lets that verse land without emphasis. The final bequest structure, gifts to mother, sister, brother, then curse to sweetheart, is a standard device called the testament, found in several other ballads.","Critics from Scott onward praised Lord Randal as the model of ballad economy. Its most famous descendant is Bob Dylan's A Hard Rain's A-Gonna Fall (1962), which borrows the question-and-answer opening, 'Oh, where have you been, my blue-eyed son?'. Scholars still argue whether the ballad originated in Italy or Scotland; most now assume a shared European tale.","Follow the questions one by one and notice when you understand. Then listen to Dylan's Hard Rain and hear the old frame carrying nuclear dread. An Appalachian version, often called Henry, My Son, shows the song stripped even barer. Notice how the refrain line never changes while the news gets worse."],"child-ballad-58-sir-patrick-spens":["The king of Scotland sits drinking in Dunfermline and wants a skipper to sail to Norway. A knight names Sir Patrick Spens; the king writes a letter; Spens reads it, laughs, then weeps, because it is the wrong season. He sails anyway. The new moon with the old moon in her arm warns of storm; the ship goes down; and the ballad closes on the ladies waiting on the shore with their fans and golden combs, and Spens lying fifty fathoms deep with the Scots lords at his feet.","Bishop Thomas Percy printed the ballad in his Reliques of Ancient English Poetry in 1765, the book that launched the eighteenth-century ballad craze and fed Romanticism. Scholars have tried to link it to real voyages, perhaps the bringing of Margaret, Maid of Norway, in 1290 or an earlier royal marriage, but no single event fits. Child printed more than a dozen versions as number 58.","The wreck is never described. The ballad jumps straight from the omen to the drowned lords' hats floating on the water, a cut as sharp as any film edit. Coleridge quoted the new-moon omen as the epigraph to his Dejection: An Ode of 1802, so a Scottish sea ballad sits at the front door of English Romantic poetry.","Percy's short text, perhaps the best-known, may have been touched up for print, and scholars debate how much of the ballad's famous compression is an editor's work. Poets have admired it without reservation: it is anthologised as literature as often as it is sung. Singers now use longer traditional texts from Scotland alongside Percy's.","Hear it sung by a Scottish traditional singer, and notice the jump from omen to aftermath. Compare the sea music of Britten's Peter Grimes, where a town also watches the water. Then read the words on the page, as the Romantics did."],"child-ballad-84-barbara-allen":["A young man lies dying for love of Barbara Allen, who comes slowly to his bedside and says only 'young man, I think you're dying'. He dies; on her way home she hears the death bell, and seized with remorse she asks her mother to make her bed, and dies too. From his grave grows a rose, from hers a briar, and they twine together in a true lovers' knot. The tunes vary endlessly, but all move at a slow, dignified walk.","Samuel Pepys heard the actress Elizabeth Knepp sing 'her little Scotch song of Barbary Allen' on 2 January 1666 and wrote in his diary that it delighted him. From London theatres and printed broadsides it spread into oral tradition across Britain, Ireland and North America, where collectors found it more often than any other ballad. Child printed it as number 84.","The rose-and-briar ending is a floating motif, shared with several other ballads, and many singers attach it only when they feel like it. Listen for which tune your singer uses: Cecil Sharp alone collected dozens in England and the Appalachians, and the American ones are often in older modes than the English.","Folklorists regard Barbara Allen as the most widely collected ballad in the English language, and it is a favourite test of the singer: to hold attention through many verses with no refrain and almost no action. Arguments continue over whether it began as a Scottish song or a London stage piece dressed as Scottish; Pepys's phrase allows either.","Pick an unaccompanied version and let the slowness work. Then hear an Appalachian singer for a different mode, and Shirley Collins for the English plainness at its purest. Purcell's Dido's Lament makes a good neighbour: the same decades, another woman who asks to lie down and die."],"ewan-maccoll-dirty-old-town":["Four short verses and no chorus, sung to a plain tune in a steady walk. The singer meets his girl by the gasworks wall, dreams a dream by the old canal, kisses her by the factory wall; then a smoky wind blows through the town and he resolves to take a sharp axe to it and cut it down like an old dead tree. The last verse turns the love song into a quiet threat against the place itself.","MacColl wrote it in 1949 to cover a scene change in Landscape with Chimneys, a Theatre Workshop play about his native Salford, the industrial town next to Manchester. It was a working playwright's quick fix, not a planned folk song. MacColl sang it in the clubs and recorded it in the 1950s, and it passed into the repertoire of singers who had no idea who wrote it.","Local councils once tried to stop Salford's streets being associated with the song, and early printings sometimes softened the line about the axe. The shift in the last verse from courting to demolition is the hinge: MacColl was a political songwriter, and the town he wants cut down is the one the mills built, not the one he grew up loving.","The Dubliners made it an Irish pub standard in the late 1960s, and The Pogues' 1985 recording made many listeners assume it was Irish and traditional. MacColl himself was ambivalent about its success. It is now among the most covered British songs of the century, and a textbook case of a new song becoming 'folk' within one lifetime.","Hear MacColl's own spare version first, then The Dubliners' and The Pogues' to see how a Salford song became Dublin's. Listen for how each singer handles the last verse: gentle, bitter or rousing. Then go back to MacColl and ask which version the words themselves seem to want, and why a song about one English town travelled so easily."],"ewan-maccoll-the-first-time-ever-i-saw-your-face":["Three verses, each one line longer in feeling than in words: the first time I saw your face, the first time I kissed your mouth, the first time I lay with you. The tune rises slowly and falls back, with long held notes that leave the singer exposed. There is no chorus and no instrumental break; the whole song is a single slow breath, and most singers take it slower than it looks on paper.","MacColl wrote it in 1957 for Peggy Seeger, the American singer and half-sister of Pete Seeger, with whom he had fallen in love while still married; the story goes that he taught it to her over a transatlantic telephone line when she was touring. Seeger sang it in the folk clubs. It was written in the idiom of a traditional love song by a man who usually wrote about work and politics.","Peggy Seeger's mother was the modernist composer Ruth Crawford Seeger, who in the 1930s and 1940s transcribed Library of Congress field recordings with minute care. The song's plainness came from a household that took traditional singing as seriously as any score. Bert Jansch recorded an early cover on Jack Orion in 1966, before anyone outside the folk clubs knew it.","Roberta Flack's slowed-down recording of 1969 became a transatlantic hit in 1972 after Clint Eastwood used it in Play Misty for Me, and won a Grammy. MacColl reportedly disliked nearly every cover, including hers, finding them over-sung. Critics still divide between the spare folk versions and Flack's six-minute soul reading.","Hear Peggy Seeger sing it unaccompanied first, then Flack, and notice what extra time does to the words. Bert Jansch's guitar version sits between them. Ruth Crawford Seeger's Three Songs are a surprising neighbour from the same family. In every version, listen for where the singer chooses to breathe."],"ewan-maccoll-the-ballad-of-john-axon":["An hour-long radio programme telling the death of a steam-engine driver through songs, instrumental interludes and the recorded voices of railwaymen, his workmates and his widow. There is no narrator. The story, a runaway goods train on a steep gradient and a driver who stayed at the controls, is told in snatches of speech that pass straight into newly written songs in ballad and work-song styles, with fiddle, harmonica, banjo and guitar, and the rhythm of the engine underneath.","On 9 February 1957 John Axon's engine lost its brakes above Chapel-en-le-Frith in Derbyshire; he stayed on the footplate, was killed, and was awarded the George Cross. The BBC producer Charles Parker commissioned MacColl and Peggy Seeger to make a programme, and the three spent days recording railwaymen on portable tape. It was broadcast on the BBC Home Service in July 1958, the first of eight Radio Ballads made until 1964.","The breakthrough was technological: light portable tape recorders let the team collect hours of working people's speech, and instead of rewriting it for actors they cut the actual voices into the programme. MacColl then wrote song lyrics from phrases the railwaymen used, so the songs and the speech share words. It is a documentary made of montage, closer to musique concrète than to a radio play.","Critics at the time hailed it as a new form, and it won the Italia Prize for radio. Some colleagues at the BBC found its politics and its working-class voices uncomfortable. Its reputation has grown with its influence on documentary radio, oral history and later folk-opera projects; the series was revived in tribute in 2006.","Listen straight through without doing anything else, as its first audience did. Notice how often you cannot tell where a speaking voice ends and a song begins. Afterwards hear Britten's Peter Grimes interludes for another tale of working men and hard nature, told in the concert hall."],"shirley-collins-folk-roots-new-routes":["An album-length duet between an English ballad singer and a guitarist steeped in blues, jazz and North African music. Collins sings old songs such as Nottamun Town, Reynardine and Pretty Saro in her unadorned Sussex voice, while Davy Graham plays behind her in open tunings, with drones, bent notes and modal runs that owe as much to Morocco and Monk as to England. Graham also takes solo instrumentals, including a version of Monk's Blue Monk.","Collins had returned in 1959 from the Alan Lomax field trip through the American South and knew traditional music as a world-wide family. Graham, the London guitarist who had recorded the instrumental Anji in 1962, had spent time in Tangier. Decca put the two together in 1964, when the British folk clubs were split between purists and people who wanted to add guitars. The record sold modestly.","Graham tuned his guitar to what became known as DADGAD for some of these tracks, a tuning he had worked out playing Moroccan music; Jansch, Page and hundreds of Celtic guitarists adopted it. Listen for the drone strings he leaves ringing under Collins's voice, which give an English ballad the sound of an oud.","Purists in the clubs complained the accompaniments distracted from the songs. Later critics have treated the album as the moment English folk opened to the wider world, a direct ancestor of Pentangle and of every folk guitarist who plays in DADGAD. It is now considered one of the essential British folk albums.","Start with Nottamun Town and listen to the guitar more than the voice. Then play Graham's solo tracks. Compare with Bert Jansch's first album a year later to hear what he took, and with Mississippi John Hurt's recordings for the American fingerpicking Graham also loved."],"shirley-collins-anthems-in-eden":["Shirley Collins with her sister Dolly, who plays a small portative flute organ, and a consort of early instruments: crumhorns, sackbuts, rebecs and recorders. The first side is 'A Song-Story', a sequence of traditional songs joined by instrumental links into a single suite about an English village before, during and after the First World War. The sound is reedy, buzzing and strange, more like a Tudor household band than a folk club.","Dolly Collins had studied composition and arranged her sister's songs on an instrument built for her, a portable pipe organ small enough to take on tour. The early-music consort came from the circle around David Munrow, then reviving medieval and Renaissance instruments in London. EMI released the album on its new progressive label Harvest, alongside psychedelic rock, in the year of Fairport's Liege & Lief.","The organ is voiced like a chamber organ of Purcell's time, so the harmony has no piano or guitar at all. Dolly's arrangements use the kind of held chords and simple counterpoint Vaughan Williams and Holst put under folk tunes, and the suite form is deliberately like a song cycle. The war story is never stated outright; the choice of songs tells it.","It was broadcast in full by the BBC and admired, but sold slowly. Its reputation climbed with the later interest in English 'wyrd folk' and pastoral psychedelia, and it is now often named, with Liege & Lief, as the other road British folk could have taken: toward early music rather than rock.","Play the whole of side one without a break, as a cycle. Listen for the crumhorns' buzz against Shirley's straight voice. Then hear Vaughan Williams's English Folk Song Suite and Butterworth's Shropshire Lad to hear the same village before the same war."],"fairport-convention-a-sailors-life":["Eleven minutes on one traditional song. Sandy Denny sings the ballad of a girl whose sailor lover has drowned, over a slow, open-ended groove; then the band lets go, and Richard Thompson's electric guitar and Dave Swarbrick's fiddle trade and build over drums and bass into a long, storming improvisation before subsiding. There is no chorus and very few chords; the drone and the modal tune carry everything.","Fairport were still largely a covers band playing American songwriters when they cut this for Unhalfbricking, released in 1969. Swarbrick came in as a session fiddler. Weeks later, in May 1969, the band's van crashed on the M1, killing the drummer Martin Lamble and Thompson's girlfriend Jeannie Franklyn; the survivors decided to make a wholly traditional record, and this track had shown them how.","It was reportedly recorded in essentially one live take, with Denny teaching the others the song in the studio. Listen for Lamble's drumming, which moves from brushes to full rock without ever settling into a standard beat. Swarbrick's playing here got him invited to join, and the improvisation is where British folk rock was really born.","Contemporary reviews noticed it as an oddity on an eclectic album. Later critics and the band themselves came to see it as the prototype of Liege & Lief, and it is often cited as the first true English folk-rock recording. Some purists still find the long jam self-indulgent; most listeners hear it as a breakthrough.","Listen for the moment around the middle when the guitar stops accompanying and starts arguing with the fiddle. Then play Liege & Lief, recorded months later, to hear the idea worked out. Compare with Coltrane-era modal jazz for the same drone-based freedom."],"fairport-convention-liege-and-lief":["Eight tracks, most of them traditional: the ballads Matty Groves and Tam Lin, Reynardine and The Deserter, a medley of fiddle tunes, and three new songs in traditional style. Sandy Denny sings, Richard Thompson and Simon Nicol play electric guitars, Ashley Hutchings bass, Dave Swarbrick fiddle and Dave Mattacks drums. The instantly recognisable sound is a Child ballad sung straight over a rock band, the fiddle doubling the guitar in the instrumental breaks.","After the M1 crash of May 1969 the band retreated to a rented house in Farley Chamberlayne, Hampshire, and rehearsed a programme of English traditional material, with Hutchings researching songs at Cecil Sharp House, the library of the English Folk Dance and Song Society. Joe Boyd produced for Island. It was released in December 1969, the year of Woodstock, as an explicit English answer to American rock.","Matty Groves (Child 81) is a murder ballad about adultery and a duel, and the band speeds up over the final instrumental until it becomes a kind of hoedown jig after the killing. Tam Lin (Child 39) is a fairy ballad. The original sleeve included notes and pictures on the history of English folk custom, making the album a small lesson in the tradition.","It was a success on release and has since been voted the most influential folk album of all time by BBC Radio 2 listeners. Folk purists at the time objected to drums and amplification. Both Denny and Hutchings left within weeks, in opposite directions: she toward songwriting, he toward stricter tradition in Steeleye Span.","Start with Matty Groves and follow the story, then listen for the instrumental that grows out of it. Tam Lin next. Then go back to the Child ballads it draws on, and on to Vaughan Williams's English Folk Song Suite for the older, orchestral way of electrifying the same tradition."],"sandy-denny-who-knows-where-the-time-goes":["A slow, rocking song about the birds leaving the sky, the tide turning, and the singer staying put while time passes. Denny's voice floats over a gentle band arrangement by Fairport Convention, with Richard Thompson's electric guitar making small, liquid comments between lines. The tune rises to the title question and resolves quietly each time, and the song ends without a crescendo, as if the singer simply stopped watching.","Denny wrote it around 1967, still in her teens, and demoed it while briefly singing with the Strawbs. Judy Collins recorded it first and used it as the title of her 1968 album, which brought Denny royalties before she was well known. Fairport's version, with Denny singing, appeared on Unhalfbricking in 1969, the album recorded just before the crash that reshaped the band.","The guitar tuning and the chord changes are more sophisticated than most folk-club songs of the time, owing something to jazz and to Joni Mitchell. Listen to how Denny breathes before the title line, delaying it slightly each time. Written by a nineteen-year-old, it sounds like the work of someone old, which is exactly the trick.","It became Denny's signature, sung at her memorials and covered by dozens of singers from Nina Simone to Eva Cassidy. After her early death in 1978 it took on the sound of an epitaph, which critics have argued overstates its sadness: it is a song about accepting time, not fearing it.","Hear the Fairport version first, then Judy Collins's for the prettier folk-pop reading. Listen for the pause before each chorus. Delius's On Hearing the First Cuckoo in Spring makes a good English neighbour: the same seasons, the same stillness. Notice how little the band plays behind her."],"sandy-denny-the-north-star-grassman-and-the-ravens":["Denny's first solo album mixes her own songs with two traditional pieces and covers of Dylan and Brenda Lee. She plays piano on many tracks, and the arrangements move between stripped guitar and bass, a full folk-rock band with Richard Thompson on lead guitar, and orchestral strings. Late November opens with a dream of drowning; John the Gun is a cold anti-war portrait; Next Time Around is an elusive song about the American folk singer Jackson C. Frank, her former boyfriend.","Denny had left Fairport at the end of 1969, formed Fotheringay, and broken it up after one album. Island released this in 1971, the same year she sang with Robert Plant on Led Zeppelin's The Battle of Evermore. She was voted top British female singer by Melody Maker that year and again in 1972, but her records never sold in proportion to her reputation.","Blackwaterside, the traditional song on side two, is the same one Bert Jansch recorded in 1966 and Jimmy Page adapted as Black Mountain Side; Denny's version reclaims it as a song. Her lyrics are full of water, birds and sea, and the title's grassman and ravens are images she never explained.","Reviews were warm but puzzled by the mix of styles. Critics have since placed it among the best British singer-songwriter albums of its period, though some find it uneven next to her later Like an Old Fashioned Waltz. Its songs, especially Late November, have become favourites of later folk singers.","Start with Late November and listen for the piano under the voice. Then compare her Blackwaterside with Jansch's on Jack Orion. Finish with John the Gun, and notice how much she leaves out. Last, hear Who Knows Where the Time Goes? again and notice how far her writing has travelled in two years, from guitar song to something closer to a piano ballad."],"nick-drake-five-leaves-left":["Ten songs for voice and intricately fingerpicked guitar, set in several tracks against string arrangements, and in others with Danny Thompson's double bass or conga. River Man, the album's centre, moves in a lilting five-beat metre with strings that seem to slow time. Way to Blue is voice and string ensemble alone. The recognisable sound is Drake's close, murmuring baritone just in front of a guitar in an unusual tuning.","Joe Boyd, who produced Fairport Convention, signed Drake to Island while he was studying English at Cambridge, and the album was recorded in London with engineer John Wood at Sound Techniques. A fellow student, Robert Kirby, wrote most of the string arrangements; River Man was arranged by Harry Robinson. Released in 1969, it sold poorly, and Drake's rare concerts were unhappy affairs.","The title comes from the warning printed in packets of Rizla cigarette papers, telling you only five sheets remain. The strings on River Man were recorded live with Drake in the room, and the arranger was reportedly told to aim for the atmosphere of Delius. Richard Thompson plays electric guitar on Time Has Told Me.","It received few reviews in 1969 and those were polite. Reappraisal began in the 1970s and 1980s among musicians, and since the late 1990s it has regularly appeared in lists of the best albums of all time. Some critics argue its posthumous romance has inflated its standing; others hear in it the most delicate arranging on any British folk record.","Start with River Man, then Way to Blue. Listen to the guitar as if it were a piano: bass, middle and melody all moving at once. Afterwards hear Delius's Brigg Fair and its sister pieces for the English string sound Kirby and Robinson were reaching for."],"nick-drake-bryter-layter":["Drake's most arranged record: songs with a full rhythm section, horns, strings, flute and even backing singers, framed by three instrumentals that open, name and nearly close the album. Hazey Jane I and II, Poor Boy and At the Chime of a City Clock bring jazz and soul colours to his guitar; Northern Sky, with John Cale on celesta, piano and organ, is the tenderest love song he wrote.","Recorded in 1970 after Drake had left Cambridge a term short of graduating, with Joe Boyd producing and John Wood engineering, and members of Fairport Convention in the rhythm section. Boyd hoped for a commercial breakthrough and gave the album a fuller, more polished sound. It sold even fewer copies than the first, and Boyd moved to the United States soon after, leaving Drake without his chief ally.","Drake wrote the instrumentals as feature pieces, but he never sings on them, so almost a third of the album is his music without his voice. Listen to the three together as a kind of suite; Robert Kirby's writing for flute and strings is close to English light music. John Cale arranged and played on his two tracks himself.","Drake was reportedly devastated by its failure, and critics of the time largely ignored it. Some later listeners prefer it to his other records for its warmth and colour; others find the arrangements dated beside the bare Pink Moon. Northern Sky is now one of his most loved songs, used widely on film and television.","Begin with Northern Sky, then Hazey Jane I. Listen for the bass and drums locking under the guitar. Play the instrumentals as a set to hear Kirby's arranging. Then play Pink Moon straight after and feel the difference. Notice how the voice never changes, whatever the arrangement does."],"nick-drake-pink-moon":["Eleven songs in twenty-eight minutes, Drake alone with his guitar and, on the title track only, a few bars of piano. No strings, no band, no backing voices. The songs are short, some under two minutes, built on hypnotic repeated guitar patterns in open tunings, and the words are compressed to near-aphorism: Place to Be, Road, Which Will, Things Behind the Sun, From the Morning. The effect is intimate and uneasy, as if the singer were in the room and slightly out of reach.","By 1971 Drake was isolated, depressed and barely performing. He approached John Wood and recorded the album in two late-night sessions at Sound Techniques in London, then, the story goes, left the tapes at Island's reception. Island released it in February 1972 with little promotion. Drake recorded only a few more songs before his death in November 1974, aged twenty-six.","The piano overdub on the title track is the only addition on the whole album, and it makes the first song sound bigger than everything that follows. Listen to the guitar on Road and Things Behind the Sun: Drake uses tunings where open strings ring as drones against moving bass lines, closer to Jansch's and Davy Graham's methods than to American folk strumming.","Island ran full-page advertisements but the album barely sold. A Volkswagen advertisement using the title track in 1999 brought it to a mass audience and sales soared. Critics now treat it as one of the purest solo acoustic records ever made, while some warn that its reception is bound up with the legend of the doomed artist.","Listen in one sitting, late at night, as it was recorded. Notice how much the songs repeat, and how small changes in the guitar pattern carry the feeling. Afterwards play Blind Willie Johnson's Dark Was the Night, another record of one guitar saying more than words."],"richard-thompson-i-want-to-see-the-bright-lights-tonight":["The first album credited to Richard and Linda Thompson: ten songs, all by Richard, split between his dry voice and Linda's clear, warm one. The title song is a Saturday-night dance number with a silver band; Withered and Died and The End of the Rainbow are among the bleakest songs in English; The Calvary Cross is a guitar-led nightmare. His electric guitar solos sound like bagpipes and Northumbrian pipes bent through an amplifier.","Thompson had left Fairport in 1971 and made one uncertain solo album. He married the singer Linda Peters in 1972 and recorded this in 1973 with members of the folk-rock scene; Island held it back and released it in 1974, into a market dominated by glam and progressive rock. It sold little. Shortly afterwards the couple converted to Sufi Islam and briefly lived in a commune.","The title track's brass band is deliberately the sound of English working-town bandstands, the same world Dirty Old Town describes. On The End of the Rainbow a father sings a lullaby telling his baby there is nothing at the end of the rainbow; its gentle tune is part of the joke. Listen for how Thompson's guitar answers Linda's vocal lines like a second singer.","Reviews were good but few. Its reputation rose through the 1980s and it appears in many lists of the greatest British albums; Rolling Stone named it among the best of the 1970s. Some listeners find its misery relentless; its defenders point to how funny and humane much of it is.","Start with the title track for the swing of it, then Withered and Died for Linda's voice, then The Calvary Cross for the guitar. Go back to Liege & Lief to hear where Thompson came from. Keep an ear on the lyrics: the jokes are easy to miss under the tunes."],"richard-thompson-shoot-out-the-lights":["Eight songs about love going wrong, sung alternately by Richard and Linda Thompson over a lean electric band. Don't Renege on Our Love, Walking on a Wire and Did She Jump or Was She Pushed? are pleas and accusations; the title track is a menacing guitar piece with some of Thompson's most famous soloing; Wall of Death closes on a fairground ride as the one place you feel alive. The sound is raw, largely live, almost without folk instruments.","The Thompsons recorded a first version with Gerry Rafferty as producer, abandoned it, and then re-recorded the album with Joe Boyd for his small label Hannibal in 1981. By the time it was released in 1982 their marriage had ended, and the tour that followed, with both on stage, became notorious for its tension. It was their last record together.","Most of the songs were written before the break-up, so the album is not the diary it seems; the events caught up with the songs. Listen for Linda's vocal on Walking on a Wire, recorded while pregnant, and for the tiny guitar figures under the verses of Wall of Death that imitate the fairground machinery.","It was the Thompsons' best-reviewed and best-selling record, especially in the United States, where it reached many critics' lists of the year's best. It is now routinely named among the greatest albums of the 1980s. The debate is less about quality than about how far to read it as autobiography.","Start with Walking on a Wire and Wall of Death. Then play the title track loud for the guitar. Go back to Bright Lights to hear how the same pair sounded eight years earlier and happier. Notice which of the two voices you believe more, song by song."],"pentangle-basket-of-light":["Nine tracks mixing traditional ballads with originals. Light Flight skips along in shifting metres of five, six and seven with scat vocals; House Carpenter is the Child ballad (243) of a woman lured away by a former lover, sung by Jacqui McShee over sitar and banjo; Once I Had a Sweetheart and The Cuckoo are English songs given jazz-trio accompaniments. Two steel-string guitars interlock, Danny Thompson's double bass is sometimes bowed, and Terry Cox plays drums and glockenspiel with a jazzman's lightness.","Pentangle had formed in 1967 and built a following in London clubs and concert halls. Light Flight was chosen as the theme for the BBC drama Take Three Girls, and the album, released on the independent Transatlantic label in 1969, reached the upper reaches of the British album chart, a rare feat for acoustic folk. It was their commercial peak.","Terry Cox and Danny Thompson were a jazz rhythm section, and the odd metres come from them as much as from the guitarists. Listen to the bass under House Carpenter: it holds long drones like a medieval instrument while the guitars move. The ballad texts come from Child and from Appalachian sources collected by Cecil Sharp.","Reviews praised the musicianship and some folk critics found the jazz elements precious. Its reputation settled as the best balance of Pentangle's many sources, and guitarists still study it. The shadow of Fairport's Liege & Lief, released the same year, has kept it slightly less celebrated than it deserves. Its hit single remains its best-known track.","Start with Light Flight and count along, then House Carpenter for the ballad. Listen for the bass under the guitars. Then play Liege & Lief and compare the acoustic and electric answers to the same question. Notice how rarely anyone plays loudly; the energy comes from rhythm, not volume."],"pentangle-cruel-sister":["Five tracks, all traditional. A Maid That's Deep in Love, When I Was in My Prime, Lord Franklin and Cruel Sister are sung ballads with delicate interplay; the second side is filled by an extended Jack Orion, about eighteen minutes long, that moves through ballad, instrumental improvisation and back. For the first time on a Pentangle album John Renbourn plays electric guitar.","Released in 1970 after the success of Basket of Light, this was a deliberate return to purely traditional material, with the band reacting against being cast as a pop act. Renbourn's interest in early music, which later produced his own medieval-leaning ensemble, shows in the arrangements. It sold far less than its predecessor. The sessions reportedly took longer than any of the band's earlier records, and the tension between the members began to show.","Cruel Sister is The Twa Sisters (Child 10), and Jack Orion is Glasgerion (Child 67), the ballad of a harper whose servant tricks his way into a princess's bed. Jansch had already recorded Jack Orion solo in 1966; here the band stretches it into a suite. Listen for the dulcimer, a folk instrument with a fixed drone, under the ballads.","It was received coolly at the time as a retreat from the band's jazz mix. Later critics have reassessed it as their most concentrated work on tradition, and the long Jack Orion has admirers and detractors in equal measure. It is a favourite of those who come to Pentangle from the ballads rather than from jazz.","Hear the title song first, next to an unaccompanied Twa Sisters. Then give the long Jack Orion full attention and notice when the instruments take over from the singer. Compare Fairport's A Sailor's Life for the electric equivalent. Listen with the texts to hand; the ballads are long and the words matter."],"bert-jansch-bert-jansch":["A voice and one borrowed acoustic guitar. Jansch's own songs, Strolling Down the Highway, Running from Home, Do You Hear Me Now and the drug song Needle of Death, sit beside instrumentals including his version of Davy Graham's Angie. The guitar is hard, fast and percussive, mixing blues bends, folk ornaments and jazz chords, with the thumb pounding the bass strings. The voice is quiet, flat and Scottish, often half-hidden behind the playing.","Recorded in 1964 by Bill Leader on a portable tape machine in his London flat, with Jansch playing a borrowed guitar, and sold to the small Transatlantic label for a flat fee of around a hundred pounds. Jansch had arrived in London from Edinburgh after drifting through Europe and was playing the Soho folk clubs. The album appeared in 1965 and reportedly sold well for such a modest release.","Needle of Death was written after a friend in Edinburgh died from a heroin overdose, and was one of the first songs in British popular music about drugs, unhip and unglamorous. Neil Young later adapted its tune for Ambulance Blues. Listen for the thumb on Angie, playing a walking bass line while the fingers play the melody, the technique that half of British guitarists then set out to learn.","It was admired at once in the clubs and by a generation of guitarists: Jimmy Page, Johnny Marr, Paul Simon and Neil Young have all cited Jansch. Critics now place it among the landmark British debut albums. Its lo-fi sound, a man in a room, is part of its appeal.","Start with Angie and just watch the guitar. Then Needle of Death, for the song. Then compare Davy Graham's playing on Shirley Collins's Folk Roots, New Routes, and hear what Nick Drake did with the same school of fingerpicking. Notice how little the voice pushes."],"bert-jansch-jack-orion":["Jansch's third album and his first given almost entirely to traditional songs: the long title ballad, Blackwaterside, Nottamun Town, The Waggoner's Lad, Henry Martin, Pretty Polly, and MacColl's The First Time Ever I Saw Your Face. John Renbourn plays second guitar on several tracks, and the two guitars weave independent lines in a style that would become Pentangle's. Jansch plays banjo on a couple of songs.","Recorded in 1966 for Transatlantic, as the London folk scene around Les Cousins club was absorbing the ballad repertoire and the collectors' archives. Jansch and Renbourn were sharing a flat and had just made a duet album. The ballads came from Child, from Cecil Sharp's English and Appalachian collections, and from other singers in the clubs. Bill Leader again made the recordings, simply and closely miked.","Jimmy Page listened closely to Blackwaterside and adapted its guitar arrangement as the instrumental Black Mountain Side on Led Zeppelin's first album in 1969, without credit; the matter was never settled in court. Listen to the guitar in Blackwaterside, which runs a drone in a modal tuning under the melody, the same method Davy Graham had used behind Shirley Collins.","It was well reviewed in the folk press, and later critics have placed it among his best. Its influence came through its musicians: Page, Sandy Denny, and Pentangle all drew on it. It is sometimes judged less personal than his first album, but it is where Jansch showed what a guitarist could do with the old ballads.","Start with Blackwaterside, then play Led Zeppelin's Black Mountain Side. Then the title ballad, all ten minutes of it, and hear Pentangle stretch it further on Cruel Sister. Compare his First Time Ever with Peggy Seeger's. Notice how the guitar keeps its drone even when the melody moves far away."],"planxty-planxty":["The band's debut, known from its dark sleeve as 'the black album'. Songs such as Raggle Taggle Gypsy, The Blacksmith and MacColl's Sweet Thames Flow Softly alternate with dance tunes and the harper Carolan's Sí Bheag, Sí Mhór on the pipes. Liam O'Flynn's uilleann pipes carry the tunes; Dónal Lunny's bouzouki and Andy Irvine's mandolin weave counter-melodies around them instead of strumming chords, and Christy Moore and Irvine share the singing.","The four came together in 1972 to back Christy Moore's solo album Prosperous, recorded in a country house in Co. Kildare, and liked the sound enough to stay. Their live shows in Dublin caused a sensation, and Polydor released the debut in 1973, when Irish traditional music was split between the céilí band and the ballad group. Planxty offered a third way.","The Greek bouzouki had come to Irish music only recently, brought back by Johnny Moynihan and adapted by Lunny and Irvine with a flat back and different stringing; this is its founding record. The name Planxty is a term from Carolan's harp music for a tune written in tribute to a patron. Listen for the counterpoint between bouzouki and mandolin under the pipes.","It was an immediate success in Ireland and is now considered one of the most influential Irish albums ever made, the source of a string-band texture copied by countless groups. Some older traditional players disliked the arrangements; within a few years they had become the norm. Christy Moore and Dónal Lunny went on to shape Irish popular music for decades, in Moving Hearts and beyond.","Start with Raggle Taggle Gypsy running into the tune that follows it and listen to the bouzouki. Then Sí Bheag, Sí Mhór for the pipes alone. Compare the Chieftains' first album, a decade earlier, and The Bothy Band two years later."],"planxty-the-well-below-the-valley":["Planxty's second album, released only months after the first, with the same line-up and the same interlocking strings under the pipes. Its heart is the title track, a strange, unresolving ballad sung by Christy Moore, in which a stranger at a well questions a woman about her children until her hidden crimes come out. Dance sets, Andy Irvine's songs and pipe airs complete the record.","Recorded in 1973 while the band was touring constantly across Ireland and Britain, it consolidated their success. Moore learned the title ballad from John Reilly, a singer from the Irish Travelling community recorded by collectors in the 1960s, whose repertoire held some of the rarest ballads in Ireland. The album also shows the band widening its repertoire, with Andy Irvine bringing songs learned on his travels and from older Irish singers into the arrangements.","The title song is a version of Child 21, The Maid and the Palmer, a ballad about a woman confronted with her sins, sometimes linked to the story of the Samaritan woman at the well in John's Gospel. Child had found only fragments; Reilly's version was one of very few complete ones from oral tradition, and Planxty's recording made it known.","Received as a worthy follow-up, it has sometimes been rated slightly behind the debut, though the title track is now among the band's best-known recordings. Its importance for ballad scholarship is its use of a Traveller source, part of a new respect for Travellers as tradition-bearers. The band split two years later, so the album also marks the end of its first phase.","Hear the title song first and notice that it never quite resolves, musically or morally. Then go back to Lord Randal for the same question-and-answer method. Finish with a dance set to hear the band at full speed. Keep the words in front of you the first time."],"the-chieftains-the-chieftains-1":["Instrumental Irish dance music and slow airs, arranged for a small ensemble: jigs, reels, slides and a hornpipe, with Paddy Moloney's uilleann pipes at the centre, tin whistle, wooden flute, fiddle and bodhrán around them. Instead of everyone playing the tune together in unison, as in a céilí band, the arrangements pass it from one instrument to another, add harmonies and drop players out, so each set has a shape like a small chamber piece.","Claddagh Records was founded in Dublin by Garech Browne, the Guinness heir, to record Irish traditional music and poetry, and this was one of its first releases. The players came from Seán Ó Riada's Ceoltóirí Chualann, which had shown in the early 1960s that traditional tunes could be arranged for concert performance. The band stayed part-time amateurs for over a decade.","The approach of passing a tune around the ensemble came directly from Ó Riada, a composer trained in European classical music, who treated a traditional dance set as a small concerto grosso. Listen for the moments where the pipes drop out and the whistle and flute carry the tune alone, then return with a drone underneath.","It made little noise on release but set a template later groups have followed ever since. The Chieftains' later career of celebrity collaborations has sometimes been criticised for diluting the music; their early Claddagh records remain the ones traditional players respect most. Collectors and players alike prize it for its unhurried tempos and for tunes that were then little known outside the regions they came from.","Pick one reel set and follow the tune as it moves between instruments. Then hear a slow air on the pipes and listen for the ornaments. Compare with Stanford's Irish Symphony, which uses tunes of the same kind in a Victorian orchestra."],"the-chieftains-women-of-ireland":["A slow air, played without words. The melody rises in long, arching phrases and is passed between uilleann pipes, fiddle and flute over a soft, sustained accompaniment, building gradually to a restrained climax with a steady drum pulse before falling away. It has the gravity of a lament, though the poem it was written for is a love song. Harp and fiddle trade the lead at points, and the pipes return for the last phrase.","The words are an eighteenth-century Irish poem by Peadar Ó Doirnín; the tune was written in the 1960s by Seán Ó Riada, the composer whose ensemble spawned the Chieftains. The Chieftains' instrumental version was recorded for their fourth album. Stanley Kubrick used it in Barry Lyndon (1975), whose soundtrack also features Handel and Schubert, and the film made it widely known.","Many listeners assume the melody is centuries old; it is a modern composition by a classically trained composer, written deliberately in the style of a traditional air. Its placing in Barry Lyndon, beside Handel's Sarabande, makes the point: Kubrick heard it as belonging with baroque music. The poem itself lists famous women of Irish legend and history.","It has become one of the best-known Irish melodies and has been recorded by singers such as Kate Bush and Sinéad O'Connor with the original Irish words. Traditional players sometimes object that its fame has overshadowed older airs; others hold it up as proof that the tradition can still grow new tunes.","Listen for the drum entering and the gradual build. Then hear a sung version with the Irish words. Finally watch the relevant scenes of Barry Lyndon, and compare with Bax's Tintagel, another composer's Celtic seascape. Notice how long the melody takes to breathe out."],"the-bothy-band-the-bothy-band":["The debut of a six-piece that played Irish reels and jigs faster and harder than anyone before. Matt Molloy's flute, Paddy Keenan's uilleann pipes and Tommy Peoples's fiddle play the tunes in tight unison over Dónal Lunny's bouzouki, Mícheál Ó Domhnaill's guitar and Tríona Ní Dhomhnaill's clavinet or harpsichord. Songs in English and Irish, sung by the Ó Domhnaill siblings in Donegal style, break up the dance sets.","Lunny formed the band in 1974 after leaving Planxty, drawing on the young musicians gathering at Dublin sessions. The debut appeared in 1975, when Irish traditional music was newly popular with a young audience that also listened to rock. They toured widely in Britain and Europe; the high tempos and volume made their concerts feel like rock shows. Their studio sound tried to catch that live energy.","Ní Dhomhnaill's clavinet, an electric keyboard associated with funk, gives the rhythm section a percussive snap. Listen for the way the three melody instruments play not quite identical versions of each tune, each ornamenting in its own style: the tiny differences are what makes the unison sound so thick. The songs, by contrast, are slow and unhurried.","Traditional musicians argued about the speed, which some felt robbed the tunes of their lift. The band's example nonetheless won out; their line-up and approach became the model for later groups such as Altan and Lúnasa. The album is regarded as one of the essential Irish traditional records. Their live album remains the favourite of many fans.","Start with any reel set and try to follow just one instrument. Then compare the same kind of tune on The Chieftains 1 to hear how much faster and fuller it has become. Play it loud. Then listen once more to a song by Tríona Ní Dhomhnaill, where the band pulls right back."],"the-bothy-band-old-hag-you-have-killed-me":["The band's second album, with Kevin Burke now on fiddle. The title comes from a jig; the record mixes long dance sets with songs, including the Scottish Gaelic mouth-music of Fionnghuala, sung by the Ó Domhnaill siblings as a rhythmic, wordplay-driven dance song without instruments. The ensemble sound is the same dense unison of pipes, flute and fiddle over bouzouki and keyboards, somewhat better recorded and more varied than the debut.","Released in 1976, the year the band was at its busiest on tour in Ireland, Britain and Europe. Burke, a London-born fiddler of Sligo parents, brought the smoother Sligo style in place of Tommy Peoples's Donegal attack. The band broke up in 1979, and Matt Molloy went on to join the Chieftains. Their sound became a pattern for touring Irish bands.","Fionnghuala is mouth music, puirt à beul, the Gaelic practice of singing dance tunes with syllables so people can dance without instruments. Hearing it between the instrumental sets shows the root of the whole style: the tunes are meant for bodies, and the voices here are doing the job of a fiddle.","Critics received it as confirming the band's standing; it is often named with the debut as their best. Some traditional commentators continued to find the tempos excessive. Fionnghuala became a favourite of later Celtic groups. The band recorded only two more albums before breaking up, making this the centre of its short career and, for many listeners, the easiest place to begin.","Start with Fionnghuala, then any reel set. Compare the fiddle with the first album to hear the different regional styles. Then hear Grainger's or Holst's folk-dance arrangements for the orchestral take on the same impulse. Keep the volume up: the density is the point."],"jimmie-rodgers-blue-yodel-no-1":["Under three minutes of one man and one guitar. Rodgers sings four loose twelve-bar blues stanzas about a woman who has left him and the pistol he intends to buy, strumming a plain bass-and-brush accompaniment with short runs between the lines. At the end of each verse he snaps up into falsetto for a three-note yodel that answers the sung phrase like a second singer. Nothing else is on the record: no band, no harmony, no reverb to hide in.","Rodgers had worked as a brakeman on Mississippi railroads until tuberculosis ended that life. He recorded for Victor during the company's rush into Southern vernacular music, made practical by the electrical microphone, which could finally catch a soft voice and a guitar without a recording horn. Victor was selling discs to rural households that had just bought their first phonograph. The record sold in the hundreds of thousands and turned a sick former railroad man into the first full-time star of the music.","Listen closer: the yodel is not Alpine. Rodgers grew up around Black railroad and levee workers, and this falsetto break belongs to the same world as the field holler, a leap into head voice used as punctuation rather than display. Notice too that he does not keep strict twelve-bar lengths. He adds and drops beats as the words need, and the guitar simply follows him. Every later country singer inherited that licence.","Victor's ledgers called it a blue yodel because nobody yet had a word for what it was. The disc effectively created the commercial category first sold as hillbilly and later as country. Scholars still argue about how much Rodgers took from the Black blues singers he heard on the railroads, and whether that is best described as theft, as exchange, or as one Southern vernacular that segregated marketing afterwards cut in two.","Play it twice: once for the story, once listening only to where the yodel lands against the guitar's beat. Then put on a Delta blues from the same decade and hear how nearly identical the twelve-bar frame is. Look for a transfer made from a clean original pressing; aggressive noise reduction thins the voice and files the edge off the yodel."],"jimmie-rodgers-blue-yodel-no-9":["The ninth in Rodgers's numbered blues series, and the one with company. Louis Armstrong plays trumpet behind him and Lil Hardin Armstrong plays piano, the three of them running through a slow twelve-bar about a man on a street corner, a woman, and a policeman who takes an interest. Armstrong does not solo so much as converse, filling every gap Rodgers leaves at the end of a line, and answering the yodels with bent, smeared phrases of his own.","The session took place in Los Angeles in 1930, arranged on short notice while all three were passing through. By then Rodgers was the best-selling country artist alive and Armstrong was remaking American singing and trumpet playing at once; the record companies filed them under different catalogues and sold them to different shops. The Depression was already closing in on the record business, and within three years Rodgers would be dead of tuberculosis at thirty-five.","Listen closer: Rodgers is the amateur here and it shows in the timing. He comes in early on at least one verse, and Armstrong simply moves, stretching a note to cover the gap. The trumpet is mixed close enough that you can hear breath in it. Armstrong went uncredited on the label, which is why the record spent decades being described as a curiosity rather than what it is, a meeting between the two most influential singers of the era.","For years the disc was filed as a novelty, an odd afternoon. It reads differently now, as evidence that the walls between hillbilly and race records were a marketing decision rather than a musical one. Some writers hear the session as unequal, Armstrong carrying a limited singer; others hear two men from the same Southern soundworld recognising each other. Both readings survive because the record supports both.","Follow the trumpet first and ignore the words entirely: Armstrong is writing a second song in the spaces. Then play it again for the voice and notice how Rodgers's yodel and Armstrong's bent notes do the same expressive job. Pair it with a Hot Five side from a few years earlier to hear how little Armstrong had to change to fit."],"carter-family-single-girl-married-girl":["Sara Carter sings alone, unhurried, over Maybelle Carter's guitar, alternating two verses that set the same woman's two possible lives against each other: the single girl going where she pleases in a fine new dress, the married girl rocking a cradle and crying. The tune barely moves, four phrases that fall and settle, and the accompaniment does nothing to comment. The flatness is the point. Sara delivers the bleakest lines without a flicker of emphasis.","Recorded at Ralph Peer's field sessions in Bristol, on the Tennessee-Virginia line, in the summer of 1927, the same fortnight that produced Jimmie Rodgers's first sides. Peer had brought portable electrical equipment south to find material he could copyright and sell. The Carters were a farming family from Poor Valley, Virginia, who had driven over a mountain road to get there. By some accounts A. P. Carter was out of the room when this one was cut, which is why only Sara sings.","Listen closer: Maybelle is barely playing. There is no picking pattern to admire, just chords and a few bass notes, and the restraint throws every word forward. Note also that the song offers no resolution and takes no position; it simply lays the two lives side by side and stops. Commercial country would spend the next fifty years learning to be that unsentimental about marriage, and mostly failing.","It was the Carters' first real seller and it financed everything that followed. Later folklorists treated their repertoire as a rescue operation on a dying oral tradition; the Carters themselves were making records for money, and A. P. copyrighted material he had collected from neighbours, including Black musicians such as Lesley Riddle who travelled with him. That double character, preservation and appropriation at once, is still the central argument about them.","Listen for what is absent: no chorus, no harmony, no moral. Then hear the same voice in the fuller family trio recordings and notice how much Sara's plainness survives the harmony. A good transfer matters here more than usual, because the whole effect lives in the grain of an unadorned voice close to a microphone."],"carter-family-wildwood-flower":["The most copied guitar part in American music. Maybelle Carter plays the melody with her thumb on the low strings while brushing chords across the high ones with her fingers, so a single guitar sounds like two instruments, lead and rhythm. Sara sings a garland of flower names and a woman abandoned by a faithless man. Between verses the guitar takes the tune alone, which is why generations of beginners learned this before anything else.","Cut in the year after Bristol, when Victor was recording the Carters steadily and their discs were reaching farms across the South. The song was not theirs: it descends from an 1860 American parlour ballad, printed with piano accompaniment for middle-class drawing rooms, which had drifted into oral circulation and come back up to them unrecognisable. The Carters recorded it as their own, in the ordinary practice of the day, and their arrangement replaced the original everywhere.","Listen closer: the words make almost no sense, and that is the evidence. The parlour original's botanical vocabulary, pale amaranthus and aronatus and the rest, was passed mouth to mouth through people who had never seen the printed page, and each singer reshaped what they misheard into something that sounded plausible. Sara sings the garbled version without hesitation. The song's own history is audible inside its lyric.","Guitarists argue about whether Maybelle invented the thumb-lead style or adapted what she had heard, and Lesley Riddle, the Black guitarist who travelled with A. P. collecting songs, is usually part of that conversation now. Nobody disputes the consequence. The Carter scratch became the default way to accompany a country song, and the tune became a standard test piece, played at every level from a first lesson to the concert stage.","Ignore the singing for one pass and follow only the thumb, then follow only the brushed chords, and hear two players emerge from one pair of hands. Compare a bluegrass flatpicked version afterwards and notice what speed costs the tune. Any decent reissue of the Victor sides will do; the guitar sits forward in the original balance."],"carter-family-can-the-circle-be-unbroken":["A hymn chorus wrapped around a funeral. The verses describe a hearse arriving, a mother carried out, a son walking behind and asking the undertaker to drive slow; the chorus lifts into three-part harmony and asks whether the family circle will be unbroken in a better home above. Maybelle's guitar keeps its steady thumb-and-brush pattern throughout, unmoved by any of it, and the harmony enters exactly on cue each time, like something rehearsed at a graveside.","A. P. Carter took a 1907 gospel hymn, kept its chorus and its question, and wrote new verses that put a specific corpse in a specific hearse. By 1935 the Carters were recording prolifically and would soon be broadcasting from a border radio station in Texas whose transmitter reached most of North America at night. The Depression had emptied the record shops but not the airwaves, and this song travelled further on radio than on shellac.","Listen closer: the verses are in the past tense and the chorus is in the future, and the song never lets the two meet. The narrator has already watched his mother go; the reunion stays hypothetical, phrased as a question in every single repetition. Country gospel usually answers its own questions. This one declines to, which is why it works equally well as consolation and as an expression of doubt.","It became the American folk revival's communal closing number, sung at festivals and funerals by people with no interest in the Carters, and its title ended up naming the 1972 album that introduced the old-time repertoire to a rock audience. Purists sometimes object that the mass-singing version smooths away the harshness of the verses. The complaint is fair and has changed nothing.","Listen to the verses alone first, with the chorus treated as an interruption, and the song turns bleak. Then listen for the moment the three voices lock; the shift from one voice to three is the whole emotional mechanism. Follow it with a Sacred Harp recording to hear the harmony tradition these singers were raised inside."],"hank-williams-lovesick-blues":["Williams enters alone on the word I, holds it, and breaks it in half with a yodel before the band has played a note. What follows is a fast, grinning complaint about a woman who has gone, driven by a shuffling bass and a steel guitar that answers each line. The vocal keeps cracking upward on purpose, sometimes twice in a phrase, so the song sounds simultaneously wretched and delighted with itself. It is over in well under three minutes.","Williams did not write it. It began as a 1922 Tin Pan Alley show song and had already passed through vaudeville and the recordings of the blackface yodeller Emmett Miller and the singer Rex Griffin, from whom Williams took his arrangement. His publisher Fred Rose thought the song was a mistake and old hat. It became the biggest hit of Williams's life and won him his Grand Ole Opry debut, where the audience reportedly refused to let him leave the stage.","Listen closer: the yodel break is a controlled crack between chest and head voice, and Williams places it on the stressed syllable of the most self-pitying words, turning misery into a joke at his own expense. Note also how little the band does. There is no solo of any length; everything is deference to the voice. The arrangement is essentially Griffin's, which makes Williams's originality a matter of delivery alone.","Rose's judgement was wrong and country's commercial history turned on it. Critics since have worried at the fact that the record that made the music's defining writer was a borrowed vaudeville number, filtered through the minstrel stage; that lineage is now discussed openly rather than tidied away. Nobody argues about the performance. It is still the standard demonstration of what a voice can do to a song it did not write.","Count the yodel breaks on a first hearing, then stop counting and just feel where they fall against the beat, always slightly ahead. Compare Rex Griffin's earlier version to see exactly what Williams added. Any well-mastered reissue of the MGM singles works; avoid the later overdubbed editions that bury the band under strings."],"hank-williams-i-saw-the-light":["Two minutes of pure uplift, taken at a brisk walking pace. Four short verses trace a sinner wandering in the night until the light finds him, each ending in a chorus so plainly built that a room full of strangers can join it on first hearing. The band plays it like a dance tune, with fiddle and steel trading brief fills, and the singer sounds untroubled throughout. No minor chord appears anywhere in the song.","Williams wrote it early, before the hits, and it became the number he closed shows with for the rest of his short life. Gospel was not a side venture in this music but its other half: the same bands played honky-tonks on Saturday and revival songs on Sunday radio, and audiences expected both from the same man. Williams took the melody from a Carter Family gospel side, which was ordinary practice, and put his own words on it.","Listen closer: the lyric is entirely in the past tense. Every claim of salvation is a report of something that happened earlier, and the song never says the light is still there. Williams, who spent his adult life in alcoholism and chronic pain and died at twenty-nine, reportedly told his mother more than once that there was no light for him. Knowing that changes the brightness of the record without altering a note of it.","It is sung in churches by people who would not otherwise own a Hank Williams record, and it has become the standard closing hymn at country funerals, including those of musicians whose lives it did not describe. Critics divide over whether to hear it straight or ironically. The most persuasive readings refuse to choose, treating the gap between the singer's life and the song's certainty as the thing the record is actually about.","Hear it immediately after one of his desolate songs; the pairing is the point, and Williams himself put the two sides of his work on consecutive nights of the same week. Listen for the fiddle, which never once darkens. Bluegrass and gospel quartet versions are everywhere, but start with the original, where the tempo is faster than most people remember."],"hank-williams-im-so-lonesome-i-could-cry":["Three verses, no chorus, almost no motion. Williams sings a series of images from a country night, a whippoorwill too blue to fly, a midnight train whining, a falling star, a silent moon behind clouds, and after each one states the plain fact of his loneliness. Don Helms's steel guitar sits very high above the voice and answers every line with two or three notes. The band plays as quietly as a band can while still being audible.","Recorded in Nashville in 1949, at the moment Williams turned from a regional draw into a national one. It was issued as the other side of a cheerful novelty number, which tells you what his label expected to sell. Country songs of the period were usually built on situations, a cheating wife, a lost job; this one has no situation at all, only weather and an interior state, which was not what the jukebox trade was ordering.","Listen closer: Williams never says why he is lonesome. There is no woman in the lyric, no departure, no cause of any kind. The images are borrowed from folk commonplaces and arranged so that each natural fact is also a person, the whippoorwill too blue to fly, the leaves that die. Helms tuned his steel unusually high for these sessions, and that thin, tight upper register is the sound most people mean when they say the word lonesome.","It is the Williams record that non-country listeners tend to know, and the one poets quote. It has been recorded by jazz singers, soul singers and rock bands without the slightest resistance from the material, which suggests how little of it is generic. Arguments today are mostly about the later MGM reissues that added orchestral overdubs to Williams's voice after his death, a practice now generally regarded as vandalism.","Take one image at a time and notice that the music does nothing to illustrate any of them. Then listen only to the steel guitar, which is effectively a second singer and often the more expressive of the two. Seek the undubbed original mix. Afterwards, try a song cycle about a solitary winter walk and hear how close the two traditions come."],"hank-williams-your-cheatin-heart":["A slow shuffle in which the singer promises an unfaithful lover a future of sleepless nights and useless tears. The melody climbs on the accusation and falls on the verdict, and Williams bends the last note of nearly every line downward, a small vocal catch that became one of the most imitated mannerisms in American singing. Steel guitar and fiddle alternate short answering phrases. The whole thing is structurally simple enough to teach in five minutes.","Recorded in Nashville in the autumn of 1952 at what proved to be one of his final sessions. By then Williams had been dismissed from the Grand Ole Opry for unreliability, was in constant pain from a spinal condition, and was drinking and taking morphine. He died in the back of a car on New Year's Day 1953, aged twenty-nine, and the single was released weeks afterwards into a country music that had just lost its centre.","Listen closer: the song is addressed entirely to someone who is not present, and the singer takes no action in it. He does not confront, leave or forgive; he simply predicts. Every verb about the other person is in the future tense, which turns the record from a complaint into something closer to a curse. Williams reportedly dictated the words to his second wife on a car journey, talking about his first.","Its posthumous release fixed it as the last word of a man who had just died, which no amount of later analysis can quite undo. It gave its title to a film, entered the standard repertoire of singers with no country connection at all, and is routinely named the single most important country song. The main scholarly argument now concerns the overdubbed reissues, and how much of Williams's recorded legacy reaches listeners in a form he would recognise.","Listen for the downward catch at the end of the lines; once you hear it you will hear it in every country singer since. Then compare a smooth pop version from the 1950s and notice how much the polish removes. The mono single mix, with the band dry and close, is the one to find."],"patsy-cline-i-fall-to-pieces":["A mid-tempo song about meeting an ex-lover in public and pretending to be fine. Cline sings almost conversationally in the verses and then opens her chest voice on the title phrase, so the collapse the words describe happens audibly in the tone. Behind her a male vocal quartet hums sustained chords where a steel guitar would once have been, a soft electric guitar plays a rocking two-note figure, and brushed drums keep everything horizontal.","Owen Bradley produced it in his Nashville studio as part of a deliberate attempt to keep country selling after rock and roll took its teenage audience. Fiddles and steel were dropped, backing choirs and light strings brought in, and the resulting style was marketed as the Nashville Sound. Cline reportedly disliked the song at first. It was climbing the charts in the summer of 1961 while she was in hospital after a severe car crash, and it made her a national name.","Listen closer: there is no drama in the arrangement at all, no crescendo, no key change, nothing to signal an emotional event. Everything is carried by the difference between how Cline sings the verse lines and how she sings the four words of the title. Notice also the vocal quartet: they never sing words, only vowels, which is what lets them function as an orchestra rather than as a gospel group.","Traditionalists heard the Nashville Sound as a surrender, country music trading its fiddles for a string section to chase pop money, and that argument has never entirely stopped. The counter-case is this record: the smoother the setting, the more exposed the voice. Cline's reputation grew steadily after her death in a plane crash in 1963, and she is now credited with proving that a woman could carry a country career without a male star attached.","Listen once for the two vocal registers and where exactly she crosses between them. Then play a honky-tonk record from ten years earlier and hear what was traded away and what was gained. Her studio recordings are best in mono; the later stereo remixes push the backing voices forward and make the arrangement sound more crowded than Bradley intended."],"patsy-cline-crazy":["A torch ballad taken slowly, with a melody that keeps climbing where you expect it to fall. Floyd Cramer's piano opens with a few unhurried chords; the vocal quartet breathes underneath; Cline enters low and stays conversational until the bridge, where she pushes into a full, wide tone and then pulls straight back. There is no instrumental solo. The record's whole architecture is one voice moving between restraint and release across two and a half minutes.","The song was written by Willie Nelson, then an unknown Nashville songwriter scraping by on demo fees, in a style closer to a jazz standard than to country. Cline initially disliked its wandering phrasing. She recorded it in Bradley's studio in the late summer of 1961, weeks after a car crash that had left her with broken ribs, which restricted how long she could hold a note; the vocal was completed once she had healed enough to sustain the lines.","Listen closer: the melody almost never lands where a country tune would. Nelson wrote long phrases that cross the bar lines and resolve late, which is why the song sits so comfortably in the repertoire of jazz singers. Notice too that Cline sings the title word differently every time it appears, four readings of one syllable. The Jordanaires' hum in the second verse quietly outlines a chord the piano has left out.","It became, by most reckonings, the most played song on American jukeboxes, and it is the record usually offered as the definitive answer to anyone who calls the Nashville Sound a dilution. Cline died eighteen months later at thirty. Debate now runs less about the arrangement than about the industry it typified, and about how many Nashville women of the 1960s were given the same production budget and the same freedom.","Start with the bridge, the one passage where she opens fully, then go back to the top and hear how carefully she has been holding back. Follow it with Willie Nelson's own version, which is almost spoken, to hear two completely different songs inside one set of words. The mono single is the intended balance."],"johnny-cash-i-walk-the-line":["Three instruments and a voice, locked into a dry chugging pulse that sounds like a freight train at moderate speed. Luther Perkins picks a bass-string figure with no vibrato and almost no decay; Marshall Grant slaps an upright bass; Cash strums and sings a vow of fidelity in a register so low it seems to be resisting the melody. Between each verse he hums a single pitch, unaccompanied, for a couple of seconds, and the whole song shifts into a new key.","Recorded at Sun Studio in Memphis for Sam Phillips, in the same building and the same year that Elvis Presley left for a major label. Cash was a former air force radio operator selling appliances door to door; he had written the song for his first wife while stationed overseas. Phillips wanted it faster than Cash intended, and the released version is the compromise. Sun sold it to country and pop markets at once, which almost nothing did in 1956.","Listen closer: the buzzing edge on the rhythm guitar is a piece of waxed paper woven through the strings, a trick Cash used to get a snare-like click from a band with no drummer. The humming between verses is not a stylistic flourish but a practical necessity; the song modulates five times, and he needed the pitch. That makes a record celebrated for its plainness one of the more harmonically restless singles of its decade.","It was Sun's biggest country seller and it fixed the boom-chicka-boom rhythm as Cash's signature for forty years. Critics have argued ever since about whether the song is sincere or anxious: a vow repeated that insistently, by a man who would soon wreck the marriage it was written for, can be heard either way. Cash encouraged both readings and never settled the question in interviews.","Try to hear the key changes on a first pass; most listeners never notice them, which is the trick. Then listen to the guitar alone for its complete lack of sustain, the sound of a cheap amplifier used deliberately. Play it against a train-rhythm piece from another tradition and hear how universal the pulse is. The Sun mono master is the one."],"johnny-cash-ring-of-fire":["The song opens not with a guitar but with two trumpets playing a bright mariachi fanfare in thirds, and they return between every verse. Underneath, the familiar chugging rhythm continues, so the record is Cash's Memphis pulse with a Mexican border band bolted on top. He sings about desire as a descending ring of fire, low and steady, and the trumpets answer each time with something almost festive, entirely at odds with the words.","June Carter, of the Carter Family and later Cash's wife, wrote it with Merle Kilgore about her feelings for him while both were married to other people. Anita Carter recorded it first, quietly. Cash said the trumpet idea came to him in a dream, and he cut his version for Columbia in Nashville in 1963, when he was deep in amphetamine use and his career was unsteady. It restored him commercially at once.","Listen closer: nothing in the arrangement is sad, and the lyric is about a destructive compulsion. That mismatch is the whole design. Notice also that the trumpets never play behind the voice, only between the lines, so the record alternates between two moods rather than blending them. The falling shape of the trumpet figure mirrors the falling shape of the phrase about going down.","Mariachi horns on a country single were startling in 1963, and the record widened what a Nashville arrangement was allowed to contain. Authorship has been the recurring argument: Cash's first wife claimed late in life that he had written it himself and credited June Carter, a claim the documented publishing does not support and most historians reject. The song's afterlife in adverts and films has worn its strangeness down.","Listen to the trumpets in isolation and notice they are playing a party. Then read the words as if the trumpets were not there. The gap between the two is the record. Follow it with a Copland score that uses brass the same way, as a bright frontier colour, and hear the shared American mythology."],"johnny-cash-at-folsom-prison":["A live album recorded in front of prisoners, opening with Cash announcing his own name and going straight into a song about shooting a man to watch him die. The set runs through murder ballads, jokes, a duet with June Carter and a long stretch of songs about confinement, with the band playing at one dynamic throughout and the audience audible all the way. It closes with a song written by an inmate who was sitting in the room while Cash sang it.","Cash had wanted to record inside a prison for a decade and Columbia had refused; a new producer, Bob Johnston, agreed. Two shows were played at the California state prison at Folsom in January 1968, and the album assembles the stronger one. Cash had just come through the worst of his amphetamine years. He had never served a prison sentence himself, only nights in county cells, a distinction the record quietly declines to make clear.","Listen closer: the whoop that follows the line about shooting a man in Reno is not what it appears. Prison officials had restricted how the men could react, and the crowd noise on the album was shaped in the mix, with applause moved and added. Listen also for the prison announcements cutting across the music; those were left in deliberately, to keep reminding you where you are standing.","It sold enormously, revived a stalled career and made Cash a figure for the counterculture as well as for country radio. It also set the terms of a long argument about authenticity, since the outlaw persona it cemented was largely constructed. Later scholarship has drawn attention to Glen Sherley, the inmate whose song closes the record, who was released partly through Cash's advocacy and whose life afterwards went badly.","Listen to the first four minutes without skipping: the announcement, the silence, the first chord and the first reaction together do more than any single song. Then attend to the crowd rather than the band. Seek the original single-disc album rather than the expanded edition, which restores both shows and dilutes the pacing that made the record work."],"johnny-cash-american-recordings":["One voice and one acoustic guitar for forty minutes, with nothing added. Cash, then in his sixties, sings thirteen songs: his own murder ballad about Delia, a nineteenth-century hymn, and pieces borrowed from a Canadian poet-songwriter, a Los Angeles barroom writer and a heavy metal frontman. The guitar playing is basic and occasionally uneven. The recording is so close that you hear the chair, the breath and the dry click of a plectrum against wound strings.","Cash had been dropped by his label and was playing dinner theatres when Rick Rubin, a producer known for hip-hop and metal, offered him a contract and a single condition: no band, no Nashville, just songs. Most of the album was cut in Rubin's living room and in Cash's Tennessee cabin, with two tracks taken from a small Los Angeles club. The idea was to record him the way a folklorist would record an old singer in a kitchen.","Listen closer: the song called Thirteen was written for him, at his request, by a metal singer whose band Cash had never heard, and it is built entirely from the imagery Cash had been singing since the 1950s. Note also what the silence does. With no drums to mark time, Cash's phrases stretch and shorten as he pleases, and several tracks are audibly slower at the end than the beginning.","The record won a Grammy in a folk category, sold to an audience that had never bought country, and began a sequence of albums that ran to his death in 2003. Some longtime admirers found the framing calculated, an old man styled as a prophet by a young producer with an ear for gravity. Others point out that the repertoire choices were Cash's and that the voice, worn and unhidden, does the persuading.","Play it at low volume in a quiet room; the recording was made for that scale and collapses through a car stereo. Start with the opening track and its slow-building dread. Then go back to a 1950s Sun single and hear the same man with forty years and no band between him and you. The debt to a solitary winter song cycle is not accidental."],"loretta-lynn-coal-miners-daughter":["A banjo figure opens it and keeps rolling underneath for three minutes while Lynn recites, in strict chronological order, the facts of her childhood in a Kentucky hollow: the shack, the father on the night shift, the mother washing on a scrubboard and reading the Bible by coal-oil light, eight children, no money, no self-pity. The melody is almost a chant, four repeated phrases. Nothing in the arrangement dramatises anything; the details do the work alone.","Lynn had married at fifteen and had four children before she was twenty, and she wrote her own material at a time when very few women in Nashville did. Owen Bradley produced it. The song appeared as country radio was reaching an audience of migrants from exactly these places, people who had left the coalfields for factory towns in Ohio and Michigan, and who heard their own family history read back to them in the third person plural.","Listen closer: there is no chorus. The song never returns to a hook, which is why it sounds like testimony rather than a single, and Lynn reportedly wrote many more verses than were recorded, with the rest cut purely for length. Notice that the poverty is described without a single abstract word. Everything is an object: the scrubboard, the coal oil, the hand-me-down shoes. The one general statement comes in the last line.","It gave its name to her memoir and to the 1980 film made from it, and it turned a singer into a representative figure for Appalachian working people, a role she accepted and occasionally resented. Critics debate whether its plainness is artless or extremely controlled; the manuscript evidence of heavy cutting supports the second reading. It is now routinely cited as the model for autobiographical songwriting in country music.","Count the concrete nouns on one hearing. Then listen for the banjo, which is doing the job a string section would do in a Nashville Sound production, and note how different the social claim sounds as a result. Pair it with an orchestral evocation of a Southern childhood and hear two classes remembering the same region."],"loretta-lynn-the-pill":["A fast, cheerful honky-tonk shuffle in which a farm wife informs her husband that the years of pregnancy are over and she is going out. The imagery is agricultural and unsparing: she has been the hen in the incubator while he was the rooster running loose, and now the maternity dresses are going in the bin. Lynn sings it grinning, at the top of her range, and the band plays it like a dance number. It takes under three minutes.","The oral contraceptive had been available in the United States for well over a decade, but access for married rural women had come slowly and the subject was unmentionable on country radio. Lynn recorded the song some years before her label would release it, and when it appeared in 1975 many stations refused to play it. It reached a large audience anyway, and rural doctors reportedly said it did more practical good than their leaflets.","Listen closer: the song never once uses a clinical word or makes a political claim. It is entirely domestic, addressed to one husband in one kitchen, and its argument is about fairness within a marriage rather than rights in the abstract. That is why it got past listeners who would have rejected a feminist statement, and it is also why the humour is so pointed. Note the bright major key throughout; nothing in the music admits to controversy.","The bans made it news and the news made it sell, a pattern Lynn had already seen with earlier songs about drinking husbands and other women. Critics then and now split over whether it is a feminist landmark or a comedy record that happened to touch a nerve; Lynn herself framed it as simple description of what women she knew were doing. It remains among the most banned singles in the format's history.","Listen for how much of the meaning sits in the delivery rather than the words, particularly the laugh she keeps almost suppressing. Then hear one of her earlier songs about a rival woman to see how consistently she wrote from inside a marriage rather than about one. The original single mix keeps the band bright and the voice forward."],"george-jones-the-grand-tour":["A man invites you into his house and shows you around. Room by room he points out the furniture his wife chose, the bed they shared, and finally a nursery with a child's things still in it; she has gone and taken the child. Jones sings in the polite, level voice of a guide, and only breaks it on the last word of each verse. Strings, steel guitar and a soft rhythm section keep the tour moving without ever raising their voices.","Billy Sherrill produced it for Epic in Nashville, working in the plush, string-heavy style the trade called countrypolitan, in which a session orchestra did the work that fiddles had once done. Jones's own marriage to Tammy Wynette was disintegrating in public at the time, and audiences heard the song against that; the writers had drafted it independently. Jones was drinking heavily and had begun missing sessions and concerts, a pattern that would define the next decade.","Listen closer: the conceit is a real estate viewing, and it never slips. Every emotional detail is presented as a feature of the property, which is why the record can describe a ruined family without a single direct statement of feeling. Notice where the strings enter, always on the move between rooms, never under the revelation itself. The nursery arrives in the final verse with no musical preparation at all.","It is the connoisseur's George Jones record rather than the famous one, and singers cite it as the hardest to bring off, because the restraint has to hold for three minutes before it pays. Countrypolitan production has been out of fashion and back again; Sherrill's strings, once dismissed as syrup, are now read as the deliberate cushion that lets a voice like this one whisper instead of cry.","Follow the floor plan. Hear it as a walk through a house and notice how the camera, so to speak, keeps moving. Then listen only to the last word of each verse, where the guide's composure fails for a syllable. Follow it with a Romantic song cycle about a man revisiting the places a lover has left; the method is the same."],"george-jones-he-stopped-loving-her-today":["A friend describes a man who kept a woman's letters and photograph for decades after she left, swearing he would love her until he died. The narrator gives the joke a turn in the title phrase, and then the bridge is spoken rather than sung: the man is dead, and she has come to see him one last time. A string section swells at the final chorus and Jones takes the song up a key, his voice thinning at the top.","Billy Sherrill spent a long time getting this record finished. Jones's addiction to alcohol and cocaine had reached the point where sessions collapsed and concerts were missed so often that promoters advertised him as No Show Jones; the vocal was assembled over many visits across more than a year. Jones reportedly disliked the melody and told Sherrill nobody would buy a song that morbid. It restored his career and is the record he sang every night afterwards.","Listen closer: the title works as a double meaning that the song withholds until the bridge, and the spoken passage exists because a sung line would have given it away too early. Notice the key change at the end, unusual in a ballad this restrained; it arrives exactly where the funeral does. The strings play a falling figure between verses that never resolves until the last bar.","It is routinely voted the greatest country song ever recorded, which has made it hard to hear freshly. Its defenders point to the construction, a punchline withheld for two and a half minutes; its sceptics call it a gimmick dressed in a string arrangement. The story of how it was extracted from a man in freefall has become part of the record's meaning, and some writers argue that biography is doing work the song cannot do alone.","Listen once knowing nothing and let the bridge land. Then listen again for the strings, and for how carefully Sherrill keeps them off the vocal until the last chorus. Afterwards try a song cycle that ends at a graveside; country and lieder solve the same problem, how to make a small voice carry a death, in almost the same way."],"tammy-wynette-stand-by-your-man":["Two short verses and a chorus that arrives twice, the second time a whole step higher. Wynette begins almost gently, advising that it is hard to be a woman giving all your love to one man, then pushes into a wide, cracking upper register on the title line, with a catch on the first word that sounds like a sob and is entirely deliberate. Strings and steel guitar rise underneath. The record is under three minutes and spends most of them climbing.","Wynette wrote it with her producer Billy Sherrill in his Nashville office, reportedly very quickly, near the end of a session that needed one more song. She had worked as a hairdresser in Alabama and had three children before her recording career began. The single appeared in 1968, into an America already arguing loudly about what women owed their marriages, and it became the best-selling record ever made by a woman in country music.","Listen closer: the verse is a warning and only the chorus is advice. Wynette spends the first half of the song saying that this arrangement is hard, that he will do things you cannot understand, and only then delivers the title. The modulation into the final chorus is what most people remember as emotion; it is in fact a mechanical device, and it works because her voice has to strain to reach the new key.","It was attacked by feminists on release and defended by Wynette as a song about love rather than submission. Hillary Clinton's dismissive reference to it in a 1992 television interview restarted the argument on a national scale and prompted an apology. Recent criticism has tended to read the record against Wynette's own life of five marriages and financial exploitation, hearing the crack in the voice as evidence against the words.","Listen to the verse and the chorus as two different songs, because they say different things. Then track the modulation and notice how audibly she is working at the top. Pair it with an opera in which a woman's self-sacrifice is presented as the noble choice, and the question the record raises turns out to be a very old one."],"tammy-wynette-d-i-v-o-r-c-e":["A mother and father are spelling out words so their four-year-old will not understand them. The song's hook is the spelling itself, letter by letter, and Wynette sings those letters in the same warm, careful tone she uses for everything else, which is what makes it unbearable rather than cute. The band plays a moderate shuffle with steel guitar fills. At the end the child's own future is spelled out as well, and the song stops without comment.","Written by the same pair who would later write He Stopped Loving Her Today, and produced by Billy Sherrill in the year of Stand by Your Man, so Wynette had two of the decade's defining country singles inside twelve months. She was building the public role that earned her the title First Lady of Country Music, singing about marriages under strain while her own were collapsing, and country radio in 1968 was still uneasy about the word divorce in a title.","Listen closer: the spelled words are not all sad ones. The device is introduced with ordinary domestic secrets before it is used for the real thing, so the listener learns the code in the first verse and is then made to decipher the second. Notice that the child never speaks and is never described, only referred to. The song's whole emotional weight rests on a person who is not in it.","Novelty spelling songs were a familiar country device and the writers used the convention against itself, which is why the record has outlasted the genre it came from. It has been parodied often enough that some listeners meet the joke before the song. Critics who dislike it hear a manipulative trick; those who defend it point out that the trick is doing something no direct statement could, which is to make the listener perform the concealment.","Notice that you decode the letters faster than the child would, and that the discomfort is exactly that gap. Then listen for how little Wynette changes her tone across the song. Pair it with a ballad in which a child is the unwitting centre of a catastrophe narrated by adults; the technique of withholding is identical."],"dolly-parton-coat-of-many-colors":["Parton tells a story from her childhood: her mother sewed her a coat out of a box of rags, stitching it while telling the Bible story of Joseph, and the children at school laughed at her for wearing rags. The arrangement is light, an acoustic guitar and a discreet steel, and Parton sings high and clear with almost no ornament. There is no chorus; the song moves straight through the incident from the sewing to the mockery to the last line.","Parton was recording for RCA under the wing of Porter Wagoner, whose television show had taken her from a Tennessee mountain family to a national audience. She reportedly wrote this on the back of a dry-cleaning receipt from one of Wagoner's flamboyant stage suits, a detail she has told often and which the receipt itself, now in a museum, supports. Country radio in 1971 rewarded songs about rural poverty, and she had lived the material.","Listen closer: the song makes a theological argument and never announces it. The mother's Bible story gives the coat its meaning, the schoolchildren refuse that meaning, and the child accepts her mother's reading over theirs, which is the only conflict in the piece. Notice also that Parton does not describe the coat's colours; she describes the box of rags and the sewing. The object is never actually shown to us.","Parton has repeatedly named it her favourite of her own songs, and it has become the anchor of a public persona built on the distance between where she started and where she arrived. Critics sometimes note how effectively that persona works as a commercial instrument, and how carefully the poverty is presented without bitterness. The song's afterlife in books and television films has made it more sentimental than the recording is.","Listen for what the arrangement declines to do: no strings, no swell at the humiliation, no key change at the end. Then hear her sing it forty years later and notice how little the reading has changed. Pair it with an orchestral setting of a remembered Southern childhood to hear the same landscape from a different class."],"dolly-parton-jolene":["An acoustic guitar figure circles a minor chord four times before the voice enters, and it keeps circling, unchanged, for the whole song. Parton addresses a rival by name and begs her not to take a man she could take without trying, cataloguing the woman's beauty in detail that sounds close to infatuation. The melody is built from a descending three-note phrase repeated obsessively. Parton doubles her own voice in places, so the plea seems to come from two people at once.","Recorded for RCA in Nashville while she was still appearing on Porter Wagoner's television show and preparing to leave it. She has said the name came from a young fan at an autograph table and the situation from a red-haired bank teller who flirted with her husband. The early 1970s were the point at which Nashville's women stopped being novelty acts on male-led shows, and this single, along with what came next, funded her independence.","Listen closer: the song has no bridge, no key change and effectively no chord progression, just the same short loop under everything. All the variation is in the voice. Notice that the narrator never speaks to her husband and never blames him; the entire drama is conducted between two women, one of whom is silent. The guitar's circling figure is also the reason the song survives transplanting into rock, metal and electronic settings without losing its shape.","It has become one of the most covered songs written in the last fifty years, taken up by artists with no connection to country at all, and it survives being slowed to a dirge or sped into a punk song. Critics have written a great deal about the desire audible in the narrator's description of her rival. Parton has always declined the reading, which has not slowed it down.","Listen to the guitar loop alone and notice it never develops; then listen to how many different emotions Parton puts over the identical four bars. Try a radically slowed cover afterwards to hear what the tempo was hiding. The original RCA single mix, with the voice close and the band dry, is still the best."],"dolly-parton-i-will-always-love-you":["A farewell sung at walking pace, with a spoken passage in the middle where Parton simply talks to the person she is leaving and wishes them well. The melody rises through the verse and settles on the title phrase without any display; she never belts, and the highest notes are the quietest. An acoustic guitar carries it, with light strings entering late. Parton's vibrato, fast and narrow, does most of the expressive work.","She wrote it as a goodbye to Porter Wagoner, whose television show and business partnership had made her career and had become a cage; she has said she played it to him in his office and that he wept and then agreed to let her go. Elvis Presley wanted to record it, but his manager required half the publishing, and she refused. That refusal became one of the most consequential business decisions in American popular music.","Listen closer: the song is a resignation letter, not a love song, and the words work as precisely for a professional parting as for a romantic one, which is why it reads differently once you know who it was written to. Notice the spoken bridge. Parton drops out of singing entirely at the emotional centre, so the most direct statement in the song is not sung at all.","Parton took it to the top of the country chart twice, in 1974 and again in 1982. Whitney Houston's 1992 recording, sung as a soul ballad with an unaccompanied opening and an enormous final chorus, became one of the best-selling singles ever made and for most listeners replaced the original. Critics comparing the two usually frame it as restraint against release; Parton has praised the cover while noting that she wrote it quiet.","Hear Parton's version first if you only know Houston's; the shock is how small it is. Listen for the spoken section and for the fact that nothing swells behind it. Then compare the two recordings back to back as two answers to the same question, whether a farewell should be whispered or sung at full voice."],"merle-haggard-mama-tried":["Two and a half minutes, fast, with a bright electric guitar figure that snaps rather than sustains. Haggard sings about a boy who would not be told, a widowed mother who did everything right, and a life sentence he is now serving. Roy Nichols plays a clipped Telecaster lead with a hard treble edge, and the rhythm section pushes slightly ahead of the beat throughout. The famous line about turning twenty-one in prison arrives in the first verse and never lets go.","Haggard had in fact served time in San Quentin as a young man for an attempted burglary, and had been in the audience when Johnny Cash played there. His records were made in Los Angeles rather than Nashville, with a band of West Coast players whose hard, trebly electric sound had been shaped in the dance halls of Bakersfield, a town full of families who had come west from Oklahoma in the Depression. That sound existed partly as a refusal of Nashville's strings.","Listen closer: the song is not strictly true and does not pretend to be. Haggard was never sentenced to life, and the exaggeration turns memoir into ballad, which is the form's old privilege. Notice that the mother is the only person described with any warmth, and that the narrator accepts full blame in every verse. Listen for the banjo buried in the introduction, a nod to the Appalachian music his Oklahoma parents brought west.","It gave its name to an album and became the centre of Haggard's prison songs, a body of work whose credibility rested on his having actually been inside. Later writers have examined how carefully that biography was managed, and the pardon he received in the 1970s. The Bakersfield sound is now heard as the first significant argument against Nashville's centralised production, and as the ancestor of every subsequent country rebellion.","Listen to the guitar tone rather than the words on a first pass; the bright, undecorated Telecaster is the whole regional argument in one sound. Then compare a countrypolitan ballad of the same year. Follow it with an orchestral outlaw ballad from a generation earlier and hear how old the American appetite for a sympathetic criminal is."],"merle-haggard-okie-from-muskogee":["A brisk, almost jaunty shuffle in which a man from a small Oklahoma town lists what they do not do there: smoke marijuana, burn draft cards, wear beads or let their hair grow long. Fiddle and steel guitar trade cheerful fills, the band swings, and Haggard sings it deadpan, with no audible irony and no audible anger either. It runs under three minutes and the chorus is designed to be shouted back by a crowd.","Written on a tour bus with his drummer while passing a road sign for Muskogee, in the autumn of 1969, with American cities and campuses in open conflict over the Vietnam War. Haggard's audience included working people whose sons were being drafted while students protested. The record sold enormously, was played at political rallies, and turned a Bakersfield bandleader into a national symbol in a fight he had not obviously enlisted in.","Listen closer: the song is written in the first person plural, and Haggard was not from Muskogee and did not live that life. He described it at different times as a joke on his own band, as a character study of his father's generation, and as something he meant. The deadpan delivery is what makes all three readings possible, and the band's cheerfulness refuses to settle the question either way.","It was received as a conservative anthem and has never quite escaped that. Haggard spent decades complicating it, later writing anti-war material and speaking with sympathy about the people the song mocked, which convinced some listeners and not others. Critics now tend to read it as the moment country music's political identity hardened in public, whatever its author intended, and as a case study in how a record's meaning stops belonging to the person who made it.","Listen for the absence of scorn in the voice, which is the strangest thing about the record and the reason the argument survives. Then hear his later songs about hard times for working people and decide how consistent a writer he was. Pair it with an American orchestral piece built on marching bands and hymn tunes to hear the same patriotism handled with more irony."],"willie-nelson-red-headed-stranger":["A narrative album about a preacher who kills his wife and her lover and rides west, told across short songs, fragments and instrumental passages that often last under two minutes. The sound is startlingly bare: Nelson's nylon-string guitar, his sister Bobbie's upright piano, a quiet bass, brushed drums, and long stretches of near-silence between phrases. Themes recur between tracks so the record plays as one continuous thing rather than a collection of songs.","Nelson had spent the 1960s in Nashville writing hits for other singers and failing as a performer, then moved back to Texas and grew his hair. A new contract with Columbia gave him complete creative control, which no Nashville artist of his standing had held. He recorded the album quickly and cheaply in a studio near Dallas. Executives who heard the tapes reportedly assumed they were demos and asked when the real sessions would begin.","Listen closer: almost nothing on the record is new. The title song is a cowboy number written for a children's radio show in the 1950s, and the album's biggest hit was a country ballad from the 1940s; Nelson wrote the connecting material and let the borrowed songs carry the plot. Notice the silences. He leaves gaps where a band would normally play, and those gaps are what make the thirty-three minutes feel like a landscape.","Columbia released it under protest and it became one of the best-selling country albums of the decade, which effectively ended the label system's argument that Nashville producers knew best. It is the record usually credited with proving that outlaw country was commercial rather than merely defiant. Critics still divide over whether the sparseness is an aesthetic choice or a budget, and Nelson has cheerfully agreed with both.","Play it whole and in order; individual tracks are fragments and make little sense alone. Listen for the guitar tone, a nylon-string classical instrument with a worn hole in its face, which sounds like nothing else in country music. Then hear a nineteenth-century song cycle about a man walking away from a ruined love and notice how similar the strategy is."],"willie-nelson-stardust":["Ten standards from the American songbook, sung by a country singer with his own road band and produced by a soul musician. Nelson phrases far behind the beat, arriving late on almost every line and catching up by the end of the phrase, which turns familiar melodies slightly unfamiliar. Mickey Raphael's harmonica takes the solos a saxophone would normally take. The arrangements are unhurried and small; nothing is orchestrated, and the whole record moves at one gentle tempo.","Booker T. Jones, the organist behind the Memphis soul records of the 1960s, was Nelson's neighbour in California and agreed to produce. Nelson chose songs he had known since childhood, including pieces by Gershwin and by Kurt Weill, and made the record at a point when his outlaw success had bought him the freedom to do something his label considered commercial suicide. Columbia expected it to fail. It stayed on the country chart for roughly a decade.","Listen closer: his phrasing is the whole record. Nelson sings almost nothing on the beat, and the band holds a steady pulse precisely so the delay is audible; it is the technique of a jazz singer applied to a country voice with no vibrato to hide behind. Notice too that several of these songs began in Broadway shows and film scores, so the album quietly reunites two branches of American song that the record industry had spent fifty years separating.","It was heard at the time as a gamble and is now read as the record that made the standards repertoire available again to singers outside pop, decades before that became a common late-career move. Some jazz listeners find the intonation approximate and the band plain. The usual defence is that a songbook this over-recorded needed a voice with no polish at all to sound like a person again.","Pick one song you know well in another version and follow only where Nelson places the words against the beat. Then listen to the harmonica solos as substitute saxophone. Pair it with a Gershwin recording to hear how far these melodies travel. The original album sequence, which opens slow and stays slow, is part of the effect."],"emmylou-harris-pieces-of-the-sky":["Harris's first album as a country singer, built around a clear, unforced soprano with a distinctive tremor at the top. The band is loose and warm, with pedal steel and electric guitar taking turns. The repertoire runs from a close-harmony duet in the Louvin Brothers manner to a Beatles song to a honky-tonk drinking number, and at its centre is her own song about a friend's death, which is the only piece here she wrote and the reason the record exists.","Harris had been a folk singer in Washington when Gram Parsons recruited her to sing harmony on his solo albums and taught her the country repertoire. Parsons died in 1973, and she assembled a group of Los Angeles session players, later known as the Hot Band, to make this. It arrived while country rock was already a commercial category, but Harris's interest ran backwards, towards the brother duets and honky-tonk records the Californian bands had only glanced at.","Listen closer: she sings the Louvin Brothers song as a duet with herself, overdubbing the harmony line, which is how a singer without a brother joins a brother-duet tradition. Her elegy for Parsons places its grief in one specific geographical image, a road between two cities, and never names him. The Hot Band's steel player and lead guitarist are audibly playing 1950s licks on 1970s equipment, which is the record's whole argument.","It established Harris as the most reliable interpreter in country music, a singer who could make any song sound like part of one tradition, and it began a run of albums that carried older repertoire to a rock audience. The recurring criticism is that she wrote very little, which she has answered by pointing at the results. Her curatorial instinct is now generally treated as a form of authorship.","Start with the self-harmonised duet to hear the technique, then the elegy, then work outwards. Listen for how the steel guitar is recorded, dry and close, unlike the reverb-washed country rock of the period. Follow it with a pastoral elegy from another tradition; the business of mourning a friend through borrowed material is very old."],"emmylou-harris-wrecking-ball":["Harris's voice, by then twenty years lower and rougher, set inside vast reverberant space. Daniel Lanois built the record out of tremolo guitars, deep drums recorded in a room rather than close, and washes of sustained sound, so that the songs seem to be arriving from the far end of a hall. The material is borrowed from rock and folk songwriters rather than country ones. There is almost no steel guitar and no fiddle anywhere on it.","Country radio had stopped playing Harris, and rather than chase the format she went to a producer known for atmospheric rock records. The album was made in New Orleans, and the songs were chosen from writers she admired, including a Canadian rock songwriter whose title track gives the record its name, a young Appalachian revivalist, and a Texas songwriter then at her peak. It was released into a Nashville market that had no idea what to do with it.","Listen closer: the reverb is not decoration but structure. Lanois records the drums far from the microphone so that the decay of each hit becomes part of the harmony, and Harris's voice sits inside that decay rather than in front of it, which is why the album sounds like weather. Notice that several songs end without a final chord, simply fading into the room tone that has been present all along.","It won a Grammy, was ignored by country radio, and is now generally credited with giving the Americana category its sound and much of its self-image. The complaint from traditionalists was that the production swamps the songs; the counter-argument is that Harris had spent two decades serving material faithfully and was entitled to a record that served her voice instead. It remains the model every atmospheric roots album is measured against.","Listen on headphones in the dark; the record is built on depth and collapses on small speakers. Start with the title track and attend to the drums rather than the voice. Then play her 1975 debut immediately afterwards to hear twenty years of wear as an instrument. An ambient piano piece from a century earlier makes a surprisingly good companion."],"bill-monroe-mule-skinner-blues":["A Jimmie Rodgers blue yodel taken at a gallop. Bill Monroe and His Blue Grass Boys drive the old tune far faster than Rodgers ever did, with a tight string band pushing hard on every beat and Monroe's voice sitting high and hard above it, leaping into falsetto yodels at the end of each verse. It lasts under three minutes, and the first thing you notice is speed: this is country music played with the urgency of a fiddle contest.","Monroe auditioned for the Grand Ole Opry in Nashville in October 1939 with this song, and he was hired on the spot; the Opry's George D. Hay reportedly told him that if he ever left, he would have to fire himself. He cut it for RCA's Bluebird label in 1940. Country music on the radio was then dominated by smooth western swing and cowboy singers; Monroe's Kentucky band sounded older and wilder at once.","Listen to how the band treats the song's blues shape. Rodgers sang it loose and conversational over one guitar; Monroe keeps the blues structure but hardens the rhythm into a relentless chop. The banjo, later the signature of bluegrass, is absent here: Monroe's band sound was still forming, and the recording shows the style half-built, with the high lonesome voice already in place and the instrumental engine still arriving.","It was an immediate Opry favourite and became Monroe's calling card for decades. Historians treat it as the moment the Blue Grass Boys' identity took shape, though most date full-fledged bluegrass to the 1945–46 band with Flatt and Scruggs. The song itself went on to a long life in other hands, from Dolly Parton's hit version in 1970 to rockabilly and punk covers.","Play Jimmie Rodgers's Blue Yodel No. 1 first to hear the relaxed model, then this. Notice how the tempo alone changes the meaning: the loneliness becomes restlessness. Then jump ahead to Monroe's 1946 recordings with Flatt and Scruggs to hear what the missing banjo would add."],"bill-monroe-blue-moon-of-kentucky":["A slow waltz of heartbreak, sung by Bill Monroe in his high, keening tenor over the classic Blue Grass Boys: Lester Flatt's guitar, Earl Scruggs's banjo, Chubby Wise's fiddle and a string bass. The fiddle sighs through the introduction, Monroe's voice soars over a steady three-beat sway, and the mandolin fills the gaps with bright tremolo. It is gentle and plaintive, a song about a moon that shines on a lover who has gone.","Monroe wrote it in the mid-1940s and recorded it for Columbia in 1946, with the band many consider the first true bluegrass line-up; it was issued in 1947. Postwar country audiences were moving to cities and factories, and Monroe's music carried the sound of rural Kentucky with it. He named his whole style after the Bluegrass State, and this song made the naming explicit.","The song's second life is the thing to know. In July 1954, at Sun Studio in Memphis, a young Elvis Presley and his trio sped it up into four-beat rockabilly for the B-side of his first single. Monroe, rather than objecting, re-recorded the song himself soon afterwards with a slow waltz opening that breaks into a fast section, answering Elvis in his own style.","It became the Kentucky state bluegrass song and one of the most recorded numbers in the genre. Critics point to it as the clearest example of how close early rock and roll stood to hillbilly music: the same song sits at the root of both. Arguments continue over whether Monroe's original or the Elvis version matters more historically; the fair answer is that each needs the other.","Hear Monroe's 1946 waltz, then Elvis's 1954 reshaping, then Monroe's later hybrid version, one after another. Listen for the fiddle in the original: Chubby Wise's long, sliding phrases are as much the voice of the song as Monroe's singing. Then notice what Elvis keeps, the melody, and what he throws away, the waltz."],"bill-monroe-uncle-pen":["A brisk, affectionate portrait of a fiddler. Monroe sings of his uncle and the old tunes he played, and the band answers each verse with fiddle breakdowns that are themselves the tribute. The rhythm is a fast two-beat, the fiddle leads rather than decorates, and the chorus lifts into Monroe's trademark high harmony. It is a song about music, and it proves its point by playing the kind of music it describes.","Pendleton Vandiver, Monroe's mother's brother, was a fiddler in Rosine, Kentucky, who took the orphaned teenage Monroe in and played him old-time tunes at country dances. Monroe recorded this memorial in 1950, by then signed to Decca and leading a band that trained generations of players. Uncle Pen had died years earlier; the song fixed him as the fountainhead of the whole bluegrass style.","Notice what the song claims about origins. Monroe consistently credited Uncle Pen's fiddle bowing and Black blues guitarist Arnold Shultz as his two great teachers. The fiddle part here imitates an old-time Kentucky style, with its rocking rhythm and drone strings, grafted onto the modern bluegrass band. You are hearing Monroe place himself on a family tree, deliberately.","It became a bluegrass standard and a staple of fiddle-contest repertoire. In 1984 Ricky Skaggs took it to number one on the country chart, confirming its reach decades later. Historians value it as Monroe's own account of where bluegrass came from, while noting that his story tends to underplay the African American threads he elsewhere acknowledged.","Listen to the fiddle breaks between verses as the real subject. Then compare with a field recording of an old-time Appalachian fiddler to hear the tradition Monroe is honouring. Ricky Skaggs's 1984 version shows how the song sat in a slicker Nashville setting."],"flatt-and-scruggs-foggy-mountain-breakdown":["An instrumental showcase built around Earl Scruggs's banjo. Three fingers pick in rolling patterns so fast that single notes blur into a cascade, while guitar, mandolin, fiddle and bass pass solos around in turn. The tune is simple, a few bars repeated and varied, but the sound is unmistakable: a bright, metallic, forward-leaning rush. Most people know it without knowing its name, as the soundtrack to a car chase.","Lester Flatt and Earl Scruggs left Bill Monroe's band in 1948 and formed the Foggy Mountain Boys. They recorded this for Mercury in late 1949. Scruggs had perfected a three-finger picking style in North Carolina that turned the banjo, a rhythm instrument in string bands, into a lead voice capable of long, flowing runs. Postwar audiences for country instrumentals were small; the record sold modestly at first.","Listen for the moment the chord sequence darkens partway through each pass, a turn to a minor-sounding chord that gives the tune its urgency. Also notice that nobody plays drums: the forward drive comes entirely from the mandolin's off-beat chop and the bass. In 1967 Arthur Penn used the old recording in Bonnie and Clyde, and a new 1968 recording won a Grammy, nearly twenty years after the original.","It became the most famous banjo tune in the world and is now a rite of passage for every bluegrass picker. The film revived Flatt and Scruggs's career and pulled bluegrass into the counterculture. Purists sometimes resent its ubiquity as a comic chase cue, but musicians still use it as the benchmark of Scruggs-style technique.","Put on the 1949 Mercury original and follow the banjo alone for one chorus, then switch attention to the mandolin's chop underneath. Then watch the chase scene in Bonnie and Clyde. For contrast, hear Doc Watson flatpicking a fiddle tune on guitar."],"stanley-brothers-man-of-constant-sorrow":["An old Appalachian lament recast by Carter and Ralph Stanley in their early bluegrass style. Ralph takes the lead in a piercing, grainy high voice, telling of a wanderer who has lost home and love and will die a stranger; the band answers with spare mandolin and fiddle fills. The song is slow and grave, closer to a ballad than a breakdown, and the sound is stark rather than showy.","The song was probably first published around 1913 by Dick Burnett, a blind Kentucky fiddler, and circulated in variants through the mountains. The Stanley Brothers, from Virginia's Clinch Mountains, recorded it for Columbia around 1950, when they were forging their own mountain-flavoured variant of Bill Monroe's style. Their version made the song central to the bluegrass repertoire.","The Stanleys' vocal sound is the lesson. Where Monroe's tenor was bright, Ralph's is raw and nasal, rooted in the unaccompanied Primitive Baptist singing of his childhood. Listen to how he bends and holds certain words, a mountain church habit. Bob Dylan put a very different version on his first album in 1962, and the 2000 film O Brother, Where Art Thou? made it a hit again for a new generation.","It is now among the best-known songs in American folk music. For decades, however, it lived mainly in the bluegrass and folk-revival circuits; the O Brother soundtrack's success brought it to mass audiences and a Grammy. Scholars still debate its origins, which may predate Burnett, and folklorists treat its many variants as a case study in oral tradition.","Start with the Stanleys' recording and listen only to Ralph's voice. Then hear the Soggy Bottom Boys' version on the O Brother soundtrack, sung by Dan Tyminski, to see how the song was polished for film. Dylan's 1962 reading shows what the folk revival heard in it."],"stanley-brothers-rank-stranger":["A gospel song of homecoming and estrangement. Carter Stanley sings the verses in his warm, plain baritone: a man returns to the home of his youth and finds every face unknown. At the chorus Ralph Stanley's high tenor enters above him, ghostly and piercing, and the song turns from sorrow to a hope of reunion beyond death. The accompaniment is light and slow, leaving the two voices exposed.","The song was written by the Missouri gospel composer Albert E. Brumley, author of I'll Fly Away, and published in the 1940s. The Stanley Brothers recorded it in 1960, in the middle of a period when rock and roll had cut deeply into bluegrass's audience and they were making a living on small labels and on the road. Gospel material was always a large part of their repertoire.","The song works by vocal handover. Carter's lead is intimate and earthly; Ralph's answering voice seems to come from somewhere else entirely, which is exactly what the words are about. Listen to the chorus as a dialogue between the living and the dead. After Carter's death in 1966, when Ralph sang it with other partners, audiences heard the song as a memorial to his brother.","It is widely considered the Stanleys' finest recording and one of the great bluegrass gospel performances. It rarely gets the pop attention of Man of Constant Sorrow, but musicians cite it constantly. Its eeriness has made it a touchstone for later Americana singers who draw on the gothic, sacred strain in mountain music.","Wait for the chorus: the entry of Ralph's voice is the whole point. Listen for how little the instruments do. Then hear the Carter Family's Can the Circle Be Unbroken for another song about home, death and reunion, and Gillian Welch's Revival for its modern descendants."],"doc-watson-doc-watson":["Doc Watson's first solo album: a North Carolina mountain man, mostly alone with a flat-top guitar, playing ballads, blues, hymns and fiddle tunes. His baritone is relaxed and warm, and his guitar is a revelation, picking fast fiddle melodies note by note with a flat pick, clean and unhurried at speeds that sound impossible. Pieces like Black Mountain Rag and Deep River Blues show the two sides: dazzling instrumental runs and easy, rolling fingerpicked blues.","Watson, blind since infancy, grew up in Deep Gap, North Carolina, and had played electric guitar in a local rockabilly and swing band. In 1960 the folklorist Ralph Rinzler met him while recording Clarence Ashley, and brought him to the urban folk revival, where he quickly became a star at Newport and in coffeehouses. Vanguard released this album in 1964 at the height of that revival.","Watson learned many fiddle tunes on guitar because his band had no fiddler and dance crowds wanted them: the style that amazed folk audiences was a practical workaround from playing square dances on an electric guitar. Listen for how his flatpicked lines copy fiddle bowing, with little slides and double notes. Much of the repertoire came from his family and from the Carter Family and Jimmie Rodgers records he heard as a boy.","It established Watson as the defining flatpicking guitarist, and the style it popularised became standard in bluegrass guitar, notably through Clarence White and Tony Rice. Folk revival audiences treated him as an authentic tradition-bearer; later writers stress how eclectic his tastes actually were, including jazz and rockabilly. The album remains a starting point for acoustic guitarists.","Start with Black Mountain Rag for the flatpicking, then Deep River Blues for the fingerpicked side. Listen to how calm his voice stays while the guitar races. Then hear Old Time Music at Clarence Ashley's to meet him in his home setting."],"nitty-gritty-dirt-band-will-the-circle-be-unbroken":["A sprawling triple album in which a California folk-rock band sits down in Nashville with the elders of country and bluegrass. Maybelle Carter, Roy Acuff, Earl Scruggs, Doc Watson, Merle Travis, Jimmy Martin and fiddler Vassar Clements lead songs and tunes, with the young band backing them. Between numbers the tape keeps rolling, catching studio chat and count-ins. It ends with the whole company singing the Carter Family hymn of the title.","In 1971 the Nitty Gritty Dirt Band, long-haired Californians, persuaded musicians from a generation and a political world apart to record with them. The Vietnam War divided country's audience from rock's, and many Nashville figures distrusted hippies. Recorded quickly in a Nashville studio, largely live, it was released by United Artists in 1972 and sold far beyond expectations.","Listen to the talk between tracks. The candid moments, such as Acuff sizing up the young players before a take, and old musicians recalling where they learned a tune, are part of the record's argument that tradition passes hand to hand. Maybelle Carter's autoharp and guitar on Wildwood Flower connect straight back to her 1928 recording.","It became a landmark of cross-generational collaboration and brought many listeners to bluegrass and old-time country. Critics praise its warmth and documentary value more than any single performance. Two sequels followed, in 1989 and 2002; the 1972 original is still regarded as the one that mattered.","Do not listen straight through the first time. Pick the Maybelle Carter tracks, the Doc Watson tracks and the closing title song, then fill in the rest over several sittings. Notice how the young band plays under the elders rather than over them. Compare the finale with the Carter Family's original Can the Circle Be Unbroken from the 1930s."],"emmylou-harris-roses-in-the-snow":["Emmylou Harris, then one of country's biggest stars, makes an all-acoustic bluegrass and old-time record. Her clear, high voice rides over dobro, fiddle, banjo and flatpicked guitar, singing mountain gospel, Carter Family and Stanley Brothers songs, the old hymn Wayfaring Stranger, and a bluegrass version of Simon and Garfunkel's The Boxer. The arrangements are spare and bright, and the harmony singing is close and old-fashioned.","Harris had built her career on country rock since Gram Parsons's death. In 1980, with bluegrass unfashionable in Nashville, she and her producer and husband Brian Ahern chose an acoustic, traditional sound, drawing on her band's young players, above all Ricky Skaggs, with guests including Tony Rice and Jerry Douglas and harmony from stars such as Dolly Parton, Linda Ronstadt and Johnny Cash. Warner Bros. released it, and it sold well.","Listen to Ricky Skaggs, who sings harmony and plays fiddle and mandolin throughout and did much to shape the arrangements; the record foreshadows his own run of hit albums that brought traditional sounds back to country radio in the 1980s. The Boxer, turned into a bluegrass song, argues quietly that the folk revival and mountain music were never far apart.","It was a surprise success and is widely credited with helping the new traditionalist movement that followed in 1980s country. Some bluegrass purists found Harris's voice too polished; most welcomed a major star lending her platform to the tradition. It is often ranked among her finest albums.","Start with Wayfaring Stranger, then The Boxer, then a Carter Family song. Listen for Skaggs's high harmony against Harris's lead, and for the dobro between verses. Then go back to Gram Parsons's Grievous Angel to hear where her country voice was formed, and forward to Gillian Welch's Revival for the tradition's next turn."],"flying-burrito-brothers-the-gilded-palace-of-sin":["Honky-tonk, Southern soul and Los Angeles rock rolled into one. Gram Parsons and Chris Hillman sing close brother-style harmonies over Sneaky Pete Kleinow's pedal steel, which is often run through a fuzz box until it snarls like an electric guitar. Songs like Sin City and Hot Burrito No. 1 sit beside two Memphis soul covers, Do Right Woman and The Dark End of the Street. It is sad, sly and strange.","After leaving the Byrds, Parsons and Hillman formed the Burritos in Los Angeles in 1968 and released this on A&M early in 1969. They wore custom Nudie suits embroidered with pills, marijuana leaves and flames, turning the rhinestone costume of Nashville into a counterculture joke that was also sincere. Parsons called his ideal Cosmic American Music: country, soul and rock as one Southern inheritance.","The two soul covers are the key. Do Right Woman had been recorded by Aretha Franklin two years earlier; Parsons sings the Southern soul songs straight, as country laments, arguing that the Black and white music of the South were one family. Listen also for Kleinow's steel imitating horns and electric guitars.","It sold poorly but became a cult touchstone, cherished by the Rolling Stones, Elvis Costello, and later the alt-country movement. Critics now rank it with Sweetheart of the Rodeo as a founding country-rock document, sometimes above it for its originality. Hillman's contribution has been reappraised as central to the songs.","Begin with Hot Burrito No. 1 for Parsons's aching vocal, then Sin City for the harmony. Then compare Do Right Woman with Aretha Franklin's version. The Rolling Stones' Let It Bleed shows the Burritos' influence on British rock."],"gram-parsons-grievous-angel":["Gram Parsons's second and last solo album, built on his duets with Emmylou Harris. Their voices twine through Love Hurts, the title song and In My Hour of Darkness, his thin, fragile tenor leaning on her clear high harmony. The band, including members of Elvis Presley's touring group, plays spare, elegant country; the mood is wistful and road-weary, shifting between honky-tonk ballads and brighter country rock.","Parsons recorded it in Los Angeles in summer 1973 and died that September, aged 26, at Joshua Tree in the California desert, after a drug overdose. Reprise released it in January 1974. Harris, a folk singer he had discovered in Washington, D.C., had been learning country harmony from him; the album made her name and launched her career.","The medley of Cash on the Barrelhead and Hickory Wind is presented as a live bar recording, but it was cut in the studio with crowd noise and chatter added, a mock-live frame for Parsons's favourite song. Listen for the laughing and clinking glasses. In My Hour of Darkness, written with Harris, mourns friends including Clarence White and sounds now like his own elegy.","Received respectfully, it grew into a cult classic and set the template for the male-female country duet in Americana. Critics praise the harmonies above almost anything else in his work. The myth of his death, including the theft and burning of his body by his road manager, has sometimes overshadowed the music.","Start with Love Hurts and listen only to how Harris shapes the harmony. Then hear the Everly Brothers' close-harmony style, which Parsons loved. After this, Emmylou Harris's Pieces of the Sky shows her carrying the torch forward."],"the-band-the-band":["Often called the brown album, after its sepia cover. Four Canadians and an Arkansan play songs that sound like old America: the Civil War, farms, travelling medicine shows, floods. Three lead singers, Levon Helm, Rick Danko and Richard Manuel, trade verses in rough, overlapping voices; Garth Hudson's organ and horns thicken the sound. The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down and Up on Cripple Creek are its best-known songs.","After backing Bob Dylan in his electric years and working with him in upstate New York, the Band recorded this in 1969 in a pool house in the Hollywood hills converted into a studio. Rock in 1969 was loud, psychedelic and political; this record went the other way, toward period dress and the past. Robbie Robertson wrote most of the songs; Capitol released it that autumn.","Listen to the instruments swapping roles. Danko sometimes plays fiddle, Manuel drums on some tracks, Hudson overdubs horns himself, and Helm's drumming sits behind the beat in a relaxed Southern shuffle. The recording was deliberately dry and close, as if the band were in a room with you, at a time when big reverb was the norm.","It was received as a landmark and has stayed one, a foundation stone of what later came to be called Americana. The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down, told from a defeated Confederate's point of view, has drawn sharper criticism in recent decades. Arguments over songwriting credits between Robertson and the others became bitter in later life.","Start with Up on Cripple Creek for Hudson's clavinet and wah pedal, then The Night They Drove Old Dixie Down. Notice how often voices overlap rather than harmonise neatly. Then compare with Sweetheart of the Rodeo, released a year earlier."],"john-prine-john-prine":["A debut of plain, sure-footed songs. Prine's voice is dry and a little rough, and his band stays out of the way. The songs are tender and funny in the same breath: Hello in There on loneliness in old age, Angel from Montgomery sung as a middle-aged woman, Sam Stone on a wounded veteran's addiction, Paradise on strip mining in Kentucky. The detail is small and domestic; the effect is enormous.","Prine was a mail carrier and army veteran in Chicago, singing at open-mic nights, when Kris Kristofferson heard him and helped him to a contract with Atlantic. The album was recorded in Memphis with producer Arif Mardin and released in 1971, as the Vietnam War dragged on. Prine was widely billed as a new Dylan, a label he shrugged off.","Paradise names the Peabody Coal Company and Muhlenberg County, Kentucky, where Prine's parents came from and where surface mining had destroyed the landscape. Listen for how gently he sings it. Angel from Montgomery was written from the viewpoint of a woman, something his male peers rarely did; Bonnie Raitt's 1974 version made it a standard.","It won strong reviews but modest sales at the time; its songs were spread through covers. It is now regarded as one of the great singer-songwriter debuts. Prine's reputation rose steadily over five decades, and his death in 2020 during the pandemic prompted a wide reassessment of his craft.","Start with Hello in There and Angel from Montgomery, then Paradise. Listen for the jokes placed right next to grief, and for how little the band does behind him. Then hear Bonnie Raitt's version of Angel from Montgomery to see how the song travelled, and Townes Van Zandt's Pancho and Lefty for a darker sibling in the same trade."],"townes-van-zandt-pancho-and-lefty":["A border ballad about a Mexican bandit and the friend who may have betrayed him. Van Zandt sings in a quiet, grave baritone over fingerpicked guitar and discreet accompaniment. The story is told in fragments, with dust, desert and a lonely hotel in Cleveland, and never quite says what happened. The melody rises and falls like an old cowboy song, but the storytelling is modern: elliptical, cinematic, morally unresolved.","Van Zandt, from a wealthy Texas family, lived much of his life in poverty, drifting and struggling with alcohol and depression. The song appeared on The Late Great Townes Van Zandt, a 1972 album on the small Poppy label. It had little impact until others sang it: Emmylou Harris in 1977, and above all Willie Nelson and Merle Haggard, whose 1983 duet was a country number one.","Van Zandt often said he did not know where the song came from and that it arrived nearly complete. Listen for what the lyric withholds: we never learn whether Lefty betrayed Pancho or simply survived him. Pancho may nod to Pancho Villa, but the song is not a history. The mystery is the design, and it lets every singer take a side.","It is now among the most admired songs in country and folk writing, frequently cited by songwriters as a model. Van Zandt himself sold few records in his lifetime; admiration came mainly from fellow writers such as Steve Earle, who called him one of the best ever. His original recording is still less known than the Nelson–Haggard version.","Listen to Van Zandt's version first, in a quiet room. Then play the Nelson–Haggard duet and notice how a hit arrangement brightens it. Steve Earle's album of Van Zandt songs, Townes, is a good next step."],"steve-earle-guitar-town":["Country songs played with rock-and-roll swagger. Earle sings in a raspy Texas drawl over twangy electric guitars and a lean band, the Dukes. The songs are about working men, small towns, cars and the urge to leave: the title track, Someday and Hillbilly Highway. It sounds like Nashville crossed with Bruce Springsteen, crisp and tuneful but with dirt under its fingernails.","Earle had spent a decade as a Nashville songwriter and scuffling rockabilly act. MCA released this debut in 1986, produced by Tony Brown and Emory Gordy Jr., in the same wave that brought Dwight Yoakam and Randy Travis: a turn back to traditional sounds after the pop-heavy country of the early 1980s. It found an audience on country radio and among rock critics at once.","Listen to the guitar sound. Earle's twangy electric parts were deliberately bright and hard, halfway between Buck Owens's Bakersfield honky-tonk and heartland rock. Someday, about a young man pumping gas and dreaming of escape, uses the same working-class vocabulary as Springsteen, but its setting is the country side of the tracks.","It was a critical and commercial success, and it has since been credited as a precursor of alt-country. Earle's later albums moved further toward rock and politics, and his troubles with addiction and prison in the early 1990s interrupted his career. After his return he became one of the elder statesmen of Americana.","Start with Someday, then the title track. Notice the tight, economical arrangements, no song much over four minutes. Compare with Merle Haggard for the older working-man tradition, and then Copperhead Road for Earle's harder turn."],"steve-earle-copperhead-road":["Earle's hardest-rocking album. The title track opens with a droning figure that sounds like bagpipes before drums crash in, telling a three-generation family story of moonshine, war and illegal crops in the Tennessee hills. The rest of the record mixes storytelling ballads, loud country rock and the Irish-tinged Johnny Come Lately, recorded with the Pogues. It is heavier and angrier than Guitar Town.","Released in 1988 by MCA's rock-oriented imprint Uni, the album was marketed to rock fans because country radio found Earle too rough. The Vietnam War and its veterans were a live subject in late-1980s America, after films like Platoon; several songs carry the war's shadow. Earle was also in the grip of addiction during this period.","The title track's narrator is a Vietnam veteran, the grandson and son of bootleggers, who brings home new techniques from the war. Listen for how the drone at the start imitates Scottish pipes, a hint at the Scots-Irish ancestry of Appalachia that the whole song trades on. Johnny Come Lately sets a World War II homecoming beside a Vietnam one.","The title song became Earle's best-known track, a staple of rock radio and line-dance floors. The album sits awkwardly between country and rock, which divided critics at the time. It is now recognised as a key border-crossing record for Americana.","Play the title track loud, listening for the moment the drums crash in after the droning opening, and follow the story across its three generations. Then hear Johnny Come Lately with the Pogues for the Irish side. Going back to Guitar Town afterwards shows how far and how fast Earle travelled in two years."],"lucinda-williams-lucinda-williams":["A lean, guitar-based record by a Louisiana-born singer with a drawling, cracked voice. The songs are about desire, restlessness and places: Passionate Kisses, Changed the Locks, Crescent City. The band plays unfussy rock and roll, country shuffle and blues, with none of Nashville's gloss. Williams sings as if thinking aloud, slurring and stretching vowels, and the directness of the writing is the point.","Williams, daughter of the poet Miller Williams, had made two folk-blues albums on Folkways in the late 1970s and then spent years unsigned; country labels found her too rock, rock labels too country. The British independent Rough Trade released this in 1988. It sold modestly but circulated among musicians and critics.","Two songs here became hits for other artists: Mary Chapin Carpenter's Passionate Kisses won a Grammy in 1994, and Tom Petty recorded Changed the Locks. Listen for how Williams's own version of Passionate Kisses sounds defiant, even impatient, while Carpenter's is sunnier: the same words, a different self.","It is now regarded as a founding document of Americana, a genre name that did not yet exist. Critics singled out her writing immediately; the recognition from a wider public came a decade later with Car Wheels. The album was reissued with bonus material in 2014.","Start with Passionate Kisses, then Changed the Locks. Pay attention to her vowels: she makes a single word carry an argument. Then jump to Car Wheels on a Gravel Road to hear what ten years did."],"lucinda-williams-car-wheels-on-a-gravel-road":["A set of songs soaked in the American South: Louisiana back roads, lost loves, dead friends. Williams's voice is weary and tough, the band loose and rootsy, with slide guitar and accordion colouring the rock rhythm. The title song remembers a child's view from the back seat of a car; Drunken Angel mourns a songwriter; Lake Charles is a road elegy. Every track has a place name in it or nearby.","Recorded over roughly six years, through several studios and producers, including a period with Steve Earle and Ray Kennedy and a final polish with Roy Bittan of Springsteen's E Street Band. Williams's perfectionism became notorious. Mercury released it in 1998, and it won the Grammy for Best Contemporary Folk Album.","Listen to 2 Kool 2 Be 4-Gotten, which echoes the Delta blues world of Robert Johnson, a nod to the region's Black musical history. Drunken Angel is about the Texas songwriter Blaze Foley, killed in 1989. The album's scrupulous naturalism, from gravel under tyres to crickets, was built through dozens of retakes, not captured casually.","It was a critical landmark and her commercial breakthrough, topping many year-end polls. Some critics found the long gestation over-polished; most heard the opposite, a record that sounds lived-in. It remains the album most often used to define what Americana means.","Begin with the title track, then Lake Charles and Drunken Angel. Listen for the concrete nouns in every line. Afterwards hear Robert Johnson's Cross Road Blues to feel the ground under 2 Kool 2 Be 4-Gotten."],"uncle-tupelo-no-depression":["A punk trio plays country songs, and the collision sounds natural. Jay Farrar and Jeff Tweedy trade lead vocals over loud, distorted guitars and thrashing drums, then drop suddenly to acoustic guitars and hushed singing. The songs deal with small-town decline, drinking and dead-end jobs in the American Midwest. The title track is an old Carter Family song about the Depression, played straight.","Farrar, Tweedy and drummer Mike Heidorn grew up in Belleville, Illinois, a working-class town across the river from St. Louis. They played punk in their teens and discovered their parents' country records. This debut was recorded in Boston and released by the small Rockville label in 1990, as grunge was forming; it sold little at first.","The album's title and its cover of the Carter Family's No Depression in Heaven, from 1936, give the game away: Uncle Tupelo heard the hard-times songs of the 1930s as the true ancestors of punk. Listen for how the band keeps the old hymn's plain melody while raising the volume, and for how Farrar's and Tweedy's voices divide the album between gravity and warmth.","It is regarded as the founding record of alternative country; a magazine founded in 1995 took the name No Depression, and alt-country fans became known by it. The band split in 1994 into Son Volt and Wilco. Critics now debate whether the label alt-country helped or trapped the music that followed.","Play Graveyard Shift for the punk side, then the title track for the hushed country side, and notice how sudden the switch is. Listen to the Carter Family's 1936 original afterwards. Then hear Son Volt's Trace and Wilco's Yankee Hotel Foxtrot to follow the two singers as their paths split after 1994."],"son-volt-trace":["Jay Farrar's first album after Uncle Tupelo. His deep, grave voice sings of rivers, highways and the Midwest, alternating slow acoustic ballads with fiddle and lap steel against loud, distorted rock songs. Windfall, the opening track, is a song about driving at night with the radio on; Tear Stained Eye and Drown follow. The mood is melancholic, the sound open and spacious.","Uncle Tupelo split in 1994. Farrar formed Son Volt with Tupelo drummer Mike Heidorn and the brothers Jim and Dave Boquist, and Warner Bros. released Trace in 1995, the same year Tweedy's Wilco debuted. Alt-country was becoming a scene with its own magazine and radio format, and the two ex-bandmates were compared at every turn.","Listen to the fiddle and lap steel on Windfall: the arrangement almost imitates an old radio signal fading in and out, matching the song's subject, a late-night AM station on the road. Farrar's lyrics are strewn with Mississippi River imagery; the river runs through the whole record like a map.","It was the better-received of the two post-Tupelo debuts and remains Son Volt's high point. Critics then assumed Farrar would be the more important songwriter; Wilco's later experiments reversed that judgment. Trace is still considered one of the purest alt-country albums.","Start with Windfall, ideally in a car at night, and listen to the fiddle and steel drifting like a fading radio. Then play Drown for the loud side of the band. Compare with Wilco's Yankee Hotel Foxtrot to hear where the other half of Uncle Tupelo went, and why fans argued about them for years."],"wilco-yankee-hotel-foxtrot":["Folk-rock songs dissolving into noise, static and electronic texture. Jeff Tweedy sings in a frail, conversational voice; beneath him the band builds melodies that keep being interrupted by radio hiss, clanging percussion and drones. I Am Trying to Break Your Heart opens in a clatter; Jesus, Etc. is a lovely string-laced ballad; Poor Places ends in a wall of noise. Country's plain storytelling is still there, but half buried.","Wilco recorded it in Chicago in 2001 with Jim O'Rourke mixing. Reprise, their label, rejected it as uncommercial and released the band. Wilco streamed the album free on their website in September 2001, just after the attacks on New York, and Nonesuch, another Warner label, released it in April 2002. Songs about towers and war suddenly seemed prophetic.","The title comes from a shortwave numbers-station recording, a woman's voice reading the phonetic alphabet, taken from The Conet Project compilation. Listen for it at the end of Poor Places. The documentary I Am Trying to Break Your Heart filmed the sessions, including the departure of multi-instrumentalist Jay Bennett, and the album's story became part of its reception.","It was hailed as a landmark and became a symbol of artists defeating a major label through the internet. Some alt-country fans felt the band had abandoned its roots. Critics now see it as proof that the Uncle Tupelo tradition could absorb experimental rock and electronics without losing its heart.","Start with Jesus, Etc., the most straightforward song, then I Am Trying to Break Your Heart. Listen for the static, radio fragments and clatter as part of the music, not interference. Afterwards hear No Depression to measure the distance travelled from Uncle Tupelo, and watch the documentary of the sessions if you want the backstory."],"gillian-welch-revival":["Gillian Welch and David Rawlings sing new songs that sound a century old. Their two voices lock in close, dry harmony; their two guitars interlock, Welch's rhythm steady, Rawlings's lead bright and angular. The songs are about mountain poverty, orphanhood, moonshine and faith: Orphan Girl, Annabelle, Tear My Stillhouse Down. The sound is sparse, slow and dusty, with occasional muted drums and bass added by producer T Bone Burnett.","Welch, raised in Los Angeles, fell in love with the Stanley Brothers at college and moved to Nashville, where she met Rawlings at Berklee in Boston. Almo Sounds released this debut in 1996. Emmylou Harris had already recorded Orphan Girl on Wrecking Ball in 1995. Welch's authenticity as a Californian writing Appalachian songs was questioned from the start.","Listen to Rawlings's guitar. He plays an old small-bodied archtop guitar with a sharp, pinched tone, and his lead lines often cut across the melody rather than supporting it. The duo's harmonies deliberately avoid sweetness, imitating the brother-duet sound of the Stanleys and the Louvins.","It won strong reviews and a Grammy nomination, and it helped create the audience for O Brother four years later. The authenticity debate has faded; Welch is now recognised as among the best songwriters of her generation. Some prefer later albums for their variety, but Revival remains the purest statement.","Start with Orphan Girl, then compare Emmylou Harris's version on Wrecking Ball from the year before. Listen closely to the interplay of the two guitars, his darting and hers steady, and to the dry, unsweetened harmony. Then go back to the Stanley Brothers' Rank Stranger to hear the sound the duo learned from."],"gillian-welch-time-the-revelator":["Just two voices and two guitars, with a banjo here and there, recorded in Nashville's old RCA Studio B. The songs are slow and long, bending mountain music toward meditation: Revelator opens; Elvis Presley Blues remembers the King's shaking hips; Everything Is Free comments on the music industry; I Dream a Highway, nearly fifteen minutes, closes the album in a drifting reverie. The mood is dreamlike and patient.","Welch and Rawlings, having left their label, founded their own, Acony Records, and released this in 2001. Napster and file-sharing were upending the music business; Everything Is Free takes up the subject calmly, as a question of how a musician lives. The O Brother soundtrack, on which Welch appeared, was then making old-time music newly popular.","Studio B was where Elvis and the Everly Brothers recorded; Welch and Rawlings chose it for its room sound. Listen to the room ambience on Elvis Presley Blues, and for how the song makes Elvis a figure of folk myth, like John Henry. The album's title plays on the old gospel song John the Revelator.","It was received as a major record and is often ranked the finest of the 2000s in its field. Some reviewers found the long songs slow; others praised the courage of that pace. Its release on the duo's own label became a model for independent roots musicians.","Hear Elvis Presley Blues first, then Everything Is Free. Leave time for I Dream a Highway and let it drift. Afterwards hear Blind Willie Johnson's John the Revelator for the gospel source of the title."],"alison-krauss-so-long-so-wrong":["Contemporary bluegrass of great delicacy. Alison Krauss sings in a clear, pure, almost weightless soprano; her band, Union Station, plays with precision and restraint. Jerry Douglas's dobro glides between verses, the banjo rolls softly, and ballads alternate with fast instrumentals. Dan Tyminski takes lead on some songs with a harder, more traditional voice. The textures are silky, the tempo mostly unhurried.","Krauss, a fiddle prodigy from Illinois, signed with the independent Rounder label as a teenager. By the mid-1990s a greatest-hits collection had made her a platinum-selling star, remarkable for bluegrass. So Long So Wrong, released by Rounder in 1997, was a full band album that won the Grammy for Best Bluegrass Album.","Listen to how the voice is placed in the mix, close and soft rather than projected, much as in pop balladry. Traditional bluegrass singers shout to carry over the band; Krauss's intimacy was a studio-age innovation that brought the genre to listeners who had never heard of Bill Monroe. The instrumentals show that the band's chops are as hard as any traditional outfit.","It was praised as a high-water mark of modern bluegrass and won the Grammy for Best Bluegrass Album. Traditionalists occasionally found Krauss's sound too smooth, closer to pop balladry than to the hard, projected singing of Monroe or Ralph Stanley, and the old argument over what counts as real bluegrass followed her for years. Her popularity nonetheless opened the genre to new audiences, and Union Station's players went on to form the core of the O Brother soundtrack.","Start with the title track, then a Dan Tyminski lead vocal for contrast between his hard traditional voice and her soft one. Listen for Jerry Douglas's dobro sliding under the singing, and for the tight instrumentals. Afterwards go back to Bill Monroe's Blue Moon of Kentucky to measure how far bluegrass singing had moved."],"alison-krauss-raising-sand":["Robert Plant of Led Zeppelin and Alison Krauss sing old and obscure songs together, softly. Their voices, his husky, hers crystalline, blend in close harmony over dark, reverberant arrangements: tremolo guitars, brushed drums, spare fiddle. Songs by the Everly Brothers, Townes Van Zandt, Tom Waits and Allen Toussaint are slowed into a hazy, mysterious mood. It sounds like a late-night radio show from 1958 heard through a desert haze.","Producer T Bone Burnett chose the songs and the musicians, including guitarist Marc Ribot. Rounder released it in 2007. Plant, the most famous hard-rock singer of the 1970s, and Krauss, bluegrass's biggest star, seemed an unlikely pair; the gamble paid off. In 2009 it won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","Listen to how the singers rarely belt. Plant, once famous for howling, sings almost at a whisper, and the arrangements leave space where one expects a solo. Gone Gone Gone comes from the Everly Brothers, and the vocal blend imitates their close fifths and thirds. Townes Van Zandt's Nothin' becomes a brooding drone.","It was a critical and commercial success and swept the Grammys. Some bluegrass fans missed Krauss's band; some rock fans missed Plant's roar. It is now seen as one of the defining records of the 2000s Americana boom, alongside O Brother.","Start with Please Read the Letter, then Gone Gone Gone, and listen to how the two voices blend rather than compete. Notice how the reverb and tremolo guitars create distance, as if the songs were drifting in from another decade. Then play the Everly Brothers to hear the harmony tradition Plant and Krauss are borrowing from."],"t-bone-burnett-o-brother-where-art-thou":["A soundtrack for a Coen brothers film set in 1930s Mississippi, compiled and produced by T Bone Burnett. It mixes old recordings with new performances in old styles: Ralph Stanley's unaccompanied O Death, the Soggy Bottom Boys' Man of Constant Sorrow, Alison Krauss, Gillian Welch and Emmylou Harris singing a lullaby, the Fairfield Four, and a 1959 prison work-song field recording. Gospel, bluegrass and blues sit side by side.","The Coens built their film around the music, recording much of it before shooting. Released in 2000 on Mercury's roots label Lost Highway, the album received almost no radio play but sold several million copies, won the Grammy for Album of the Year in 2002, and spawned the Down from the Mountain concert tour. It turned Americana into a mass-market category.","George Clooney mimes in the film, but the voice of Man of Constant Sorrow belongs to Dan Tyminski of Union Station. Listen for the field recording of Po' Lazarus, made by Alan Lomax at a Mississippi prison; its lead singer, James Carter, was traced decades later and presented with royalties. Ralph Stanley's O Death is sung in the old unaccompanied Baptist style.","Its success astonished the music industry and prompted a revival of interest in bluegrass and old-time music. Some traditionalists grumbled that the album polished the old sounds; most musicians in the field credit it with reviving their careers. It remains one of the best-selling soundtracks ever.","Start with Ralph Stanley's unaccompanied O Death, then the Soggy Bottom Boys' Man of Constant Sorrow, then the prison work song Po' Lazarus. Notice how old recordings and new ones sit side by side without jarring. Then go to Harry Smith's Anthology of American Folk Music for the older originals the album echoes."],"carolina-chocolate-drops-genuine-negro-jig":["A string band of young Black musicians playing old-time Piedmont fiddle and banjo music. Rhiannon Giddens's strong voice leads ballads and blues; fiddle and banjo drive dance tunes; bones, jug and beatboxing supply rhythm. The repertoire mixes traditional tunes with modern covers, including Blu Cantrell's R&B hit Hit 'Em Up Style played as a string-band song. The sound is lively, rough-edged, and plainly joyful.","Giddens, Dom Flemons and Justin Robinson met at the Black Banjo Gathering in North Carolina in 2005 and apprenticed themselves to Joe Thompson, an elderly African American fiddler. Their mission was to reclaim the Black roots of the string band, erased by the racial marketing of records in the 1920s. Nonesuch released this in 2010, produced by Joe Henry; it won the Grammy for Best Traditional Folk Album.","The title probably comes from an old 19th-century tune title; the band reclaims the offensive label deliberately. Listen for the banjo, which descends from West African gourd lutes brought by the enslaved. The album quietly argues that bluegrass and country began partly as Black music, a history that the Stanleys and Monroe knew and the record business obscured.","It was widely praised and became a touchstone for the growing movement to recognise Black contributions to country and folk. Giddens went on to a major solo career and a MacArthur fellowship. Some critics found the album's range uneven; its historical argument is now widely accepted.","Start with Hit 'Em Up Style, then a traditional fiddle tune, and notice that the two do not sound as far apart as you expect. Listen for the bones and beatbox driving the rhythm. Then hear Elizabeth Cotten and Mississippi John Hurt for earlier Black Piedmont traditions that this band set out to honour."],"j-d-crowe-and-the-new-south":["An all-acoustic bluegrass album with a modern feel. J.D. Crowe's banjo leads a quintet with Tony Rice on guitar, Ricky Skaggs on mandolin and fiddle, and Jerry Douglas on dobro. They play bluegrass standards alongside songs by Gordon Lightfoot and folk writers, with smooth harmonies and crisp, clean solos. The tempo is brisk, the sound polished and exact, and the dobro and guitar play melody lines as fluid as jazz.","Crowe, a Kentuckian who had played with Jimmy Martin, had led an electric band in the early 1970s. For this Rounder release in 1975 the group went acoustic, but kept its pop and folk repertoire. The small independent Rounder label, founded by students in Massachusetts, was becoming the home of new bluegrass, and this album became one of its best sellers.","Listen to Tony Rice's guitar solos: he built on Doc Watson's and Clarence White's flatpicking and made the guitar a full lead voice in bluegrass. The cat number, Rounder 0044, is how bluegrass players still refer to the record. Almost every member went on to lead a major band: Rice, Skaggs and Douglas shaped the next thirty years of the music.","It is often called the most influential bluegrass album of the 1970s, bridging Monroe's tradition and the progressive bluegrass that followed. Critics praise its balance of polish and drive: the repertoire drew on Gordon Lightfoot and contemporary folk writers, yet the playing stayed acoustic and disciplined. Players still refer to it simply by its catalogue number, as jazz fans do with Blue Note records, and its line-up is regarded as one of the great bluegrass ensembles.","Listen to one track three times: once for the banjo, once for Tony Rice's guitar, once for Jerry Douglas's dobro. Notice how clean and exact the ensemble is. Then compare with Foggy Mountain Breakdown to hear how the bluegrass band sound had evolved in twenty-five years, and follow any player whose sound you like into his own records."],"jason-isbell-southeastern":["A quiet, mostly acoustic album of songs about sobriety, love and death. Isbell's voice is clear and steady; the arrangements are spare, with fingerpicked guitar, soft fiddle from Amanda Shires, and occasional full-band rock songs. Cover Me Up is a love song about recovery; Elephant describes a friend dying of cancer; Live Oak is a dark ballad about a man hiding his past. The mood is intimate and direct.","Isbell had played in the Alabama band Drive-By Truckers until 2007 and struggled with alcohol. In 2012 he went to rehab and married Shires. Producer Dave Cobb recorded this in Nashville, and it came out on Isbell's own Southeastern Records in 2013. It became his breakthrough, embraced by Americana radio and critics, largely outside the Nashville country establishment.","Listen to Elephant: the song names the illness only once and circles it, refusing melodrama, much as John Prine's Hello in There describes old age through a few household details. Isbell's craft lies in plain, exact particulars rather than big images. Cover Me Up, the opening song, is about fear of relapse as much as romance, and Amanda Shires's fiddle and harmony, heard across the record, tie the album to the marriage it describes.","It won Album of the Year at the Americana Music Honors in 2014 and ranks among the most admired records of the 2010s, largely without help from mainstream country radio. It re-established the confessional singer-songwriter as central to Americana after a decade dominated by bands. Critics often compare Isbell's craft with Prine's and his Southern working-class settings with Springsteen's; some found the record too quiet after his Drive-By Truckers work, a view that has faded.","Start with Cover Me Up, then Elephant, and listen for the small details that carry the weight, a name, a place, a joke. Notice how rarely the band gets loud. Then compare with John Prine's Hello in There, another song that finds grief in ordinary rooms, and with Lucinda Williams's Car Wheels on a Gravel Road."],"sturgill-simpson-metamodern-sounds-in-country-music":["Honky-tonk country with a cosmic twist. Simpson's deep baritone recalls Waylon Jennings; the band plays straight classic country, with pedal steel and twangy guitar, until songs veer into psychedelic sound: backwards guitars, swirling reverb. Turtles All the Way Down muses on religion, mind-altering experiences and love; It Ain't All Flowers dissolves into a tape-loop haze. A cover of the 1980s synth-pop song The Promise turns into a country ballad.","Simpson, a Kentuckian and former Navy sailor and railroad worker, made this on a small budget in Nashville with Dave Cobb and released it on his own label in 2014. It appeared when mainstream country was dominated by polished pop-rock, and it was hailed as a return to outlaw roots. The title nods to Ray Charles's 1962 album Modern Sounds in Country and Western Music.","Listen to It Ain't All Flowers: the backwards guitars and tape effects come from Beatles-era psychedelia, not from country at all. Simpson reportedly took the idea of metamodernism from writing on contemporary philosophy, and the album's mix of sincerity and irony follows it. The Promise, originally by When in Rome, was chosen to show that any song can be a country song.","It won widespread acclaim and made Simpson a leading voice of independent country; his next album was nominated for Album of the Year. Some critics found the outlaw-revival label reductive; he himself resisted it. It remains a touchstone for country musicians working outside Nashville.","Start with Turtles All the Way Down, then The Promise, and listen for the moment classic honky-tonk gives way to swirling psychedelic sound. Notice how short and tight the whole record is. Then hear Willie Nelson's Red Headed Stranger for the outlaw tradition, and the Beatles' Tomorrow Never Knows for the tape tricks."],"ike-turner-rocket-88":["A jump-blues boogie about a new Oldsmobile, sung by saxophonist Jackie Brenston over Ike Turner's band, the Kings of Rhythm. Turner's piano opens with a hammered boogie figure, the saxes riff in unison, and underneath everything sits a fuzzy, buzzing guitar doubling the bass line. It lasts under three minutes and never leaves its groove. The sound you remember is that guitar: dirty, blurred and loud, a noise no respectable record of 1951 was supposed to make.","The band drove up from Clarksdale, Mississippi, to Sam Phillips's Memphis Recording Service in March 1951. Phillips, not yet running his own label, leased the master to Chess in Chicago, which credited it to Jackie Brenston and His Delta Cats, leaving Turner's name off. It became a number-one rhythm-and-blues hit. Car songs were already a jump-blues staple; the Rocket 88 was Oldsmobile's fast new V8, a symbol of postwar Black mobility and money.","The fuzz was an accident. On the drive up, guitarist Willie Kizart's amplifier fell from the car and its speaker cone was torn; Phillips reportedly stuffed it with paper and recorded it anyway. That broken speaker is one of the first distorted guitar sounds on a hit record, the ancestor of every fuzz pedal. Listen, too, for how closely the tune follows earlier boogie car songs such as Jimmy Liggins's Cadillac Boogie; it was a genre piece, made new by its sound.","Its claim to be the first rock and roll record was made by Sam Phillips himself and repeated by historians ever since, though it has rivals going back to the 1940s and many musicians hear it simply as good jump blues. The argument matters less than what followed: the royalties built Phillips's Sun label, Bill Haley covered it within months, and Turner nursed the grievance of the missing credit for the rest of his life.","Play it loud and listen past the vocal to the guitar and bass under the saxophones; that grinding low buzz is the point. Then play Louis Jordan's jump records from the late forties to hear how little the song form changed and how much the sound did. Follow with Bill Haley's cover and Little Richard's Good Golly Miss Molly, which borrowed Turner's piano introduction."],"big-joe-turner-shake-rattle-and-roll":["A twelve-bar blues at an easy, rolling tempo: Big Joe Turner's enormous voice, a riffing saxophone section and a backbeat you could set a clock by. Verse after verse he orders his partner out of bed and into the kitchen, with the title phrase shouted back by the band as a chorus. It is relaxed rather than frantic, which is exactly why it swings; the power comes from the size of the voice, not the speed.","Turner was a Kansas City veteran in his forties when Atlantic recorded this in New York in early 1954. The song was written by the label's arranger Jesse Stone under the name Charles Calhoun. Atlantic's rhythm-and-blues records were beginning to be bought by white teenagers, and within months Bill Haley's Comets had covered it for Decca, with the lyrics cleaned up, and taken it into the pop charts.","Compare the two versions side by side and the story of early rock and roll is in the differences. Turner's lyric is frankly adult and full of blues double meanings; Haley kept the tune and the chorus but swapped out the suggestive verses for harmless ones. Elvis Presley's 1956 version mixes the two. Listen also for the saxophone riff that answers each vocal line: it is the Kansas City big-band habit of riffing, shrunk to a small combo.","It topped the rhythm-and-blues chart and has been in the canon of rock's origins ever since, a standard example of how Black records were covered and sanitised for white audiences. Turner's own career revived on the back of it, and later critics, rediscovering his 1930s boogie-woogie sides, came to hear him as a bridge between swing-era Kansas City and the rock and roll of the fifties.","Start with Turner's original and notice how little he strains; the voice is huge without shouting. Then hear Haley's cover and ask what was lost and what was gained. Turner's earlier Roll 'Em Pete with pianist Pete Johnson shows where the style came from; the Atlantic compilations of his fifties singles are the best way in."],"bill-haley-rock-around-the-clock":["Two minutes and ten seconds of countdown and release: Haley counts the hours from one o'clock onward over a stop-time rhythm, then the band crashes into a twelve-bar shuffle with slapped double bass, drums hitting the backbeat and a honking sax. A guitar solo of rapid-fire repeated notes rips through the middle. The shape is simple, the tempo brisk, the mood cheerful; it is a dance record by a band that had come up playing country and western swing.","Recorded in New York in April 1954 for Decca, with Milt Gabler producing; Gabler had made Louis Jordan's jump-blues hits and aimed to give Haley the same drive. On release it did only modestly. In 1955 the film Blackboard Jungle used it over the opening credits, and teenagers danced in the cinema aisles; it went to number one and began rock and roll's spread across the world, including riots at screenings abroad.","Listen to Danny Cedrone's guitar solo: its breakneck repeated-note run had already appeared, almost note for note, on Haley's earlier record Rock the Joint. Cedrone died within months of the session, before the record became famous. The song itself, by Max Freedman and James Myers, had been recorded first by another band; Haley's slapped bass and drum backbeat are what made it different.","For decades it was spoken of as the record that started rock and roll, a claim later historians complicated by pointing to the Black rhythm-and-blues records that came before it. Haley himself, older, plump and friendly, was quickly displaced by Elvis. But its sales and its film moment made it a genuine turning point in the music business, and it remains one of the best-selling singles ever made.","Listen first to the stop-time opening and the moment the full band hits; that release is the whole trick. Then listen for the slapped bass clicking under the beat. Put it beside Louis Jordan's records, which Gabler also produced, to hear the lineage, and beside Elvis's Sun sides to hear how much wilder the next year sounded."],"elvis-presley-thats-all-right":["Three men and no drummer: Elvis strumming an acoustic guitar and singing, Scotty Moore's electric guitar picking bright country licks, and Bill Black slapping a double bass for rhythm. The song is a blues about a lover who has gone, taken fast and light, with Elvis's voice leaping up into a high, playful register. The room echo, Sam Phillips's slap-back, makes three players sound like a band. It is under two minutes of pure buoyancy.","On 5 July 1954, at Sun Records in Memphis, a session for ballads was going nowhere when, during a break, Presley began clowning on Arthur Crudup's 1946 blues. Phillips asked them to do it again and recorded it. Disc jockey Dewey Phillips played it repeatedly on WHBQ within days. The B-side was Bill Monroe's bluegrass waltz Blue Moon of Kentucky, sped up: a Black blues and a white country song back to back.","The pairing on the two sides of the single is the whole programme of rockabilly: Crudup's Mississippi blues on one side, Monroe's bluegrass on the other, and both played the same way. Listen to how Moore's guitar never plays the blues licks you might expect but country fingerpicking. And listen for the absence of drums; the rhythm is carried entirely by Black's bass and Presley's strumming, which is why it floats.","Local radio listeners reportedly assumed the singer was Black, and some country stations would not play it. It sold modestly outside the South. Its reputation grew backwards: once Presley was famous, this first single was recognised as the moment the synthesis happened, and critics from Greil Marcus onward have treated the Sun recordings as his finest work. Crudup, for his part, received little money from it for years.","Hear Arthur Crudup's original first, then this. Notice how Presley keeps the melody but changes the feeling from resignation to delight. Listen to the whole run of Sun singles in order, ideally in a mono transfer, and pay attention to the way the echo makes the small room sound enormous."],"elvis-presley-mystery-train":["A train song driven by a chugging, locomotive rhythm: Scotty Moore's guitar plays a looping figure, Bill Black slaps the bass, and Elvis sings of a long train that has taken his woman and might bring her back. The song is a blues but the feel is ominous and propulsive, with the echo thickening everything. It builds to a lighter, triumphant final verse, as if the train is now bringing her home.","Junior Parker had recorded the song for Sun in 1953 as a slow, brooding blues. Presley's version was his fifth and last Sun single, released in 1955; by the end of that year Sam Phillips had sold his contract to RCA for about $35,000 to keep the label solvent. The record reached the national country charts, and Elvis was already touring the South as a sensation.","Moore's guitar figure was borrowed from another Parker record, Love My Baby, grafted onto this song. At the very end, listen closely and you can hear Elvis laugh, a moment of release left in the take. Critic Greil Marcus named his 1975 book on American music after this record, reading the train as a symbol of everything that carries American music forward and away.","Many critics consider it the best of the Sun sides, the one where the country and blues sources are most completely melted together. It stands at the end of Elvis's Sun period, and so has often been heard as a farewell to the small, strange world of Memphis before the fame machine. The debt to Junior Parker is now credited and discussed as fully as the performance itself.","Play Junior Parker's 1953 original, then this, and listen to how the tempo and the guitar transform a lament into a charge. Listen for the laugh at the end. Then hear Johnny Cash's train songs from the same studio for a sense of the Sun sound across artists."],"elvis-presley-heartbreak-hotel":["A slow, stark blues about loneliness, sung almost in a whisper and then a moan, with a walking bass and a guitar that stabs a chord at the end of each vocal line and then leaves silence. Floyd Cramer's piano tinkles in the background, and a bluesy guitar solo arrives halfway. The whole thing is drenched in echo, so the voice seems to come from the end of a long empty corridor. It is less than two and a half minutes of gloom made thrilling.","Elvis's first RCA recording, made in Nashville in January 1956 with his Sun trio plus Cramer and Chet Atkins. The song was by Mae Boren Axton and Tommy Durden, reportedly prompted by a newspaper story about a lonely man's suicide. RCA executives worried it sounded nothing like the Sun singles. Television appearances that spring pushed it to number one, and it became his first million-seller.","The echo was not added electronically in the usual way; the engineers reportedly used a hallway in the building as an echo chamber, which is why the voice sounds cavernous. Listen to how much of the record is silence: the band stops at the end of each line, and the tension comes from the gaps. It is, in effect, a torch song played as a blues.","Its success settled RCA's gamble and made Presley a national figure within weeks. In Britain it was a revelation: John Lennon, Keith Richards and others later remembered hearing it on the radio as a turning point in their lives. Some purists preferred the lighter Sun sound, but the record's darkness made rock and roll seem capable of real emotional weight.","Listen to the gaps: count how long the band stays silent after each line. Then compare it to That's All Right to hear how the same trio became something heavier in a bigger studio. Seek a mono release; stereo remixes blur the echo that makes it."],"elvis-presley-hound-dog":["A furious, fast twelve-bar blues that barely lasts two minutes and a quarter: drums like gunfire, Scotty Moore's guitar spraying two wild solos, the Jordanaires chanting behind, and Elvis snapping out insults at a worthless dog of a man. It hits hard from the first beat and never lets up. The drum fills between choruses, D.J. Fontana rattling around the kit like a machine gun, are the sound most people remember.","Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller wrote the song for Big Mama Thornton, whose growling 1953 version was a rhythm-and-blues number one. Elvis learned a comic rewrite from a Las Vegas lounge act, Freddie Bell and the Bellboys, and performed it on television in 1956 to outrage. He recorded it in New York in July 1956, working through dozens of takes; it was coupled with Don't Be Cruel and sold in millions.","The lyric Elvis sings is not quite Thornton's: the Bellboys had changed it into a song about an actual dog, with a line about rabbits that makes little sense next to her version, which is an adult woman telling off a freeloading man. Leiber reportedly disliked Elvis's take for exactly that. Listen, too, to the way the Jordanaires' clapping and chanting sits almost buried; it is a gospel quartet habit turned secular.","It was a huge double-sided hit and became a battleground: his hip-swinging television performances of it drew condemnation from critics and clergy, and Steve Allen had him sing it in a tuxedo to a basset hound. Since then the debate has shifted to Big Mama Thornton, whose original is now often preferred and whose small reward from the song has become a standard example of how the business treated Black women artists.","Hear Big Mama Thornton's 1953 original first, a slow, menacing blues with Johnny Otis's band, and then this. Notice the tempo, the drums and the change of meaning. Listen to the guitar solos for how raw and clumsy they are allowed to be; that roughness is part of the excitement."],"chuck-berry-maybellene":["A car chase in two and a half minutes: the singer in a V8 Ford chasing an unfaithful girlfriend in a Cadillac over the hills, reported like a sports commentary. The beat is a fast country two-step turned to rhythm and blues, Johnnie Johnson's piano rolls underneath, and Berry's guitar solo in the middle is jagged and bright. His diction is crisp and precise; every word lands, which was new in a genre used to shouted blues.","Berry came to Chicago in May 1955 carrying a tape; Muddy Waters sent him to Leonard Chess, who was more interested in his country-flavoured song than his blues. Maybellene was adapted from Ida Red, a western-swing tune made famous by Bob Wills. Disc jockey Alan Freed was given a co-writing credit, a common payola arrangement, and plugged it heavily. It went to number one on the rhythm-and-blues chart and into the pop top ten.","Under the rock and roll surface is a hillbilly fiddle tune: listen to the song's structure and the chorus melody and you can hear Ida Red. That country root is why the record sounded so strange on Black radio and so familiar on white. Freed's name in the credits, and that of another associate, took a share of Berry's royalties for decades.","It was an instant national hit and established Chess as a rock and roll label as well as a blues one. Historians now often put it forward as the true beginning of rock and roll, because it fused country and rhythm and blues so naturally in the hands of a Black writer. Berry's fight to recover his credit became an early chapter in rock's long argument over authorship and exploitation.","Listen to it as a story: the chase, the rain, the engine, the catch. Then find Bob Wills's Ida Red to hear the tune's country origin. Follow with Johnny B. Goode to hear how much Berry's guitar sound developed in three years."],"chuck-berry-roll-over-beethoven":["A manifesto disguised as a party song. It opens with Berry's ringing guitar introduction, then his voice asks a disc jockey for rhythm and blues and tells the great classical composers to make room. The band is a tight quartet with Johnnie Johnson's piano filling every gap. The tone is joking rather than angry, but the message is clear: a new music has arrived, and it has a beat.","Released by Chess in 1956, when rock and roll was being attacked by parents, preachers and the press. Berry reportedly wrote it with his sister Lucy in mind, who monopolised the family piano with classical pieces when he wanted to play rhythm and blues. Its claim that the old music should step aside matched a moment when radio was fragmenting and teenagers were buying records for themselves.","Berry names Tchaikovsky as well as Beethoven, telling the older composer to pass the news along, which gives the song its comic twist: it is written as a message to be delivered. Listen for the way the introduction's double-string guitar figure recurs as the solo; the whole song is built on that riff. The Beatles' 1963 cover, sung by George Harrison, turned it into a British invasion standard.","Critics have always read it as a statement of rock and roll's cultural challenge to high art. Its tone is now heard as playful rather than hostile; Berry admired classical music, and the song mocks snobbery more than Beethoven. It entered the rock canon through its covers, especially the Beatles', and remained a concert staple for Berry for sixty years.","Hear it next to the opening of Beethoven's Fifth, the music Berry is joking about. Then listen to the Beatles' version for how a British band hears it seven years later. Pay attention to the drive of Johnson's piano, which does as much as the guitar to push the song forward."],"chuck-berry-school-days":["A day at an American high school told in sharp, crowded verses: the classroom, the teachers, the lunchroom, then the release of the final bell and a run down to the jukebox. Each sung line is answered by a guitar phrase, voice and instrument trading like two people in conversation. The final verse turns from school to a hymn of praise for rock and roll itself. It is loose, fast and witty, a miniature film of teenage life.","Released in 1957, the year Berry toured the country on package shows and appeared in rock and roll films. It became one of his biggest hits, topping the rhythm-and-blues chart. Berry was then thirty, far older than the teenagers he wrote about; he observed them from outside, with a novelist's eye for detail. American high schools were newly desegregating, and the song's schoolroom is pointedly undescribed by race.","The call-and-response between voice and guitar comes from the blues and from gospel, where a preacher and congregation answer each other. Berry turns it into comedy: the guitar seems to comment on each line. Listen for the closing tribute to rock and roll, which became a slogan in its own right and gave its title to a 1987 documentary about Berry.","It has always been praised as one of the most observant of Berry's teenage songs, a model for the narrative songwriting of the Beach Boys and the Beatles. Some critics find the formula wearing thin by this point in his run, but the lyric's detail and the guitar dialogue keep it fresh, and its closing lines are among the most quoted in rock.","Listen to it as a conversation: hear each vocal line, then wait for the guitar's answer. Follow the day from morning to the jukebox. Then hear Sweet Little Sixteen and Johnny B. Goode, recorded soon after, to follow Berry's portrait gallery of American youth."],"chuck-berry-johnny-b-goode":["The most famous guitar introduction in rock: a burst of ringing double-string phrases over a stop, then the band falls in. The song tells of a poor country boy near New Orleans who can play the guitar like ringing a bell and dreams of seeing his name in lights. The chorus is a crowd urging him on. It is the rock and roll success story in two minutes and forty seconds, and the guitar tells it as much as the words.","Recorded in Chicago for Chess and released in 1958, at the height of Berry's run. It is largely autobiographical, and named partly after Goode Avenue in St. Louis, where Berry grew up, and partly after his pianist Johnnie Johnson. Berry later said he changed the hero's race in the lyric so that radio would play it, a small act of self-censorship that speaks to its moment.","The opening guitar figure is not wholly original: it closely follows the introduction Carl Hogan played on Louis Jordan's 1946 jump-blues record Ain't That Just Like a Woman, which Berry acknowledged. The debt runs straight from jump blues to rock. In 1977 NASA placed the song on the Voyager Golden Record, making it the only rock and roll recording to have left the solar system.","It was a pop and rhythm-and-blues hit and has since been named repeatedly by critics and musicians as the defining rock and roll record. Virtually every guitarist of the sixties learned the introduction. Debate now focuses less on its greatness than on Berry's own paradox: a Black artist writing the archetypal American rock success story, then watching others become far richer playing it.","Listen to the introduction a few times on its own, then hear Louis Jordan's Ain't That Just Like a Woman to catch the source. Notice how the stop-time verses build tension. Then hear it played by others, from the Beatles' circle to Jimi Hendrix, to see how it became a common language."],"little-richard-tutti-frutti":["It begins with a shouted string of nonsense syllables, a drummer's rhythm put into the mouth, and then the band slams in. Little Richard pounds the piano in hard, even eighth notes, the saxophones honk, Earl Palmer's drums drive the beat, and every verse is punctuated by whoops and a high falsetto shriek. The words are about two girls, but the words hardly matter. It lasts two and a half minutes and feels like being shoved.","Recorded in September 1955 at Cosimo Matassa's J&M studio in New Orleans, with producer Bumps Blackwell and a band of the city's best session players. The session was going badly until, in a break, Richard played a bawdy song from his club act; Blackwell had songwriter Dorothy LaBostrie clean up the words on the spot. Released by Specialty, it became a rhythm-and-blues hit, and Pat Boone's tame cover sold even more.","The famous opening shout was, by Richard's own account, a vocal imitation of a drum pattern he had been singing to himself while washing dishes in Macon. Listen to the piano: those relentless, even eighth notes replaced the lilting shuffle of older rhythm and blues and pointed toward the straight rock beat of the sixties. The original club lyric was sexually explicit; LaBostrie's version kept only the tune and the excitement.","It was received as a novelty by many adults and as a shock by others; its gender-blurring, gospel-shouting performer frightened the respectable. Since then its standing has only risen: it was among the first records added to the Library of Congress's National Recording Registry in its category, and critics often call it the moment rock and roll found its voice. The Boone cover is now remembered mostly as a cautionary tale.","Play it loud and follow the piano's even hammer rather than the voice. Then play Pat Boone's version for the contrast. After that, hear a gospel shouter like Marion Williams, whom Richard admired, to understand where the whoops come from."],"little-richard-long-tall-sally":["A breakneck twelve-bar about a gossipy uncle, a woman who knows how to have a good time, and a lot of ducking back in the alley to avoid being seen. It is even faster and harder than Tutti Frutti: the piano hammers, the saxophones blast short riffs, Lee Allen tears off a tenor solo, and Richard's voice leaps into falsetto at the end of lines. It lasts barely two minutes.","Recorded for Specialty in New Orleans and released in 1956. Bumps Blackwell later said they deliberately made the words tumble out too fast for a white crooner to copy, after Pat Boone's cover of Tutti Frutti had outsold the original. Boone covered it anyway. The song was credited to Richard, Blackwell and Enotris Johnson, a woman who reportedly brought in the idea for a song about a relative.","Listen to the pace of the lyric: whole phrases are compressed into a breath, a deliberate barrier to imitation. The song's hero is a man hiding from his wife, a comic scenario borrowed from vaudeville and blues. Richard's falsetto whoop at the end of lines was studied by Paul McCartney, whose version with the Beatles in 1964 copies it almost exactly.","It topped the rhythm-and-blues chart and made the pop top ten, outselling Boone's version this time. It has since become one of the most covered rock and roll songs; the Beatles made it the closer of many shows. Critics regard it as Richard at his most concentrated, the pure distilled essence of his Specialty sound.","Try to follow the words at speed; then give up and ride the piano. Compare McCartney's version with the Beatles to hear how closely a young Liverpool singer copied a Georgia shouter. Then hear the rest of the Specialty sides collected on Here's Little Richard."],"little-richard-heres-little-richard":["Twelve tracks, most under two and a half minutes, collecting the singles that made Little Richard's name: Tutti Frutti, Long Tall Sally, Rip It Up, Ready Teddy, Slippin' and Slidin', Jenny, Jenny and others. Heard together they have astonishing consistency: the same pounding piano, honking saxes and New Orleans rhythm section, and the voice switching between shouts, groans and a high gospel whoop. A few slower blues give breathing space, but the pace rarely drops.","Released by Specialty in 1957, Richard's debut album was assembled from recordings made in New Orleans and Los Angeles over the previous two years. In the fifties the album was an afterthought to singles in rock and roll; this one was a collection of hits for fans who wanted them in one place. Within months of its release, Richard announced on an Australian tour that he was quitting rock and roll for the ministry.","Listen to the backing band, much of it the same New Orleans players who backed Fats Domino and others at Cosimo Matassa's studio. Drummer Earl Palmer's playing across these tracks is a document of the shift from shuffle rhythm to the straight rock beat. Richard's gospel upbringing is everywhere: the moans and whoops come from Pentecostal worship, turned to profane use he later regretted.","It became one of the most celebrated albums of fifties rock, regularly placed in critics' lists of the greatest ever made. Its reputation rests on the songs rather than any album concept. Richard himself, torn between church and stage, spent much of his life alternately disowning and reclaiming these records. Today he is honoured as a founder who received too little credit and pay in his prime.","Play it straight through and let the sameness become a force. Listen for the moments where the saxophone solo takes over and then Richard's voice crashes back. Pair it with Fats Domino's hits, cut in the same studio, to hear how different a singer can make the same band sound."],"fats-domino-the-fat-man":["A rolling New Orleans boogie: Domino's left hand walks the bass while his right hammers repeated chords, the band riffs behind him, and he sings a cheerful self-portrait about his size and his nickname. In the middle he hums a wordless solo that imitates a muted trumpet. It is relaxed and bouncy rather than wild. The piano is the whole engine; everything else fills in around it.","Recorded at Cosimo Matassa's J&M studio in New Orleans in December 1949 and released by Imperial early in 1950. It was the first product of Domino's partnership with trumpeter and bandleader Dave Bartholomew, and it reportedly sold a million copies over the following years. It was adapted from Junker Blues, a song about addiction associated with Champion Jack Dupree, rewritten as a harmless piece of self-mockery.","Listen to the wordless vocal break: Domino imitates a trumpet with his voice, a trick from the New Orleans street tradition. The drumming, possibly by Earl Palmer, uses a heavy backbeat that would become standard in rock and roll. The song's model, Junker Blues, had a darker subject, mentioned only in passing here, which Bartholomew replaced with a comic portrait.","Its commercial success made Imperial and Domino and has been cited as one of several claimants to the first rock and roll record, years before the term was current. Critics praise it now as the purest example of New Orleans rhythm and blues and the gentle, piano-led strain of early rock that ran parallel to the harder Memphis and Chicago sounds.","Follow the left hand of the piano: it is a boogie-woogie bass line that the whole band follows. Then listen for the humming solo. Hear Jelly Roll Morton or James P. Johnson for the long New Orleans and stride piano lineage behind it, and then Domino's later hits for the smoother sound."],"fats-domino-aint-that-a-shame":["A lament about a lover who has left, sung with such warmth and good humour that it hardly sounds sad. It opens with Domino's voice alone on the title line, then the band falls in with a steady backbeat. Domino's piano triplets roll under the whole song, and a tenor saxophone takes a brief solo. At under two and a half minutes, it is a model of economy: two verses, a chorus and a lilt.","Written by Domino and Dave Bartholomew, it was released by Imperial in 1955, just as rock and roll was being noticed by white audiences. It became Domino's first big crossover pop hit. Pat Boone's cover went to number one on the pop chart, a notorious example of white artists covering Black records; Domino's original also sold strongly, and his audience kept widening.","John Lennon said this was the first song he learned to play, on a banjo his mother taught him. Listen for the piano triplets under a straight backbeat; that combination is a New Orleans hallmark. Boone reportedly wanted to correct the grammar of the title, which tells you what the cover versions were designed to do.","It was received as an irresistible pop record and has remained a standard, covered by Cheap Trick and many others. The story of the Boone cover has come to stand for a whole period of the business. Domino's own version has long since outlasted Boone's in critical esteem, and his easygoing style is increasingly recognised as a crucial ingredient of early rock.","Listen for the triplets in the piano: count three in each beat. Hear Pat Boone's cover once, then return to Domino to feel the difference. Follow with Blueberry Hill to hear his style applied to an older pop standard."]});